<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/0108587d8205169ad8a003bbd4431de1fb0a28a019c84ce4a259bb8cb723706c> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Two Homeric couples,
      Helen and Paris, Andromache and Hector, and Kebriones, Hector's squire. All the figures are
      inscribed. In the center of the scene, which is not symmetrically arranged, Hector takes his
      leave from Andromache; on the left are Helen and Paris, and on the right Kebriones with
      horses. From left to right, Helen, with a band on her head, wrapped in a purple himation,
      turns her face away from Paris who is looking at her. The white of her face is fairly well
      preserved. Near her legs there is the inscription of her name: (retr.) <foreign
      lang="Greek">*E*L*E*N*E</foreign>. Next to her, Paris (Alexandros), facing to the
      left, is dressed like an archer, in short purple chiton carrying a bow in his hand a bow-case
      and quiver on his back. He wears a winged boots and is named <foreign
      lang="Greek">*P*A*R*I[*S]</foreign> (retr.) in the inscription in front of his
      head.</P><P>The next figure to the right is Andromache, who, dressed in a long
      chiton, opens up to her husband her purple himation which covers most of her body as well as
      her head. The white which covered her flesh has largely gone, leaving a dull black surface.
      The inscription <foreign lang="Greek">*A*N*D*R*O*M*A*X*E</foreign> (retr.) is
      behind her, near her legs. She is conversing with Hector who is the main and tallest figure of
      the composition. He faces to the left, dressed like an hoplite, with Chalcidian helmet, purple
      metal corslet, purple greaves, shield and carries a spear. On the shield is represented a
      swooping eagle as blazon. His tall helmet breaks the decoration on the shoulder. Under the
      helmet there is the inscription (retr.) <foreign
      lang="Greek">*E*K*T*O*R</foreign>. His face is contoured with
      incision.</P><P>Behind Hector in the right part of the vase a youth mounted on one
      horse and leading another. He must be Hector's squire and is called Kebriones by the
      inscription (retr.) <foreign lang="Greek">*K*E[*B*R*I]*O*N*E*S</foreign>. Behind
      him is an eagle in flight. The second horse is presumably for Hector who is leaving for the
      battlefield.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: Two youths dressed in
      short purple tight-fitting chitons are galloping on horses. In the free spaces of the side
      there are four birds. Arias suggests that one of them seems to be a duck, the rest are birds
      of prey; Simon suggests they are a vulture, a swan, and two other kind of birds. Much use of
      the purple for details on the horses and birds which are between the horses' legs, or flying
      in the background. The horses are large black magnificent animals. The one at right is bending
      his head down.</P><P>Under the handles there are two small figures. Under the left
      one, a bearded nude male running; his face is contoured with incision. Under the right, a
      walking man wearing a purple chlamys.</P><P>There is almost no doubt about the
      interpretation of the scenes in this extraordinary Chalcidian krater. It was assumed that the
      presence of the two Homeric couples on side A set the scene in Book 6 of Homer's <hi
      rend="ital">Iliad</hi> (<bibl n="Hom. Il. 6.340ff">Hom. Il.
      6.340ff</bibl>; <bibl n="Hom. Il. 6.484ff">Hom. Il. 6.484ff</bibl>), the
      leave-taking for the battle of Paris and Hector. But probably it refers to two different
      moments of the <hi rend="ital">Iliad</hi>. Simon, and with her most of the authors
      in <bibl>LIMC</bibl>, for example, claim that, while for Hector and Andromache
      this is the moment of the farewell described in <bibl n="Hom. Il. 6.484ff">Hom. Il.
      6.484ff</bibl>, Paris is not leaving, but arriving from the battlefield released by
      Aphrodite as in book 3 of the <hi rend="ital">Iliad</hi> (<bibl n="Hom. Il.
      3.427ff">Hom. Il. 3.427ff</bibl>). In this regard Paris' winged shoes are considered
      a proof of the divine intervention of Aphrodite, saving him from the attack of Menelaos and
      bringing him safe back to Helen. Simon claims that the painter's intention was to put together
      in opposition two very different Homeric couples in two different and contrasting moments:
      Helen and Paris, with her turning her face away from him and regretting seeing him back safe
      from the attack of his former husband Menelaus, and Hector and Andromache at the moment he is
      leaving for the battle. However, their son Astyanax, an important character in this tense
      moment, is not present in the picture. The result is a thoughtful composition consciously
      asymmetric in the form and in the meaning. The figure of Kebriones, Hector's squire in some
      sources his half brother is of special interest, in that he is surprisingly represented as a
      horseman, instead of as a charioteer, as he is generally shown.</P><P>The
      representation on side B seems to be mostly decorative. This may be no more than a new
      interpretation of the ancient frieze of galloping horsemen, but it might also be seen as the
      battle that is going on while the meetings on side A are taking place.</P><P>The
      figures of small naked youths and men running under the handles recall the same type of
      figures used many times on the works of The Affecter (see the figures on <rs
      type="vase">Wrzburg L 176</rs>). The presence of this type of figures gives a sense
      of continuity to the scenes on both sides which is not completely real. It seems to respond to
      a phase in the concept of the decoration of the entire vessel: half way between the continuous
      frieze and the two separated scenes, each one with a complete sense.</P><P>The lip
      of the krater flares out slightly and is decorated with a dense stepped pattern. The neck is
      black but decorated on each side with four solid circles in purple surrounded by white dots.
      At the junction of neck and shoulder a tongue pattern, black and purple. Underneath the figure
      scene a zone of linked buds and flowers of buxom type typical of Chalcidian, then vertically
      sloping zig-zag lines, followed by rays springing from the base the foot. The disk is black on
      its top surface and purple on the upper third of the side. The rest of the side is reserved. A
      thick fillet painted purple at junction of body and foot.</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Lid</hi>: On the lid is painted an old animal motif. From the center
      outwards rays, then linked flowers and buds, then a circular frieze consisting of seven large
      black boars moving to the left. It recalls the old animal friezes on the Chalcidian vases, but
      the trick of overlapping two of the animals changes the impression and turns it from a simple
      frieze to a living, running flock of boars. Much purple and incision. Filling
      rosettes.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/0113797249042425f1ba9709c3e45a877baf17802a93e6c86a78a4729f3d0ce6> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Standing male in short, belted tunic"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/011ae80d906315f6369f3536d66017c188e452ef7018b4bbf4c0c102fe5ddeaa> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<bibl>LIMC</bibl>, 7.141 s.v. Paidia no. 3 (A. Kossatz-Deissmann);
      <bibl>Shapiro 1993</bibl>, 83, 183, 238 no. 32, figs. 36, 41; J.H. Oakley, <hi
      rend="ital">CVA, The Walters Art Gallery</hi> 1 (1992) 35-36, pl. 38.1-3, fig. 11.2
      (with previous bibliography).""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/011b37ab33cf67bbfff92ccea355eada0c60e40d8ce431d172a49a7423d5b337> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Panel: warrior between bearded man and a woman"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/01288114c8d72ad70dcc15fe93e9cfb59b489fc779f5d66062e260708412219e> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Persephone (Kore), wearing a thin chiton, is seated on the right, while her mother, Demeter, wearing a peplos and a himation veiled over her head, stands behind her."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/012a0991c4bdc0904c198d65b2e947b01ab59fdba43fadc29a09e476f1676b40> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Comstock & Vermeule 1976</bibl>, 204-205 no. 324" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/0131c26600eb01e49b23d74d5668a34130dde17cf9d1321cf4dcd71fb645cc6b> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>In the tondo, bordered by a red band, a bearded man is
      embracing a youth. The man is on his feet, while the youth is seated on a block, with a
      walking stick at his side. Both figures are partially draped.</P><P>On the
      exterior on side A, four naked youths are practicing sports--the long jump and the diskos
      throw--while a draped flutist plays in the center of the scene. At the far right, a partially
      draped youth loosens soil with a pick, to prepare the ground for the
      jumpers.</P><P>On side B, four naked bearded athletes practice javelin tossing,
      while a fifth moves forward carrying in his left hand a diskos decorated with an image of an
      owl. An altar is visible behind this figure on the right. Above it are hanging an aryballos
      and a sponge.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/013457a97600d167c9c93424cad21136452ccd3964ee8674b313dac3486e06e9> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Lewes 1920</bibl>, no. 23" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/0137ccbae1c2f66784e2f3412283179fb39901eb507970b57bf3e5f2fa80d8f2> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><bibl>M. Moore and M.S.P. Philippides, Attic Black-figured Pottery
      (Princeton, 1986), 62, 63.</bibl>; <bibl>CVA, 1926, Great Britain, British Museum
      (London, 1926) 3-4</bibl></P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/013cb5d208090bc92f1a3d35cf7b381c9bee68d21bc51595ec0cb99149fa89f6> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A:</hi> Amazonomachy. In the
      center, Herakles attacks Andromache to his right, aiming his sword at her mid-section and
      grasping her helmet by the crest support. Herakles is wearing his lionskin and a chitoniskos,
      as well as a baldric from which are slung his scabbard and quiver. Andromache is fleeing to
      the right, turning her head to look back at her attacker. In addition to her helmet, she wears
      a corslet, nebris, and chitoniskos, and a baldric over her left shoulder to attach her
      scabbard. She carries a spear and shield with tripod device; the shield is positioned to
      protect her from Herakles's sword. To the left of Herakles, an Amazon archer flees to the
      left, while looking back over her shoulder at the fight. The archer wears a cap, chitoniskos,
      nebris and quiver; she is holding a bow in her left hand. To the right of Andromache, an
      Amazon hoplite approaches in defense, wearing an Attic helmet, corslet, and chitoniskos, and
      carrying a spear and shield with a partly visible white ball as device. Her scabbard is
      hanging from a baldric over her right shoulder.</P><P>Added red is used for the
      skirt of the archer's chitoniskos, the border of her quiver, folds in Herakles's skirt, the
      borders of Andromache's and her defender's shields and the skirts of their chitoniskoi. Added
      white is seen on the flesh of the three Amazons, Herakles's baldric, sword hilt, dot clusters
      on his chitoniskos skirt, the shield devices and baldrics of Andromache and her defender, the
      dotted inner edges of their skirts, and a few dots on the crest of Andromache's helmet and the
      hilt of her defender's sword.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B:</hi>
      warriors leaving home. In the center, a naked, bearded warrior is attaching a greave to his
      left leg, spreading the sides to fit it onto his calf. He has already attached the right
      greave. In front of him stands a woman in chiton and himation, holding a pair of spears and a
      Boeotian shield with two panther masks as device. Facing the naked warrior from behind stands
      an already armed warrior with high-crested Corinthian helmet, round shield, greaves, and two
      spears. This warrior's shield device appears to be a ploughshare. On the extreme left, facing
      the two warriors from behind, stands an elderly man wearing chiton and himation. Behind the
      woman on the right stands a third warrior dressed and armed like the second with Corinthian
      helmet, shield, greaves, and a pair of spears. The device on this warrior's shield is a bull's
      head. Like the other figures he stands passive yet ready for action, watching the scene in the
      center.</P><P>Added red on this side appears in every other fold of the older
      man's himation, the border of the left-hand warrior's shield, the arming warrior's hair and
      beard, three folds of the woman's himation, the border of the shield she holds, as well as the
      dots on the border of the right-hand warrior's shield. Added white includes the older man's
      hair and clusters of dots on his himation, the three shield devices, the woman's flesh and
      clusters of dots on her himation.</P><P>The vessel has triple handles, unglazed
      inside; surrounding them are nearly identical triple lotus and quadruple palmette designs. The
      shoulder of the vessel has tongues that alternate black and red. A molded ridge separates the
      shoulder from the neck, whose design is a double chain of lotus and palmette, with added red
      at the cores of each and at the tips of some lotuses. The surface of the mouth is reserved;
      its interior and that of the neck are glazed. Under the main panel appears a thin groundline,
      followed by a left-pointing key pattern, a chain of buds, and a band of rays. A red base
      fillet completes the design. The foot is reserved
      underneath.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

