<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/05ddd3defbcd34a72dacfce47a9e0ce9ad30aac0a939b627e5b58d10fb71fa83> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Woman and youth at a tomb. A wide, altar-like tomb with a
      meander ornamented molding at the top stands in the center of the scene. A lyre and large box
      stand on top of the monument. Five vessels (three lekythoi, an oinochoe, and a pyxis with a
      high stem foot) stand on the upper of the two steps at the front of the tomb. A youth with
      shoulder-length hair stands on the left holding out a wreath. He wears a mantle. A woman
      stands on the right facing foward, her head turned toward the tomb and her right hand extended
      toward it. She wears a peplos, and her hair is worn in a bun at the
      back.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/05f1d1e1189cca5f9dc39db0f72d31faa56db9ec2fd9e5b1140afe15aa715adc> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Exterior: Side A:</hi> Triptolemos
      in his winged car is about to set off and bring wheat to humankind. He holds a bunch of grains
      in his left hand and an elaborately decorated phiale in his right. He wears a long, sleeved
      chiton and himation draped over his left shoulder. His wreathed hair is worn long with
      dangling curls in front of his ears and framing his face. Over him runs his name, <foreign
      lang="Greek">*T*R*I*P*P*T*O*L*E*M*O*S</foreign>, Triptolemos. The car itself is
      magnificently decorated with great spreading wings and arching serpents hissing on either
      side, as well as Ionic columns supporting the seat itself. Persephone stands on the right,
      looking center. She holds a blazing torch in her left hand and pours a farewell wine libation
      from an oinochoe with her right into the phiale of Triptolemos. She wears a radiated diadem
      and earrings on her head and a long, sleeved chiton with an elaborately bordered himation. Her
      name is inscribed, <foreign lang="Greek">*F*E*R*O*F*A*T*T*A</foreign>,
      Pherophatta. On the extreme right is a young nymph, Eleusis, a personification of the
      locality. She is dressed like Persephone in a long, sleeved chiton and himation which she
      wears modestly draped over her crowned head. With her right hand she holds up a flower and her
      left lifts the edge of her chiton. Her name is also inscribed <foreign
      lang="Greek">*E*L*E*U*S*I*S</foreign>, Eleusis. Behind the chariot is the goddess of
      grains, Persephone's mother, Demeter, who holds grains in her left hand and a flaming torch in
      her right. She wears an embattled diadem, earrings, and long, sleeved chiton. Her himation is
      richly embroidered with small winged figures, dolphins, eagles and other birds, panthers,
      chariot races, foot races, pegasoi, and humans in black silhouette. The borders consist of
      spirals and upright palmettes. Her name is inscribed, <foreign
      lang="Greek">*D*E*M*E*T*R*E</foreign>, Demeter. Beneath the right handle and to the
      right of the nymph Eleusis, is the king Eumolpos who originated the rites of Demeter at
      Eleusis. He is seated to the right, facing left, holding his scepter in his left hand. The
      bearded king has his hair long and wears a sleeveless chiton and a bordered himation draped
      over his left shoulder. His name is inscribed, <foreign
      lang="Greek">*E*U*M*O*L*P*P*O*S</foreign>, Eumolpos. Beneath the other handle, seated
      to the left but facing right, towards Demeter, is the god Poseidon, identified by the scepter
      of authority which he holds in his right hand and the dolphin which he holds in his left. He
      is bearded and wears a sleeveless chiton and himation which is draped over his left shoulder.
      His name is inscribed beside him, <foreign
      lang="Greek">*P*O*S*E*I*D*O*N</foreign>, Poseidon.</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Side B:</hi> At the feet of Eumolpos is a swan which follows Zeus, who
      walks to the right. He carries his attribute, the thunderbolt, in his right hand, and his
      scepter of authority in his left. He has his wreathed hair short, with a long curl behind his
      ear, and wears a mustache and long beard. The god wears a long, sleeved chiton and a bordered
      himation over his left shoulder. He is further identified by his name, <foreign
      lang="Greek">*Z*E*U*S</foreign>, Zeus. The central figure on this side is the god
      Dionysos, who stands facing right and holding a sprouting staff in his left hand. He has his
      hair long in back, a mustache, and beard, and wears a bordered himation over his long chiton.
      In his hair he wears an ivy wreath. His name, too, is inscribed, <foreign
      lang="Greek">*D*I*O*N*U*S*O*S</foreign>, Dionysos. The scene is related to that on
      Side A since the female figure furthest right, Amphitrite, wife of Poseidon, looks off right
      in his direction. She is identified by her attribute, the dolphin, which she holds in her left
      hand, and her name which is inscribed, <foreign
      lang="Greek">*A[*N]*F*I*T*R*I*T*E</foreign>, Amphitrite. She moves to the left,
      wearing a fillet, earrings, a necklace, and a chiton with overfold. With her right hand she
      holds up her himation for her husband in a gesture which is associated with brides and wives.
      The figures walk on a reserved double line which runs round the entire
      exterior.</P><P>The event on Side A takes place at Eleusis. The torches held by
      Persephone and Demeter are typical of the equipment of the Eleusinian mysteries, held in their
      honor. They also indicate that the action is taking place at
      night.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

