<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/06e8c1c69d780518075c8a3f5a841a2af0c2c230c564579558d025a3a7029fc2> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Athena Promachos standing in a frontal pose"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/06eaa2232515abb74d85ab5c8715bf4ad724fb0aa1cb6ca761e973fb3b88b2d2> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Three metopes around neck: bird (r.), doe (l.), and bird (l.).
      The bodies of the animals are lined and the filling ornament is basically the same in each
      frame: dotted rosettes and circles with "x"-centers surrounded by dots, sometimes with tails
      or stems of dots. Three glazed bands on the interior of the vase near the top; lines in
      triplicate abound on the vase to define and separate each metope from a vertical band of
      zigzag, which in turn is bordered by vertical glazed lines. This "triglyph and metope" frieze
      is enhanced above by two pairs of lines and a row of dots and below by four lines, a row of
      dots, and three more lines. On the belly, a row of connected or running dots and five evenly
      spaced, well-drawn lines; then a zone in black glaze at the bottom. There are three lines on
      the handle-strut and two "x"-filled metopes on the handle-face, having nine lines above the
      first metope, three lines between the two metopes and nine lines between the second and the
      bottom of the handle.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/070750a54b4681bf1dd7ebf610294de10f348d97dfbe290cafca3af750354620> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Svoronos 1903-12, 292-93, pl. 37.1</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/0716f42af5bd05df64c222ec688837a12bad662064a7b1aa0e67aa18c0a25bff> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Comstock & Vermeule 1971, no. 407</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/0717dfcd3083699cd33997949199b4bf46a08ca86e666fbfc14831bbe5152e56> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The scenes on both the obverse and reverse depict episodes from
      the Aethiopis. <hi rend="head">Side A</hi>: the struggle over the body of the dead
      Achilles. The scene is enacted by four figures. The foreign Amasos struggles with Menelaos on
      the left, while a warrior attempts to lift the dead Achilles on the right. The warrior
      straddles the body of Achilles, his weight on his forward, right leg as he bends over to lift
      Achilles, supporting his neck with one arm while he pulls him up by one of his wrists. His
      cape, decorated with a snake and a panther, flies out behind him. He also wears greaves, a
      short tunic or chiton, and upper body armor decorated with spirals. Achilles lies in profile,
      face upward and head to the right. His head tips back as he is lifted, and his helmet falls
      behind him. He is dressed in greaves, spiral leggings, a short tunic decorated with meanders
      and stars in a checkerboard pattern, an undergarment with a pleated hip skirt decorated with
      incised stripes, and a black cape elaborately decorated with rosettes and a meander border. On
      the left, Amasos struggles with Menelaos, leaning away from him to the left, his legs already
      buckling under him as he bleeds from a chestwound. He holds a club in his right hand and is
      looking at Menelaos. Menelaos lunges at Amasos, legs spread wide, holding his spear in his
      raised right arm and his shield in his left. Amasos has Negroid hair and features, and is
      naked but for a small cape around his neck which is decorated with a zig-zag pattern. Menelaos
      is wearing greaves, spiral leggings, a short-sleeved tunic with a meander border and a helmet.
      His shield is white and is decorated with a dog at its center. Amasos, Menelaos and Achilles
      are labelled.</P><P><hi rend="head">Side B</hi>: the rescue of the
      body of Antilochos. Large sections of this side are missing, but the scene appears to have
      involved five figures. The dead Antilochos lies along the bottom of the scene, his head to the
      left and his arms at his sides, palms down. He has a red beard and wears a white tunic, red
      greaves, and a short fringed garment. On the left, two naked male figures run away from the
      fallen Antilochos, their speed indicated by their wide spread legs and their forward feet,
      which are off the ground. They are pursued by two helmeted warriors carrying spears and
      shields. The shield of the foremost of the two superposed warriors is white with a bird in the
      center. The back shield is black with a red border. Antilochos is labelled, as is one of the
      figures on the right, Euphorbos.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/071fa8292401ed06b467e7cc0deef935053a29d73f5e674db367b51f9c9e13f1> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
