<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/108598040bf76f6bd8a6f86923e66774c44fb11c64e6c9ced63dd0dff2026344> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Bands of geometric patternwork, with animals in metopes."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1089884934cbe270176d51837921063dbb7da9d419152ad9da211f32f37959a1> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>A woman, dressed in a long chiton and himation, stands facing
      right, putting clothes in an open chest that stands before her. Her hair is tied back and held
      in a snood, the style of an unmarried woman (see Karouzou 1970), and she wears a plain fillet.
      In her extended arms she holds a garment (identified by the weight at its corner), bunched
      rather than folded, over the chest into which she will place it. Adams (1983) suggests that
      she is placing a wrapped object into the chest, but the drapery weight argues against this
      interpretation. The chest has lion's paw feet and is decorated on the side with a six-pointed
      star/rosette. The lid is hinged, indicated by a dot within a circle, and its front edge has a
      lip that would overlap the front edge of the chest when closed. Behind the woman stands a
      chair (<hi rend="ital">klismos</hi>), its seat cushion decorated with stripes and
      XXXs. A short fringe hangs from the back of the cushion. A mirror and a fillet (decorated with
      dots and stripes, or of dotted fabric tied into a roll) hang in the field, indicating the wall
      of the room.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/108db2441fb8bd9ffac22f666696e9843244e9b2d2e41c4fda4008ab8c6b1d5e> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Apollo."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/108e3d12c95fbe9b3383c702ca060a94ef74129e4a2d2e19689fadb0fb9210f4> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>On front is a large octopus, with four arms extended on either
      side and the head painted red. Below and on either side of the head are dolphins. Running
      toward octopus on either side is a nude red-haired youth with a red-spotted cloak over one arm
      and holding a spear. The youth at left carries a spear, while the spear of the right youth is
      largely effaced. Shoulder: a siren with red and black feathers stands between two males
      cloaked in himations.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/108fef224a3b5a4c4d02478ab8c80d9cc49a930c4b9233d2540a44608ecc0247> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Epione, Asklepios, Hierodoulos, and two families of worshippers"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/109396241bfb663b408651d1dd52083614a4bbb68004c42204d0e295a5b4f6fd> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Attribution qualifier"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Recalls the" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300056109> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/10a3ef06f29ac2284d7e2deab2dfa7918d8e6bb589a7e3535143282abdd7c826> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Side A: Arming. A youth puts on a corslet while a youth in
      Scythian costume looks on from either side. At the left of the composition an unbearded youth
      in Scythian costume is shown in profile, facing right. He wears elaborately decorated
      close-fitting trousers and long-sleeved shirt, as well as a Scythian cap with rounded crown
      and long neck and cheek pieces. A quiver is attached to his waist. He holds up a bow in his
      left hand and a shield, presumably belonging to the youth who is arming, held in his right
      almost touches the ground. The shield is shown in profile view and is decorated with a dancing
      satyr. In the center, a youth looks down with concentration while he fastens a leather corslet
      over his chitoniskos. He is depicted as unbearded, but the first growth on his cheek is shown
      with dilute glaze. He wears a fillet rendered in added red in his hair. The youth in Scythian
      dress at the right of the composition in profile faces left. He is outfitted like his
      companion on the left, but instead of a shield he holds an axe in his right hand. The figures
      are labelled. The majority of scenes of Scythian archers are produced in the period 530-500.
      Pinney has pointed out that the costume loses any specific ethnic meaning and is often used to
      represent a generic archer, <bibl>Pinney 1983, 130</bibl>.</P><P>Side
      B: Athletes and trainer. In the center of the scene, a naked youth, shown in profile facing
      right, lifts a discus over his head with both hands. This youth is labelled as Phayllos, a
      famous pentathlete. At the right of the composition, a trainer stands facing this youth. He is
      older and bearded with a mantle wrapped around his torso and draped over one shoulder. He
      gestures with his left hand and holds out a forked stick in his right. A second naked
      unbearded youth is shown in profile facing right at the left of the scene. The first growth of
      beard is shown on his cheek in dilute glaze. The two youths wear fillets in added red and the
      man a wreath. The figures are labelled.</P><P>Many elements of this vase are found
      in other work by Euthymides. The painter appears to have been very fond of three figure
      compositions, often breaking the frame. Also the extensive use of inscriptions is typical of
      his work. More important, however, is the painter's interest in rendering anatomy and
      perspective. Euthymides and the other Pioneers were concerned with rendering the human body in
      motion and seen from different angles. The earlier Archaic convention of showing a body with
      profile legs and frontal torso is replaced with attempts to render the torso in proper profile
      and three quarter views. This becomes most difficult when trying to show the naked body
      without drapery which can be used to hide awkward transitions in anatomical rendering.
      Euthymides' scenes of athletes and komasts show the artist attempting to master a more
      accurate rendering of the body seen from several views. His foreshortened views such as the
      profile shield and the frontal foot on Side A of this vase are relatively successful. His
      interest in anatomy is also seen in Euthymides' extensive rendering of interior musculature
      with dilute glaze lines.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/10aa45b925686b627e682345ede5f71dc4506e13721a5f942379f27f260512ad> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Hermes carrying lamb in his left arm"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/10c4defe8f8f60279a5ced0b1c1f02b190f17022c15df64fd9c5b09936493808> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Singing reveler with barbiton"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/10e55df94d843b1118933d6acea278deae129d4a56dc8f96cc294b75cdc05e1e> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Three uninterrupted friezes of real and fantastic animals and
      one human figure. In the center of one side of the upper band, a swan stands to the right
      between two sphinxes; the swan has a red stripe on its upraised wing and rows of white dots on
      its wing, neck, and head. The sphinxes are seated and have long hair, fillets, red coverts and
      secondary feathers, and rows of white dots on the shoulders and wings. To the right of the
      second sphinx is a bearded man dressed in a red tunic; he is a komast, a so-called "padded
      dancer," engaged in a vigorous dance and slapping himself on the rump. To the right of the
      komast, under the handle, a lion or leopard is face to face with a grazing goat, both with red
      stripes and rows of white dots on their bodies. Continuing on to the other side of the cup, a
      lion is face to face with a bull; both are now largely effaced, but must also have been
      decorated with added color. To the right of the bull, a leopard faces a grazing goat, followed
      by a lion, who approaches the sphinxes and swan mentioned above; leopard, goat, and lion are
      decorated with red stripes and rows of white dots.</P><P>In the middle frieze,
      from left to right, are the following animals: a lion facing a bull; a leopard facing a bull;
      a swan standing between a lion and a leopard, both of which face away from it; a grazing goat.
      All of these animals are decorated with red stripes and rows of white dots. In the lower
      frieze, which is more narrow than the two above, six hunting dogs course around the lower body
      to the left; each has added red on the neck.</P><P>Parallel stripes on the upper
      rim. In the handle zone on either side is a band of vertical, parallel zigzags; at either end
      of each band, flanking the handles, are pairs of rosettes framed by groups of vertical
      stripes. The three animal friezes are framed and separated from one another by slender black
      bands decorated with white rosettes. Throughout all three figure friezes, filling every
      possible gap, are numerous rosettes acting as filling ornaments. On the lower body is a band
      of double rays. Stripes on the handles; foot and interior
      black.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/10e8cc95e7b29a5ce225b7701ad68175b32cbd5f4d7460a25964b72b50cd9016> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
