<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/143e9bf1d15599a5cc4c433cd16fe896ca8992a1788415ca9fe6d68014e8b345> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """The black lip contrasts with the ray-decorated neck, divided from the
      shoulder by a narrow, raised, black band above a beaded frieze. The neck frieze illustrates a
      prowling panther (left) and lion (right), both shown in profile, approaching each other (the
      lion turns back to the right). They are divided by palmette from which two lotus buds emerge.
      The scene on the body illustrates the hero Herakles (with lion-skin helmet) fighting Kyknos
      (with Doric helmet), to punish him for robbing sacrificial cattle in the sanctuary of Apollo.
      Herakles is characteristically armed with his club, in his right hand, while his sword and
      quiver fall at his left side; Kyknos defends himself with shield and lance, and a sword also
      falls at his left side. Two men stand behind each combattant.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

