<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/16ae9e03f49943a33f914224c90ffbabd37b8a0b22d46ccd11262ab3e8a86654> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Side A: Fight, with Athena and Hermes watching<lb>Side B: Citharode"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/16b1a532ed03fb0e96186c3ce3fe23a05bd93d5925cb130e4834340d73a01540> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Above both scenes runs a frieze of lotus buds. Below the scenes
      is a band of rays. Red lines circle the vase on the foot, above the band of rays, on the neck
      above the top handle attachments, on the upper edge of the rim, and two just under the main
      scenes. In addition, a red line extends just above both scenes from right to left handle
      attachment.</P><P>Side A depicts the judgement of Paris. On the far right Paris
      himself is seated. In front of him is growing a shrub; his seat is shown only by a black line
      that curves with his body. Paris is wearing a mantle and holding a six-stringed lyre. He
      raises his right hand as if in greeting or amazement. In front of Paris and facing him stands
      Hermes, wearing a chlamys, winged shoes, and a pilos, and holding a wreath in his left hand
      and a kerykeion in his right. Hermes is leading the draped goddesses Aphrodite, Athena, and
      Hera into the presence of Paris. The first and last goddesses' chitons are decorated with
      white flowers, while Athena's peplos has red dots and white crosses as well. The first and
      third goddesses wear red-striped cloaks over their shoulders and down their backs, as well as
      headbands, while Athena wears an aegis and a high-crested helmet and holds a spear in her
      right hand. Red is used for parts of the garments as described, Paris's frontal hair and
      beard, Hermes's beard and wreath, the goddesses' headbands and the pupils of their eyes, the
      lower border of the first goddess's chiton, and the upper border of Athena's helmet crest.
      White is used for the goddesses' skins and the edge of Hermes' pilos, as well as dots on
      Athena's helmet crest.</P><P>On side B, Menelaos, armed as a hoplite, is leading
      Helen. Menelaos is wearing a short sleeved chitoniskos, greaves, and a high-crested helmet,
      and he carries a shield with a tripod as its device in his left hand. His sword in its
      scabbard hangs from a strap over his right shoulder. With his right hand, he grasps the left
      hand of Helen to pull her along. Helen is wearing a peplos decorated with white blossoms and
      red dots, a mantle pulled up over the back of her head and a necklace. Behind her on the far
      left is another hoplite, walking away from the pair but turning his head to look back at them.
      He is dressed similarly to Menelaos, except that his helmet has a low crest, and no strap is
      visible on his shoulder or back. His shield, which he carries in his left hand, has the skull
      of a steer for its device. Red is used on this side for the chitoniskoi of the hoplites, the
      shield and greave edges, Helen's pupils, the lower edge of her peplos, and the folds in her
      mantle. White is used for Helen's skin, the scabbard straps and the sword handles and tips of
      the scabbards, as well as portions of the helmet crests.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/16b70e2a6a66f70ff66097d3d38536779d6cde5536ba0ecb903ddb24dfe56603> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>A youth chases a hare in the vicinity of a tomb. The locale
      indicated is a rocky hill slope, rising to the upper border on the left. At the foot of the
      hill is a plain stele, surmounted by a cornice and resting on a quadruple plinth. Around it is
      tied a taenia, another is laid on the top, and two more rest on the plinth and rocks below. A
      hare runs up the hill past the stele, closely pursued by a large hound which is urged on by a
      nude youth on the right. He carries a mantle on his left arm and a club over his shoulder and
      extends his right towards the hare. On the left, under the hill, a second youth strides
      forward with his left foot raised on a rock. He hurls a stone at the hare with his right hand
      and grasps a club in his left. He wears a mantle over his left
      shoulder.</P><P>There is a quite unusual conflation of two scenes: the tomb and
      the hare hunt. What resulted is a personal vision of a funerary scene appropriate to a
      deceased youth who had enjoyed hunting hares.</P><P>The body is covered with a
      white slip, on which the design is drawn in brown outline. Above the design is a band of
      unbroken meanders. On the shoulder is a pattern of three palmettes, painted in matt brown on
      white. Above is an egg pattern. Preliminary sketch is visible in transparent, shiny
      lines.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/16c5a827ef0b24cda945ad1926cd5e2bb5a203704f03aa9829e24ff04450bf0c> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Upper panel border alternating palmettes and lotus flowers;
      lower border meander broken by squares with crosses; side border narrow reserved lines. Volute
      with spreading tendrils on top of handle, band of tongues beneath; volute at base of handle.
      Incised line at junction of foot and body and near lower edge of foot. Interior lines in
      slight relief.</P><P>Two figures walk toward the right, a bearded man playing a
      double flute (<hi rend="ital">aulos</hi>) and a youth holding a drinking cup and a
      gnarled stick. The slender bodies have a light, elastic quality, and a sense of easy walking
      movement is suggested by the trailing foot on tiptoe. The aulist is in pure profile, but the
      youth turns back to face him, his upper body in three-quarter view. Both wear only a chlamys,
      the folds shown in straight, almost parallel lines and the edges in a sharp zigzag. On the
      youth's chest the sternum is marked by a double line, an unusual feature, and the line of his
      left pectoral muscle swings into the upper arm. His torso has other anatomical details in very
      faint lines; the <hi rend="ital">linea alba,</hi> interrupted by two diamond
      shapes, and the <hi rend="ital">serratus magnus,</hi> in a series of curves
      resembling stitching. Other anatomical details are few: genitals with the pubic hair in the
      form of a cross, angled lines at the knees, and cursory marks for the ankle bones. Both men
      have short hair edged with a series of dots, and parted to show the small C-shaped ears; the
      aulist's wavy beard and a suggestion of sideburns on the youth seem to be dry-brushed. While
      the aulist's eye is drawn frontally, that of the youth is in profile</P><P>The
      walking figures form an open composition: the youth's turned head and the extended <hi
      rend="ital">aulos</hi> serve to connect the figures. The bodies are well-spaced, and
      the general verticality of the composition is varied by the extended arms of the aulist and
      the diagonal line of the youth's stick. While the first impression of the well-knit and
      graceful figures is very pleasing, closer inspection shows that the work is inept in several
      ways. The lines are frequently faltering and even broken, the hands are awkward, and the lower
      legs and feet are badly contoured.</P><P>Identification of the subject is not
      easy. Wine-drinking and music are a normal part of the <hi rend="ital">komos</hi>
      or revel scenes frequently depicted on red-figure vases. The theme may be derived ultimately
      from the Dionysiac revels of satyrs and maenads, but probably most are merely human activities
      part of the trend toward scenes of daily life and away from the mythological emphasis of the
      black-figure style. Certainly the subject is appropriate for wine cups and pitchers. However,
      the <hi rend="ital">komos</hi> scenes almost always show much more exuberant
      figures than this rather sedate pair. In view of the disparity in age, the vase might allude
      to the homosexual fondness of mature Greek men for young boys, yet the scene scarcely looks
      like a seduction. Connection with the <hi rend="ital">komos</hi> seems more
      likely, the quietness perhaps a reflection of the Berlin Painter's disinclination to portray
      highly dynamic scenes.</P><P>Certainly the artist of the Krannert oinochoe was
      strongly influenced by the Berlin Painter (see <rs type="vase">Ann Arbor
      77.7.1</rs>). The slender, springy figures are one of his most prominent
      characteristics, unmatched by any other Greek painter, and numerous details such as the torso
      muscles of the youth, the unedged lips, the arc of the nostril, the forms of eyes, ears, and
      genitals can also be paralleled in the work of the master. However, the mediocre quality of
      the draftsmanship and the lack of such characteristic features as hooked clavicles, the
      marking of the great trochanter muscle on the buttocks, and the double curve of the ankle
      bone, make it clear that the vase cannot be attributed to the Berlin Painter himself. His two
      chief followers were Hermonax and the Providence Painter, but the Krannert oinochoe does not
      fit well in the oeuvre of either man. Hermonax prefers fully-draped figures in which the folds
      are drawn in curving lines, and his figures never have the elastic quality seen here. The
      Providence Painter uses much heavier bodies, which tend to be either completely static or to
      have very mannered poses. One must assume this to be the work of an unknown follower of the
      Berlin Painter.</P><P>The dating is also difficult. The Berlin Painter's career is
      thought to have lasted from the late sixth century into the seventies of the fifth century,
      and his followers continue still later. Some elements of the Krannert oinochoe, such as the
      profile eye of the youth, the double line of the sternum, and the upper border design with the
      strongly curved leaves framing the palmettes, suggest the Early Classical phase of Attic
      red-figure, generally considered to begin c. 480 B.C., so a date around the end of the Berlin
      Painter's career is plausible.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/16c8728966b903bc41b96b48f9d8abad8c3d88f0fbff20505ae426e6a9cc70bc> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
