<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/17005b4ed18acf0a28192e1cb5b0f2d6255b0d074cf3b3f36bcd89a7fa377dc4> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The designs are painted in dilute glaze on a ground of chalky
      white slip, which covers the shoulder and three quarters of the body. In the scene on the
      body, a woman stands at the right, her body frontal and her head turned to face a second
      woman, who stands at the left. The second woman, who is probably a maid, carries a shallow
      basket decorated with a battlement-pattern framed by stripes. The basket is the type used to
      bring offerings wreaths, fillets, lekythoi to the tomb of a loved one. This basket seems
      empty, but the offerings may have been represented in fugitive added color. The hair of both
      women is tied up in chignons; their hair was painted with thinned glaze, darker than the
      dilute glaze of the outlines. To judge by the remains of the outlines, the maid wore only a
      chiton, without himation. Her mistress, the woman at right, wore a chiton (of yellow?) and a
      red himation. A mirror hangs on the wall at upper right.</P><P>Above the figures
      and extending half way round the vase on either side is a band consisting of groups of three
      maeanders alternating with saltire squares. The grouped maeanders run alternately to the right
      and left. The paired stripes that frame the band extend all the way around the upper body. On
      the shoulder, below a band of egg-pattern at the base of the neck, are three palmettes
      enclosed and linked by coiling tendrils. The groundline is a narrow black stripe. The handle,
      mouth, neck, lower body, and top of the foot are glazed.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/170831559f2f800f2e98b8e95483ed4ca97dc88b275a81275d2601c793da98e1> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
