<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/17b5b9012e8819523b41ac7db3340a4c506084108a2b54c9d29a8a75902aa6e1> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi> Athena stands facing
      left, her right leg advanced with its knee flexed and her weight on her straight left leg. In
      her outstretched right hand she holds a high-crested Attic helmet by its cheekpiece. Her
      flexed left arm is held down at her side, and with her left hand she supports a spear, its
      butt resting on the ground in front of her left foot. Barefoot, the goddess wears a long
      chiton with an overall pattern of crosses on the skirt; fine pleats are shown on the sleeves.
      Over her chiton she wears a short undecorated himation, and over both a snake-bordered scaly
      aegis. As jewelry, she wears a necklace with a cross pendant, and a red bracelet wrapped
      around each forearm. Her hair is parted in the center and drawn in waves back to a long bun.
      The bun is enclosed in a <hi rend="ital">sakkos</hi> tied with a chord at the nape
      of the neck, and she also wears a <hi rend="ital">stephane</hi> with upright
      leaves.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi> Hermes stands facing right,
      his left leg advanced and his weight evenly distributed on both feet. He holds a <hi
      rend="ital">kerykeion</hi> in his extended left hand, while his right hand rests on
      his waist. The bearded god wears a short chiton, with an overall pattern of crosses, under a
      voluminous chlamys, fastened at his right shoulder. A red wreath encircles his head and a
      petasos hangs down behind his back, suspended from the red cord tied around his neck. He wears
      laced boots with a curved tongue at the top, above a band of three
      lines.</P><P>The figures of Athena and Hermes on the Yale amphora are closest to
      those on <rs type="vase">London E 268</rs>, a large amphora from the painter's
      middle period. The Berlin Painter combines Athena and Hermes on only one other surviving vase,
      an early amphora of panathenaic shape in the Vatican (ARV2, 197, 5). Both, like the Yale
      amphora, show a single figure on each side of the vase, a format greatly favored by this
      artist. Beazley considered the Yale vase to be from the Berlin Painter's middle period,
      manifesting somewhat less detail and less precision than works, such as the early Vatican
      panathenaic cited above. The arrangement of the folds on Athena's skirt, the fact that only
      the front of her aegis is shown, and the cross pendant on her necklace, among other features,
      link this vase to others designated as middle period works. The motif of Athena holding her
      helmet became popular in the late archaic period, probably in response to the Athenian
      victories over the Persians. As <bibl>Kunisch 1974</bibl> has shown, this peaceful
      type of Athena, calm and partly disarmed, was appropriate for an Athens that no longer needed,
      at least for the time being, an agressive warrior goddess, her spear at the
      ready.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

