      kalathos and the man's legs on side A; crack in handle A/B.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435429> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1d09fead425cb12cf48decf9cf406ba86396e8d01b977b4ac13e360e0c607a62> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Toynbee 1944, 149, n. 25, pl. XLVII, 5 and 6 (neither exact), pl. 47.5</bibl>; <bibl>Cohen 1880-1892, 6.290, no. 376</bibl>; <bibl>Gnecchi 1912, 2.118, no. 24, IX th. var., pl. 120, no. 5 (13 specimens)</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1d149f79ab9f3ab6c4446ebb142a878b45aed59ea61fb3361e15126052676931> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A:</hi> <P>A quadriga
      moves to the right. In it stand the bridal pair; the beardless bridegroom holding the reins,
      the bride veiled. Both wear embroidered himatia. Behind them is the beardless parochos, facing
      right in a long chiton and embroidered himation. To the right of the horse walk three women in
      single file, with embroidered peploi carrying objects, such as a liknon (a winnowing fan used
      to clean wheat and having connotations of fertility and plenty) or a vannus, for carrying
      offerings at sacrifices and used in the worship of Iacchos. The middle one carries either a
      basket or a sieve. They all wear embroidered peploi. In front of the horses is a bearded male
      figure, probably the proegetes walking to the right and looking back at the pair. He wears a
      petasos, an embroidered chlamys, and endromides (high boots).</P><P>R. Hague
      (<bibl>Hague 1988, 32-37</bibl>) believes the anachronistic touch of the bridal
      couple riding in a chariot was intended to elevate the proceedings to the level of the epic or
      divine.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B:</hi> Theseus and the Minotaur
      are in the center with Theseus attacking from the left. He leans forward and with his left arm
      forces the Minotaur to his knees, grasping him by the neck with one hand and burying the sword
      in his throat with the other. The hero wears a fillet on his close-cropped hair and a short
      embroidered chiton, girt at the waist. He has beginnings of a beard. The Minotaur, with a
      bull's head on a man's body, struggles to get free, clutching at Theseus with his right hand
      and trying to aim a white stone at him with his raised left hand. Behind each figure there are
      two standing figures framing the action. Are these Athenian youths and maidens anxiously
      awaiting their moment of freedom? Both girls have white skin, a peplos and embroidered
      himation. The beardless youths wear long chitons and embroidered
      himatia.</P><P>There is a double border of entwined honeysuckle at the top of the
      design panel. On the lower body there are rays.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1d1501fa5ed886b5199ee094d0a9d75fd4a6df215a453eb81c3406a0fe8595f0> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
