<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1d5f12a4f674c7527472d74b8700561d0fc494ef478c8037657e4d73609c2e77> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Men and youths"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1d73ae56975c917636d306346ef487efed6ff13613dcf66abc109180ee4ef8b6> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>In the tondo, a young man stands beside an altar, playing a
      double flute (<hi rend="ital">aulos</hi>). The figure is nude, except for a
      voluminous himation or chlamys draped over his left shoulder and hanging down behind him,
      nearly to his ankles. He leans back as he plays, against a staff that protrudes from his
      drapery and rests against the tondo border, and he carries a large provision basket held with
      red cords over his left shoulder. He wears sideburns but no beard, and a red vine wreath
      encircles his short black hair. He is enfibulated. To the right of his leading foot, and
      behind it from the viewer's point of view, stands the short altar, with red flames rising from
      it. The exterior is undecorated.</P><P>Beazley considered this a late work by the
      Brygos Painter, because of the lack of exterior decoration, and he noted (ARV2, 377) that the
      cup recalls the Foundry Painter. A similar flautist leaning back appears on a cup by the
      Foundry Painter formerly in Berlin (<rs type="vase">Berlin F 3198</rs>; ARV2, 402,
      13). The komast carrying a provision basket over his shoulder and playing a lyre or a double
      flute is a characteristic figure in komos scenes, as, for example, on a cup by the Brygos
      Painter in Copenhagen (<rs type="vase">Copenhagen, National Museum 3880</rs>;
      ARV2, 373, 36). A flautist who like the Yale komast is nude except for a chlamys appears with
      a basket and walking stick in the tondo of a cup by the Brygos Painter in London (<rs
      type="vase">London E 271</rs>; ARV2, 372, 29), while another, this time fully
      enveloped in a himation and shod in boots, stands next to a symposion couch on a Brygos
      Painter cup in the Louvre (<rs type="vase">Louvre G 313</rs>; ARV2, 377, 106),
      confirming the relation of these figures to the symposion. The flaming altar in connection
      with such figures, however, is unusual.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1d7b07c0c507a7e3614c17cbd4af441797c19445b8c2948acc566d2747929104> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Side A: an acrobat wearing a Chalcidian helmet with a low crest
      and carrying two shields stands on an inclined board preparing to do a somersault. The near
      shield's device is a defecating dog. The top of the platform is tied with a red fillet. At
      left, on the ground is a rock or earthen mound (altar?)</P><P>Side B: a youth
      wearing a phorbeia, a chiton and a short tunic (ependytes) stands next to a potter's wheel
      holding an aulos in each hand. At right hangs the catskin flute case with an attached box for
      the mouth pieces. He is going to accompany the acrobat.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1d8033c269f80c2f454820f61576e4890f0ad756eb6abf6ed18b5947a66b607c> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Young male figure with both arms raised over head"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1d837a4a4c9784a91cd941850d254600fd78984ef4ddd753d2d136eabc1b75d4> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Side A: Dionysos between two dancing satyrs<lb>Side B: Three naked youths in a
      procession (Komos)"""@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1d8d2999968666c65b17a2a646b6a1aa40699808aad38b61fd4334ec01927a4a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Woman says farewell to an arming warrior"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1d931c344821add6e266aef7d0eb63d6bf369d4473629de0bed550bc50cb48c4> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl><hi rend=\"ital\">BMCRE</hi></bibl> III, 323, no. 656; pl. 59, 18 " ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1da229da3dc893bc60cf0736b44b6c755b35af4c0dd3496a7d8c78d254e057ac> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Boreas flies right, his left arm outstretched and his right
      bent at the elbow. Barefoot and bearded, he wears a short chiton with a black border at the
      bottom of the overfold.</P><P><bibl>Pollitt 1987, 13-14</bibl> relates
      this figure of Boreas to the popularity of scenes showing the god of the north wind in the
      wake of the destruction of the Persian fleet in a windstorm off Euboea in 480 B.C. Boreas was
      given credit for this victory, and the Athenians built a temple to the god on the banks of the
      Ilissos River to the southeast of the Acropolis. In contrast to the more common narrative
      scenes of Boreas and Oreithyia, such as <rs type="vase">Munich 2345</rs> by the
      Oreithyia Painter, Pollitt views the isolated figure of Boreas on the Yale Lekythos as
      emblematic, a virtual personification of the force behind the maritime power of
      Athens.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1dac696ba9981185eb1a7b3da86539138fa0c411b625039c8295d3fbab14fb29> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Condition"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The cup is in fairly good condition. Some of the black glaze fired red. There
      is some encrustation and discoloration of the surface.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435429> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1db1b5737d60ce040654ac824b4d41bb70015784dbb737ac52a4b00e17132070> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Asklepios' sons, a servant, and a worshipper"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1db5b410e89134903829215207a7ec00a71665429477a1a707b3b42549d77b09> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl><hi rend=\"ital\">BMCRE</hi></bibl> I, 152, no. 36" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1dc410660b943b8b9ff352aef0a62c4566e53250ce1c8ca8668e49404b4619d9> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>In the main panel, Herakles holds the three-headed Kerberos by
      a leash. The terrified Eurystheus has taken refuge in a large storage vessel. He rises out of
      it in further alarm, confronting Kerberos and a myriad of snakes that coil from the dog's
      heads and feet. Eurystheos is wearing a black short-sleeved garment with a red wrap over his
      right shoulder. His skin and Herakles's are rendered in white. The main body and first head of
      Kerberos are black; its second and third heads are red and white. Herakles is wearing a black
      short-sleeved chiton with his red lionskin on over it. In his right hand he holds his club
      high, while in his left he grips the red leash. The snakes are black with white
      dots.</P><P>In the rear handle zone two opposing eagles begin a flying dive after
      a hare, who runs desperately to the left.</P><P>The shoulder and handle zones are
      decorated with alternating red and white tongues. Under the handles are spreading branches
      with leaves. The neck has two meanders and a stylized spiral-cross design with dots resembling
      buds emerging from each end and side. Below the main panel is a chain of lotus and palmettes,
      below that, a band of red, white, and black rays, and on the base red and white tongues with
      black borders.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1dc735c6139da124020c0b492b3af30215b7c6d0c578c497f55620ee9685b48c> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Nude torso of Poseidon from the Parthenon, West Pediment"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1ddd4d4320217404255c1832e4d79bbd4a728e572d7b286a8d1b0b0572a9863e> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Thebes Guide 1981, 40</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1de8ad134d3a3463401790d3f317eff140dc8ff4f9153e0c5ab3371770dffd3b> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
