<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1e6eb0a7cc9713eb7bd53600b7a5b03db62581ff3d427a58bd0780b9ecff07e5> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Side A:</hi> Apotheosis of
      Herakles. In the center is a quadriga, facing right. Herakles in profile, facing right, steps
      into the chariot. He wears the lion's skin over a short chiton, his quiver strapped on his
      back and his sword at his side. He grasps the reins in both hands and holds a long spear in
      his left hand as well. To the extreme left, behind Herakles, stands Iolaos. Beardless to
      indicate youth, he wears a short chiton and has a red fillet in his hair. His sword hangs at
      his side. He carries the hero's club in his right hand and a bow in his left. Behind the
      horses, in the center, Athena stands in profile facing left, but with her feet pointing to the
      right. Her gender indicated by her white flesh, she wears the aegis over a long, sleeveless
      chiton, decorated with rosettes, and an Attic helmet with a high crest. She carries a spear in
      her right hand. Beside her Dionysos stands, wearing a long chiton and himation, the bottom of
      the chiton is decorated with a meander band. He has a red beard and a garland of ivy leaves on
      his head. At the right of the design stands Apollo, facing right, in front of the horses.
      Beardless and with a wreath of laurel on his head, he wears a long white chiton and a
      red-spotted himation. In his left hand he holds the kithara which he plays with his right. In
      the field there is conventional foliage.</P><P><hi rend="head">Side
      B:</hi> Theseus and the Minotaur. In the center Theseus attacks the Minotaur, who seeks
      to escape. Theseus strides to the right, his head, hips, and legs shown in profile, his chest
      frontal. With his left arm, he holds the Minotaur's head and with his right, he grasps his
      drawn sword and aims it at the Minotaur's breast. Theseus wears a short spotted chiton with a
      meander band at the selvage, and his scabbard at his side. In imitation of Herakles, he wears
      an animal skin over this. With his hair worn in a long loop, gathered up with a fillet, he is
      shown beardless to indicate his youth. In this action pose the arm and foot of the Minotaur
      overlap with Theseus's body and incision is used to indicate important anatomical details. On
      either side two figures witness the action. At the left, Ariadne stands in profile, directly
      behind Theseus. She wears a long spotted chiton and a himation lined in red. Her hair falls in
      a long corkscrew curl just behind her ear. With her right hand at her side, she sniffs a
      flower held in her left. Behind her to the left, in profile facing right, stands a youth, in
      chiton and himation, holding a long spear and watching the action. Two similar youths frame
      the action on the right and stand in profile, facing left. These are probably the Athenian
      youths rescued by Theseus after he slaughtered the Minotaur.</P><P>There are many
      decorative patterns used. Lotus buds decorate the lip. There are palmettes above the handles,
      from which spring tendrils ending in lotuses. On the shoulder at the junction with the neck
      there is a tongue pattern (alternating in red and black), extending the width of the design
      panels. Along the side of the lip, from handle to handle, and along the sides of the panels
      there is an ivy leak pattern. On the body, where it joins the foot, are rays. The neck,
      handles, lower body, and foot are black.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/1e736b6508134fb99d5988f5afd71c11f79907c00d98a9c4258ae4a3d2752b80> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
