<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/206ed8fbea3842d9d20cc3f0676676362dc5f7ef94ea23d1589e99e4e581ffb5> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The decoration on the body is a continuous frieze of different
      kinds of animals. On side A, as a main scene, there is a sphinx between two lions. The sphinx,
      in the center facing right, has a lion body, open wings and a female upper body and head. Her
      hair is long and curly; her face, in profile, shows a large eye, frontally drawn and also a
      large ear. The two wings of the sphinx are of different kinds: the right one recurved, the
      other one plain. The sphinx is standing, an unusual position in Corinthian pottery. The two
      lions on either side are identical, following the Assyrian type typical of middle Corinthian
      pottery. They stand in a static pose facing inwards, with open mouths and schematic manes. The
      colors are red and black.</P><P>On side B, a siren between two lions is the other
      main scene on the frieze, symmetric to the one on A. The siren, in the center, half bird, half
      woman, has her wings folded and her long neck turned back to the left. Her face is very
      similar to the sphinx's face, but in this case, head and neck are black. The lions on either
      side are also identical to one another, but differ from the ones on A: instead of looking to
      the center, both lions turn away their faces with open jaws.</P><P>Between both
      scenes two single animals on either side make part of the frieze: a buck and wild goat . The
      buck has long ears and the goat long horns.</P><P>On the lid, a similar animal
      frieze goes all around a central rosette. Two sirens with open wings flanking a bird, and are
      flanked in turn by two panthers. On the opposite side two swans flank a lotus flower and on
      one side there is a panther. Between the two main scenes there are again one buck and one
      goat.</P><P>It seems as the painter were trying to follow the same pattern for
      body and rim: two main scenes with groups of animals facing the center like a decorative
      motif, mixing real and mythical creatures or mixing birds and wild animals, and between the
      main scenes, herbivorous animals contrasting to the figures in the main scene. On the lid,
      this pattern is not followed, as if the painter had miscalculated the space and missed one of
      panthers to complete the symmetrical composition. The filling ornaments are the typical in
      middle Corinthian vases: rosettes and small flowers.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/207d0ee92ade240242f0cc21ada933e0fe1d2e930c92e89514aa57cf2a6d22d5> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Condition"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<P>There is some discoloration and pitting of the surface.</P>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435429> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/209051e8736c8d2f4d7956322d64e26558ce046a1fac505198231361015b351f> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl><hi rend=\"ital\">BMCRE</hi></bibl> VI, 178, no. 643" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/2092bed87e810e2a22d212c1e6dc7c97ba318777281e3d5bd5ee1ee06f2391b4> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A four horse chariot in the apobates contest passes in front of a marshall"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/20aab33395dafbd13a9402c4e305aa13361e2756659e53081462623640fc81e9> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The scene on Side A is bordered at the top and sides by a
      palmette design, and at the base with a lotus chain. Side B has the same palmette design at
      the top and lotus chain at the bottom, but on the sides is a leaf pattern. Above the foot is a
      pattern of rays. The handles are decorated with rows of ivy.</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Herakles and Apollo are shown fighting over the tripod.
      Herakles is on the left. He stands frontally, holding the tripod, which is slung over his
      shoulder, by his left hand. He holds his club in his right hand. He wears a red wreath in his
      hair, but is otherwise nude. He looks back at Apollo, who reaches out to grab him with his
      right hand, holding his bow in his left. He is also nude, except for a quiver on his back. In
      between them, in the background, is a palm tree. By the appropriate figures are inscriptions
      <foreign lang="Greek">*E*R*A*K*L*E*O*S</foreign> ("Herakles"), and <foreign
      lang="Greek">*A*P*P*O*L*L*O*N*O*S</foreign> ("Apollo"). Between them is written
      <foreign lang="Greek">*K*A*L*O*S</foreign> ("Beautiful").</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Side B</hi>: A maenad dances between two satyrs. She is in the center of
      the composition, walking to the right, and looking over her shoulder. She wears a sleeved
      tunic and a long cloak with zigzag borders. She wears a headband in her hair, and carries a
      vine in each hand, with which she wards off the satyrs' advances. Both of these are nude,
      ithyphallic, with long hair and pointed beards. The one on the left steps toward her, holding
      his left hand above his head to protect himself from the vine branch. The satyr on the right
      has grabbed the maenad's arm with his right hand, and also steps toward
      her.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/20ba9a7d97b9774b02aacdbcc5283524bca816618ee0dee500d5074c1478503f> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The edge of the foot is clay-colored. Under each handle is a
      palmette decoration. Above and below the scene on side A are interrupted bands of meander and
      crossed squares. In the same places, side B bears simple meander bands.</P><P>On
      side A, Apollo stands in right profile on the left, wearing a mantle, a red wreath in his
      hair. In his left hand he holds a laurel branch upright. In front of him a Muse sits in left
      profile on a rock. She is wearing a chiton, mantle and headband, and she holds a lyre in her
      right hand. The figures are outlined in relief.</P><P>On side B, a woman is
      running to the right, turning her head to look behind her as she moves. She is wearing a
      chiton and mantle. Her figure is outlined in sketch form, but no relief is
      used.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/20c3ab5e2f8c52516a391c502e74a0a08ac556cdd85fdfbbfa9b491a74a833c5> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<P><bibl>Clairmont 1993, 3.303, cat. 3.404</bibl>; <bibl>Richter 1954, no. 86, pl. 69b</bibl>.</P>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/20c7bf89cf32ef35191584ec2f2ecc3b4c95d0d80d216683cf2737c62f54a0c4> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Vermeule & Comstock 1988, 69-70 no. 71</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/20d94d62ce8b65e204d13f944a83322c489f65e16f741f417c61207f58493441> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Side A: death of Achilles. Side B: death of Antilochos."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/20e334545952a9a3109e3d4379eb091983937fb76044d49cda61846797c07b40> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: A unique and lovely
      scene set in the women's quarters of a private dwelling. Two women are removing their pubic
      hair by singeing them with lighted oil lamps, a delicate procedure to say the least. The woman
      at right is being assisted by Eros himself, who kneels before her, his wings stretched out
      behind him. He wears a laurel wreath but is otherwise nude. With his left hand he holds the
      lamp, the flame of which is drawn with added white, and with the fingers of his right hand he
      pulls away the singed hairs. The woman holds her cloak up with her left hand, revealing her
      nudity, which is otherwise interrupted only by the crossed cords over her shoulders and
      between her breast. Her chiton, which the cords would help to hold in place when worn, lies
      rolled up on the stool at right (a <hi rend="ital">diphros</hi>, with a cushion
      embroidered with stripes and dotted lines). A mirror and a laurel wreath with white berries
      hang on the wall above Eros. At the left, a second woman is seated to the right on a klismos.
      She sits on her himation and is nude except for a thigh-band drawn with dilute glaze and
      crossed cords like those worn by the first woman (these cords cross over the right breast, not
      between them). She holds a lighted oil lamp in her left hand and picks at her pubic hairs with
      the other, her face a frowning mask of concentration. Her curly hair, drawn with thinned
      glaze, is tied in a chignon on top of her head; the hair of the first woman is pulled up and
      tied in back, not on top. Above the seated woman, a fillet with white tassels hangs on the
      wall.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: Three draped youths stand in
      conversation, the two at left facing the one at right. All three wear himations and apicate
      fillets of added white. The youth in the center has longer hair than the other two, and
      although he is the same height, this may indicate he is younger; in support of this
      interpretation is the fact that the youth at right reaches toward the central youth's head
      with his right hand, as though accosting him.</P><P>A laurel wreath circles the
      vase below the rim. Bands of egg-pattern nearly circle the handle roots; the area between the
      handle roots is reserved. Reserved stripes circle the side of the foot at top and bottom. The
      groundlines consist of groups of three maeanders to right alternating with cross squares on
      side B, and two cross-squares and one saltire-square on side
      A.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/20ec2ae8fdb8ee7a4789c7ec4f3d0c1d9f91cc66f5bdd5670720d6ecc06aaf02> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
