<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/2278c8803fa305f54c30aa259d9412eed6037f32898c4865e501cd2c11752527> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: The visit of Theseus
      to his divine father, Poseidon, who in some accounts slept with Theseus' mother Aithra on the
      same night as Aigeus. Challenged by Minos to find a ring thrown into the sea, Theseus was
      helped by Amphitrite. In the center, Poseidon and Theseus. Poseidon wears a fillet, a dotted
      chiton with folds in dilute glaze, and a himation. In his left hand he holds a tall trident,
      which overlaps the frieze of tongues above. Theseus wears a wreath, a chitoniskos, and has a
      fringed shawl over his shoulders. A sword hangs in a scabbard at his waist, presumably the
      weapon left under a rock at Troizen by Aigeus and later found by Theseus. His hair is shorter
      than Poseidon's and is tied in a short queue; thinned glaze is used for his budding sideburns.
      He holds a round object in his left hand, possibly a fruit. Nereus and a Nereid stand at left
      with a slender Doric column between them. The Nereid holds a black oinochoe in her right hand
      and a phiale in her left, the latter lowered to pour a libation. Nereus, leaning on a tall
      staff, turns back to left to look at his daughter, who wears a sakkos, earrings, an
      embroidered peplos, a bordered himation, and a long fillet, the ends of which trail on her
      shoulders. The overfold of her peplos has a fringe drawn with dilute glaze. Nereus wears a
      chiton and bordered himation. His stubbly beard and close-cropped hair are rendered in dilute
      glaze. Amphitrite stands at far right, behind Theseus. She raises her right hand to crown the
      hero with a wreath of added red, only the ghost of which is now visible. She wears earrings, a
      chiton, and a himation; the end of the long fillet in her hair trails on her
      shoulder.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: At left, a youth in a
      himation gestures at a bearded man, also in a himation, who carries a staff. At right is a
      second pair: a youth playing a kithara faced by a youth wearing a fillet and himation and
      leaning on his staff. The second youth is identified as a judge or trainer by the forked wand
      (<hi rend="ital">rhabdos</hi>) in his right hand; a drop of black glaze mars his
      right elbow. The kitharist holds a plektron in his right hand. The left hand is not visible,
      and the strap that normally loops around the left wrist to hold the instrument steady so the
      fingers of the left hand may touch the strings is instead shown looped over a bulge in the
      sound box. The kitharist wears a fillet and a long gown with a black border; it is a festal
      gown and perhaps indicates that he is performing in an actual contest rather than just
      practicing (if so, the youth at right is more likely a judge than a
      trainer).</P><P>Pairs of horizontal palmettes decorate the side of the obverse
      rim, arranged back-to-back and enclosed in lyre-shaped tendrils; on the reverse is an ivy
      vine. Lotus buds on top of the mouth. On each handle-plate is a single black palmette framed
      by coiling tendrils. The scenes have lateral frames of ivy-vines and upper frames of black
      tongues. The groundlines are simple reserved lines. Rays on the lower body. Stripes of added
      red circle the lower foot, the inside of the mouth just below the rim, the top of the band of
      rays, and the lower body just below the groundlines.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/2280902bf3ec60c93a1fd8a937740b9da01f3a1b77918565662ed32116a7b174> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
