<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/2416c68681baeeceb0f9be78d89543e3aaac42178a94b703fd54d42fd059af1c> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Side A</hi>: horseman and four
      standing men. A mounted rider occupies the center of the scene, with two male figures standing
      to either side. The horse and rider are at rest, facing toward the right. Only the right arm
      and the right side of the male rider are preserved. He wears a short-sleeved, short red chiton
      with a black border. Of the horse the hindquarters, the two front legs, the outline of the
      chest, and its face are preserved. It is painted black with red markings on the rump and
      incised details. To the left of the horse and rider stands an older man carrying a spear and
      gesturing toward the rider with one hand. He is bearded and wears his hair long and bound with
      a fillet, and he is dressed in a long red chiton and a black mantle decorated with red dots
      and white rosettes. On the far left stands a second man whose head is not preserved. He is
      dressed identically to the first man, carries a spear, and raises his left hand in the same
      gesture. A third man stands to the right of the horse and rider. His body faces forward,
      although he appears to be moving toward the right while looking back toward the rider on the
      left. His right arm is bent in an awkward downwards gesture and he carries a spear in his
      right hand. The man is bearded and has long hair bound with a fillet. He wears a long black
      chiton with red dots and a red mantle. The man on the far right is preserved from his abdomen
      downward. He stands facing right in a wide legged stance, balancing himself as he leans
      forward slightly. He holds a large ring, about the size of a diskos, in his right hand. His
      left hand is held up at chest height, palm downward. A bird flying to the left above the
      horse's hindquarters completes the scene.</P><P><hi rend="head">Side
      B</hi>: five figures around a tripod. Three men stand to the right and two to the left
      of a large tripod vessel, of which only the three legs are preserved. Only the feet and lower
      legs of the two most central figures are preserved. The figure on the left faces the tripod,
      while the man on the right turns away. Both wear long chitons; the edge of a mantle on the
      left figure is also preserved. The figure on the far left is preserved from the chest up. He
      faces the center of the scene, carrying a staff and dressed in a chiton. He is bearded and
      wears his hair falling down his back and bound in a fillet. The figure second from the right
      runs toward the tripod. His forward arm is not preserved. He is bearded and has long hair
      bound in a fillet, and wears a short black chiton and a black mantle decorated with red dots.
      The man on the far right walks toward the center holding a spear. He is also bearded and has
      his long hair bound with a fillet, and he wears a long chiton and mantle. A bird flies to the
      left behind him.</P><P><hi rend="head">Handles</hi>: under the handle
      to the right of Side A is the Chimaera. A Pegasus stands under the handle to the
      left.</P><P><hi rend="head">Neck, Side A</hi>: the entire center of
      the neck is missing, but two male figures are partially preserved on the right and left sides
      next to the handles. The figure on the left stands in profile to the right, holding a spear in
      his right hand and gesturing with his raised left hand. He is bearded and has long hair, and
      is dressed in a long, red chiton and a black mantle decorated with red dots and white
      rosettes. The figure on the right is preserved from the knee down. He faces right and is
      dressed in a long red chiton and a black mantle.</P><P><hi rend="head">Neck,
      Side B</hi>: footrace. Two bearded male figures dressed in chitons and mantles look on
      as two nude males race to the right. The two central figures are depicted with wide-spread,
      bent legs to show that they are running. The cape flying behind the right, central figure also
      indicates motion. The upper bodies of the runners are not preserved. The figures watching may
      be judges or spectators.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/241840642111b3d945d9257a462d5559afd15e7ed64a4fab4b7a85f1483a6844> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Two sets of compass-drawn concentric circles on shoulder with
      pairs of vertical wavy lines between and near handles. Horizontal bands on lip, base of neck,
      below shoulder decoration, and on lower body. Deep brown
      paint.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/241987a294e7f65ebf9b0e8222505bcac21b45f5d86aca15c0da9391d0e6c8e5> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Bruneau 1983, 61</bibl>; <bibl>Richter 1970b, 69 no. 47, figs. 174-75</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/241a20262a5fae8ff9b6f2a871adf451e8577365382cf05d5a2268b0824c3fd4> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Most of the footplate and underfoot are glazed in black. The
      tondo is framed by a lefthanded meander pattern between reserved lines. The pattern is broken
      every three squares by a checkered square with dotted reserved areas. The odd square lies in
      the upper left side of the circle, with half a meander missing. An empty exergue is separated
      by a relief line from the three figures sitting and standing above. Behind them fine lines,
      now mostly obliterated, in added red paint indicated a rocky landscape. A delicate
      three-branched plant reaches down as far as the exergue.</P><P>The woman on the
      left of the tondo stands in profile facing right, with her left foot raised. She bends forward
      and gestures with her right hand to the woman sitting in front of her. She is wearing a
      transparent belted chiton with dark stripes running down the sides. The stripes are oblivious
      to the folds of her skirt. Behind her back, an extra bunch of cloth appears above the cord
      that holds her bodice in place. She has a krobylos in her hair, a lozenge-patterned stephane,
      a necklace and a white earring. The seated central woman is in three-quarter view to the left,
      with her head slightly bowed. Her left foot is drawn up on a ledge, and her left hand supports
      her on her rocky seat. With her right hand resting quietly in her lap, she appears to be
      listening to the first woman's conversation. Like the first woman, she wears a belted chiton
      fastened with a cord at the shoulders, but hers has sleeves as well. Around her legs is
      wrapped a himation of thicker, star-patterned material. The lower edge of her chiton has a
      stripe woven into it, and a second stripe extends irregularly across her midsection. Her hair
      is bound at the nape of her neck, although part of the back of her head is damaged and not
      visible. A sort of bun appears on her left temple, and she wears an apicate stephane with a
      meander decoration, a necklace, bracelet and earring. The third figure is Eros, who stands
      behind the seated woman on the far right, resting his right hand on her shoulder. He is naked
      and stands frontally, but both his spread wings and his head are turned toward the two women.
      The presence of Eros makes it probable that the identity of the standing woman is Peitho, the
      goddess of Persuasion, advising the seated woman about love. The central woman is identified
      as Demonassa by an inscription above her head.</P><P>The exterior of the vase
      offers similar scenes on each side, with an interrupted meander, like that of the tondo,
      running around the bottom of both scenes. Both handle regions are decorated with dense floral
      patterns--addorsed acanthus-like lotus buds, acanthus leaves, spiraling volutes, ribbed
      fronds, single fronds, and miniature bulls' eyes--all appearing to grow in natural
      luxuriance.</P><P>On side A six women are depicted, conversing and adorning
      themselves. Five are standing, while the third from the left is sitting on a klismos. The
      woman on the far left stands in profile to the right, holding a now faded, added red or white
      laurel wreath in front of her. The second woman on the left stands facing right as well, with
      her body frontal and a mirror in her left hand. The next woman sits on the klismos, with her
      legs and torso facing the two on the left, but her head turned back toward the three women on
      the right. Her left hand is raised in an empty gesture; her left elbow leans on the chair
      back. The next two women face each other with their arms raised in conversation, while the
      woman on the far right stands frontally with her head looking back at the others and her hands
      outspread, holding a long painted cord, now mostly gone, with tasseled ends. The seated woman
      and the second woman from the right are wearing transparent chitons wrapped with heavier
      himatia, while the other four women wear Doric peploi, some girded and some ungirded. The
      second and third women from the left wear bracelets, while the first on the left has her hair
      in a sakkos, the third a white painted apicate stephane, and the fourth a series of narrow
      bands tied around the head, and decorated with little leaves. The fifth woman once had a long
      fillet in added red tied around her krobylos.</P><P>On side B, a similar scene
      appears. Here the seated woman is third from the right. The first two women on the left are
      turned frontally, and their heads are turned to face each other in conversation. The first
      woman places her left hand on the second's shoulder and her right hand on her own hip. The
      second woman raises her left hand to hold a painted flower, now faded. The third woman faces
      right and bends over the seated fourth, who faces away from her and gestures with her left
      hand at the fifth. This figure and the woman on the far right are turned frontally with their
      heads in profile facing back toward the seated woman. As on side A, four women wear Doric
      peploi, while the seated woman and the woman on the far right have transparent chitons covered
      with himatia. The far lefthand woman once had a fillet in her hair, similar to that on side A;
      the seated woman wears a sakkos, while the far righthand woman has a stephane decorated with a
      cyma pattern.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/242f06235756c0a28d0e7ade0dcf25752da6c58ade5ccb75b48064a573674484> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><bibl>Fairbanks 1928, 177, pl. 50</bibl>; <bibl>Payne
      1931, 177</bibl>; <bibl>M. Maximova <hi rend="ital">Les Vases Plastiques
      dans l'Antiquit (poque Archaque)</hi> (Paris, 1927):
      110</bibl></P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/242f696158eef426ebee0d84f13cb728fa5fd86864897b31c19ff6f86759cd1a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Grave stele with legs of a warrior, and a chariot below"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/24311c3cd05924c072322e82ff9a3111b9af714eff20e5b5a990f4f9ed909efd> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """A continouous figural frieze decorates the outside of the pyxis. Between
      two columns stands a woman, reaching to (kneeling before?) an altar; a wreath is hung on the
      wall behind her. In the next intercolumniation flies a winged Nike, with a wreath, toward a
      low altar. A woman, swathed in a himation, is seated profile to the right on a stool, between
      the last column and a door, to the right of which runs another woman, shown in 3/4-view to the
      right, toward a basin, although she turns her head profile to the left, toward the
      door.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/2436eb7857307010fd7ee1d95ccd2a6ecdc37438efdbee5e162a0283a1e550e1> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Tondo: A man vomiting, accompanied by a dog<lb>Sides A and B (exterior):
      Warriors"""@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/243d70196f8755028c6b38c0df3bb56fdddc9e6b8aebbfdca9e43fd5106d9181> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Sides A and B: Dionysiac revelry."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/24443f06094535accfcc3f72e053d43392192f3b88a8cd4a05767573f3763743> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Interior</hi>: cup-bearer. A nude
      youth carrying a kylix in one hand, has stepped up to a column krater to pour out wine or
      water from the trefoil oinochoe he carries in his other hand. He looks behind himself as he
      does so as if summoned by someone. His hair is bound by a taenia sprigged with vine leaves.
      The krater is garlanded with vines. A nonsense inscription runs along the inner edge of the
      tondo from the krater to the youth's left heel.</P><P><hi rend="head">Side
      A</hi>: centaur battling with two hoplites. The centaur gallops to the right. He is
      wielding a small tree and reaches out dramatically with his left arm which is covered with the
      panther skin tied over his shoulders. The hoplite he is attacking has fallen wounded to one
      knee. He now turns his body to face his attacker, protecting himself with his shield and
      aiming his spear at the centaur's chest. Blood pours from his left shoulder. A companion
      hoplite is fleeing in the other direction, but turns awkwardly, stabbing the centaur in the
      buttocks with his spear. Both hoplites are naked but wear greaves and Chalcidian helmets with
      long crest-tails and nose guards. The device on the shield of the wounded warrior is a bull's
      head. The shield device of the fleeing warrior is a scorpion. The fleeing warrior also carries
      a sword at his waist. The centaur has a heavy face and coarse features. Although balding on
      top, he has long hair and a beard. He wears a fillet looped twice around his
      head.</P><P><hi rend="head">Side B</hi>: fight between an archer and
      two hoplites. On the left, a nude archer has set an arrow in his bow and is drawing back on
      it, both arms straight. In the center is a fallen warrior, his left leg buckled under and his
      right extended. Although dying from the wound in his chest, he still holds his sword and
      shield. A second warrior on the right confronts the archer, lunging forward and leaning on the
      spear he appears to have planted between the thighs of his companion. He also carries a shield
      and wears a sheathed sword hanging from a double baldric at his waist. His round shield is
      fitted with a fringed apron. Both hoplites wear greaves and Corinthian helmets. The fallen
      warrior sports a baldric and scabbard. The shield device on both their shields is a snake. The
      archer is naked but wears leather boots, a gorytos suspended from a leather strap, and a
      Scythian cap made of leather. His bow is also Scythian.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/2451104169c24fadd53223f0bfee88e2949ceec968a041472fb4e9072087d42a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The vase has a rounded body with a flat bottom, and the edge of
      the base ring is grooved. It has a small neck, wide flat lip projecting below, and a narrow
      strap handle. The decoration is in a slightly lustrous brown to black glaze, with some details
      incised. On the shoulder are radial bars, while on the base ring, under the base, and on the
      lip are concentric lines and bands.</P><P>In the main zone is depicted the ship of
      Odysseus with the beak in the form of a boar's snout, rowed by helmeted warriors towards the
      right. Odysseus is tied to the mast, while over the boat fly two birds (a swan and an eagle?).
      In front at the right are cliffs on which stand two sirens with raised wings, and behind them
      a crouching woman. Under the handle is a large, latticed, altar-like
      structure.</P><P>The crouching woman is interpreted by Payne (321) as Chthon,
      mother of the sirens.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

