<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/28303d28200dcd8c67dc864c7912b4c13ee1a1f50e626caefaa882d091159df2> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: A warrior is leaving
      home. His equipment consists of a sword, a spear, and a shield decorated with a large eye. He
      is wearing a short chiton and loin cloth, and he is raising a phiale to toast his wife
      standing in front of him. She is dressed in a peplos, a taenia decorated with two blossoms,
      and earrings. Her head is bowed and she lifts a part of her peplos in a gesture of grief. The
      warrior's old father is looking on from the left and his mother from the right, her hand
      raised in farewell. Next to the crest of the warrior's helmet is the white inscription
      <foreign lang="Greek">*K*A*L*O*S</foreign> ("[he is] beautiful"), below it
      <foreign lang="Greek">*K*A*L*E</foreign> ("[she is] beautiful"). In front of the
      old man's head is again <foreign lang="Greek">*K*A*L*O*S</foreign>, above the
      mother's head <foreign lang="Greek">*K*A*L*E</foreign>. <bibl>Buschor,
      Griechische Vasen, 209</bibl> and <bibl>Das Kriegertum der Parthenonzeit,
      47</bibl> interprets the scene as the last encounter of Hektor and Andromache in the
      presence of Priam and Hekabe.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: Three
      youths dressed in mantles are standing turned to each other, one of them is resting on a long
      stick.</P><P>The areas above and below the handles are decorated with large
      palmette scrolls painted in red.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/284029e2b42aba8778729507f6756cbe7771cbddcb5dd83145320477f26c6861> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
