<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/289204cbb89952e4a20eaa8be53ecf7f13b883460aade8de35511bc46a76e7fa> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Interior and exterior: revelers"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/28a0a6d832b6a503e427016cd7bd2ac62177b99f3d5cf7a18bf14bac4e2f0614> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Body</hi>: Eros chasing a hare. A
      panel is created by egg-and-dot borders on the shoulder and lower body; dot sprays dangle from
      the border above. Here a very feminine looking Eros with generous breasts, bangle bracelets,
      necklace, and beribboned tresses runs after a hare. Male genitalia lead one to speculate
      whether a distinctly feminine Eros or hermaphrodite is being shown. The figures are bracketed
      by wispy floral sprays, indicated by a row of dots. This androgynous portrayal of the youthful
      god of love is characteristic of the late fourth century. The hare is a traditional love gift
      brought by an older admirer to a youth. Since the lekythos often carries funerary subject
      matter and has generally been found in graves, perhaps one should consider the scene of Eros
      chasing the hare as referring to the provision of love gifts for the deceased loved one even
      in the face of death.</P><P>Vertical stripes decorate the neck. Beneath the
      egg-and-dot frieze below the panel is a reserved area, partially filled by two horizontal
      lines in black glaze and, beneath these, a wave pattern, again set off against a reserved
      background. The foot and spout are undecorated save for the black
      glaze.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/28a29daf29027537513ac4c0118f0842df95ffa25ce22330275ee167b763bd52> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl><hi rend=\"ital\">RIC</hi></bibl> IV.3, 135, no. 115; pl. 12, 16" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/28a9927bdff2a4cc7e51921f9d41c9146796981a792f121880a0ebc74cfc481a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The two unframed scenes are essentially continuous. The subject
      is a komos, a drunken revel. <hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: In the center, a woman
      walks to the right while playing a cradle kithara decorated with a pair of eyes. She wears a
      chiton, a cloak draped over both arms, and a sakkos with a fillet of added red tied around it.
      She is singing her head is thrown back and her mouth is open but does not appear to be playing
      her instrument, for the plektron hangs limply from the cord in her hand (both plektron and
      cord are in added red). She may be strumming the strings with her left hand, but normally
      these fingers are used to stop the strings at certain intervals, so that different notes are
      produced when the strings are struck by the plektron. At left is a nude youth wearing a red
      wreath and with a himation draped over his left shoulder and right arm. He is walking away to
      the left but looks back to the right. He carries a staff in his left hand and a barbiton in
      his right, the red plektron tied to one arm of the lyre with a red cord. At far right, a
      second youth advances toward the singing woman. He bends over to lift a large column-krater
      decked with a wreath of ivy; the krater is either empty or is being emptied on the woman's
      legs (if fluid was poured from it, this has been lost with the adjacent fragment). In the
      youth's hair is a wreath of added red, and there is a himation draped over both shoulders. On
      the ground between him and the woman is a stool (<hi rend="ital">diphros</hi>)
      with a bundle of clothes on top.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>:
      Three nude males are in drunken procession to right, the first one bearded, the other two not.
      All three wear wreaths of added red and have himations over their shoulders. The youth at
      center is playing the double-pipes (auloi), his cheeks puffed out with the strain. The second
      youth walks to the right but twists around to gaze at the piper, his torso turned frontal. He
      carries a wineskin in his left hand and an oinochoe in his right, the latter tilted as though
      for pouring. At far left, the bearded man stands to the right, a pointed amphora cradled in
      his left arm and a lyre in his right hand. The lyre is not a barbiton, but the simpler <hi
      rend="ital">chelys</hi>); like the barbiton on Side A, however, added red marks the
      points where the strings meet the pegs of the cross-bar. Both the amphora and the wineskin are
      decked with a wreath of ivy, like the krater on Side A.</P><P>Circling the lower
      body and acting as groundline is a band of key-pattern to right. Above the figures on either
      side is a band of dotted lotus-buds, pointing downwards. Reserved band around the inner rim. A
      stripe of added red circles the neck; another circles the lower body below the
      key-pattern.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/28bda66316cedbc22bd53b020a79a740f7c5d699d682b1d2e74ecbc915ea4a5c> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A:</hi> Combat scene. A
      quadriga with two men moves from right to left. Traces of added color show that horses' manes
      and forelocks were white. The horses stand on their hind legs, their forelegs bent and pawing
      the air. The charioteer, bearded and bareheaded, has a Boeotian shield (shown foreshortened)
      and breastplate strapped to his back. The chariot's wheels are successfully foreshortened. The
      shield device-a tripod in added white- is largely gone. The other man is largely hidden behind
      the horses. He wears an Attic helmet with a high crest and a large Boeotian shield (device,
      white dots) and carries two long spears. Under the horses' hooves lies a dying warrior. He
      wears a cuirass over a short chiton, a Corinthian helmet with a high crest ending in white
      horsehair, greaves, and carries a round shield (device, bukranion). Two white straps cross on
      his chest to support his sword, which is sheathed. His left leg is bent beneath him, and he
      sinks back on his right arm.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B:</hi>
      Apotheosis of Herakles. Herakles, clad in the lion's skin, walks from left to right, carrying
      his club in his left hand and his bow in his right. On his shoulder is a quiver, at his side,
      a sword, fastened to his belt. In front of him, at the center, Athena (flesh white) moves to
      the right, her head turned back towards Herakles. She wears a long chiton with white dots, her
      aegis draped over her upper torso, and an Attic helmet with a high crest. In her right hand
      she holds a long spear. At the right is Hermes in a white-dotted chlamys, a petasos with a
      pointed crown, the brim decorated with dots, and winged boots. In his left hand he holds the
      kerykeion; his right hand extended downwards. He is shown in profile to the left, walking
      towards Athena. On both sides the figures' helmets extend into the tongue pattern above. On
      both sides the figures overlap the palmettes and tendrils beneath the
      handles.</P><P>On the neck is an incised double palmette-lotus chain. At the
      junction of neck and shoulder, red and black tongues alternate. Below the handles, tendrils
      dangle, ending in palmettes and lotuses. Below the main design runs a band of running
      meanders, then a band of lotus buds, and below this, rays. The lip, handles, and foot are
      black.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/28bf8772afbf0569c942c970d77e83bc1b63ffdd3102d2e69437afdce0504940> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
