<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/2944262e4d843c084924539f6abd3c4aee5dfd29426c5b58669796f3d8bbf295> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Six worshippers approach a banqueting god and goddess, attended by a nude serving boy, shown in a 3/4-back view; a horse's head is shown in low relief, to the left"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/2946a646fdfd0a07cc95bd3c6c286814a934883890ae3ce7ee65c7730c827be8> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Body:</hi> Cocks and sphinxes. In
      the center two roosters are confronted. Added red is used for combs, wattles, and there are
      red stripes near the base of the wings and red dots on the wings and tails. The wing and tail
      feathers are incised, as are the beaks, eyes, and outlines of the two cocks. White is also
      used on the tails. Between them is an addorsed palmette-lotus with a red base, black petals,
      red bud, and incised detail. Long lotus tendrils spiral up from behind the cocks. On either
      side of the vase in back of the cocks is a sphinx with frontal face in added white and red,
      and incised details. Seated on their hind legs with forelegs straight, they have red and black
      wings and long manes. Their tails are s-shaped. Incised details of anatomy are rare, although
      incisions occur for heavy eyebrows and neck wrinkles. Red dots are used to accent eyeballs,
      cheeks, and mouths over added white and over black glaze, two red strokes show the beginning
      of the breasts. In the field above the sphinxes are rosettes. At the junction of body and
      foot, a continuous band of rays.</P><P>Design in a large panel covers 70% of vase.
      The back is left in black glaze except for the junction of body and foot which is decorated
      with a continuous band of rays. On the shoulder tongues articulate the junction with the neck.
      The main decoration on the shoulder is an ivy vine with leaves and berries in groups of
      threes, unconnected to the main stem.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/2946c96b72d46d010c631feebfb91345bc26e7a0cfa21eb5b57bda4d6fe2fe49> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
