<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/2d5e3a8cdff3356730bbb8b4e92d5b73808bb68193ef69a4fc080a09b47e7548> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Sides A: Triptolemos<lb>Side B: Three women and two men, one bearing two
      torches<lb>Tondo (white-ground): Hera"""@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/2d64b028e0d4538690dd2098556385a1ac7f157b4ed17378d337a000d5fa538d> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The interior of the bowl is surrounded by a black-glazed band
      on the inside of the lip, with two thin reserved stripes at the rim and along the midsection
      of the band. A ring of pomegranates with double petals faces inward just inside the band; the
      ring is interrupted by figures that extend to the edge of their field.</P><P>In
      the center scene, a young, bearded Bellerophon runs in Knielauf position to attack the
      Chimaera, who rears up in front of him. Bellerophon is wearing a sleeveless chiton that
      reaches to his knees and purple boots. The chiton has an incised diamond pattern, while its
      belt and hem are black bands with double transverse incisions. With his right hand Bellerophon
      thrusts his spear into the Chimaera's belly, while in his left he holds the reins of Pegasos,
      rearing up behind him, forming a heraldic counterpart to the Chimaera. Pegasos is attacking
      the Chimaera with his hooves. The horse's upper wing is purple, while the spreading feathers
      are black. The tail is also purple, and simple purple lines cover the neck, belly and
      hindquarters. The three-headed Chimaera is defending itself, turning back her lion's head to
      avoid the blows of Pegasos's hooves that now hit her neck. She reaches with her forepaws
      behind Pegasos's chest, having missed her target. The lion's head with open mouth and long
      drooping tongue looks directly into the face of the Chimaera's second, goat head, which
      emerges from her back. The goat's head is long-horned, bearded, and fire-breathing. The flames
      are depicted by three lines emanating from her nostrils. The third head is at the end of the
      tail that changes into a snake. With this snake head she reaches forward to the goat's horn
      tips. The snake's head is in profile, and its body details are carefully incised. The body of
      the monster is huge, with incised hair on the belly and wavy markings on the trunk (nine
      incised alternating with seven purple lines). Two purple jets of blood spurt from the
      monster's belly where Bellerophon's spear has entered. The eyes of Pegasos, Bellerophon and
      the lion's head are incised, with curving upper and lower lines and a central open area for
      the pupils, added on Pegasos. The goat and snake heads have simple circles for eyes.
      Bellerophon's knees, elbows, and ear are also incised.</P><P>On the exterior the
      main decoration appears in three ornamental friezes encircling the bowl's underside. The
      lowest frieze consists of a row of pomegranates with double petals; the next is a band of
      upright, alternately black and purple tongues, and the top is a line of broad upright rays.
      The pomegranates are separated from the foot attachment by four concentric black bands. The
      three friezes are each separated by a wide purple band between three brown lines. Above the
      top frieze a group of bands provides the lower boundary to the handle zone. In the handle zone
      are four handle-palmettes. The lip jog is marked by a thin line, while the lip itself is plain
      but for a pair of thin lines below a thick band at the rim. Over the whole exterior of the cup
      is a creamy slip. The foot is black glazed on interior and exterior with a plain resting
      surface. The bowl join has a fillet covered by a slip.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/2d79d579ed3d0d8bd353bbc859679b9c4627a37b4f7317be3954face58022bf0> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Body: Side A:</hi> Woman stands
      between youths. In the center stands a woman in profile, facing right. She wears a long chiton
      and a himation, and her hair is done up in a sakkos or snood. She gesticulates with her right
      hand and holds out her garment with her left. At the right, facing her, is a youth, draped in
      a himation which covers the back of his head. While leaning forward on a staff, he has his
      right hand on his hip and he points downward with his left. To the left, behind the woman,
      another youth stands to the right, his head turned to the left, away from the central scene.
      He is similarly clad with his himation draped over the back of his head. Also leaning on a
      staff, he holds a wreath in his right hand.</P><P><hi rend="head">Side
      B:</hi> Mistress and maids. In the center a woman sits in profile, facing right, on a
      stool with a dotted cushion. She wears a long Ionic style chiton with loose elbow sleeves and
      decorated with two broad stripes; over her left shoulder is draped a himation. On her head she
      wears a sakkos or snood and earrings. She leans forward, gesturing with her right hand, palm
      up. On the right, and facing her, a woman stands in profile, similarly clad, with a kalathos
      in her right hand. Behind the seated woman, to the left, another woman stands in profile,
      facing left. She gestures with her left arm raised, similarly clad, but without the
      snood.</P><P>The knob is black. Where it joins the lid, there are rays. On the
      upper surface at the edge of the lid there are rosettes. The neck and handles are black. Below
      the handles are sphinxes. Above the design is tongue pattern where the neck joins the
      shoulder. Below the design is a reserved band and broad black stripe. Below this are rays
      which terminate in a black ridge where the body joins the foot.</P><P><hi
      rend="head">Foot: Side A:</hi> Poseidon pursues a woman. Bearded Poseidon, partially
      clad in a himation, is identified by the trident which is placed obliquely beside him. He
      rushes to the left in hot pursuit of a young woman. He extends his left arm to catch her and
      holds together his garment with his left hand. The young woman flees to the left, her head
      turning back to the right to check on the sea god's advances. She gestures with her left hand
      as if to push him away. With her right, she holds up her garment so as to be able to run
      faster. With her hair gathered up in a sakkos or snood, she wears a long Ionic style chiton
      with loose elbow sleeves and a row of dots above the selvage. Perhaps the scene above is
      related in theme with the woman trying to evade the clear interests of the young
      men.</P><P><hi rend="head">Side B:</hi> Youth pursues woman. To the
      left is a youth draped in a himation. Striding to the right, he pursues a young woman. With
      his right hand raised as if to catch her, he holds onto his garment with his left. Daintily,
      she holds up her dotted chiton with her right hand and carries her kalathos off with her
      left.</P><P>Beneath the ridge which joins the body to the foot, there is a band of
      vertical zigzags; below the design is a reserved band and
      rays.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/2d886c9f9a4f8edc1394205f5af31e7c6eb752608f6d184df75b44e463190e24> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
