<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3086dd5fe840ba49ad226bb918d45c6ed90790eb3dbf23b19a9547c2ff33d5aa> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>On the body, the return of Hephaistos to Olympus with Dionysos
      and silens in a continuous frieze around the entire vessel. In the center of Side A,
      Hephaistos is riding a female mule (donkey in other interpretations), moving to left. Dionysos
      is standing before him, at left, holding with his right hand one of the bridles; the other is
      held by Hephaistos himself. Behind the mule, at right, there is a dancing silen, holding the
      mule's tail with his left hand and a drinking horn with his right. Hephaistos is naked and the
      abnormal shape of his left foot, the only foot visible, indicates that he is lame. He has a
      slim figure, beard and red long hair, and his head breaks the frame of the scene, over the
      tongue's border on the shoulder. The mule is a big animal with a red saddle on her back, on
      which Hephaistos rides. The inner part of her right rear foot is also
      red.</P><P>Dionysos faces Hephaistos and his mount. He is dressed in a black
      chiton and red himation and his pointed beard and hair are also red. He seems to be talking to
      the mule, doing a calming gesture with his left hand. The silen behind Hephaistos is, as the
      other eight represented on the dinos, naked and ithyphallic, with horse tail and feet, bearded
      and with long red hair. On his back, like a back pack, he carries a full wineskin. Behind him
      is another silen following the dance, holding a drinking horn in his left hand and turning his
      head to the right to look over his shoulder to a couple of silens behind him. He has long red
      hair and a leafy beard, masklike face and horse tail and ears, but his feet are human. The two
      following silens are struggling over a wineskin. They have the same characteristics as their
      companions, but the color of their bodies is red, instead of black, probably due to a
      misfiring. The one at right holds the full wineskin in a complicated posture and the one at
      left, facing to the right, is trying to get it and is already grabbing it with his left
      hand.</P><P>On side B, two silens dance facing each other beside a dinos on a
      tripod. The silens act intoxicated, lifting their legs and moving their heads. The one at left
      moves to the right, turning his head back to the left and opening his arms out. His partner
      faces left and dances with his arms against his chest, turning his head to the right to look
      at the dinos behind him. The dinos, standing at right, is black and is decorated with a
      silen's mask.</P><P>The last part of the scene, on the portion of the vase between
      sides B and A, going from left to right, is occupied by the last three silens in the thiasos.
      Two of them, the first and the second from left, are dancing back to back. Their frantic
      dance, with lifting legs and moving heads, follows the music made by the silen at right,
      playing a double flute (aulos). All the silens are nude and ithyphallic, all have horse tails,
      beards and long red hair and some of them have also horse feet. All the figures are drawn in
      white.</P><P>All over the top of the rim, there is a wreath of red and black ivy
      leaves. All around the shoulder goes a border of red, black and white tongues. Under the
      picture there is a chain of red lotus buds and black open lotus flowers, connected between
      themselves by the petals and the stems. Under them a crown of black beams is coming from the
      whole black bottom.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/30a61ad54d4634e366e6862e048570070ed29d01d3900c1c1d1c18b0625079c5> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
