<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3132aeae3f4d0ef7184fcbff41223b958cff3ed6728e8a7400ca41a6ec73200d> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The designs are executed in black-figure on a white ground. A
      kithara-player is mounted on a low platform decorated with egg pattern. He wears a chiton and
      himation, and cloth tassels hang from his instrument. At right, a man dressed in a himation
      leans on his staff to right. He is either a simple listener or a judge of these musical
      contests, which may be those held at the Panathenaia. The man faces a three-stepped platform
      on which two males dressed in himatia stand to the right. The male at right is fully wrapped
      in his himation, with both arms covered. We know from other representations of this subject
      that he is a singer and that the male behind him is playing the double flutes (<hi
      rend="ital">auloi</hi>). The singer is thus an "aulode" and his accompanist an
      "aulete." To the right of the platform and behind the kitharist is a slender, fluted column,
      probably Doric.</P><P>The groundline is a slender black stripe, below which is a
      broader black band framed by reserved stripes. Lower body
      black.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/313564d69ef4ed68fc6a931eb98c015c972871743baa5d0c117e45ad704bdbc5> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """The so-called Hunt Cup. Beaker decorated by trailed slip technique;
      hunting scene including two hounds chasing a hare and a stag in relief between two rows of
      stylized leaves.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

