<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/32f60288923dda527879c91884557a1b8163e63b7b1ea4b1bb64c1229f40ca40> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Herakles sits, in 3/4-view to the left, just left of center. He
      is nude, but reclines on his himation, beneath an apple tree that indicates the locus of the
      scene in the garden of the Hesperides (the daughters of Hesperos), to where he has come to
      fetch the apples of immortality, as his last labor. At left stands a draped Hesperid, profile
      to the right; a second Hesperid sits on a rock, in 3/4-view to the right, although she turns
      her head profile to the left, to look at Herakles. A third Hesperid stands in a near frontal
      pose, with her head turned profile to the left. She is completely draped and veiled, while the
      seated Hesperid wears a dotted garment that only covers her lower torso, legs, and left arm. A
      Pan (woodland god) perches on a rock at the far right, and also turns his head profile to the
      left, to face the hero. The snake, Ladon, is coiled in the tree, above Herakles, and a winged
      Eros hovers above the seated Hesperid.</P><P>Additional decoration includes an
      egg-and-dot frieze above and below the figural scene on both sides (the lower frieze encircles
      the entire vase); a thin egg-and-dot frieze above the main egg-and-dot frieze on side A; and
      palmettes at bottom of each handle.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/32fae84fb6120a731665878f64cf2135d447ac7f281f197481d6aebb63155ad2> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
