<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/34fd9a69f88670d7fff37b03507d5435f19e2a8aac21561ab95246f3a1557bf1> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Side A: maenads at Dionysiac festival. Side B: three women standing."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/350bce5d432d9a5d9407dd73f173041cbce13ba930d3733b6bbb096e16a54c16> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>A: An inscription, <Foreign
      lang=Greek>[*T*O]*N*A*Q[*E]*N*E*Q*E*N*A*Q*L*O*N</Foreign>, along a black column on a
      two-stepped base, with a white Ionic capital and a white plinth (only preserved on the left
      side), and a broader black plinth, on which stands Eirene, 3/4-view to the right, with her
      weight on her left leg, wearing a rayed black stephane, and a peplos belted over a long
      overfold, holds a black scepter in her upraised right hand, and cradles in her left arm
      Ploutos, a small, white boy (of which only the ghost remains), with black hair and a keras;
      Athena Promachos, striding profile to the left; an inscription, <Foreign
      lang=Greek>[*K]*A*L*L*I*M*H*D*H*S*A*R*X[*W*N]</Foreign>, along a black column, like
      that on the left, but with a statue above (only the bottom of which is preserved) on which the
      drapery (a black peplos) is dissimilar to that of Eirene.</P><P>B: A bearded male
      figure, probably the referee, standing 3/4-view to the right, with his head profile to the
      right, wearing a white olive wreath, a black himation draped over his left shoulder, holds his
      right hand at waist level; two wrestlers, still standing, both with short wavy hair; a winged
      white female figure (almost completely faded, neck and head missing) standing frontal, with
      her weight on her left leg, wearing a black himation, rests her left hand on her
      hip.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/350c52a9054f5486d9abf2ebedb444079a8e91ee2ac32c26904339d3d15b7a96> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Below both handles are large palmettes. The groundline on both
      exterior scenes is a band of crossed squares and meanders that circles the vessel. The lip
      bears a frieze of ivy and berries.</P><P>In the tondo, Apollo is killing Tityos.
      Apollo stands in right profile on the left, naked except for a cloak draped over his left
      shoulder and hanging on his back. A strap over his right shoulder holds his striped scabbard
      at his left side. He has a rich floral wreath on his head, with applied gilded berries, and a
      bracelet on his left wrist. His hair is long down his back. In his right hand he holds his
      sword high over his head. In his left hand are his bow and two arrows. Tityos is on his knees
      in the center of the scene, in frontal position with his head raised to the left to look at
      Apollo. He too is naked except for a cloak over his shoulders, decorated with a stripe around
      its border. He has a headband in his hair. His right arm is raised in empty defence while his
      left is crossed over his chest to guard against the brandished sword. His eyes are wide in
      fear. Behind Tityos on the right is a female, probably a goddess, in belted peplos and veil.
      She is lifting the dotted veil from her face with her right hand, revealing a patterned
      headband underneath. With her left hand she lifts the overfall of her garment as she moves
      away from the scene. This figure is taken to be the mother of Tityos, the goddess Ge,
      revealing herself at the moment of his death. At least one scholar, however, has suggested
      that she is really the mother of Apollo, Leto, who is being saved from
      Tityos.</P><P>On the exterior, both sides depict one seated and five standing
      figures. All the standing figures are draped to their knees or below. On side A, the first and
      fourth from the left lean on staffs, while the righthand standing figure carries a lyre. The
      third figure from the left is seated on a stool facing right, with his draped garment pulled
      up over his head. Scattered in the background are a number of objects difficult to recognize
      individually.</P><P>On side B, the same figures have changed position slightly.
      Here the second and sixth from the left lean on staffs, while the third holds a lyre and the
      fourth is seated facing left.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/351152cddd3f0f19686f99ca7ef684944ade0fdc44b86844600b23f33ebf9ef8> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
