<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/358c89805395a3b88255f251fcc4c2905527f715946b5a5ca30b98f649bad439> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Interior: Greek and Amazon. Side A: Gigantomachy. Side B: Departure of chariots. Under
      foot: tripod and two bearded men."""@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3590abcdfa5a08086ace24115be942a32ac04e44eeb0a01ca200bdee3fa89fea> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A:</hi> at left, the bride
      wears a chiton embroidered with crosses, draped over head. She wears a crown with six upright
      leaves. Under the chiton she holds the hand of the groom facing her. To far left, the head of
      a woman is preserved wearing a crown and carrying a torch. The groom wears a himation, a
      wreath of laurel or myrtle and a fillet. Between them, flying to the right and looking back to
      the bride is a small winged Eros who carries a wreath. At right, behind the groom, stands a
      winged Nike holding two torches in her right hand and wearing a chiton, himation, necklace,
      earrings, and a headband with ovulo pattern. The shoulder has tongues and a band of
      ovulos.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/35910c1faee5024aafa09da44c9caffea7eeb0a220e00db47481fb53728316cb> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl><hi rend=\"ital\">BMCRE</hi></bibl> IV, 569, no. 1147; pl. 76, 8; <bibl>Cohen 1880-1892, 4, no. 31</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3596f33d22ddb0078d3e773d102836e67b8fb67db7c7c5fd020e0e9e2781b80b> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Dionysos and giants. Dionysos runs in from the left. He has
      plunged his spear into the side of a giant, fallen opposite the vase's handle. A second giant
      runs to the rescue from the right (too late), thrusting his spear at Dionysos. Dionysos wears
      only a mantle flung over his shoulders, and his characteristic wreath. A small panther rides
      on his outstretched left arm. The fallen giant has been brought down by the snake which is
      wrapped around his legs and waist. He bleeds from his abdomen, where Dionysos' spear is
      entering, and from another wound on his thigh. Although fallen, he still holds his sword in
      one hand and his shield in the other, his left arm through the shield strap. He has a long
      beard, and is naked but for greaves and an elaborate helmet with two high crests. His would-be
      rescuer is also bearded and wears greaves and a crested helmet. He carries a shield with a
      shield-apron; the shield device is not preserved.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/35ac223e2d2f80adb0e0d4486f7424c5b1b0e8b36d7c49f1158dcc3482659a2b> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
