<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/371442171dca607035ab88322a8bd1994228cd780628e89bc3cbb588ddce48af> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Side A</hi>: Apollo and Artemis.
      Apollo and Artemis stand on either side of a low altar, holding offerings. Artemis, on the
      left, faces the altar holding a bow and arrows in her left hand while she tips an oinochoe
      toward the altar with her right. She wears a chiton and himation and a diadem. Apollo stands
      to the right of the altar, facing forward but looking toward Artemis. His lyre is tucked under
      his arm and held against his body as he strums it with his left hand while pouring a libation
      from the phiale in his right. The pouring liquid has been painted with a thin white wash.
      Apollo wears a chiton and himation and has a laurel wreath around his head. The altar, topped
      with volutes, sits on a low base. There are eight strokes of thin red paint, some crossing one
      another, on the front of the altar.</P><P><hi rend="head">Side B</hi>:
      Dionysos and a maenad. A maenad, carrying a snake and an oinochoe, rushes towards Dionysos,
      her long ringlets flying behind her. Dionysos stands facing her, holding a thyrsos in his left
      hand and a kantharos in his right. The maenad wears a long sleeved, low-belted chiton and a
      leopard skin, and has a headband over her hair. Her left arm, holding the snake, is held out
      toward Dionysos, while she swings the oinochoe behind her with her other hand. Dionysos faces
      the maenad, resting the thyrsos against his left shoulder and offering her the kantharos. He
      wears a wreath, chiton and mantle, and has long hair and a long beard. Both figures are
      barefoot.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/371a4c0ca8d1b219937fca06fa91ff550d1651d1a6a13a683488e12a64a22151> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The cup is decorated inside only and the picture is framed by a
      maeander.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Tondo</hi>: a young man offering a
      hare to a seated boy. A youth dressed in a mantle offers a hare to a seated blonde boy
      completely wrapped in a mantle. The youth stands at right, bending down to the boy and holding
      the hare by the ears with his right hand and by the rear feet with his left. He seems to carry
      also a long stick in his left hand, but it is not clear because of the damage of the picture.
      The hare seems to be a love gift (<bibl>Simon
      1975b</bibl>).</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

