    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3a67ae88bec58e139f85f1914ee7bb69aefff726bfdfa0740d0422e85730ba76> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Five worshippers and two children advance to the right"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3a6f45e6f26367d4126a0954aee64d8048c2d94ff4cc269253fa08a1ee513e69> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Danae and Perseus."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3a70a8eed0c1fb0de6b72492c8dfd255d9af6e43eaca3ee276e2186bb5dbd0ff> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A man reclines on a couch, attended by a nude serving boy; the draped legs of a female figure are visible at the left"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3a714e0a0ebff6d8361932a3a38d67af3ca3903b33cd8f07c62a024b773f9ae8> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Side A:</hi> Cavalcade. Only the
      dotted breast of a bird and the back of a filleted head remain below the tongue
      pattern.</P><P><hi rend="head">Side B:</hi> A cavalcade proceeds to
      the left. The frieze is composed of three mounted warriors, each leading his relief horse and
      followed by a bird in flight. A daisy rosette as filling ornament occurs beneath the belly of
      each horse. The horses have elegantly groomed manes, in stripes of red and black and
      elaborately braided tails. Each horseman carries two spears in his right hand and a shield
      with whirligig in his left. Each wears a helmet and greaves. Grounded birds bracket the
      procession at either end; the better-preserved one on the left bends his head back in the
      gesture of a mating crane. Sirens stand under the handles; only a tiny wing fragment of the
      right one is preserved. In the animal frieze below that of the cavalry, a bull or antelope
      moves right. Above the cavalry frieze is an alternating red and white tongue
      pattern.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3a73a69d39e2860ea09775f68a35c102dacef492f29a57f139ec3e86cf44cf5a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Condition"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Good. Small parts of some figures are missing at the joins. The added red of
      the silen's hair is worn.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435429> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3a7b0fc65ef94ca77ac056f85a00d2fc0cc713151309abab395c3dd877d1e13b> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Woman seated at her toilet with four women. In the center a
      woman sits, facing right, and wearing a long Ionic chiton and himation. She holds a mirror in
      her right hand. Her hair is bound with a fillet which holds her diadem in place. At her feet
      and behind her chair are tall baskets or kalathoi (for wool?). In the field over her head
      another fillet hangs. In front of and facing her stands another woman similarly clad, with a
      heavier himation that conceals her left arm and hand. In her right hand she holds a casket
      which she offers to the seated woman. The fillet in her hair holds no diadem. Behind her hangs
      a wreath. She is followed by a third woman almost identically clad, who stretches out her
      right hand, palm up. This woman is partly concealed by the right handle. Behind the seated
      woman a fourth advances towards her, draped in a long Ionic chiton and himation, her left arm
      bent at the elbow with a rather unconvincing attempt at foreshortening, her right arm at her
      side. Her hair is bound in a fillet. Behind her comes yet another, a fifth, similarly clad,
      holding in her right hand a branch with spiral tendrils. She is partly hidden by the left
      handle.</P><P>On the rim of the lip and at the junction of handles and body there
      are egg-and-dart patterns. There is a palmette-lotus at the foot of the neck in front only.
      Meanders and saltire-squares occur below the design.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3a7d729a75c2466ae9e8ffb4dc161374988940ff03b1c4419bc4d2a3ecf7d912> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Mollard-Besques 1954, 21, cat. B 122 (with previous bibliography), pl. 16</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3a8b6e90864317b2d32001acea4ab5da126b806418dfa05f7b66cd86b7956c6e> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The base of the vessel is decorated with a ray pattern, above
      which is a band of plaits. Above this is an animal frieze depicting three hounds closing in on
      a hare. A thin black line separates this frieze from the main one above. Here Bellerophon is
      on the winged horse Pegasos, flying to the left to attack the Chimaera. There is a tiny lizard
      in between them. Behind the Chimaera and Bellerophon are two walking sphinxes, facing a floral
      column. The field is decorated with dot-rosettes, crosses, pendent spirals, and chevrons.
      Above the main frieze is another black line, and above that on the shoulder is a band of lotus
      flowers backed by palmettes (compare to Friis Johansen, 119, fig, 77). On the top of the rim
      is a band of spirals, around which is a tongue pattern. The handle is decorated with a
      guilloche pattern.</P><P>Benson(List 12, no. 4) attributes this vase to the
      Sacrifice Painter, while Dunbabin and Robertson (179) think it belongs to the Macmillan
      Painter.</P><P>Amyx (626) notes that this vase and <rs type="vase">Aegina
      K253</rs> are good representations of a theme which was popular in Attic Black Figure,
      but not generally common in Corinthian.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3a973f2154e039f3c0c235798753e8ca77d53b7ce6ebb579304fa9950e567bac> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Stewart 1990, fig. 446</bibl>; <bibl>Karouzou 1968, 59</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3a98c77af157e3a293fcd0025fb1a5f592febb3b711ca9dd7690de0f0e96369a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Side A: Dionysos in his chariot<lb>Side B: Dionysos with maenad, satyr and a male
      figure"""@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3a9cfda05ac9e733e6c16cac7864718a216ba580516d9180d4d165de0d2f79ab> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: A scene of arming of a
      young warrior. He stands at left of the scene with his sword in its scabbard in his left hand,
      about to put it on. He has a very long hair, falling down his back and tied with a taenia or
      band. In front of him, facing left, is his wife (or perhaps his sister), a woman dressed in
      long chiton with her hair tied up with two fillets. In her left hand she holds his shield,
      which has a threatening snake as device. In her right hand she offers him his helmet, holding
      it by the cheek flaps. The helmet has a very long crest and a device in
      front.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: Scene of departure or
      arming. A man with a spear is shown at the moment of leaving for the battle. He is standing at
      left, wrapped in a himation and holding a staff in his hand. In front of him, a woman with
      long hair tied with a diadem is offering him a phiale in her right hand. She holds a jug (a
      hydria or an oinochoe) in her left, from which she just filled the phiale. She is dressed in
      long chiton and himation.</P><P>The figures are over a decoration band. Under
      either handle there is a palmette.</P><P>The two scenes depict two different
      aspects of such arming and departure scenes: the more technical aspect of arming and the more
      ritual aspect of the libation.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3aa9f221bbc89e4d14d8eb2179a4b920952441d2db45fef93b96ad7b6e96660a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Trendall 1989, fig. 61</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3aad226307f77c73fff8a63ebdb6fe8a8fd16825c6684c06a90f62291c3550ab> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
