<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3c3f988fd38f7293146f5967a53dc2af5d825fb94150951c2cd07b4b3b24ddd3> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Body: friezes of chariots and warriors. Neck, side A: six men mourning. Neck, side B:
      five women mourning."""@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3c62754f90ecdeb6a872ac91713e21062f8afee3972d0b398a67a69697ff3d56> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Interior: Tondo:</hi> Herakles,
      youthful and beardless, bends forward to the right. He has locked both arms from above around
      the neck of the Nemean lion. With his head pressed downwards against its mane, compresses the
      throat so that its jaws are open and its tongue protruding. The lion's forepaws are planted
      firmly on the thighs of Herakles, whose club has fallen behind him. The contest takes place on
      rocky ground, indicated by an irregularly shaped space left red. The tondo is encircled within
      a band of single meanders interspersed with six red cross squares.</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Exterior: Side A:</hi> Herakles moves to the right behind three bulls,
      the foremost of which, drawn almost in a frontal pose, turns back to look at Herakles. The
      hero is bearded and wears the lion's skin over his head and girt at the waist. He holds his
      club in his right hand and a cornucopia(?) in his left. In the background beside the hindmost
      bull is a leafless tree with six branches.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Exterior:
      Side B:</hi> Herakles, bending forward to the right, has caught the bull by both horns
      as it springs forward and seems to be forcing it backward. His head passes under that of the
      bull so that the bull's muzzle rests on the shoulders of Herakles. The hero is youthful here
      as in the tondo, distinguishable by the club which has dropped to his feet. In the background
      beside the bull, a bearded man stands looking on and raises his right hand as if in surprise.
      He is wearing a mantle and leans forward on his staff. On the right another bearded spectator
      carries a club on his left shoulder and raises his right hand in surprise. He wears a short
      girt chiton, the lower part of which is decorated with a border of vertical black rays, and a
      skin (a nebris?).</P><P>Beneath and beside the handles there is a quadruple
      palmette ornament.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3c639d1f509092858747393883cb980ba497ab26b0c2b79d5af05d6a4a7353cb> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Gnecchi 1912, 50, 2, pl. 25.8</bibl>; <bibl><hi rend=\"ital\">RIC</hi></bibl> IV.3, 177, no. 164" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3c7a01943043a433aa582f706e86293c64b301bb378545497056708dc4915ede> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Condition"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The remains of five bronze rivets are found in the bowl, as well as two empty
      holes connected by a deep channel, made for a different repair.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435429> .

