    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The necks and heads of two overlapping horses, profile to the left"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3e679ad5f34c4699622e2d3a45ec2464b6a087d511094634482a86aaf06e0b88> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Antheia, standing 3/4-view to the left, with her head profile
      to the right, wears a white, belted chiton, a dark himation draped over both arms and held in
      both hands, and a sakkos. Peitho, standing profile to the right, with her weight on her left
      leg, and her right leg relaxed, wears a sleeveless, belted chiton with a long kolpos, a
      sphendone, and two bands on each wrist, holds a small baton in her left hand, and reaches her
      right hand to an unidentifiable object (a game for the child?). A female figure (Eukleia?),
      seated profile to the right on rock, wearing a medium-sleeved chiton, a dark himation wrapped
      around her legs, a sphendone, and earrings, raises her left hand thoughtfully toward her
      lowered head, and holds a small bird in her raised right hand. A young, nude boy, Kephimos?,
      runs profile to the left, wearing amulets around his chest, left foot, and wrist, reaches both
      arms to the bird. In front of a laurel (?) tree stands Eunomia, in 3/4-view to the left,
      wearing a sleeveless, belted chiton with a kolpos and long hair. She reaches her right hand to
      the tree, and holds a plate of fruit in her left hand, at waist level. Paidia, standing
      profile to the left, wears a sleeved chiton, a himation draped over her left shoulder, a
      sphendone, and earrings, holds an unidentifiable object in her left hand, and dangles a beaded
      necklace from her right hand.</P><P>Machaira interprets Antheia as one of the
      Horai. Jucker (67) has interpreted the unlabelled character as Eukleia on the basis of her
      cult association with Eunomia, or Aphrodite (who is usually accompanied by Peitho); an
      alternate interpretation has been provided by Kron (291) who identified her as Herse, mother
      of Kephalos, on the incorrect assumption that the boy is the Attic hero Kephalos, rather than
      Kephimos. On the speculative basis that this white-ground lekythos served a distinctly
      funerary purpose, the unidentified mother, and the child Kephimos may represent merely a
      mortal mother and son, as paralleled on grave stelai. Although Paidia and Peitho are both
      laden with erotic overtones, the innocent nature of this scene discourages us from ascribing
      particular erotic connotations in this context.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3e69b704eddd3e04c10e165c5cd057ec9395e64bd550dc8442da22b947ba1104> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>This is the largest hydria preserved. <hi
      rend="ital">Side A:</hi> Herakles murdering Busiris and ten of his servants.
      Hemelrijk maintains that Reichhold did not accurately reproduce all the details; there are
      eleven figures in the throes of death illustrated on this side. <hi rend="ital">Side
      B:</hi> Bodyguard of Busiris. Hemelrijk thinks that Sides A and B belong together.
      <hi rend="ital">Lower Frieze:</hi> Boarhunt. The boar to left is being attacked by
      eight youths and seven dogs. A white dog clings to the neck and a red dog clings to the belly
      of the boar. Hemelrijk does not believe this scene to be related to the
      others.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

