<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/3f0688d1c967aa585897fae803b8e92d013f7c3ebaaeb44876f516994a9036f1> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Nike and anabates
      (victorious athlete). On the left, the Nike is shown in profile, facing right. She holds a
      victory wreath out to the rider with her right hand. She wears earrings and bracelets, and her
      chiton is decorated with a wavy black border (the folds of the drapery are typical of the
      Anabates Painter's early work). Towards her comes the rider on his horse. He is shown in the
      process of leaping off, and his feet (as well as all four of the horses' hooves!) are off the
      ground. He is nude, and carries a small shield. The horse wears a bridle. These two figures
      are rather awkward looking, particularly because, as mentioned above, they seem to be
      levitating in midair. Behind them is the turn-pole, with a fluted column and Ionic
      capital.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: Three standing youths. The
      youth on the left is in profile to the right. He is wrapped in a himation, so that only his
      head and feet show. One of his hands is bunched up under the cloth near his stomach. In the
      center of the composition a youth stands frontally, with his head turned in profile to the
      left. His himation is draped to leave his right arm and shoulder free, and in that hand he
      holds a staff. The youth on the far right is in profile to the left, also swathed in an
      himation. His left hand under the cloth appears to be on his hip.</P><P>Around the
      rim of the vessel is painted an olive wreath. The lower border is a band of stopped meanders
      alternating with patterned squares (crosses and dotted saltires). Under the handles is a
      floral pattern, while around the handles is a tongue
      pattern.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

