<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4229d87580b8da50e4955226877e8fbff359408b05931703f38d63a9ef4b6eb5> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Athena mounting chariot, Herakles."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4234f0f67c34a608c65d34bbd32f5d96bb788fca35db30094cc8165cdebc6f5f> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Tondo</hi>: a bearded satyr dances
      in left profile, his weight on his left leg, with his right leg raised. His right arm is
      extended. His left hand holds a panther skin behind his back; the feet of the skin are brought
      forward over his shoulder. There is a drinking horn in the field before his left foot. The
      tondo is bordered by a leftward meander between reserved bands.</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Side A</hi>: a maenad dressed in a chiton with overfold and sakkos
      reclines to her left against a rock, with legs drawn up and a thyrsos resting against her left
      shoulder. She looks to her left at an approaching bearded satyr, shown in left profile with
      his arms outstretched. A drinking horn (rhyton) is visible in the field between the figures.
      Behind the maenad is another bearded satyr standing frontally with right knee bent, and his
      left leg extended out behind him. His right hand grasps his phallos, while his left arm is
      extended toward the maenad. A drinking horn rests in the field behind him. Relief contour is
      used, as well as dilute glaze for hair, the satyr's beard, and some drapery
      folds.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B:</hi> the left leg of a satyr
      running toward a maenad, of whom only the lower part draped in a chiton survives. To her left
      is the hand or tail of another satyr. Dilute glaze is used for the chiton. There is an ivy
      leaf under each handle. (Other fragments of this vase are preserved in Florence: Beazley,
      <hi rend="ital">Campana Fragments in Florence</hi> [London, 1933] 16,
      #42.)</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/42389a33c6844330624dd4fff2c41fdd96f1c5bfb4baae177bbb2ea6ea1c6ac0> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<hi rend=\"ital\"><bibl>BMCRR</bibl></hi> I, 311, 2448 ff.; pl. XXXVIII.1; <bibl>Babelon 1885-1886, 2.406,1</bibl>; <hi rend=\"ital\"><bibl>RRC</bibl></hi> no. 348/1, pl. XLV" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4239a1447388fedbaca5a0280d93c9a4e33b2b4c100aa924ec12b92db93c20bd> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Interior: Pegasus<lb>Exterior, side A: satyrs and maenad <lb>Exterior, side
      B: komos"""@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/424474abc578483d02ebe6a9b2c3e22b26f98be203b0e99606d10f985fbab45e> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A:</hi> At center stands
      Athena, facing left between two Doric columns surmounted by cocks. Her head, waist, and feet
      are shown in profile, while her shoulders are frontal. She holds her shield with her right
      hand and raises a spear high above her head with her left. She wears a helmet, the high crest
      of which overlaps the tongue frieze on the upper border of the panel, the shield bears the
      device of a wheel in added white. She wears a long, sleeveless chiton embroidered with
      patterns of stars and spirals, girt at the waist, over which hangs her aegis decorated with a
      scale pattern and inner and outer fringes of snakes. Rapid movement is indicated by the folds
      of her chiton. Her face and arms are in added white for female flesh. In front of Athena is
      inscribed <foreign lang="Greek">*T*O*N*A*Q*E*N*E*Q*E*N*A*Q*L*O*N</foreign>, "(I
      am) one of the prizes from Athens." On the shield is inscribed, <foreign
      lang="Greek">*E*U*F*I*L*E*T*O*S *K*A*L*O*S</foreign>, "Euphiletos is
      beautiful."</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B:</hi> There are four bearded
      nude athletes, competing in the pentathlon. They proceed from left to right. On the left is
      one with both arms raised, holding halteres (jumping weights) painted in added white, and
      about to leap. The next is about to hurl a spear which he holds by the amentum (throwing
      strap) in his right hand. His left hand is raised for balance. His left knee is also raised,
      about to lunge forward. The next walks, carrying his discus in his right hand, his left hand
      raised. On the right another figure, walking right, turns his head to the left. He gets ready
      to throw his spear with his right hand. He holds the spear by the amentum (throwing strap)
      with the point down and keeps his left hand raised and out of the way of the
      javelin.</P><P>On the neck there is a lotus-palmette chain. Beneath a raised
      fillet at the junction of neck and shoulder is a circumscribed tongue pattern in black and
      added red. On the lower body above the foot is a band of
      rays.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/424762edf6cc3ab56a001c322aed971b7078c1e50fe9352c72e15e0c2ac36db0> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Side A: three komasts. At left, nude youth wearing a red fillet
      has a himation over left shoulder. He dances to right while playing auloi, his cheeks puffed.
      His penis is infibulated. His staff leans in background. In center, nude komast with a
      himation over both arms dances to right. His staff is in his right hand and a kylix in his
      left. All three have red wreaths. At right a bearded man cloaked in a himation walks right
      looking back to left, a staff in his right hand.</P><P>Side B: three komasts. At
      left, a nude man dances to right with body turned frontal, head to left. He wears a red
      wreath, has a cloak over his shoulder, and a staff in his hand. At center, a nude bearded man,
      with a cloak over his shoulders, plays a barbiton, his head thrown back in song. At right, a
      youth in himation wearing a red wreath walks right, and bends over, a staff in his right
      hand.</P><P>Below the handles is a single palmette flanked by tendrils. An ivy
      leaf appears on either side of each handle.</P><P>Tondo: a youth in a himation and
      red fillet stands to right holding a knotty red staff in both arms. His incipient beard is
      shown in dilute glaze. Behind at left is a stepped structure with stripes in dilute glaze
      (rock?). Hanging behind from a peg is an aryballos with trailing cords. The frame is made of
      groups of three meanders to right alternating with cross
      squares.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/42499b338aec9f55838400a1def7a74e38f8072cd70c8c99cb3fc42aff8410b1> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Seated Asklepios"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/424cc16d72462a6993a501a3adc0b2987ddb8582c5242011fa218aeb180d237c> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Of the vase, a quarter of the rim remains, with one handle and
      handle plate (also the adjoining part of the neck and a little of the shoulder). On the handle
      plate is depicted a seated ithyphallic man in an attitude of dejection. He sits on the ground
      with his knees drawn up slightly. He rests his head in his hands, and looks down at his feet.
      He wears a headband and exomis, and has a short beard and short hair. His exomis and the tip
      of his penis (showing under his legs) are painted purple. His beard is outlined in incision,
      which is typical of black figure painting, but then both hair and beard are picked out in
      incised dashes, to signify that they are bristly (Seeberg, 26). There are reserved areas of
      paint on his wrists, ankles, and an amoeba-shaped one on his shoulder. These indicate that he
      is a bound prisoner with a tattoo.</P><P>Seeberg notes a similar representation of
      a prisoner on a plaque from Penteskouphia, now in Berlin (Seeberg, pl. IIIa). This man is not
      bound, but has the same bristly hair and beard and wears an exomis like the man in the Boston
      fragment. Possibly the Boston fragment represents a scene from comedy (Seeberg, 31)especially
      because the man is ithyphallic.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4252a98026fb630cf16371d9aaee99055d6637d9ca46e004302dc97a701998f1> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
