<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4908c4dee19841cd8b99e1885ba5d383f58db43bfdbfa5b32baab47551e60fc7> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The inside is glazed except for the reserved line just below
      the glazed rim and the tondo, which is bounded by three concentric circles. On the outside, a
      narrative zone encircles the bowl, enclosed above by a single and below by a triple line.
      Around the lower bowl, separated from the narrative zone by a single line, are 53 base rays,
      alternately glazed and reserved. They are set between triple and double lines. A glaze band
      and a double line complete the pattern above the glazed fillet at the junction of the bowl and
      the foot. The foot has a reserved edge, and a reserved base. The stem is glazed within,
      misfired red, and the bottom of the bowl is reserved. The handles are glazed outside, reserved
      within.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: A frontal head of Dionysos,
      wearing an ivy wreath. It is executed in outline technique, and is placed between feminine
      apotropaic eyes. The god wears what might be a false beard, attached above the ears by strings
      or wires (<bibl>CVA, 44</bibl>). Surrounding the god's head are small-leaved
      grapevines, laden with small clusters of grapes. Large-leaved, fruit-laden vines grow around
      the handles and the outer sides of the eyes. The leaves of the wreath in Dionysos' hair are
      red, as are the pupils of his eyes, and the first concentric circle of the apotropaic eyes.
      The Second concentric band is painted white.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side
      B</hi>: The same as Side A</P><P><hi rend="ital">Int.</hi>: A
      Gorgoneion is depicted. It has a red tongue, pupils, and a wrinkle on its nose; alternating
      locks of hair are also painted red. The other locks of hair, teeth, and irises are painted
      white.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4914dce3f133739238be002622af6016588844a1114a860527c9f2e570202e6a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Boreas pursuing
      Oreithyia in presence of Athena. In the middle of the scene, Athena stands between Boreas and
      Oreithyia. At left, moving to right, Boreas, the god of the North wind, is running with his
      hands outstretched, pursuing Oreithyia, the daughter of the king of Attica, who is fleeing to
      right. Athena is a tall figure, dressed in a pleaded belted peplos and wearing a diadem in her
      hair and holding a spear, leaning on her left shoulder. Boreas is bearded, wearing a short
      chiton, and has large wings, winged shoes and a wreath in his hair. Oreithyia is dressed in a
      long chiton and himation and wears a band in her hair.</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Side B</hi>: Two maidens and Erechtheus. Two young women come fleeing
      from both sides to the centre of the scene where Erechtheus, the king of Attica, stands
      holding a scepter. Erechtheus is a bearded man, wrapped in a himation and wearing shoes. The
      maidens, dressed in chiton and himation, seem to be two companions of Oreithyia, perhaps
      Aglauros and Pandrosos fleeing to Erechtheus to report the events. The one at left, a short
      and black-haired young woman, comes walking to meet Erechtheus with her left hand extended and
      her right concealed in her himation. The one at right turns her head to look backwards at the
      scene on side A. She has the hair tied up in a sakkos or another type of hat. Her left hand is
      concealed in the himation and her right is lifting her dress.</P><P>The picture
      represents the pursuing and abduction of Oreithyia by Boreas. As is ususal in such scenes of
      abduction, the maiden (often a goddess or heroine) is in the company of other maidens (often
      nymphs), who are the witnesses to the facts. In this case Oreithyia is the daughter of
      Erechtheus, the legendary king of Attica. The setting of the abduction is Attica with the
      presence of its goddess, Athena.</P><P>The composition on both sides seems to be
      symmetrical.</P><P>Under the handles large addorsed palmettes with florals growing
      from them.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4916e9a556ea3246a945f70f785c4092f98de5f07957034b44818d538a798c51> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Vermeule & Comstock 1988, frontispiece</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/491d011a44763f78eb26630e591f6db8e50fb1771ed88243d3b8aeab3ff6d56f> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Three friezes. <hi rend="head">Top</hi>: gorgoneion
      between sphinxes and a lion. The gorgoneion has incised locks, dots around the eyes and on the
      cheeks, spiral curls on the forehead and a red circle on the bottom lip of the grinning mouth.
      She is flanked by seated sphinxes with curled wings and tails. The maned lion, his mouth open,
      stands left behind the sphinx on the right (under the handle).</P><P><hi
      rend="head">Middle</hi>: tripod between a pair of horsemen with panthers and a padded
      dancer. A small tripod with ring handles, situated beneath the gorgoneion in the top frieze,
      is flanked by mounted horsemen dressed in short, red chitons. The horses' manes are drawn with
      alternating red and black locks. A lion, its head turned frontal face, stands behind each
      horseman. A padded dancer follows the one on the right, facing left. He dances with his knees
      bent and one arm up and one arm down. He is bearded and has shoulder-length
      hair.</P><P><hi rend="head">Bottom</hi>: eagle with displayed wings
      flanked by lions and a grazing stag. The eagle stands to the left, his head and neck stretched
      to the left. The lions stand open-mouthed, the one on the right followed by a grazing
      stag.</P><P>Rosettes incised with arcs at the edges and crossed blobs fill the
      space around the figures in these friezes.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/491d451f0ee790c5780006190f6a81dc70dc19591f5eabc12c37a8aea3ac4518> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
