<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4b7f61a8bb4cf1ef3431ae1c203012033ab47a05b85e8e7ee0cb77bf89520d64> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Dionysos Mainomenos
      ("the frenzied Dionysos"). Dionysos walks to the left, his hands held above his head. In his
      right hand, which is raised before him, he holds the hindquarters of a fawn (or kid) which he
      has torn in half. He holds the forequarters in his other hand. He looks downward with his eyes
      wide open and staring. An ivy wreath is in his hair, and he wears a loose-fitting, crinkly
      chiton which falls in even folds below the red belt at his waist. A spotted panther's skin
      flung around his shoulders, with the paws tied at the neck. His feet are clad in high, laced
      boots.</P><P>While it is common to see maenads, the followers of Dionysos, tearing
      up animals, it is unusual to see the god himself doing this (Maffre,
      204).</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: A satyr walks to the right,
      playing auloi. He wears an ivy wreath in his hair, which falls in long ringlets on his neck
      and over his bald forehead. In front of his face is the inscription <foreign
      lang="Greek">*K*O*M[*O*S]</foreign> ("song").</P><P>At the mouth of the
      vessel are two rows of dotted egg-pattern, on the shoulder are tongues. The lower border on
      both sides is a meander alternating with cross-squares. The decoration is Nolan style, but the
      small palmette at the base of the handles is not orthodox (Philippaki,
      54).</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4b947e844f4bcb1e8f717299dee52aacf796d815d7b4a4e81bce2336a2ed7c49> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
