<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4d3c5197dcde746b2c9fb8c6f1d093ef63fa64ae374b66f86d8b31e6106d4553> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Antikenmuseum 1988, 31 no. 11</bibl>; <bibl>Rolley 1983/1986, fig. 33</bibl>; <bibl>Antikenmuseum 1980, no. 4</bibl>; <bibl>Boardman 1978a, fig. 8</bibl>; <bibl>Himmelmann 1964</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4d3ed36013b093b9d23cd562cd153f11470c1dcb6530bf2d2bb0e2efb6727a20> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>A man and a woman stand on either side of a tomb. The beardless
      man stands at left in a contrapposto pose with his weight on his proper left leg and his right
      arm at his side. He is shown in three-quarter view with his head in profile to the right. He
      wears a chlamys, fastened at the shoulder with a disc brooch, and high laced sandals. His
      curly chin-length hair is uncovered, while a petasos hangs down his back from the string tied
      around his neck. He holds two spears, slightly different in length, in his left hand, their
      butts resting on the ground. The young man turns toward a marble grave stele encircled by six
      tainiai. The stele, which has a plain shaft resting on two steps and an echinus top set off by
      two lines, is topped by a palmette and scroll akroterion. To the right of the stele stands a
      woman, facing left in three-quarter view. Her hair, worn in a bun, is uncovered, and she wears
      a small pendant earring of crescent shape. She once wore a long Doric chiton with a long
      belted overfold, now mostly gone, and sandals tied above her ankles. The outline of her body
      above the waist, once hidden by the chiton, is preserved, in contrast to the lower body and
      legs, which were not drawn in outline. She holds a bronze phiale in her right hand and a
      tainia in her left, while she carries another tainia draped over her left arm at the
      elbow.</P><P>The Painter of Munich 2335 decorated both red-figure and white ground
      vases, the latter generally superior in quality. He was one of the last artists to work in
      both techniques. <bibl>Oakley 1990, 65</bibl> has shown that the Painter of Munich
      2335 decorated vases in the workshops of the Schuwalow Painter, the Achilles Painter, and the
      Phiale Painter, useful evidence for understanding the interaction of vase painters and the
      structure of workshops.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4d40ae407e7b86c81fbe6aa335e323451d07db83994bab5dd5e6c0b2910370ad> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Interior: a Scythian archer running to left, looking back. He
      holds his bow with his right hand and draws an arrow with his left from a quiver slung around
      his shoulder.</P><P>He is beardless, and wears a dappled jacket, trousers, and a
      kidaris or Scythian hat whose flaps hang down onto his chest. Inscription around scene,
      <foreign lang="Greek">*E*P*I*K*T*E*T*O*S *E*G*R*A*S*F*E*N</foreign> ("Epiktetos
      painted it").</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4d4379e13630b9a68f17cd9ca3f29498bbf496d50e2b76f47e1b4db434d524d3> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
