<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4ddab2871d101ad36471f9479971ab60a28631cd0663c178292c37247ace51b3> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>In the center of the composition on both sides of the vase, a
      naked boy has climbed an olive tree and is picking the crop. On the ground at the left, a
      naked youth with a mantle around his shoulders and a fillet in his hair appears to dance. On
      the right, a bearded man dances, also naked except for a cloak and a
      wreath.</P><P>Although the boy in the tree finds a parallel in the depiction of
      the olive harvest by the Antimenes Painter (<rs type="vase">London B 226</rs>),
      the scene on the Mississippi vase clearly differs from such depictions of the harvest. The
      wreathed dancers appear to designate the scene as some sort of harvest
      festival.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4de099053ec71f03d93238e37a7c385f24dca3f5318d17819c4b28f5cbe2ddb7> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Peribolos wall; surrounding the Sanctuary of Apollo on the eastern slope of Mount Parnassos."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4de56b5153d6aeade3be05507ba710e4088d22514bd6edd5b0f79a15662e578d> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Karouzou 1968, 149</bibl>; <bibl>Svoronos 1903-12, 329, pl. 39.2</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4deab70fc0b7a8173e16d5e670956f12429e03d5d3176871e736b0549dface9e> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>A woman stands at left, holding out a rolled-up mantle to her
      attendant. She wears a sleeved chiton with long overfold; her hair is bound by a purple band.
      Before her, her maid, wearing a similar dress, holds out both hands to take the mantle. Above
      the two figures, <foreign lang="Greek">*U*G*I*A*I*N*O*N *K*A*L*O*S</foreign>
      ("Hygiainon is beautiful").</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4df091ab2a98a98b443d4cacdaff222c4d8aa630623dd4211a6cf8738f4e0153> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Black figure on white ground on the body panel. Theseus
      advances to right plunging his sword into the Minotaur which falls to right while looking back
      at Theseus. With his right hand, the Minotaur holds up a red rock which Theseus parries with
      his left hand. The Minotaur holds another red rock in his lowered left hand. The monster's
      body is covered with red circles and he has a red snarling mouth. The horned head has an
      almost lionine appearance. The nude Theseus wears a red fillet and a red sword baldric. The
      tip of the scabbard is also red. Flanking the action is a pair of women, possibly Pasiphae and
      Ariadne. The woman at left gestures with both hands and has a pointed nose and chin; she wears
      a peplos and himation, both with red dots, and a red fillet. The woman at right, whose hand
      and face are missing, is mantled in a red-striped himation, and wears a red fillet. In the
      background at left and right are sinuous grape vines.</P><P>In panel on shoulder
      (red ground) are linked black palmettes framed above by a band of net pattern and below by a
      key pattern. Bands of dicing on a white ground frame the scene on sides. The ground line is a
      double red stripe encircling the vase. Red stripes also surround the exterior and interior of
      the mouth, and the foot.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4df7fab67f4743f729001e5a336bc11dfc7831239076a7375fc68803ff93ff88> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Apollo with bow and quiver"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4df96e4f8e6ad65f50df7c31490e0cbdcf14e5f4348a84e237be4323bccf8781> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Winged female figure, labelled Ananke, running profile to the right, her
      straight left leg advanced, while her right leg is bent back, wearing a medium-sleeved belted
      chiton with a long kolpos, and a red sakkos, from which a single black lock of hair protrudes,
      bends both arms and holds her skirt in her left hand, lowered at her side, while she holds a
      torch upright in her right hand.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4dfad86c2250c3707d01434502f1591cfb0e894531d1df635417b1e7fdacebe0> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Mionnet 1806-1838</bibl> Suppl. II, 234, no. 171; <bibl>Sear 1982, 200, no. 2127</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/4dfb6dc20d4ca27996cfec3e830124c50a756824d66f30f747578a707d47d2b8> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """A female figure (perhaps Oinanthe), standing profile to the right (only
      the top of her left hand is preserved), holds and object (perhaps a flower) in her upraised
      right hand; a male figure (?) standing profile to the right, holds two spears upright in his
      left hand (only the front of his face and spearheads are preserved); bearded Dionysos
      (labelled) (head, top of thyrsos, left shoulder, and upper arm are preserved), seated profile
      to the left in a chair, wearing a long, broad taenia with a vertical flap, holds a thyrsos
      upright in his right hand (?) and leans the top of his left hand on the back of his chair;
      Opora (labelled) (preserved above the waist), standing 3/4-view to the left, wearing a peplos,
      a sphendone, and black disk earrings, raises a bowl (phiale?) in her right hand, and hangs her
      left arm at her side.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

