<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/5544cc8664e9678e76895c7c88450e4147ac9be764b9356cf558f05b53593676> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Karouzou 1968, 156</bibl>; <bibl>Svoronos 1903-12, pl. 67</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/5545b8f1efbab78ff0efbb8c72e230352920f930e9c05a7482874004c6362c60> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>A: A nude satyr (preserved below mid-torso), standing profile
      to the right, with his weight on his right leg, his left leg bent, reaches his right hand to
      the right; Paidia (labelled in the field to the left of her head) standing near profile to the
      left, with her weight on her right leg, and her left leg bent, wearing a nebris draped over
      her left shoulder, belted over a sleeveless chiton, two bands on each wrist, and long hair,
      cradles a thyrsos in her left arm, and hangs her right arm at her side; a satyr running
      3/4-view to the left (shoulder/head and middle of legs are not preserved), both legs bent,
      advances his right leg; a female figure standing profile to the right, is barely preserved,
      except for the lower portion of her drapery; a satyr standing profile to the left (only
      preserved below the waist) crosses his legs.</P><P>B: A satyr advancing 3/4-view
      to the right, bent at his waist and both knees, with his right leg advanced, holds, in both
      hands, to the right a piece of drapery; a maenad, perhaps Paidia, or more likely Eudia,
      standing 3/4-view to the left, wearing a belted peplos, and a himation, holds a kantharos in
      her right hand, and cradles a thyrsos in her left arm; a satyr standing 3/4-view to the right,
      with weight on his left leg, and his right leg bent, rest his right hand on his waist, and
      holds a stick in his left hand; a maenad standing profile to the right, wearing a chiton with
      a long kolpos, holds a thyrsos diagonally in her left hand, and reaches her right hand
      forward; a satyr standing profile to the left, with his weight on his left leg, and his right
      leg bent back, wears a nebris draped down his back.</P><P>Tondo: Peitho
      (labelled), standing profile to the right, wearing a peplos, a himation, and a stephane,
      holding her right hand up (in a speaking gesture?), and resting her left hand on the shoulder
      of a small Pothos (labelled), standing 3/4-view to the right, legs crossed, rests his right
      arm on Peitho, and hangs his right hand at his side; Ariadne and Dionysos are entwined,
      Ariadne wearing a chiton (?), a sphendone (?), and 2 black bands on each wrist; bearded
      Dionysos, wearing an ivy wreath, long leather boots, a short tunic, and a himation draped over
      his right arm, holds a thyrsos in his left hand, and a kantharos in his right hand, which he
      reaches to the right; nude, young satyr, Komos, standing profile to the left, with legs
      crossed, holds a torch in his left arm, and a handle of Dionysos' kantharos in his upraised
      right hand.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/554688a882d0267ad2863540ce25606fce64b064a37a5b71669aa31087bcf03b> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Condition"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The vase is in fairly good condition. Some of the fragments are less well
      preserved than others. Some have lost most of their surface or are scraped or discolored,
      while others are quite well-preserved.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435429> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/555ef8b9acbad3bef0352e8174573bec712f3ae2c43a2e31c2d351cd02fd7f65> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The vessel is in the form of a hare, with legs extended and
      head thrown back. The mouth of the vessel is at the base of the throat. The eyes are
      platically modelled, and the upper part of the baody is stippled with fine dots of black
      glaze.</P><P>The dead, suspended hare was an Ionian type, reused in Corinthian art
      (<bibl>Payne 1931, 177</bibl>). It is a common motif in vases and wall painting;
      the dead hare symbolized the successful hunt (<bibl>M. Maximova, <hi
      rend="ital">Les Vases Plastiques dans l'Antiquit (poque Archaique)</hi>, (Paris
      1927):110</bibl>).</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/5567fc3fa915a6b32c00df5448296a0e39b692268bcfe9e8fc583e597edbb906> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Svoronos 1903-12, 635, pl. 140</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/55705674a12ac0056978151a3221fac02322551f4a5605fde7716cdd5b8098e4> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Condition"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Unbroken; considerable chipping and pitting of the rim and interior. Lusterless
      glaze with a rough, granulated surface.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435429> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/558593f6b0e15e019d6cdb76bb80496592b04c96a70d148cde32bc623639ea89> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Side A: a bull facing left, with his head to the ground and
      kneeling on one leg, is attacked by two lions biting his shoulders and haunches Added red is
      used for dots in the lions' manes and for blood under their mouth and claws. In the field
      above is the inscription <foreign
      lang="Greek">*I*S*I*S</foreign>.</P><P>Side B: Two bulls at a basin, the
      one at right drinking from the basin. Grapevines in the background. Triple palmette group on
      each side of neck. Band of tongues above pictorial zone. Staggered row of dots below ground
      line. Rays at base.</P><P>The vase was attributed to the Michigan Painter, who was
      identified by Beazley as the same as the Painter of Brussels R 312. The scene on Side A was
      known from temple pedimental sculpture of the last half of the sixth century
      B.C.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/55860ffd4f16e8e3ca8a87e0cb3f5b181161c79ee5db10ec76d78807a5910bad> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
