<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/55c3d59257036fd1eec76954c9a62cf1c1fd3cbc6ff0fc50e6710cf4cc9e4ebe> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Side A:</hi> Herakles, at the left
      of the composition moving right, drives Kerberus in front of him. Herakles wears a belted
      lionskin over a short chiton with a decorated border. The lion's head covers his own and the
      hero is bearded. He carries a quiver on his back. Herakles carries his club in his right hand
      which he raises over his head threatening the monster. His left hand grasps the chain holding
      the dog. Kerberus is depicted as a monstrous dog having two heads, a long mane whichcovers
      both necks and continues down the back, and a snake for a tail. Kerberus moves to the right in
      front of Herakles, with one head looking back at him. Although this depiction of Kerberus does
      not follow descriptions of the beast in literature (Hesiod [<bibl n="Hes. Th.
      310-312">Hes. Th. 310-312</bibl>] describes Kerberus as having fifty heads and
      Sophocles [<bibl n="Soph. Trach. 1098">Soph. Trach. 1098</bibl>] as having three),
      the portrayal with two heads and a snaky tail is the usual iconography in Attic vase-painting.
      Other Archaic wares routinely portray Kerberus with three heads. Depictions of Herakles and
      Kerberus begin in Attic vase-painting in the mid sixth century B.C. The scene in which
      Herakles drives Kerberus before him, rather than leading him, is found only in black figure.
      This vase is unusual in that it shows only Herakles and the dog, without Athena or Hermes. The
      artist has used added red for the chiton, belt, quiver, and beard of Herakles and the collar
      of the dog; and added white for alternating locks of the mane.</P><P><hi
      rend="head">Side B:</hi> This side of the vase depicts two scenes taking place in
      Hades. At the left of the composition, four small, unbearded, winged figures, wearing short
      belted chitons, carry hydriae to fill a very large half-buried pithos. The figures are placed
      symmetrically around the pithos, two at the base and two at the mouth pouring water from their
      hydriae into the pithos. At the right, Sisyphos, naked and bearded, rolls the rock, emphasized
      by the artist's use of added white, up the hill.</P><P>The scenes on the two sides
      of the vase appear to be related. Depictions of Sisyphos have been collected by
      <bibl>Schauenburg 1958</bibl>. The small winged figures are best interpreted as
      amyetoi, the souls of those uninitiated in the mysteries. Plato (<bibl n="Plat. Gorg.
      493">Plat. Gorg. 493</bibl>) tells of the uninitiated in Hades eternally filling a
      leaky pithos. The depiction of the amyetoi in Greek art is very rare, known only on three
      Attic vases from the Archaic period and the fifth century painting of the Nekyia by Polygnotos
      in the Lesche of the Knidians at Delphi. Pausanias (<bibl n="Paus. 10.31.9">Paus.
      10.31.9</bibl>) records that in the painting Polygnotos labels the water carriers in
      Hades as the uninitiated. The depiction of the Amyetoi with Sisyphos in Hades, as on the
      Munich amphora, is also found in the later painting by Polygnotos. The combination on the
      Munich amphora of the scene of those punished in Hades with Herakles and Kerberus has been
      interpreted as an intentional contrast. <bibl>van Straten 1975</bibl> argues that
      the two sides of the vase are meant to contrast the varying fates of the initiated versus the
      uninitiated in the underworld, following a tradition that Herakles was an Eleusinian initiate.
      <bibl>Boardman 1975</bibl> connects certain changes in Herakles and Kerberus
      iconography in the late sixth century with Peisistratean political
      propaganda.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/55c4bcd5e847506bfb33b7caef5ad77dfb8f3bf6023032ea4b16a9ea9d1e66e1> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
