<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/56bb12409f5809d2840de71cf843d9aeee7c28bda26ad80f20ef46eb9b68a871> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Body</hi>: Polyphemos reclines on
      the ground, while Odysseus makes his escape under the ram. The giant lies in the lower right
      hand corner of the panel, propping himself up with his left arm. He is nude, with a long
      scraggly beard and hair. His left leg is extended, while his right is pulled up toward his
      body. He reaches out toward the ram with his right hand, and seems just about to touch
      Odysseus. Behind the giant is a vine tree, the branches of which fill the background. On the
      left, the foreparts of the ram are visible, and it seems to be walking toward Polyphemos.
      Clinging underneath is a bearded man, definitely Odysseus.</P><P>The panel has
      vertical borders of dots, and the upper border is upright bars.</P><P>The story of
      Polyphemos is told by Homer (<bibl n="Hom. Od. 9.105-566">Hom. Od.
      9.105-566</bibl>). The giant Polyphemos was blinded by Odysseus and his men, so that
      they could escape from his cave. This depicts the moment after the blinding, when the wounded
      giant lets all of the sheep out of his cave, not knowing that Odysseus and his men were
      clinging underneath them. The figure depicted must be Odysseus, because he was the only one
      who used a ram, instead of a female sheep, for his escape.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

