<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/5c8f0f793f5b750a3726c6d9d1372407c0a868679190855ef9f5c7959e0f1b0a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl><hi rend=\"ital\">RIC</hi></bibl> IV.3, 103, no. 267; <bibl>Cohen 1880-1892, 5.162, 18</bibl>; <bibl>Romans and Barbarians, 86, no. C49, illus.</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/5c8f224c87d3bc79a3289949827e02a504d65b328dd8649cdc383d2e6675a174> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Side A: Dionysos, maenad and goat. Side B: Athena arming."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/5c9938a1657381e1305a800b83bdc0a2d172f9eb1bc1597a0b6d84bedc5621cf> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Sides A and B</hi>: the amphora
      shows the same scene on both sides, with the same group of five figures. Dionysos stands in
      the center of the scene, flanked by two naked youths, flanked in turn by two men draped in
      himatia. Dionysos holds a kantharos in his hand and a heavy-fruited branch of a grape-vine in
      his other hand. The god, looking towards right, is richly dressed with chiton and himation or
      mantle decorated with red dots and white rosettes. He also wears on his head an ivy wreath. On
      either side of Dionysos there is one figure, one youth and one bearded man walking towards
      right, making exaggerated gestures with their arms and turning their heads to look backwards.
      The one at right seems to look at Dionysos and the one at left seems to look at the man behind
      him. Both of them are naked except for the fillets they wear in their heads. The
      interpretation of the red band or stain on their chests is uncertain. On either side of them
      there are two standing men dressed similarly to Dionysos, but not as richly. Both are looking
      inwards to the center and make the same gesture with the hand.</P><P>Under both
      handles there are figures, something that is very common in the Affecter's works. Under the
      right handle, two naked running men one of them is carrying a round bowl. Under the left
      handle, one man or youth running naked. All of them have the same red band or stain on their
      chests. The Affecter frequently paints handle figures connected or not with the subject of the
      main scene. They are smaller figures, usually youths and men walking or running, naked or with
      chlamys or cloaks over the shoulders. Their identification is not clear yet. Beazley in this
      case tentatively proposed komasts, perhaps because of the bowl that one of them is
      carrying</P><P>The interpretation of the scene, repeated identically on both sides
      of the vase, is somewhat difficult. Beazley identified the scene as the welcoming of Dionysos
      by Ikarios, but there is no clue to identify any of the figures represented in this amphora as
      Ikarios. Neither the youths or the men have any attribute grapevines, wine cups, thyrsoi, etc.
      to identify them as part of a Dionysiac scene either; only the presence of Dionysos places the
      scene in this realm. Anonymous youths appear also with Dionysos in thirteen works by the
      Amasis Painter. The relationship between the god and the youths in some of them is not
      evident. Concerning Beazley's interpretation of Dionysos and Ikarios on nine vases by the
      Affecter, no attributes differentiate the figures Beazley identifies as Ikarios from the other
      stock figures greeting and departing on the Affecters's vases. There is, in fact, no evidence
      to support an identification of any black figure scenes as the meeting of Dionysos and
      Ikarios.</P><P>Dionysos appears in at least seventeen of The Affecter's vases. He
      always wears a lush ivy wreath; on all but one, he carries a kantharos; and on all but the
      latest he carries a heavily-fruited branch of grape-vine and he wears a
      chiton.</P><P>Most of the scenes by The Affecter seem to be simply decorative,
      without further significance. In this regard, Carpenter states that the figures of The
      Affecter are stock figures, merely decorative and repeated, used like models and patterns. The
      Affecter is a formalist and employs similar stock figures in the majority of his scenes. He
      lacks a sense of coherent narration, so that Beazley thinks that "the pictures are decoration
      and nothing beyond and that one can seldom tell what is going on". Furthermore, in this case,
      the fact that the scene is exactly the same on both sides suggests that it is simply a
      decorative intention.</P><P>On the neck a double floral design of palmettes and
      flowers.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/5c9984254d66607b0efc30e7fc75e58f35f7b568e046c7076fbf4c72bdd1742d> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><bibl>Moon 1979, no. 88, pp.
      152-153</bibl></P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/5ca30cd5a50f5229afe9c1225458a8b5fe60ca0689334b359594fe1571da01c8> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Woman brings gifts to the grave. On the left, a young woman
      approaches a central ovoid grave stele with gifts. She stands in profile facing right. On her
      veiled head she wears a sakkos to cover her hair and earrings. She is draped in a long
      transparent Ionic style chiton with loose elbow sleeves over which is draped a himation. Her
      left arm and hand support a basket with fillets, her right hand holds a black lekythos or oil
      jug for the grave. The ovoid monument sits on a platform of three steps. To the right of the
      scene is a young warrior who faces left towards the stele. He is shown striding, his spear
      raised high in his right hand. His left arm holds a foreshortened shield, on which is
      emblazoned the club of Herakles. He wears the a corslet over a short chiton, plumed Attic
      helmet and greaves. Over his back and shoulders is draped a himation.</P><P>The
      neck, mouth, handle, lower portion of the body, and base are glazed. The remainder is covered
      with a white clay slip, over which figures are drawn in lustrous glaze varying in color from
      black to yellow-gold. At the base of the neck are tongues. A palmette design decorates the
      shoulder. Below this at the junction of shoulder and body a band of meanders in groups of four
      broken by a blackened, checkered square runs around the vase at the top of the
      panel.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/5ca5c5952034e1dc1f007c661b09224ecbab6ae95b49340f613229792d1b0baf> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
