<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/626f54a29a1a1594e963340303a9433d072aa6e08dd544a4d340c41596564f92> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Orpheus. Orpheus sits
      on a rock or hillock playing on a large lyre, his head tilted back and his mouth open in song.
      His mantle is pushed down around his waist to leave his upper body free, and his head is
      wreathed. Four men in Thracian riding mantles stand listening to him. On the right, facing
      Orpheus, a young man leans on his spear, one foot propped on the hillock. An older, bearded
      man stands on the far right. His mantle is wrapped closely around him, hiding his hands, one
      of which holds a spear. To the left of Orpheus, a young man stands facing forward, his eyes
      closed as he listens to the music, his weight resting on his spear. Another young man leans on
      his shoulder, his eyes fixed on Orpheus. He holds two spears in his right hand. All four of
      the men listening wear fur caps which have a striped cloth neckpiece hanging from the back to
      protect the back of the neck from sun. The three young men wear their mantles flung over their
      shoulders, exposing their bodies, while the older man is wrapped tightly in his. The cap of
      the youth on the far left is worn hanging behind his back.</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Side B</hi>: two couples in conversation. In both couples the woman
      stands on the right, the man on the left. The woman in the right-hand couple plays catch with
      a ball. A young man faces her holding his staff and conversing. The woman wears a chiton,
      himation and sakkos, the man a mantle. The woman in the left-hand couple stands facing
      forward, her head turned toward her companion on the left who is pointing at her. She looks
      down at the pointing finger. The woman wears a chiton and himation, her right hand tucked into
      the himation. The man wears a mantle and carries a crooked staff. Jumping weights hang on the
      wall between them.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/626fd77b8bad397925bb96737a9c626f7f93d63fcb3e2e34524b1cbcea1e44e5> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Oedipus and the Sphinx."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/627ac46f9355d9b38307b710f7de6235d8d933de3cbb2580b33a51df6c258d67> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The vessel has a flattened spherical body with a base-ring and
      lower lip. The cover is low, with a flat knob. The decoration is in black glaze (burned red in
      places) and applied red.</P><P>On the body near the rim is a band of radial bars,
      and below that two bands of alternate checks. The animal frieze is below this. In the frieze,
      a human-headed bird (siren?) stands with lowered wings between two seated sphinxes with
      raised, sickle-shaped wings. To the right is a panther, then an eagle between two birds with
      raised, sickle-shaped wings. Then there is a panther, a bull, and another panther. Below the
      frieze is a black band with red lines and a reserved band, then a red band inside and outside
      the base, and concentric circles on the bottom. On the lid are red and black circles and a
      band of fine checker pattern.</P><P>This pyxis is in the "Delicate Style," first
      identified by Payne (64) as having neatly incised, if somewhat uninspired, figures and
      degenerate filling ornament. It is most common on pyxides, of the later part of the period
      (Amyx, 385).</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

