<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/64d2cdf149f6bfd6ae59fe949b97bc9823109d4ec5827b3d06eb157753e1844a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Side A: Aging reveler carrying barbiton<lb>Side B: Young reveler"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/64d42c3469364a0aa4492ea0e3c4b06cc2a19fe85021e98805d7525b050ef858> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Marriage of Herakles and Hebe. The marriage is shown in a
      circular scene on the lid of the pyxis. The procession proceeds to the right, where Zeus is
      seated on a throne, Hera standing beside him. Beginning with the seated group, from right to
      left the characters are: an erotes, Zeus, Hera, Athena, a female torchbearer, an erotes,
      Herakles, Hebe, an erotes, and two female figures. Zeus and Hera face left, where the wedding
      procession approaches. Zeus is reclining with his left arm casually draped over the back of
      the throne, a long, striped staff held in his right hand. He wears a decorative mantle over
      his lower body. His upper body is bare. Hera stands beside him, leaning with her left arm
      resting on the other side of the throne back and holding a striped staff in her right hand.
      She wears a chiton with elaborate decoration on the hem and bodice. Both Hera and Zeus wear
      purple diadems. An erotes standing behind the throne also leans on its back, partially resting
      on Zeus' arm. A thymiaterion stands on the ground between the erotes and the throne. Athena is
      seated to the left of Zeus and Hera, her seat turned toward them but her head turned to watch
      the approach of the wedding procession. She wears a white bodice over her thin chiton, a
      crested helmet, bracelets, earrings and a bead necklace. She holds her spear resting against
      her left knee, and sits on a mantle draped over her seat. To her left stands a female figure
      facing left and holding two torches. She is wearing a thin chiton with a decorative bodice and
      a wide band around her hair. A large bird stands on the ground in front of her. The wedding
      party is led by an erotes carrying a torch. He lunges to the right but turns his head to watch
      Herakles and Hebe. Herakles is leading Hebe by the hand. He is naked but for a piece of cloth
      draped over his shoulders, and he carries his club in his left hand. Hebe walks beside him
      dressed in a white, ankle-length skirt and tunic with elaborate patterns done in added
      red-brown paint. She also wears a bracelet on each wrist and dangling earrings. An erotes
      flies behind her, lifting or adjusting her veil. A rectangular chest stands on the ground
      behind the erotes. Next come two girls, one carrying a box and a sash, the other a
      loutrophoros-hydria. Both wear thin chitons belted high, and turn to each other to chat as
      they walk. Behind the girl with the box stands another rectangular chest. The skin of Hebe and
      the three erotes is done in white paint.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/64ec139f0788188b88dd1849fccb2c805472a925f9763f518622923d202a9fc8> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><bibl>CVA, Munich 12, 4,
      24-25.</bibl></P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/6501dbba6f7e353c7429d3c340115c2104f419cf7a3d639eb8a034cf4be52caa> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: two draped men in
      conversation between two sphinxes. In lower register: palmette-lotus hybrid between two sirens
      which are flanked by lions with turned-back heads.</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Side B</hi>: the Judgment of Paris with the three goddesses (not
      distinguished) in profile to left with Hermes in front with kerykeion, the messenger's staff.
      Hermes extends right hand to Paris, represented as well-dressed, bearded man. Owl on ground
      line between Hermes and Paris.</P><P>In lower register: a cock between two sirens
      which are flanked by lions with turned-back heads. On neck: lotus and palmette chain with
      alternating black and red tongues below on upper shoulder. Rays on lower body above
      foot.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/6508330809abb774f90a47388bd9a55ab1eaa6030048a55b7749e9545dea875d> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Bands of running dotted circles appear on the body, as well as
      a single row of lozenges below the metopes on the broad neck. The three metopes are separated
      by triglyphs with chevrons. Two water birds with crosshatched bodies flank the central metope
      of a circle of connected dotted circles within a large circle with rays. Dark brown paint is
      used.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/651984c58d2eb06c3ec0a8ae7031a70422af9464f0718537f04bb42305a2a719> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Condition"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Spout chipped." ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435429> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/653374236ee72c08c374b9d052cd8acfd1337f9fcc39b8a8143abe4d41c049b7> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>On the lip of Side A, a swan facing right appears between
      heraldically placed sirens. In the handle zone, fragments of three letters are visible.
      Palmettes grow from the handles. On the lip of Side B, a siren facing right is placed between
      two heraldic swans. The vase is signed in the handle zone by Xenokles as potter. On the
      interior of the cup, a naked youth riding a Hippalektryon, a composite monster part horse and
      part cock, toward the right appears in a reserved tondo.</P><P>Composite and
      monstrous animals are a staple of archaic Greek art. Sirens, especially antithetical sirens,
      are a frequent motif on lip and band cups. A horse-bird appears on Crete in the ninth century.
      The proper, with the front legs of a horse, first appears in Attic vase-painting in the 560's
      in the work of the Tyrrhenian Group. The animal becomes popular from the middle of the
      century, and at about the same time acquires a rider.</P><P>Little Master cups are
      a favored cup form beginning in the mid sixth century. They are decorated in a distinct
      miniaturist style. On lip cups such as this, the lip is reserved and decoration, generally
      comprised of one to three figures, is placed in its center. One feature of the Xenokles
      Painter's style is that he often used more figures in his compositions. The decoration of the
      handle zone on lip cups is consistent. Palmettes almost always grow from the handles. The main
      part of the handle zone holds an inscription, often a potter's signature, which becomes an
      essential element in the decoration of the vase.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/65372e1a2bcb3175804a0b3af5c8a90752079254925f515f8bcaf6cfac4f64b9> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
