<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/689fa3fc9705ea69d8371fb8481ecc1efa5d70d3fa4a706b69416725e6ba3ab4> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Three warriors move to the right, carrying severed heads in
      their left hands and drawn swords in their right. The severed heads still wear their helmets,
      grisly trophies for the victors. They all wear high-crested helmets, short chitons girt at the
      waist, and greaves with volutes. Perhaps this may represent a three-fold repetition of
      Amphiaraos with the head of Melanippos (<bibl>Haspels 1936,
      179</bibl>).</P><P>The upper part of the neck is black as are the lower body
      and handle. The shoulder has rows of black pendants on a reserved ground. The body has a white
      ground. Above the panel a meander runs to the right.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/68b12ccb966b0d43b33511f8eeb56bf052874c9b8357021ad638ebb6d339acfd> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
