<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/6ce51dc3ea82cffec4047abb8b6a3f797c40e7904230c53b04a891fe1dd6669a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The edge of the foot is clay-colored, while its top is glazed
      with a red edge and a red stripe in the center. The body-foot join is painted red. The strap
      handles are glazed on top and bottom, decorated laterally with rosettes, and their upper edges
      bearing a red stripe. A delicate red bulge appears at the neck attachment. The neck is glazed
      on its exterior and interior, while the rim itself is decorated outside and inside with red
      stripes. There are two more red stripes on the neck interior. On the lower body of the vessel
      is a glazed zone bordered above and below by red stripes. In this area on both sides are the
      scenes, interrupted at the shoulder by a band of rosettes with red dots in the center. A band
      of red and black tongues borders the shoulder scenes at the body-neck join. The main scene on
      side A is bordered laterally by ivy leaves, on side B by diagonal zigzag marks. On both sides
      of the neck is a chain of opposed lotuses and palmettes, elaborately rendered, with red center
      leaves on the lotuses. The upper half of the decoration is repeated at the
      rim.</P><P>On side A, a bearded man beckons favor of a youth, while bearded men
      dance on either side of them. All four figures are naked. The youth holds a wreath with white
      dots on its edge in his left hand, and with his right he clasps the left hand of the man who
      is courting him. Both center figures, as well as the dancer on the left, wear white chain
      necklaces. The lefthand dancer hops on his right leg with lively upraised left leg and left
      arm, while the right arm punches the air behind him. The righthand dancer is in the midst of a
      leap, with both feet off the ground. Over his left shoulder, presumably as a love gift, he
      carries a deer with red throat and white belly stripes. All four figures have red hair, and
      the two on the left have red beards. In the background are nonsense
      inscriptions.</P><P>On side B a frontal chariot is depicted, with a bird flying to
      the left above the two horses on the right. Red is used for the four horses' breast bands, as
      well as the three lefthand horses' manes, two stripes on the chariot, the charioteer's throat,
      and two wing stripes on the bird. The horses' breast bands were decorated with rows of white
      dots. The two horses on the outsides have their forelocks tied into a
      tuft.</P><P>Both shoulder scenes depict two wrestlers flanked by two onlookers.
      Here the drawing is noticeably more careless than in the main scenes. On both sides the
      lefthand wrestler has his opponent locked in a firm grip. The spectators all lean on staffs,
      although the staff of the lefthand spectator on side A is so thinly painted that it is hardly
      visible. All are holding mantles over one arm. Both wrestlers on side B and the righthand
      wrestler on side A are bearded. All beard and head hair is red. Both mantles on side A and the
      lefthand mantle on side B are striped in red, while the righthand mantle on side B is dotted
      in red. Above each righthand wrestler a garment is hanging at the upper edge of the
      scenes.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

