<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/6d534fadb34da2e9ec35cbfcf0934a22cf3403cc0aa99ad4f73dc5f817b12030> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The flaring neck of the vessel is glazed inside and out, with a
      painted line around the top of the inside. The reserved shoulder panel stretches approximately
      between the centers of the side handles, and is bordered by tongues above and a glaze
      groundline below. The reserved body panel is set inside the near ends of the side handles,
      enclosed at the sides by ivy vines (with berries at the interstices of the leaves), running
      between single and double lines. Below is a groundline of dilute glaze, which separates the
      panel from a predella containing ten and one-half up-and-down palmettes. There is a reserved
      patch beneath the left handle. A double painted line encircles the vase under the predella,
      and a single painted band runs above the 47 base rays. A painted fillet separates the body and
      the glazed disk foot, which has a painted line near the bottom of the disk's edge. The
      vertical handle with a central rib widens at the junction with the rim to form two
      rotellae.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Body</hi>: Herakles and Kerberos are
      depicted. Herakles, on the left, bends toward the dog. He carries his club and a lead chain in
      his right hand, and touches one of the animal's muzzles with his left. Herakles wears his
      lionskin belted over a short red chiton with a white dot border, and carries an open quiver
      (alternate panels of which are red) on his shoulder and a sheathed sword at his side, both
      held by crossed white baldrics. Kerberos, who looks rather tame, stands before Herakles,
      facing left. One of his heads his raised to Herakles' hand. His neck and the alternate locks
      of his mane are painted red; his belly stripe is white. Behind Kerberos, and partly hidden by
      him, is Hermes, walking to the right. He holds his kerykeion, and turns back to look at
      Herakles and gesture to him with his right hand. He is bearded, and wears a patterned, belted
      short chiton with white dots, a white-dotted chlamys with red folds, boots, and a petasos. He
      walks toward Persephone, on the right, standing at the portals of Hades. These are indicated
      by a white Doric column with an epistyle. She gestures to Hermes. She is wearing a peplos with
      white dot crosses and a red fillet. To the left of Herakles, in profile to the right, is
      Athena. She is wearing her aegis, peplos, and crestless Attic helmet with a painted red spiral
      on it. Her peplos has a red hem border and shoulders, and is decorated with white dot crosses.
      She holds a spear in her right hand and gestures with her upraised left. She and Persephone
      have white skin (most of the white is worn away), and Hermes and Herakles have red beards. In
      the field is the inscription <foreign lang="Greek">*F*I*L*I[*P*O*S]
      [*K*A]*L*O*S</foreign> ("Philip is beautiful").</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Shoulder</hi>: The combat between Achilles and Memnon over the body of
      Antilochos is depicted. Antilochos lies in the center of the composition, holding his spear
      across his abdomen and falling back on his shield. He wears a corslet with painted red
      spirals, greaves, and a high-crested Corinthian helmet. The device on his shield is a tripod.
      Memnon lunges at him from the left, armed with a raised spear and a Boeotian shield (seen from
      the inside). He wears a corslet, red-striped chiton, red crested helmet, and a scabbard slung
      on a red baldric. Achilles advances from the right, his shield (with a running leg device and
      red rim) on his left arm and spear upraised. He wears a corslet over a chiton with white dot
      circles, a low-crested Corinthian helmet (with red crest holder), and greaves. Flanking the
      combat are the mothers of Achilles and Memnon, Thetis on the right and Eos on the left, each
      followed by a spear-bearing horseman. The women wear himatia with red folds and white dots,
      and chitons; their skin is white. The rider on the left is dressed in chlamys with red
      folds.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

