<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/71cadd5076097269d95bd0741b88aa40bd37c3b0b6cf24c96c87eefaaa4777a0> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: The madness of
      Lycurgus is depicted. The composition is arranged in two rows of figures, one over the other.
      In the center of the lower scene stands Lycurgus. He is about to kill his wife, whom he has
      thrown down and his holding by the hair with his left hand, while he pushes her down further
      with his left knee. In his right hand he brandishes a double-edged axe. His hair and beard are
      shaggy and disheveled, and his chest and stomach are covered with hair. He wears a panther
      skin cap, a chlamys thrown over his shoulders and fastened in the front with a circular
      brooch, and high, lace-up boots. His sword hangs at his left side, suspended by a
      shoulder-belt. His wife has fallen with her left leg doubled under her. She raises both hands
      above her head, and tries to loosen his grip with her right hand. Her hair is disheveled, and
      a stream of blood flows from the wound above her left breast. She wears earrings, armlets, a
      double necklace, a chiton, embroidered peplos, and sandals. Her chiton is belted at the waist
      and fastened at the right shoulder, leaving her left shoulder and breast uncovered. Behind
      Lycurgus, to the left, is a youthful male looking on at the scene. He raises his left hand as
      if chastising Lycurgus, and his right is placed at the back of his head in a gesture of
      despair. He has moderately long hair and whiskers, but no beard. He is nude except for a
      chlamys flung over his left arm, and high, lace-up boots. A sword hangs suspended from a
      shoulder strap on his left side. Behind him is a (shorter) paidogogos, advancing slowly and
      carrying a crooked staff in his right hand. His hair amd beard are white, amd his face covered
      with wrinkles. He wears a knee-length chiton, belted at the waist, a chlamys which hangs over
      his left arm, and high lace-up boots. Under his chiton he wears a tight-fitting long sleeved
      top, decorated with a lozenge pattern, each lozenge containing a white spot. To the right of
      Lycurgus, a male and female carry away his dead son. The female is closest to Lycurgus, and
      looks toward him with an expression of sorrow. She wears earrings, a necklace, a chiton which
      is belted at the waist, a tight-fitting blouse under the chiton, which is decorated with
      zigzag and horizontal lines, and shoes. She holds the dead body with both hands. The head of
      the dead youth falls backwards, and his right arm hangs down lifelessly. Blood flows from a
      wound in his right side, and a chlamys is thrown over his body. The male supports his legs; he
      also looks towards Lycurgus. He wears a sword, chlamys, and high lace-up boots. Both he and
      the dead youth have disheveled hair and whiskers, but no beard. In the center of the upper
      scene, above Lycurgus, is a winged figure, probably Lyssa. She hovers in the air, partially
      surrounded by a triple radiate circle, and with her right hand points a kentron (goad) at
      Lycurgus. Around her left arm are twined two snakes; she wears a cap, earrings, a necklace,
      and a long embroidered chiton (which rises up to her knees in her rapid descent). Two shoulder
      belts cross her breast diagonally, and her chiton has a belt decorated with white studs. On
      the ground below her (but still above Lycurgus' head) are a hydria lying on its side and a
      flower. Behind her are a male and female group; the male is probably Ares. He is seated on a
      stool, holding a spear in his left hand and looking sorrowfully on the scene. The female
      stands on the far left of the composition. She is stooping forward, resting her left foot on
      rising ground and moving both hands as if speaking to him. Her hair is bound up, and she wears
      earrings, a necklace, armlets, an embroidered chiton, and sandals. On Lyssa'sr right is
      Apollo, seated in three-quarter position to the left on a throne. He is beardless, and his
      hair falls over his shoulders in long ringlets. He wears a laurel wreath and a chlamys is
      thrown over his lap. In front of him is an altar with a burning offering. In his left hand he
      holds a heptachord lyre, and he raise his right to the side of his head. His head is turned
      towards Hermes, on the right. Hermes stands in profile to the left, with his right foot
      resting on a rock. He holds out his right hand, as if addressing Apollo, while in his left he
      holds the caduceus. His petasus (cap) is painted white, and hangs behind his head. He holds
      his chlamys over his left arm, and wears high boots with wings on
      them.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: The contest for Hippodameia
      is depicted. The composition is again arranged in two groups of figures. In the lower
      register, a large basin on a pedestal (both painted white) occupies the center of the scene.
      In front of it and on lower ground is Pelops, seated on rocks. He is nude and beardless,
      wearing high lace-up boots and sitting on his mantle which is thrown over a rock. He looks to
      Hippodameia on the left, while supporting his left elbow with his right hand, which rests on
      the pommel of his sword. He seems to address Myrtilos on his right with the gesture. Between
      him and Myrtilos is a tall Ionic column (it reaches into the upper scene), with a tripod on
      top. Part way up the column hangs a bucranium, with a fillet. Myrtilos, wearing high lace-up
      boots, a chlamys, and a petasus (cap), stands to the far right of the lower scene. His left
      foot is turned to walk away, and he holds a chariot wheel as he looks at Pelops. Both he and
      Pelops have whiskers, but no beard, and curly hair. Between them, near the column, is a tall
      flower. Hippodameia stands to the left of Pelops. Her hair is bound up, and she wears
      earrings, a double necklace, armlets, an embroidered chiton with looped up sleeves, a bordered
      peplos, and sandals. Her right hand is on her hip, and her left extended to Pelops. Behind her
      is another female, either Hera or Sterope (Hippodameia's mother), who pushes her forward with
      her left hand. Her hair falls in long ringlets over her shoulders, and she wears a diadem,
      earrings, a double necklace, armlets, an embroidered chiton and peplos, and sandals. Her
      peplos is pulled over the back of her head, and her right arm and hand arm hidden in it. In
      front of her, on the ground, is a flower. In the upper scene, Aphrodite is seated above
      Hippodameia. She sits in three-quarter profile to the left, on a stool with meander
      decoration. She wears a cap from which her hair projects in a mass behind, and a veil at the
      back of her head. She wears earrings, a necklace, armlets, a chiton with looped up sleeves, an
      embroidered peplos, and sandals. She rests her left hand on the stool, and holds a corner of
      her veil with her right. Before her to the left stands Pan, leaning forward with his left foot
      raised on a rock and holding a laurel branch tied with a sash in his right hand. He is
      youthful, wearing a wreath of reeds and having a short tail. On the other side of Aphrodite,
      above the basin, is Eros. He is seated on a rock, on which his chlamys is spread, and he holds
      a myrtle wreath towards Aphrodite, but looks to Myrtilos. He wears a cap, shoulder belt, and
      sandals. To the far right, above Myrtilos, is a female figure. She is perhaps a local nymph;
      seated on a decorated cista, she looks toward Hippodameia. Her hair is bound up, and she wears
      earrings, a double necklace, armlets, a chiton, an embroidered peplos, and sandals. Her chiton
      has looped up sleeves, and has slipped off of her right shoulder.</P><P>Around the
      rim of the vessel are palmettes. The area below the figural section is
      fluted.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

