<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/7453a47a8b6ce3fd60c98c3bd3e98300d5ef5458ca20fb2987660a33909559c2> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><bibl>Smith 1896, 391-392</bibl>; <bibl>Murray &
      Smith 1896, pl. 17</bibl>; <bibl>Pfuhl 1923, pl. 200, no.527</bibl>;
      <bibl>Williams 1985, 36-37, fig. 42</bibl>; <bibl>Robertson 1992,
      185-90</bibl></P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/7459ccef3c41d168d23127140d6a9ddd654119de03d4cc90e20b05d63925d16e> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Interior:</hi> Athlete using a
      pick. In the tondo, a naked youth, shown in profile facing right, leans forward grasping a
      pick in both hands. He wears a wreath added in red.</P><P><hi
      rend="head">Side A:</hi> Arming. Two unbearded warriors are arming on either side of
      a woman who stands in the middle. The warrior on the left is shown in profile facing right. He
      wears a chitoniskos, and a Corinthian helmet pushed up on his head. He leans forward picking
      up a shield. The woman faces to the left and holds this warrior's spear in her right hand. She
      wears a peplos and mantle, and a wreath in her hair. The warrior on the right of the
      composition faces to the right in profile. He wears a chitoniskos, a decorated corslet, and a
      Corinthian helmet with a crest pushed up on his head. He leans forward holding his spear in
      his right hand and his shield in his left. His shield is shown in full back view. This
      contrasts with the artist's depiction of the shield of the warrior on the left, where the
      shield is depicted in an odd combination of profile and three-quarter back
      view.</P><P><hi rend="head">Side B:</hi> Youth leading a horse. A
      youth leads a horse to the right. He is naked except for a chlamys fastened at the neck and a
      wreath. He turns back to the left, looking at the horse and gesturing to it with his right
      hand. He holds the reins and two spears in his left hand.</P><P>On either side and
      underneath the handles, this cup has a full palmette
      complex.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/745be4a8e41e227afffc1b0072f6c273148cd6f603933596c66a6e25ec993c2f> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Exterior</hi>: youths and revelers.
      Ten men and youths dressed up as women, wearing long chiton, himation, sakkos and earrings,
      and one female attendant dance around the entire circumference. Some participants carry wine
      cups, two play flutes, two others play krotala (castanets) and the girl and one older figure
      hold parasols.</P><P>From left to right, the scene begins with a column krater
      under the signed handle. 1) a man in a long chiton and himation, carrying a knobbed staff in
      his left hand and a skyphos in his right; 2) another man in similar dress, holding his
      himation up in both hands; 3) a youth playing an aulos, wearing long chiton, himation and
      sakkos; a long spotted fillet and a pair of castanets in the field; 4) a man turning back and
      offering the youth a skyphos in his right hand, his left hand hidden under his himation; 5) a
      figure (head broken away) holding a parasol back to the left. On the other side, 1) an older
      man looking back to his left; 2) a small girl holding a parasol over the man to her right; 3)
      an older man playing castanets, his head thrown back in ecstasy; a spotted fillet and a pair
      of castanets in the field; 4) a man standing frontally, his head turned up and to the left,
      lifting his himation with both hands; 5) a youth playing an aulos, and 6) a youth playing
      castanets. The only interruption to the rightward movement are the parasol carrier, who is
      turned to the left beside one handle, and the large column krater under the other, signed
      handle, which not only holds the wine for the komos but begins and ends the composition. Such
      transvestites are fairly common subjects on Attic vases of the late sixth and early fifth
      centuries BC.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Tondo</hi>: youth and hetaira. A
      youth carrying a knobbed staff offers a flower to a rather disinterested-looking woman, who
      carries a mirror. Behind them, a bed. The woman holds her right arm behind
      her.</P><P>Signature inside one handle: <foreign lang="Greek">*B*R*U*G*O*S
      *E*P*O*I*E*S*E*N</foreign> ("Brygos made it")</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

