<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/771b8a6708d605f02114d9c5ff199b631b24ae6030af90484615be338eb05259> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Interior</hi>: lyre-player. The
      youth stands holding his lyre, body frontal and head turned to the left. The lyre is held
      horizontally in his lowered right hand. His left hand is tucked inside his tightly wrapped
      mantle. The mantle is wrapped to leave his right shoulder exposed. He wears a fillet over his
      short hair. To his right is a fluted column with echinus, abacus block, architrave and
      platform, representing a building.</P><P><hi rend="head">Side A</hi>:
      boy with hare between two youths. The boy with the hare stands in the center facing the youth
      on the right. He is holding the hare at the base of the ears and holds it out to his
      companion. The hare is wriggling, holding out its left forepaw. The youth on the right is
      leaning on a crooked stick, staring at the youth with the hare. His right hand is on his hip.
      Behind the central youth is an unfluted column with a capital block, narrow architrave and
      base. The youth to the left of the column looks and gestures to the left. His right hand may
      once have held a fillet for there is a large loop preserved above his right forearm. He leans
      his left elbow on his staff.</P><P><hi rend="head">Side B</hi>: two
      youths and a bearded man. The central youth and the bearded man look toward the youth on the
      left. The youth on the left faces right, and is holding a stick in his right hand. His other
      hand is hidden under his mantle. To his right, there is an unfluted column with capital block,
      architrave and base. The central youth stands with his body frontal and his head turned to the
      left. He is wrapped in a mantle which is pulled over his head and which he seems to be
      unwrapping with his right hand. To the right, facing left, is an older, bearded man carrying a
      staff on his right forearm. A writing case and stylus hangs on the wall between the two
      figures.</P><P>The six male figures on sides A and B all wear mantles wrapped to
      leave their right shoulders exposed.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/77223fb4895d5fe6b7ba54e6560459c96739d13bdd37955a0a838a3a9dba017a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The vase has been broken and mended with parts from another
      skyphos. The lower part of the woman on Side A does not belong, and the piece of the base with
      the meander pattern from the break below the youth on the front toward the right to below the
      right foot of the left-hand figure on the back, is from another vase. The piece below the
      handle, which appears as plaster on the outside, is glazed on the inside and joins perfectly
      with the base. From the marks in the glaze on the inside it would seem that this piece, with
      the base, belongs to the same skyphos as the fragment used to restore the lower part of the
      female. The second skyphos may have been a companion piece by the same
      painter.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: An armed youth is shown
      following a girl. The youth is on the left, moving right. He wears a black bordered chlamys
      fastened at the shoulder and draped over his left arm, a petasos hung on a string around his
      neck, and high boots. On his head is a plain fillet. He carries a sword in his right hand. The
      girl running in front of him looks back at him. She wears a sakkos in her hair and a Doric
      peplos. Above her head is the inscription <foreign lang="Greek">*K*A*L*E</foreign>
      ("beautiful"). Between the two figures in the upper field is the artist's signature
      <foreign lang="Greek">*P*O*L*U*G*N*O*T*O*S *E*G*R*A*Y*E*N</foreign> ("Polygnotos
      drew [me]").</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: At the left is a woman
      wearing an elaborate chiton, with a himation drawn up over her head. She walks to the right,
      following a girl who wears a full pleated chiton and a wreath in her long hair. She looks back
      at the woman, but points forward with her right hand. With her left hand she holds up her
      skirt. In the field between the two figures, and also over the head of the one on the right,
      is the inscription <foreign lang="Greek">*K*A*L*E</foreign>
      ("beautiful").</P><P>These two scenes could depict Paris and Helen (according to
      the CVA) or Telemachos and Penelope going to meet Odysseus (according to Touchefeu-Meynier,
      264).</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

