    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/77995b36a4129d3b7e41c53b7c45ca36bc70e30318269bda7b2229430834711c> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A:</hi> Komos with two pairs
      of revelers. At the left the bearded male, nude except for a himation draped over his left
      arm, leads his rather unwilling partner with his right arm around her neck and grasping her
      left wrist. The body hair on his torso is articulated in dilute glaze. He holds a gnarled
      staff and wears a dotted band and a broad fillet in his hair. The woman has light brown hair
      rendered in dilute glaze, and wears earrings and a dotted and bordered himation over an Ionic
      chiton with a bit of decollete. The second couple is similarly dressed, with the man's
      himation draped around his shoulders, otherwise nude. The same treatment of hair appears on
      both. The man puts his left arm around the woman' shoulders; she draws her himation up at back
      in her left hand in a veiling gesture. A servant boy cloaked in a himation closes the scene,
      walking to the right in front of a tree. He holds an enormous cup and an oil lamp (the smoke
      from which is added in purple) to light the way of the revellers. Under the handle a woman,
      dressed in a revealing Ionic chiton and wearing a fillet in added purple, plays the <hi
      rend="ital">auloi</hi> (double flutes).</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side
      B:</hi> Komos with two pairs of male figures with hetairai who move to the right. The
      males wear only himatia with embattled borders (characteristic of the painter,
      <bibl>Simon & Hirmer 1976, 114</bibl>), the left figure with a broad band
      around his head (and sideburn curls done in dilute glaze), and right one with a dotted band.
      The women wear Ionic chitons and dotted and bordered himatia; the left woman has a broad
      fillet in her hair and the right one wears a sakkos. The left male figure fondles his partner
      as she grasps the ends of her fillet. The bearded lyre-playing male at the right throws his
      head back while singing a song written above in added purple. The plektron of the instrument
      is attached by a cord painted in added red. The player's body hair is articulated along the
      central line of his body. A tree is at the end of the scene behind the woman. Under the handle
      is a double flute player holding the instrument in the right hand while walking right; the
      figure, whose face is missing, wears an Ionic chiton and plain himation draped over the
      shoulders. A second figure under the handle is a servant boy draped in a himation and wearing
      an added purple wreath and short boots (<foreign lang="Greek">a)rbu/lh</foreign>);
      he holds a large cup while moving right.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

