<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/77b8bf226ae4ac83df388d66e6d8d63f52a59ed7482ba8441fb415952f66675a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl><hi rend=\"ital\">BMCRE</hi></bibl> IV, 318, no. 1917, reverse variable; <bibl>Cohen 1880-1892, 3, no. 1012</bibl>; <bibl>Romans and Barbarians, 82, no. C 26, (ill.)</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/77c13c5163f0721fe253611a8851d61ee4554a99d551e7c8784cdeeb8dca7ed8> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Interior:</hi> Horseman riding to
      the right wears a chitoniskos, himation, petasos, and high laced boots. He carries two spears
      in his right hand. The horse's tail and back of the petasos extend outside into the continuous
      meander border (running left) surrounding the tondo. The horse's reins, straps of the hat, and
      boot laces are done in added red. The drapery is patterned.</P><P>Side A: A
      horseman, standing, calms his horse holding the reins out in front. He wears chitoniskos,
      himation, flapped boots, and a foxskin hat (<foreign
      lang="Greek">a)lopeke/h</foreign>). A young groom moves to the left behind the horse
      at a column. He holds up two spears in his left hand and a switch in his right. To the right
      of these figures, a horseman walks his horse to the left carrying two spears and wearing a
      chitoniskos, himation, high laced boots, and petasos (added red for reins, hat straps, and
      boot laces.</P><P>Side B: Three horsemen holding javelins walk their horses to the
      right, the front one crossing in front of a column. All three wear a chitoniskos, himation,
      and high laced boots. The first (badly preserved) and third wear the petasos at the back and
      the middle one has a dot patterned himation. The reins, hat straps, and boot laces are done in
      added red.</P><P>Devambez suggests that the scenes (A and B respectively)
      represent the departure for the <foreign lang="Greek">dokimasi/a</foreign> (the
      annual inspection of cavalrymen and their horses by the Athenian council) on the one hand, and
      the arrival of the <foreign lang="Greek">i(ppeis</foreign>, horsemen or knights,
      on the other.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/77c27fc9e0a79d559ec3ebc7f0a9f9a417b74b9c6c78d030afab9dd49a486556> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
