<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/78313b88014a498b387fdd9e35e718fd65d4ce8781d4043c6c406d2ed7bdab1c> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Side A:</hi> Herakles and the boar.
      Herakles occupies the center of the composition, holding the boar upside down over his left
      shoulder. Herakles leans forward threatening to drop the animal on Eurystheus who hides in a
      pithos set in the ground. Herakles is bearded and wears a corslet over a chitoniskos. He
      carries a quiver on his back, a sword at his waist, and his club rests against the rim of the
      pithos. The figure of Eurystheus is damaged, but appears to have been bearded. He holds his
      arms out pleadingly to Herakles. Herakles is accompanied by human and divine onlookers. Iolaos
      stands at the right of the composition, facing right, but looking back over his shoulder at
      the action. He is bearded and wears a corslet over a short chiton with a decorated hem. A
      sword hangs at his waist and he holds two spears in his right hand. At the left of the
      composition, Hermes and Athena are seen as the hero's protectors. Hermes stands facing left,
      gesturing with his right hand. He wears a short decorated chiton with a mantle wrapped over
      one shoulder, and a petasos. He is bearded. Athena also stands facing left but she looks back
      over her shoulder at Herakles, gesturing with her raised right hand. Athena wears an
      embroidered peplos and a snaky-edged aegis, as well as a helmet with a tall crest. She holds a
      spear in her left hand. Athena's flesh is painted white. Added dots are used throughout the
      scene as ornamentation. Herakles, Eurystheus, and Iolaos are
      labelled.</P><P>Archaic Attic art emphasizes the fact that Herakles had to capture
      the Erymanthean boar alive. Artists represent the hero carrying off the subdued boar rather
      than the actual struggle. This emphasis on the live capture is found in the most complete
      ancient reference to the story, <bibl n="Diod. Sic. 4.12.1-2">Diod. Sic.
      4.12.1-2</bibl>, which also has the tradition of Eurystheus hiding in the pithos. The
      actual delivery of the boar to Eurystheus, rather than scenes of Herakles carrying the boar on
      his way back, are most popular from the mid sixth century on.</P><P><hi
      rend="head">Side B:</hi> Dionysos and woman reclining. At the right of the
      composition, Dionysos, bearded and with long locks of hair, reclines facing left with his left
      elbow resting on a pillow. His lower body and one shoulder are wrapped in a decorated mantle
      and he wears an ivy wreath. He holds a kantharos in his right hand. Four round, flat loaves of
      bread are placed in front of Dionysos. At the left a woman reclines in the same posture with
      her left elbow resting on a pillow, but she turns her head back to the right and looks at
      Dionysos. She also has her lower body and one shoulder wrapped in a mantle, leaving the rest
      of her torso bare, and wears a red fillet in her hair. The upper part of the composition is
      filled with a large, spreading vine, the trunk of which rises from the center of the scene.
      The vine is filled with bunches of grapes and four small satyrs clamber about in it picking
      the harvest. Added white is used for the flesh of the woman, the kantharos, the bread, and
      various ornament.</P><P>The precise interpretation of this scene is somewhat
      difficult. Scenes of Dionysos reclining with a woman are generally interpreted as Dionysos and
      Ariadne on Naxos, as this vase has been described in <bibl>CVA, Munich 8</bibl>
      and <bibl>LIMC, III, Dionysos no. 758</bibl>. <bibl>Carpenter 1986, 114, n.
      67</bibl>, however, calls into question whether Ariadne, a divine wife, would be shown
      bare breasted, and prefers to see such women shown reclining in a Dionysiac context as
      maenads.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/7838a9f2af24bb363f05f243b69fba57592968994fbbe26ef46fc13ef1f50f93> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><bibl>Coldstream 1968, 76</bibl>; <bibl>Schweitzer
      1971, 48, 51, pl. 61</bibl>; <bibl>Fairbanks 1928, 81, no. 269b, pl.
      23</bibl>; <bibl>Davison 1961, 83-86</bibl>; <bibl>T. Rhombos <hi
      rend="ital">The Iconography of Attic Late Geometric II Pottery</hi> (Jonsered 1988)
      316, 322, 352, n. 293</bibl></P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/784370871781b3cdc9300afa5d22ec47a7f2a2b3e13abe73bc8e089b8d6de566> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Side A: Two satyrs lying on the ground masturbating. Side B: An eye made into a siren.
      Under the handles, dogs defecating."""@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/784b65036e081224c4fe9a8a4d7bd0eb9b75c78cbeffb6a03625e97b580b26ce> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """A white temple, shown in 3/4-view, sets the scene. To the right of the
      temple sits Marsyas, a balding, bearded satyr, 3/4-view to the right. He wears high leather
      boots and a chlamys, and plays double pipes. In front of him sits Apollo, wearing high boots,
      a belted, short Oriental tunic, a chlamys, and a wreath. He sits profile to the right, playing
      a kithara. A muse, also seated profile to the right, wearing a belted, short-sleeved chiton
      and a himation (draped around her hips), with a kithara on her lap, turns her head profile to
      the left, to face Apollo. She rests her upraised right arm on a shield (?) behind her. Another
      muse (?), seated profile to the right, dressed similarly to the first muse, but with drapery
      veiled over the back of her head, holds a wreath in her left hand, and opens a box with her
      left hand. Opposite her is another muse, seated profile to the left, with her upper torso in
      3/4-view. She wears the himation wrapped around her hips and legs, over a belted, sleeveless,
      chiton. She holds a barbiton in her lowered left hand, and raises her right hand in front of
      her face (a speaking or singing gesture?).""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/784e61f9ab61e7547f33797c35edd2c196213532e4665f47ceabfdd7d7a0fc49> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Side A: Five warriors fighting (one fallen to the ground). Side B: Dionysos with four
      satyrs."""@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

