<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/79f57458cfd10ed30a0fd3cea5adb11814a79ae8396d160036100aa3b4fc041e> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "South Stoa, SW of the main altar in Sanctuary of Hera, Samos"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/7a0c110c35ee925f2d20784020fdfd2992665c132eeae402b80c286cfec5ac7c> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Herakles and the lion.
      Herakles moves to the right, naked, with a chlamys draped over his left arm, attacking the
      lion which springs at him. He holds a club in his left hand, pulled back behind his body ready
      to bludgeon the animal. <hi rend="ital">Side B</hi> has the same general scene but
      with differing details. On this side, Herakles holds the club in front of his body, and the
      lion is not leaping.</P><P>The first labor of Herakles was to subdue a lion living
      near Nemea. Diodorus Siculus (<bibl n="Diod. Sic. 4.11.3-4">Diod. Sic.
      4.11.3-4</bibl>) stresses that the lion was invulnerable to iron weapons. Most artists
      choose to illustrate Herakles strangling the lion, as a variety of literary sources relate the
      myth: the Diodorus passage cited above, <bibl n="Theok. 25, 266-271">Theok. 25,
      266-271</bibl>, and <bibl n="Apollod. 2.5.1">Apollod. 2.5.1</bibl>. The
      painter of this skyphos chose to illustrate an episode related by <bibl n="Theok. 25,
      253-261">Theok. 25, 253-261</bibl>, that before Herakles strangled the lion, he
      knocked it senseless with his club. The schema with the lion prepared to spring and Herakles
      with his club finds its greatest popularity in the last quarter of the sixth century. In such
      scenes, the general composition for a fight or battle, as on <rs type="vase">Mississippi
      1977.3.75</rs>, is applied to Herakles and the lion.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/7a106c8bffd4d2a3ce00b759030f936f559ba07d0c5d57b480ef2e1038cf162a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
