<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/7dc16446c29159c8d0a70800482b4b6610375a79a178fad693188b5dd3cf5a60> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Clairmont 1993, 2.100-101, cat. 2.152 (ill.)</bibl>; <bibl>Karouzou 1968, 50</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/7dc68082c75e44c71d227e000e80a7021a9e43b8786de43c00bb595e4488b11b> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The decoration is distributed in three different fields. The
      principal field is on the shoulder; subsidiary friezes on the lower body and neck. On the
      shoulder, a frieze of centaurs encircles the vase, interrupted in the handle area. On each
      side are three centaurs marching to left. The third centaur on both sides is not shown
      complete; only half or less of his body is visible, as if coming from behind a curtain onto
      the stage. The centaurs, as typically in so-called Pontic vases, have human torsos and horses'
      bodies. They all have horse ears, long red hair and wear white fillets or bands in their hair;
      but there are differences between them. On side A, the first centaur has a red beard, the
      second white and the third is unbearded. On side B, the centaurs at left and right have red
      beards and the one in the middle is again white-bearded. All of them carry a five branched
      tree over their left shoulders with the roots dangling down over their chests. Under the
      centaurs' bodies are walking birds, perhaps storks.</P><P>On the neck, three
      youths on either side of the vase. They are naked, simply rendered, walking from right to left
      with their right arms forward and left arms behind them in a posture resembling Egyptian
      figures. Between the youths are large walking birds (4 on one side, 3 on the other), similar
      to the birds beneath the centaurs on the main frieze.</P><P>The second frieze on
      the belly of the vase is separated from the first by two bands, black above and red below.
      This frieze has a sequence of animals, alternating sphinxes and lions. On each side a sphinx
      is followed by a lion. The sphinxes have red-striped bodies and open wings. Their faces are
      completely white and they wear their hair in a black sakkos with white stripes. The lions open
      their mouths showing their tongues, and raise their tails. Under each handle, there are two
      heraldic lions, flanking a complex of a palmette over two tendrils in volute shape, with lotus
      buds and more palmettes. These lions have red manes and are turning their heads back, showing
      their open mouths. All of them have erect ears more appropriate for a wolf or a
      panther.</P><P>Below the animal friezes, a band with network decoration in black
      with red dots or daisies in the inner fields. Rays coming from the bottom. The rim or lip and
      the handles and the area underneath are black.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

