<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/80885e40035171e4214229e3eecde7029e5c68a001d0113266c79a2f55fc877e> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Many roomed house; west and outside the Sanctuary of Demeter and Kore, within the Precinct of the Hiera Oikia."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/80a0cd9704c90e9785c55321d22490b98ab2744818f161946879ec58ad84d0c8> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Herakles and Acheloos.
      Acheloos, in the form of a centaur, tries to escape, but Herakles has grabbed him by one of
      his horns, pulling his head back. Acheloos raises his arms, his mouth open in a cry of alarm.
      His long hair falls in tresses down his sides and back, and he has a long beard. Behind
      Herakles and Acheloos, on the left, Hermes sits on a chest or box, his right hand raised in a
      salutory gesture. He wears a chiton, mantle, petasos and winged boots. Herakles, red-bearded,
      wears a short tunic and his lion skin. His quiver hangs from a strap behind his back and his
      sword is sheathed at his waist. Herakles' club leans near Acheloos' forelegs. A plant (fern?)
      grows next to it, its branches spreading over the entire background of the scene. On the far
      right, a singing bird is perched on one of the handle palmettes.</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Side B</hi>: hoplite and archer leaving home. Two warriors, a hoplite and
      an archer, prepare to leave home. An old man and a woman (their parents?) bid them farewell.
      The two warriors stand side by side with the hoplite in the foreground, his shield covering
      their bodies from chin to thigh; the shield device is a running leg painted white. A dog
      stands behind the legs of the archer, looking up at them. The hoplite wears a crested helmet
      and greaves and carries a spear as well as a shield. The archer wears an archer's cap, a short
      tunic and greaves. His quiver hangs from his waist. The old man on the right, white-haired and
      white-bearded, holds a tall sceptre or a staff with a finial, and is wearing his mantle
      wrapped closely around him. On the left, the woman, her skin painted white, raises her
      himation-covered left hand in a gesture of farewell or dismay. The lines drawn on her neck
      show her age.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/80a1ac3d5c84f148b281d51611a5d4f26142118f7615e54315b6d45782de45bc> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><bibl>CVA, USA fasc. 20, pp. 9-10, pls.
      77-79</bibl></P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/80a88778bcdc7cf289d6ecd592843a5662360429cb167758f623f436cb1f85fa> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A woman seated beneath a tree"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/80bcdfd7a8084025e2039a1c1e0a197a668ac1a9de3993ba286e3b397e221a9f> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A:</hi> Menelaos and Helen.
      Helen is fleeing right toward an altar, her arms outstretched to it. She is wearing a peplos
      and stephane. She looks back left at Menelaos, who is running in pursuit. His sword has fallen
      out of his right hand; in his left he holds a circular shield. He wears a Thracian helmet,
      chitoniskos, and baldric, and his black hair is blown back on the side, showing the speed of
      his chase. His chitoniskos has chevrons, dots and circles decorating its hem and shoulders. A
      young tree is growing behind the altar; its leaves are added red</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Side B:</hi> youth departing. On the left a woman holds out a phiale to a
      youth opposite her. The woman is wearing a chiton, himation, and sakkos; she holds an oinochoe
      in her left hand. The youth is clad in a chlamys with a petasos over his left shoulder, and he
      holds an upright spear in his right hand. Relief contour is used for the woman's forehead and
      nose.</P><P>Under the rim runs a band of laurel leaves. Below the main panel is a
      band of stopped meanders in groups of three, with cross squares in between. The inner edge of
      the rim has two reserved bands about 5 cm. apart. The handle spaces and insides are
      reserved.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/80bfe382bc41cc414960df804ebaa97dfb60b8e0186703fc4cb59954d915d624> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Interior</hi>: A satyr and maenad
      stand facing one another with bowed heads. They seem to be dancing rather than fighting. The
      maenad, at left, wears a belted chiton with voluminous sleeves and overfold. The folds of the
      chiton are drawn with relief lines, as is the outline of her body, visible through the
      diaphanous gown. She wears a red wreath, and her long hair, tied at the nape, hangs loosely in
      back. A spotted leopard or cheetah skin is draped over her upraised left arm. The satyr raises
      his left arm over his head, the elbow extending beyond the frame and thus unseen. His right
      arm is destroyed, but was lowered toward the right hand of the maenad, which is also effaced.
      His right leg is bent, the left one straight and extended. He wears a red wreath, and a single
      corkscrew curl hangs on his shoulder.</P><P>Around the exterior, five maenads are,
      with some success, fighting off the unwanted advances of seven satyrs. All of the satyrs are
      ithyphallic and all have a spotted leopard or cheetah skin draped over their extended left
      arm. Some of the skins have round or oblong spots with smaller dots between, others have
      irregular blobs with dots; it is not clear which is leopard and which is cheetah, but one of
      those with oblong spots, worn by the central satyr on side A, has a central stripe down the
      back, which may indicate it is a cheetah skin. Confusing the issue is the fact that on the
      skins worn by two of the maenads, there are round spots around the shoulder and irregular
      blobs on the back. All of the maenads wear belted chitons with voluminous sleeves and
      overfolds; the red belts of the first two maenads on side A are represented, but not the
      others. The folds of the chitons are drawn with relief lines, as are the outlines of the
      bodies, visible through the diaphanous gowns. The two sides are divided by the figures under
      the handles. A balding satyr wearing a red wreath and leaning on his left hand crouches under
      handle A/B, his head down and his right hand raised. He may be about to ambush the right-hand
      maenad on side A. Under the opposite handle (B/A), a shaggy goat with long horns and upright
      tail faces the action on Side B. The hairs of its beard and back are drawn with strokes of
      dilute glaze, which is also used to drawn the ribs and minor musculature of the
      satyrs.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Three pairs of satyrs and
      maenads are locked in combat, with the maenads facing left and the satyrs right. The first two
      satyrs are in nearly identical stances, with the right leg advanced, both knees bent, and both
      arms extended. The first one has a full head of hair, tied at the nape, and wears a red
      fillet; the second is balding and wears a red wreath. The two maenads are also in similar
      positions, leaning back, with the right arm extended and one leg overlapping that of the
      attacker, but the first maenad advances her right leg and the second one her left. Both carry
      thyrsoi, the butt of which they point toward the satyr; they hold the shaft with different
      grips. The first maenad wears a skin over her chiton, with two paws knotted at her throat. Her
      hair is tied in a krobylos by a red wreath. The satyr grabs the shaft of her thyrsos to ward
      off her blows. The second maenad wears a red fillet, wrapped twice around her head; her long
      hair flies out behind her as she jerks away from the satyr. The third satyr, at right, leans
      over toward his prey, so that his phallos hangs between his legs like a second tail. He wears
      a red wreath, and although balding, has long hair down his back. He reaches for the maenad
      with both hands, his left hand grasping the shaft of her thyrsos, which she holds like the
      preceding maenad. Her hair also flies back in a similar manner, but is circled by a wreath
      rather than a fillet.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: On this side
      the arrangement of figures is more varied, as the maenads are outnumbered three to two. At
      left, a satyr advances to the right, his right leg planted straight out before him, his left
      leg drawn back. A wineskin hangs on the wall behind him. He wears a red wreath over his full
      head of hair, which hangs in ringlets down his back. He extends both arms toward the back of
      the maenad at right; in his right hand he holds a drinking horn. The maenad backs away from a
      second satyr at right, her left leg extended and her right leg drawn back. She extends both
      arms toward her attacker, and wields a thyrsos in her left hand. Her hair is tied in a
      krobylos by a red fillet that circles her head twice. The satyr advances toward her from the
      right, his cheetah skin hanging over both of his legs (the left one advanced, the right one
      drawn back). He is balding and wears a red wreath. A single, long, corkscrew curl hangs on his
      shoulder. Unlike the other satyrs, his long beard is unfringed. He bends over slightly as he
      parries the thyrsos with his upraised right arm. At right, a second maenad runs away from a
      third satyr, her legs in motion to the left but her head and upper body turned back toward her
      assailant. She wears a skin over her chiton, and a red wreath, which holds her hair in a
      krobylos. With he left hand she fends off the satyr and with her right hand raises her thyrsos
      to strike a blow. The satyr bends both knees and extends his right leg; both hands reach out
      toward his reluctant prey. Although balding, he has long hair, tied at the nape; a red fillet
      is wrapped twice around his head.</P><P>The tondo is framed by a band of
      interlocked maeanders. The exterior groundline is a single reserved stripe. Two narrow
      reserved stripes circle the rim inside and out. The inside of the handles and the areas
      between the handle roots are reserved, as are the side of the foot, the resting surface, and
      the interior of the stem.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/80c76f0e8cc371d89975ec6c93234d36257a28b38a709e9725704083a08ce388> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Sphinx on a cavetto capital"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/80cce9db43ffbb9d5af9d79253ba2b6a7460b4c8d9f1f3a79d64dbc5b3973af7> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Side A:</hi> Herakles and Apollo
      struggle for the tripod. Herakles moves to the right, looking back at Apollo who pursues him.
      Herakles swings his club above his head with his right hand and grasps the tripod in his left.
      He wears the lionskin belted over a short chiton with a sword and a quiver over his shoulder.
      The artist has attempted to show a back view of Herakles, as is indicated by the dorsal mane
      on the lionskin. Apollo moves to the left after Herakles, clinging to the tripod with his
      right hand and grabbing the end of Herakles' club in his left. Apollo is depicted as
      unbearded, with a wreath in his hair, wearing winged boots. The figures are
      labelled.</P><P>The theme of the struggle for the tripod becomes popular in the
      last quarter of the sixth century. While this depiction of the scene shows only the
      combatants, the favored Attic composition adds two onlookers, Artemis and Athena.
      <bibl>Shapiro 1989, 63</bibl> points out that in this period in these struggle for
      the tripod scenes, a new iconography of Apollo becomes popular, which had only limited use
      previously, that is the depiction of Apollo in a short chiton with a bow, rather than in a
      long garment with a kithara.</P><P>The Lysippides Painter depicts the struggle for
      the tripod on this vase and on <rs type="vase">Munich 2080</rs>. There is a marked
      similarity in the rendering of several features on these two vases: the movement is from left
      to right, Apollo grabs Herakles' club, Herakles is shown in back view, and Apollo wears winged
      boots. (Although winged boots are most commonly associated with Hermes or Perseus,
      <bibl>Gialouris 1953, 315</bibl> has pointed out that other heroes and deities do
      wear them occasionally.) The Lysippides Painter has a clear and distinct iconography for the
      struggle for the tripod. <bibl>von Bothmer 1977, 56</bibl> has seen the lack of
      correspondence between the iconography of these vases and the earliest surviving versions of
      the myth by the Andokides Painter, <rs type="vase">New York 63.11.6</rs> and
      <rs type="vase">Berlin F 2159</rs>, as evidence that the black figure Lysippides
      Painter and the red figure Andokides Painter are not the same artist.</P><P><hi
      rend="head">Side B:</hi> Dionysos and satyrs. In the middle of the composition,
      Dionysos stands facing right. He wears a long chiton with a diagonal mantle and a wreath in
      his hair. He holds a kantharos in his right hand. At the left a satyr, facing right, plays the
      double flute. At the right another satyr, facing left, carries a
      wineskin.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

