<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/8157f51db8f68702f92b99d41e9e5c825d9a10a8289e617acb74479950f81fdc> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>In a scene from the <hi
      rend="ital">Ilioupersis</hi>, Ajax, the son of Oileus, drags the prophetess Kassandra
      away from the statue of Athena at which she has taken refuge during the sack of Troy. This is
      the earliest surviving version of the scene in red-figure vase painting, although there are
      numerous black-figure antecedents. Ajax moves in from the right, striding left. He has grasped
      Kassandra by both arms as he tries to pull her away from the statue. Ajax wears a crested
      Corinthian helmet and a short chiton. A sword in a striped scabbard hangs from his shoulder
      (not across the chest in the usual manner), the baldric indicated by two lines. He appears to
      be barefoot, an ironic concession to the sanctity of the sanctuary he is otherwise violating,
      and he wears an ankle bracelet around his proper right ankle.</P><P>Kassandra, a
      nude diminutive figure, moves unwillingly to the right while looking back at Athena. The
      prophetess has hooked her right arm around Athena's shield-bearing arm and grasps one of the
      statue's drapery folds with her right hand. The conjunction of Ajax's grasping hand with these
      two interlocked arms produces a remarkable dramatic effect, framed by the inside of the
      shield, and possible only in red-figure. Beazley (<bibl n="CB iii p. 64">iii p.
      64</bibl>, note 6) raises the question of possible erotic connotations of Kassandra's
      nudity, a subject explored most recently by <bibl>Cohen
      1992</bibl>.</P><P>Athena stands facing right with her feet together and
      firmly planted on the ground. She holds her shield, with the inside shown, on her left arm,
      and her right arm is raised holding a spear as if to throw it. She wears a long undecorated
      chiton and himation and an Attic helmet with a high crest (cut off, as is her spear, by the
      black band inside the tondo border). A spiral adorns the helmet. Her forehead curls are
      indicated in relief. Athena's pose recalls that of the <rs type="sculpture">Athena
      Promachos</rs> type, known from Panathenaic amphorae, for example, except that here she
      stands rather than striding forward. Her static pose reinforces our understanding of her as a
      statue rather than the living goddess. As Beazley notes (<bibl>Caskey & Beazley,
      vol. 3, 63, note 6</bibl>), Athena is always a statue in these scenes, or else Ajax
      would be dead.</P><P>Paseas worked in both black- and red-figure, decorating
      mostly plates, cups, and plaques. Initially called the Cerberus Painter by Beazley, after the
      plate with Herakles and Cerberus in Boston (<rs type="vase">Boston 01.8025</rs>,
      the painter's ancient name, Paseas, was discovered by <bibl>Boardman 1955</bibl>
      on two fragments of a black-figure plaque from the Acropolis in Athens (<rs
      type="vase">Athens, Acr. 2583</rs>). Beazley (ARV2, 164) accepted the ancient name,
      but still liked his own and used them both. According to Beazley (ARV2, 163), the painter
      closest to Paseas in style is Psiax.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

