<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/82066313dabebc4660bd5cf0f05f5a0156d47ad002d6aac5db00355a3cda5b5e> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<P><bibl>Leekley and Efstratiou 1980, 94-95</bibl>; <bibl>PECS, 685-86</bibl>; <bibl>Rossiter 1977, 525-26</bibl></P>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/821846bd4d6771bb3db7eabdc7e3f63a96db218209a6e621ac90878c5303b6fd> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Comstock & Vermeule 1976, 234 no. 367</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/8225cdc6c9b20730d9c1b5f792d319c3582074385e02b11fe6ed9b07408f3ed3> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Interior:</hi> Dionysos in a ship,
      sailing amongst dolphins. Dionysos reclines like a symposiast in his ship, holding a rhyton in
      his right hand and leaning back on his left arm. The white sail of the ship spreads above him,
      and climbing the mast of the ship, a grapevine with clusters of grapes fills the field of the
      cup. The prow of the ship is decorated with eyes; the body of the ship with two leaping
      dolphins and the stern post bends up into a swan's head. Around the ship, seven dolphins jump
      and sport, surrounding Dionysos like maenads and satyrs. The field is painted in coral red,
      forming a seamless background encompassing both sea and sky which fills the whole interior of
      the cup rather than being confined to a central tondo. The composition is unusually well
      suited to the circular field of the vase.</P><P><hi
      rend="head">Exterior:</hi> on both sides A and B, large apotropaic eyes like those on
      East Greek cups, with eyebrows and diminutive noses. Beneath and on either sides of the
      handles, battles over fallen heroes (fight for the body of Patroklos?). On one side the
      warrior has already been stripped of his armor; on the other, he is still
      armed.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/822da06e0cd4951ce718428a53a48e02cc3ff89677c4826eeba03d2d8f6b65de> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Svoronos 1903-12, 577, pl. 96</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/822ee579f8a5ff774776d66ef14feef4d6454fe7a5aa7847d219f62dbdc9ddef> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><bibl>Williams 1984, 139, no.
      102</bibl></P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/8230215c8e151450a2c2f0bba7b06f016d722004fd5d581bfb37cadc61442e7f> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Apollo kitharode
      between columns with cocks on them. In the center of the scene, Apollo stands looking to the
      right, with a band on his head, playing the kithara. He wears a long chiton and himation. On
      either side there are two Doric columns with cocks. On the background there are branches and
      on either side, between the columns and the handles, on each palmette a
      raven.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: Dionysos and a maenad on
      donkey. At left, a woman with a wreath on her head and dressed in a short chiton or dress
      rides a mule or a donkey towards the right. She carries a branch of grape-vines that goes all
      over the picture. Her face, arms and legs are white. The donkey is a big animal with the mouth
      painted in added white. In front of her marches a man carrying a stick. He is moving towards
      the right, dancing and turning his head backwards to look at the woman on the mount. He is
      naked except for the red ivy wreath in his head and the decorated himation or cloak over his
      right shoulder. Added red is used in his clothes, and his beard is also red. The woman seems
      to be a maenad, and the man is surely not a satyr, but may be the god Dionysos
      himself.</P><P>The amphora does not have the usual Panathenaic theme, but the
      presence of the cocks on the columns and the shape itself suggests that the amphora might have
      been a prize vase or may be souvenir. The themes represented belong, though, to two different
      worlds: Apollonean and Dionysiac. They are often found together and opposed in Greek
      art.</P><P>Under the handles is a decorative floral motif consisting in lotus
      flowers linked to four palmettes growing from the handles: two on either side, one upwards and
      one downwards. The motif is similar to some of the Antimenes Painter's decorations (see <rs
      type="vase">Wrzburg L186</rs>).</P><P>Under the picture an animal frieze
      goes around the entire vessel. This ancient motif consists in a procession of lions and boars
      drawn in silhouette. Under the frieze, rays coming from the
      foot.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/8233ba0b93dfd1498eea816dac53113820ff2cd88980d59c2bd43a04f0d2caf3> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Comstock & Vermeule 1976</bibl>, 192-193 nos. 305-306" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/823766792f86770a6b20ada45535601dfa9b600f0800ab37440c90779a584868> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Sides A and B: Komos"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/823a477190c2d4db598d0f60b9c3b0504351b3de136a24386539efc270307c6b> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<P>Robinson 1956.</P>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/823b0bc035a24821efbdfaba6c1fa47d97eced2ad01653a8d0986489fe8073c9> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The entire body of the vase is painted black, with a pattern of
      rays in the reserved area near the base. The handles are decorated with an ivy pattern on Side
      A, and a spiral chain on Side B. A maeander and addorsed palmette chain are above the figural
      band on the neck, while the inscription <foreign lang="Greek">*N*I*K*O*S*Q*E*N*E*S
      *E*P*O*I*E*S*E*N</foreign> ("Nikosthenes made [me]") is on the
      rim.</P><P>The figural decoration is on the neck of the vessel. <hi
      rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Hermes, Iolaos, Herakles, chariot with charioteer, Eris,
      Athena, Ares, Zeus, Phobos and Kyknos with chariot.</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Side B</hi>: Battle scene with chariot.</P><P>Williams (p.
      34) compares it to metal prototypes like the bronze vase in the British Museum (<rs
      type="vase">London GR 1980.10-29.1</rs>), which is also signed on the rim. The black
      painted body may suggest a metal prototype. D. Kurtz (as cited in the <hi
      rend="ital">CVA</hi>) has pointed out a bronze version published in
      <bibl>Hornbostel 1977, 91f., no. 63</bibl>; and also that the placing of the
      signature on the lip of the British Museum vase reflects the practice with metal
      vases.</P><P>Kyknos was the son of Ares and Pelopia, daughter of Pelias. He would
      prey on travelers, killing them and dedicating the spoils to his father, Ares. He did this to
      travelers going to Delphi, which angered Apollo who then had Herakles fight him. Herakles
      killed Kyknos, prompting Ares to avenge his son's death. The outcome of the story has two
      versions; that Athena helped Herakles to wound Ares, who ran off to Mount Olympos, or that
      Zeus interfered and wouldn't let them fight at all. Both Athena and Zeus appear in this
      version.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/823f176ed182141527f1278478b3d8f6c4ffce498acebb69558b3cccc4f2a18c> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A: Dionysos; B-C: Winged Nikai"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

