<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/8955cdc06fc038091f6a1971fa624bf89a55180767228451581eb76f0ed91603> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Side A: Herakles and the Nemean Lion<lb>Side B: Dionysos and Ariadne."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/8956070b5bd856b89b1986cbe483937a4981806695dc82bdc1e638ca1d46c430> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Animal frieze with decorative motifs. <hi
      rend="ital">Side A</hi>: The main scene, framed by vertical meanders, consists of a
      bull and a water bird to the left of a central ornament. To the right a goose and a lion with
      raised tails and heads in outline. The ornament in the center of this main side is a floral,
      whose components are two juxtaposed pairs, one of the top of the other, of spiral volutes with
      their outer band filled with dots. Above and below at the corners are placed two equilateral
      triangles, subdivided in two smaller triangles and a lozenge, each filled with a dot. At the
      center a lozenge is drawn, subdivided in four smaller lozenges filled with dots. The two
      lateral corners are defined by a striped border within which are two horizontally disposed
      triangles again subdivided into triangles and lozenges.</P><P>The leftmost figure
      of the frieze is a piebald bull preceded by a water bird, both moving to the right. The body
      of the bull is executed partly in silhouette and partly in outline with a large curved areas
      ending in spirals. The water bird appears only once in Chian pottery, in this chalice. It is
      drawn wholly in outline, with a long neck turned backwards and the bill pecking at its
      feathers; the rear part of its body with the horizontal stripes is identical of that of the
      goose in the same frieze; the front part is flecked. It has no stylistic affinities with other
      birds appearing in Chian vase painting.</P><P>To the right of the ornament, a
      large goose and a lion are moving towards the left. The goose's head, neck and body are in
      silhouette, its legs in outline; the long wings over the rear part of the body are reserved
      with the feathers striped with horizontal or diagonal lines, while the front part is rendered
      with a reserved bow, heart-shape like. The lion responds to the second type of lion in Chian
      pottery, coming from the Assyrian prototype with wide-open mouth, square pointed muzzle and
      heavy network-like mane; the lolling tongue, instead, belongs to the Neo-Hittite type of lion.
      The underside of the lion is also reserved. The two vertical meanders, above the handles,
      framing the picture are a complex consisting of four quadruple maeanders juxtaposed in pairs
      and flanked by vertical rows of squares and dots on the inner edge and a paired of hook simple
      meander on the outer.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: Three wild
      goats marching to right in a frieze. Before them a small goat leads the procession; only its
      head is preserved. This leading goat is climbing on a floral. Heads and undersides of belly in
      outline. The stylized type of the goat with his strong horns are widespread in Chian pottery.
      The disposition of the motifs and the animals around the vase is not totally
      symmetrical.</P><P>The Orientalizing ornaments filling the blank spaces between
      the animals consist of triangles, rosettes, circles, lozenges, daisies, corner ornaments and
      roundels (below the hind legs of the wild goats).</P><P>On the lip is a paired
      hook simple meander band, underneath which is a frieze of tongues above an arcading line.
      Underneath the figure frieze on side A a double guilloche pattern, a meander on side B,
      between a tongue ornament growing from the handle roots. Below this handle zone a broken
      meander between band and line and rays springing from below. The presence of the guilloche on
      side A demonstrates that this is the obverse of the vase</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/89593c63d970be4f8346a8476655e033f95a5cd985e7578ec4db2f80f3bdf0d5> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
