<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/908ba1bb466fcad96b00de705851407300987ef291ba2ad4b3828dfc1bf9aa3d> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Herakles and Acheloos
      fight. Herakles, nude and wearing a wreath in his hair, is to the left and partially hidden by
      Acheloos, whom he is grabbing in a wrestling hold. He grabs Acheloos by the horn with one
      hand, and has the other hand at his throat. With his left foot he straddles the snake body of
      Acheloos. Acheloos' body from the breasts up is human (or satyr, with pointed ears and snub
      nose), but the rest of him is a Triton, with scales and dorsal fins. He grabs Herakles' wrists
      and tries to free himself, unsuccessfully. There is the inscription <foreign
      lang="Greek">*F*A*N*F[*A*I*O*S] *E*P*O*I*E*I</foreign> ("Pamph[aios] made [me]").
      Bruhn (113) also notes the inscription <foreign lang="Greek">*E*R*A*K*L*E
      *A*X*E*L*O*I*O</foreign> ("Herakles, Acheloos") on Side A, and <foreign
      lang="Greek">*O*R*E*I*Q*U*A</foreign> ("Oreithua") on Side B, but no one else notes
      this and it is not visible in photographs. Herakles and Acheloos is a rare subject on red
      figure vases, but common on late sixth century black figure ones. Oltos is unique in showing
      Acheloos as half-satyr, half-triton (Philippaki, 2). It is also suggested that portraying him
      with a bull's horn and a Triton body is actually showing all at once the successive phases of
      his transformations (Ahlberg-Cornell, 100).</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side
      B</hi>: A dancing maenad is shown, wearing a chiton, himation, and animal skin. She
      carries a tree, crotalon (castanet) and ivy wreath. Opposite her is a flute-playing
      satyr.</P><P>At the shoulder on both sides, tongues and an ivy pattern. At the
      base there are rays, and around the handle attachment points are tongues and
      palmettes.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

