    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/9493d2d2173eac5fede469d9cade77d3e88ac80de70eefaff48c53bffa4ada28> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<P><bibl>Kunze 1992, 137, cat. 45</bibl></P>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/949a88ae62a09288dc6a3b4052403dbaba83513300379ac6e211cc981c4c0eb9> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Battle scene, with a
      group of three hoplites fighting on the right and a chariot with four horses driven by a
      charioteer. The fighting group consists of three hoplites. One of them, in the middle, is
      kneeling and seems to be defended by the hoplite at right. The hoplite at left seems to be
      attacking the fallen soldier. All the warriors wear greaves, short chitons, metal corslets and
      Corinthian helmets and all three carry spears and shields. The shield of the attacker is seen
      from the back. The only difference between the attacker and the other two hoplites is the
      color of the helmets the attacker's is black, the enemy's red.</P><P>On the right
      is a four-horse chariot driven by a charioteer towards the right, as if leaving the scene. The
      charioteer is dressed in a short purple chiton and is turning his head back to look at the
      fighters. The four horses are superimposed by pairs. The two behind hold their heads up, while
      the two before have them down. They have white manes. The details of the bodies of the horses
      are well rendered: the nose, the bridles and bit, dotted and white, and the hair over the
      forehead, also white. Behind their necks the yoke is in the shape of a head of a snake looking
      backwards.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: Erotic scene. Three
      couples and a group of three, two men and a woman, in erotic attitudes. On the left, a couple
      making love lying on a couch or bed. The man, who is over the woman, is turning his head back
      to look towards the right. Both have black bodies contoured in white. They are naked and the
      man has a short red hair, while the woman has a very long hair falling from the edge of the
      couch, marked by a wavy white stripes.</P><P>In the center of the scene are two
      men pursuing a woman. She is grabbing the man at left by the beard. The man at right is
      missing. All of them are naked and have black bodies. Their hair is red and long; the woman's
      hair is contoured in white.</P><P>At right, next to the group there are two more
      couples. The man of the first one is also turning his head to look at the group of three.
      Their bodies are missing, only their heads remain. They seem to be embracing each other. The
      rightmost man and woman, also half preserved, are making love in a standing position, the man
      behind the woman. Part of their bodies are missing. The treatment of bodies and hair is the
      same as in the figures already described. As in most Ionic or Chalcidian vases, the bodies of
      the women were white, but the added white is often missing.</P><P>As frequently in
      Archaic vases, there is a strong contrast, almost an antithesis, as Simon suggests, between
      the representations on both sides. In this case the contrast between the tense scene of the
      battle and the relaxed atmosphere of the men among the hetairai is
      obvious.</P><P>The decoration of the amphora occupies only upper part, while the
      large space between the pictures and the foot is filled with a band of decorative patterns: a
      band of linked lotus flowers and buds, a solid black band and again a different type of
      pattern of lotus flowers and buds. At the foot are rays.</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Neck</hi>: The neck is decorated with a floral ornament consisting of
      stylized palmettes like flowers linked by volute-like tendrils, and on both corners two birds
      flying over the vegetal composition. The motif is the same on both
      sides.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

