<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/95c4e892ea3c31356048f6a02c55d6b3614250e6529049e7a40c561b60165b95> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Athena Promachos in a 3/4 back view"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/95c69e856d5997a584135b40171dc434e8a4ab50d183515f80609622ac3d6b27> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Dionysos with two
      pairs of satyrs and maenads. Dionysos is in the center, moving to the right but looking back
      to the left. He wears a chiton and himation. His hair is in a krobylos secured by a wreath of
      ivy. In his raised left hand is an ivy branch; in his lowered right, a kantharos, which the
      satyr at left hastens to fill from a trefoil jug. This satyr is in profile, while the one at
      right, on the other side of the god, turns to face the viewer, his mask-like head cocked at an
      angle, as though curious as to whom we might be. He is ithyphallic and wears a red ivy wreath
      (all the other satyrs wear red fillets), and like the other satyrs on the cup, his ribs,
      abdominal muscles, and other subtle anatomical features are rendered in brown, diluted glaze,
      which in his case is also used to suggest the hairs of the chest. Two maenads frame the scene
      at either side. Both wear chitons and himations and carry thyrsoi, and both gesture toward the
      central trio with hands concealed by the sleeves of their chitons (so-called "wings). The one
      at left is badly damaged; she stands frontally, but it is not clear which way she faces. The
      one at right moves away from the action but looks back toward it. <hi rend="ital">Side
      B</hi>: Two satyrs and three maenads. A satyr stands at the left carrying a drinking
      horn (<hi rend="ital">keros</hi>) in his right hand and gesturing with his left
      hand at a maenad, whose right arm crosses his in defense. The satyr's torso and left leg are
      frontal, but his right leg is in profile to the left and his head to the right. His hairy
      chest is rendered with dilute glaze. In front and to the right of the maenad is a second
      satyr, who attacks another maenad at right, grabbing her throat with his left hand and making
      a fist with his right. The maenad gestures imploringly as she flees to the right, her head
      turned back toward her attacker. The satyr wears a red fillet. At far right, a third maenad,
      badly damaged, gestures with a "winged" limb. All of the maenads wear chitons, himations, and
      sakkoi; those at left and center carry thyrsoi. <hi rend="ital">Tondo</hi>: Satyr
      and maenad. An ithyphallic satyr has seized a maenad by the wrist and shoulder, pulling her
      close to him. She tries to flee to the right, but turns to stare into her attacker's eyes. In
      her left hand she holds a thyrsos, which judging from scenes on other vases she may use to
      beat off the impending rape (e.g. <rs type="vase">Harvard 1925.30.130</rs>,
      below). The ivy leaves on the thyrsos are carefully and individually drawn with relief lines,
      unlike the black leaves of the thyrsoi in the exterior scenes. The maenad wears a sakkos,
      chiton, and himation, the latter with a darker border than those of the other maenads. The
      satyr wears a red fillet, which the artists has supplemented over the brow with a band of
      reserved circles, perhaps beading or a poor attempt at a wreath (or an even poorer substitute
      for the <hi rend="ital">Buckellckchen</hi> of the satyrs on Side B). At lower left
      is an amorphous shape representing a rock or hummock, above which floats a drinking horn. The
      figures stand on a reserved exergue.</P><P>The tondo border consists of single
      stopt maeanders alternating with blackened saltire squares, a scheme often employed by Douris.
      The exterior baseline is a simple reserved line. Beneath each handle are a pair of addorsed
      palmettes with coiling tendrils, the latter extending up to enclose a palmette on either side
      of the handle. The underside of the foot is black, save for a reserved band around the
      edge.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/95cd025ef393da1629012af5752d627e49c30f85a4c389a8e8104db29f36844f> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Large rectangular building; just outside the southern perimeter of the central Sanctuary of Asklepios, east of the Temple of Artemis."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/95cd39811eb46e9727eeb51a4c102771a9da7f3dfaf4c46ac4f56e316a6e6811> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><bibl>Diana Buitron-Oliver, "A Cup for a Hero," Greek Vases in the
      J. Paul Getty Museum 5 (1991): 65-74</bibl>.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/95d0891da3d5670d00cbfa1804625689976f0f21e811c5ce82ced5d802f24224> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Body:</hi> Hatched running meanders
      decorate the design panel around the maximum diameter. Above this panel and beneath the rim,
      three reserved bands alternate with three in black glaze to delineate the shoulder area which
      is decorated with vertical zigzags. Below the panel, three reserved bands alternate with three
      in black glaze to frame the meanders and delineate the lower body. Below these is a dog tooth
      pattern. Just above the base three reserved bands alternate with two in black glaze and a
      broader black band. On the interior of the base there is a cross with spaces between arms
      filled with chevrons. On the underside of the base, at center, there is a cross with spaces
      between arms filled with chevrons.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Lid:</hi> On
      the conical top of the knob reserved bands alternate with those in black glaze. Beneath this
      the globular part of the knob is decorated with horizontal zigzags. Below two ridges,
      highlighted in reserved and black glaze bands, there is an expanse of black glaze broken by
      horizontal reserved bands. Below these are vertical zigzags. At the outer edge, three reserved
      bands alternate with three in black glaze.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/95d1152dae916191dbd327d1ad71c4aa888fb13b4107a312ead02ff05f217c8a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Exterior: Side A:</hi> Theseus and
      Andromache. On the left Theseus, clad only in a helmet, lunges forward on his left leg with
      his sword hanging at his side. Holding his shield up to protect himself with his left arm,
      with his right he aims his spear at Andromache's breast. She flees to the right with her bow
      in her left hand, but turns to face him and with her right arm raised above her head she
      strikes at him with her battle axe. She is armed with bow and quiver and clad in cuirass and
      short chiton with a kidaris (eastern head-dress), spotted to indicate animal hide. Both are
      named by inscriptions, <foreign lang="Greek">*Q*E*S*E*U*S</foreign>, Theseus and
      <foreign lang="Greek">*A*N*D*R*O*M*A*X*E</foreign>,
      Andromache.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B:</hi> Phorbas and Alexandre.
      From the right the Greek hero Phorbas attacks the figure of an Amazon on the left. He runs
      over a small rise and lunges forward on his left leg, thereby giving the viewer a rear view.
      Helmeted and sword at his side, he wears a mantle loosely draped across his shoulders and
      caught by the rushing breeze. He protects himself with his shield on the left arm and attacks
      with a spear held high in his right hand. The shield device is a lion, drawn in black outline
      with only the hind part to be seen. Alexandre backs off to the left with her shield on her
      right arm to cover her retreat and her spear raised in her left. She wears a helmet rather
      than the kidaris (eastern head-dress), a jerkin decorated with dots and wavy lines, a short
      girt chiton with broad double vertical lines at center, and anaxyrides (eastern pants)
      decorated with alternating rows of large and small dots. Both are named by inscriptions,
      <foreign lang="Greek">*A[*L]*E*C[*A]*N*D*R*E</foreign>, A(l)ex(a)ndre <foreign
      lang="Greek">*F*O*R*B*A*S</foreign>, Phorbas.</P><P>On the exterior curve
      of the rim there is an egg pattern. The same runs in a frieze just below the main design at
      the point of carination. Around the foot is a reserved
      band.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

