<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/967861880ed1183a3c1bb1566482d678ab8563dae73b876485d1f21c8efbfb47> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Side A</hi>: mule and three satyrs.
      The beleaguered mule brays in distress as a satyr pushes down his head and two others sit
      astride his back. The standing satyr bends over, pushing at the mule's head with both hands.
      The satyr sitting astride the center of the mule's back, having had too much to drink, sits
      slumped forward, a kantharos in the hand hanging by his side. A third satyr sits astride the
      rump of the mule facing backward and leaning forward to hold on either side of the mule's
      tail. All three satyrs have long hair and beards and pointed ears, but appear to lack
      tails.</P><P><hi rend="head">Side B</hi>: satyrs eating grapes. Much
      of this scene is missing, but parts of two satyrs are preserved in a vineyard. A tall grape
      vine grows from the ground left of center, the twisted vines extending to the right, where
      bunches of grapes hang. A satyr stands to the left of the vine, eating from a clump of grapes.
      A second satyr sitting or kneeling in the center of the scene is just about to put a grape in
      his mouth. The tail of a third satyr may be identified on the lower
      right.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

