    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><bibl>Amyx 1988, 507, 508, fn. 274, 630</bibl>;
      <bibl>Brommer 1973, 70, C-1</bibl>; <bibl><hi rend="ital">Museum of
      Fine Arts, Boston. Bulletin</hi> 61(1963): 159-164, figs.
      10-12</bibl></P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/9a3491e6913d73b6160dde1b516e40f93996af8f3c4e14ea19c5bd30a17dde3d> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Boardman 1978a, fig. 91</bibl>; <bibl>Freyer-Schauenburg 1974, 106-30 nos. 58-63, pls. 44-53, 73 (with previous bibliography)</bibl>; <bibl>Richter 1968, 49-50 no. 67</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/9a367f60be95dd186e623523ad924ad11fb55c3beb647980d44a785b6c165424> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The two scenes occupy the same architectural setting--a
      Mycenaean palace indicating by Aeolic columns (dividing the two scenes) supporting a
      continuous entablature.</P><P>Side A: A young woman, perhaps Chrysothemis
      (Agamemnon's youngest daughter), wearing a long chiton and himation, runs 3/4-view to the
      left, with an arm passing behind the Aeolic column, although she turns her head to the right,
      to view to the scene. She is slightly overlapped by Klytaimnestra, similarly dressed, who runs
      3/4-view to the right, perhaps to aid her lover in killing Agamemnon. She stretches her left
      arm to her right (toward Agamemnon?), and holds an axe in her lowered right hand. Aigisthos,
      bearded and wearing a short chiton, lunges profile to the right at Aigisthos, shrouded in a
      filmy cloth. He holds Agamemnon's head with his outstretched left hand, and a sword in his
      lowered right hand. He seems to have already struck the king, who bleeds from his chest. The
      king, nude except his shroud, bearded and long-haired (just out of his bath, as observed by
      Prag), leans back, and supports himself feebly with his bent right leg. Behind him is Elektra,
      running 3/4-view to the left, with her right arm stretched toward Klytaimnestra, and her left
      hand raised behind her (in surprise or mercy?). Behind her, hiding almost behind the Aeolic
      column, is another young woman, perhaps Agamemnon's "prize," Kassandra (as she is shown in the
      dress of a slave: short-haired and without a himation), with her arms raised above her head,
      fleeing to the right, although she turns back to look at the others.</P><P>Side B:
      Klytaimnestra, wearing chiton and himation, advancing 3/4-view to the right, raises her axe
      above her right shoulder, and stretches her left hand toward Orestes' shoulder. Orestes,
      dressed as a soldier, with a helmet, strides 3/4-view to the right, in a similar posture to
      Aigisthos on the obverse: he holds his victim's head (that of Aigisthos) in his left hand, and
      pulls his weapon (a long sword) back in his lowered right hand. Aigisthos, bearded, is seated
      to the left on a klismos, or backed chair, wearing a himation draped across his thighs, while
      blood spurts from his chest. He stretches his right hand to his assailant (seeking mercy?),
      and holds a barbiton in his lowered left hand. Elektra, behind him, runs 3/4-view to the left,
      in a similar posture as in her appearance on side A, except that she holds her left hand in
      front of her chest.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/9a41bbd83c62d4e1f4ef3e406a7862a35878294b797bfac25aef414d951954d6> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Side A: Demeter and Persephone, Triptolemos, other Eleusinian deities, Herakles as
      initiate<lb>Side B: Hermes receiving divine child from goddess; Athena"""@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/9a451dbe052816aa569ada51b457eaead39ea41bc4acfcee4a5de4e019c23057> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The vessel is divided into three scenes. On the first side
      Athena is shown emerging from the head of Zeus. Zeus is seated on a stool in the center,
      wearing a short-sleeved garment that covers him to the ankles. His feet are propped on a low
      hassock. In his left hand he holds a flaming lightning bolt, in his right a long staff. Athena
      is fully dressed and armed in chiton and helmet, holding her shield and spear. To either side
      of Zeus stand the goddesses of childbirth, the Eileithyiae, each dressed in a patterned
      himation and raising one hand in the air. Behind the lefthand goddess stands Hephaistos in a
      short chiton, mantle, and winged boots, holding his double axe. Behind the righthand goddess
      stands Poseidon in himation and mantle, with his trident in his right hand. On the far left
      and right stand two goddesses, dressed similarly to the Eileithyia, but
      unnamed.</P><P>The second scene depicts the judgement of Paris. In the center
      Hermes leads the three goddesses, Hera, Athena and Aphrodite to the right. Hermes wears a
      short chiton and mantle, winged boots, and holds his scepter in his left hand. With his left
      he gestures to the goddesses, who are all dressed in patterned himations and mantles. On the
      far right stands Paris, also in himation and mantle. Between him and Hermes is Iris, facing
      Paris, dressed like the other goddesses and holding a wreath in each
      hand.</P><P>In the third scene, a bride and groom stand in a chariot, the groom
      holding the reins and a kentron, the bride with her mantle modestly pulled forward. Three
      women approach the chariot, each holding a wreath in her hand. This trio has been interpreted
      as the Charites or the Horai, with the bride and groom as Zeus and
      Hera.</P><P>Around the rim is a frieze of warriors in
      combat.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/9a4dad68b6765e10ad70c90caca59c34e87cf40dc698678bf3f4b48786bd0c5d> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The feet and lower legs of a man, wearing a himation, walking to the left"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/9a567337a9c3887d1c3a5dfca40c9854e992b18369f49287926be01c75575fd2> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Side A: A nude, spotted satyr, with a long beard, stands
      3/4-view to the right, holding an axe (?) above his head, with which he attacks a grave mound,
      upon which sits a sphinx. Another nude, spotted satyr, with a long beard, stands 3/4-view to
      the left, similarly holding an axe above his head.</P><P>Side B: A nude, youthful
      athlete runs toward the left, with his arms outstretched; his body is shown in 3/4-back view,
      although his head is turned profile to the right, and tilted down. He seems to run around
      narrow poles. A youthful male musician, in the center, stands profile to the right, wearing a
      long himation draped around his shoulders, and playing a double flute. On his right is another
      nude, youthful athlete, running profile to the left, with his left foot advanced, although he
      tilts his upper body back; he holds halteres (jumping weights) in both hands, at waist
      level.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/9a6d421dc1e4c08d4aeeb4289a809555bf9945c1ea87db9b34269f7b30e3a762> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
