<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/9f95bfc9535cff744ea901cb71687eeb0ae41bf9515a69551953ecbfb9c463e9> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Hermes, holding his kerykeion and wearing a petasos, stands with a nymph"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/9f9eef2bcf0123998470fe9352144af8326636528028b715ad8abab38ae45c26> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Interior:</hi> In an interior scene
      with a column and chair (with striped cushion), a man embraces a boy. The man wears a chiton,
      himation, and short laced boots; he leans on a staff. The boy is draped in a himation. The
      tondo ornament is a stopt meander running left.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side
      A:</hi> A figure wearing a chiton and short laced boots looks on at two Nikes dressed in
      Ionic chitons flying to a youth and about to present him with fillets. The Nike at right has
      an added red belt. An altar is near the youth. At the right a male figure with stippled beard
      wearing a fillet (in added red) and short laced boots, leans on a walking stick and looks
      on.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B:</hi> A male figure at left draped in
      a himation raises his hand in greeting as two Nikes dressed in Ionic chitons do the same as on
      side A. The left one has an added purple fillet. At the right a figure dressed in a himation
      and added purple fillet, holding a spear looks on. A double reserve band serves as the ground
      line.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/9fbf3c5526bdc055b5d2ccdacba6e6af6466d3fe5d10e9f7dbde5d82c61258b8> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<P><bibl>Karouzou 1968, 58, pl. 28a.</bibl></P>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/9fcdc48d669b98e284e713e15babdf903231d8034965428f5be633174b8671b0> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Body</hi>: Prothesis. The prothesis
      is depicted four times around the body of the jug, the most elaborated version placed opposite
      the handle. Here, six mourning women surround the bier on which the male corpse has been laid
      out. The woman standing nearest the bier on the right holds a palm frond over the head of the
      corpse. The rest of the women hold both hands to their heads in the gesture of mourning, their
      two arms depicted as an unbroken arch. The sex of the women is made clear by the breasts
      projecting from each side of their triangular chests. The crosshatched shroud covering the
      corpse is depicted as an arch over the corpse rather than as covering it. A pillow, also
      crosshatched, supports the head. The bier itself, supported on four sturdy legs, is decorated
      with a checkerboard motif. A regardant agrimi rests underneath it. To the immediate right of
      the bier, is an unidentifiable oblong motif. Moving to the right, three women mourn a second
      dead male, laid out on an almost identical bier. His head is again supported by a pillow, but
      there is no shroud. Instead, two palm leaves, though shown above his body, are to be
      understood as having covered it. The women on the right side of the bier holds a palm frond
      over the corpse; the woman on the left also holds something over the corpse, but it cannot be
      identified. An agrimi is again shown resting under the bier. The third prothesis involves
      four? mourning women and a male corpse, once again covered by a crosshatched shroud drawn
      surrounding the body to leave it visible. Bier, pillow and agrimi are as before. All four
      women are mourning with both hands to their heads. A second regardant agrimi floats in the
      space between the bier and the nearest woman on the left. In the fourth prothesis, five
      full-sized women and a smaller female mourn a male corpse. The bier and shroud differ slightly
      from the others, in that the bier is crosshatched, no pillow is depicted, and the shroud is
      decorated with simple Parallel lines. Between the body and the arch of the shroud are three
      palm fronds, which can again be understood as having covered the body. The women standing on
      either side of the bier hold palm fronds over the corpse. The others mourn as usual, including
      the half-size female figure positioned to the right of the bier, between it and the full-sized
      women holding the palm. As in the other scenes, an agrimi rests under the bier. Blobby
      chevrons, dots and crosshatched triangles fill all available space.</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Shoulder</hi>: agrimi and birds. A regardant agrimi is represented in the
      central panel of the shoulder decoration. To either side, a bird is shown perched on a
      projecting 'nipple.'</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

