<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/a082e9c3504e23408c2ea0cf3572b30c0e23c164b9b0559732d767a7129a02e5> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<bibl>CVA, Paris 1, plates 4-5.</bibl>; <bibl>Boardman 1975,
      32-33, fig. 23</bibl>; <bibl>Arias & Hirmer 1961,
      65</bibl>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/a088001368ebebce9bbc0e0a1b2aba907342cdab3bb7cb1fa1106193a85196ca> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The vase has a cyma foot and is reserved around the mouth. The
      top and bottom surfaces of the rim are reserved. There is a band of egg-and-dart around the
      rim, and a meander and cross design below the figural panel. Above the panel, around the base
      of the neck, is a band of slanting double palmettes. One double palmette is used on the body
      of the vase, below the vertical handle; there is a tongue pattern where the horizontal handles
      are attached. The figural panel goes around to the back of the vase.</P><P>On the
      left of the scene, between the attachment points of one of the horizontal handles, is a woman
      seated on a white rock, wearing a chiton, himation drawn up over her head, and sandals. Beside
      her is a thyrsos. She is looking at a maenad who stands frontally before her, her left foot
      turned out, and head in profile to the left. She wears a chiton and dotted nebris, an ivy
      wreath in her hair, and a brown painted necklace. In her right hand she carries an oinochoe,
      and in her left a kantharos. Beside her is a fawn, moving to the right and looking back. In
      the center of the composition is a bearded satyr, with a purple ivy leaf on his bald head. He
      is seated on a rock, playing a double flute (the mouth-band of which is seen across his
      cheek). Behind him is a thyrsos. His rock was also painted white; the paint is almost gone. To
      the right of the satyr is a standing female in frontal pose, with her head in profile to the
      left. She wears a Doric peplos, a scarf and a necklace, and holds a torch in each hand. The
      figure under the handle at the right is a standing woman, wearing a chiton and himation and a
      wreath in her hair. She stands in profile to the left, and holds a drinking-horn in her right
      hand. At the back, on either side of the vertical handle, are two figures. On the left, a
      satyr walks to the right with his hand outstretched toward a maenad on the right side of the
      handle. She wears a Doric chiton, and holds a thyrsos in her left hand. With her right she
      makes a gesture of refusal.</P><P>Robinson (406-407) thought that this was an
      illustration of a satyr play, but Webster (18, fn 32) thought the subject to be simply a
      Dionysiac scene, not a play.</P><P>The vase is said to have been found in the same
      grave as two other vases: a hydria attributed to the Painter of the Yale Oinochoe (CVA,
      Robinson Coll. 2, 27, pl. 34) and a hydria by a pupil of the Niobid Painter (possibly the
      Painter of the Berlin Hydria?) (CVA, Robinson Coll. 2, 27-28, pl.
      35).</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/a08aeab66d54496f8ca90b7336dae2afef94419b8e1ca6e30f7351486794f117> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
