<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/a2dda642e5bd31e23ea38079594aebc68c01b03f5cd5285fdbb164ba1ab6e8d2> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Side A</hi>: kitharode and two
      seated men. A man stands playing the kithara, while two men sit listening. The kitharode
      stands in profile to the right, holding his kithara upright against his shoulder, having just
      played or getting ready to play. He is dressed in a red chiton and wears a long, black,
      sleeveless tunic which drapes down his front and back, leaving the sides open. He is bearded
      and wears a red fillet over his hair. The man seated on a stool to the left is wrapped in a
      red and black striped mantle, his arms at his sides. He wears a red fillet and has a red
      beard. The second man is seated on a stool to the right, his hands resting on his knees. He is
      wrapped in a short black striped mantle and has a black beard. His face and the top of his
      head are missing. All three men are barefoot.</P><P><hi rend="head">Side
      B</hi>: kitharode. A kitharode stands in profile to the right, playing his kithara, the
      fingers of his left hand splayed across the strings. He is bearded and barefoot, and wears a
      black chiton and a mantle with broad stripes of red and black. His head is crowned with a
      laurel wreath. The laurel wreath, the upper bridge of the kithara, the kitharode's beard and
      the red stripes of his mantle are painted in added red. The top part of the kithara is white.
      White is used on both sides A and B to outline the features and clothes of the
      figures.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

