<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/a8ef896c8eb909d0866839ed4ee796b8161273fd0ed0e5a363ca77b2bca2b808> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Boardman 1985a, fig. 155</bibl>; <bibl>Blmel 1966, nr. 17</bibl>; <bibl>Friis Johansen 1951, fig. 17</bibl>; <bibl>Clairmont 1972, 49ff.</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/a8f0bac42dfe08afd1c23b9b167f61309d7a9503c49c7ebdc2beb32a39e99210> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Above the scene on side A is a lotus-palmette frieze, and above
      side B's scene is a chain of opposing lotuses and palmettes. The lower body bears a band of
      rays. Three red lines circle the vase on its foot, two above the band of rays and under the
      scenes, and one above the handles. Two red lines provide the upper borders of the scenes. The
      outer edge of the lip is red.</P><P>On side A, Theseus is shown fighting the
      Minotaur, sticking his sword in its neck. Theseus is wearing a short-sleeved chitoniskos with
      broad red vertical stripes, white dots on the sleeves, and a white wave pattern with dots on
      the lower border. With his left hand he grips the Minotaur's head, while with his right he
      plunges his sword in. The Minotaur has fallen to his left knee; he holds a stone in his left
      hand. Blood flows out of his wound. Two women in red striped chitons and peploi stand to the
      right and left of the action. Their garments have white dots on the lower borders and sleeve
      edges, as well as white cross and dot patterns on the sleeves. Each has a necklace and
      earrings, and the lefthand woman wears a headband. On the far left and far right, behind the
      two women stand two naked youths, also watching the action. Red is used for the hair, beard,
      and stripes on the chiton of Theseus, the pupils and stripes on the peploi of the women, the
      lefthand woman's headband, and the youths' hair. White is used for the women's skin, Theseus's
      scabbard strap and sword blade, and the dots on Theseus's and the womens' garment
      borders.</P><P>On side B, Herakles is in the process of killing the lion by
      stabbing it in the neck. Herakles is naked; in a position similar to that of Theseus on other
      side, he grips the lion's head with his left arm and hand and plunges in his sword with his
      right hand. Blood streams out of the lion's wound. On the left stands a woman in chiton,
      peplos, and headband, and on the right is the naked Iolaos; both are watching the struggle.
      Red is used for vertical stripes on the woman's peplos and lower chiton edge, her headband,
      Herakles's hair, beard, and sword blade, the lion's mane, and Iolaos's hair. White is used for
      the woman's skin and Herakles's scabbard strap.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/a8f919639ddf4cd268a928d7f0021f9652bbe1194adc91051582800c20329b45> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Condition"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<P>Misfired red on Side B.</P>" ;
