    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/a93242973a050c92c0368c962e1b2211b924a8055af03397473d11ede88bb5d0> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A:</hi> Goddess Athena faces
      right. Unhelmeted, she wears the aegis over a long spotted chiton of Ionic style with full
      elbow sleeves and a himation with decorative stripe over her arms. Her right arm is
      outstretched as if exhorting a hero to action while her left hand carries a spear, point down.
      Her hair is bound in a fillet with long streamers and falls to the nape of her neck. One foot
      faces front while the other is turned to the right. Below is a band of
      meanders.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B:</hi> A crudely drawn youth,
      draped in himation, facing right, leans heavily on a staff. Below is a band of
      meanders.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/a936b62c83ebba007cf47abca0270eb866867634e2359d39e4b6f86cc5d03b90> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The decoration is in black glaze with traces of applied red,
      elaborate detail is incised in fine lines. On the body, there are two tall standing panthers,
      facing each other. In between them is a flying eagle, spread-winged as if it were flying
      sideways (this is typical of the Corinthian depiction of flying birds). In the field are small
      crossed rosettes and larger rosettes with incised detail. On the bottom is a simple rosette in
      black, while there is a tongue pattern on the top of the lip and a row of dots on its
      edge.</P><P>Payne (46) notes that two of the defining characteristics of Early
      Corinthian pottery are that there are lots of rosettes used to decorate the field, and the
      figural frieze takes up the entire width of the vase (instead of being confined to a narrow
      band, as in Protocorinthian and Transitional vase painting). Both traits are exhibited in this
      vase.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/a94a76af415eb416ce5ee72532e7d502ddd6296e83327df881fa749cb09c18e4> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
