<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/a9eb5ad2f946a05a8a5689981471119f7861bc7d74576004fa388b634951df8f> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Svoronos 1903-12, 470-71, pl. 79</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/a9ebc397a4e577a0b6c4f23000f7cd0518a76b4380f44eebfa7f2c5f0931aebe> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<bibl>Beazley Addenda 2, 244</bibl>; <bibl>Jahn 1854, 58-59 no.
      184</bibl>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/a9fec0282cf2b4b6dc21ae1616ee639297f1c982c3628f44cf1e7f5c5a3d9e69> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Tondo:</hi> cock. The bird stands
      facing right; his feet are on a reserved exergue. A reserved circle forms the border of the
      scene. Added red is used for the cock's comb and wattle, and for the potter's signature in the
      background.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side A:</hi> Herakles and Kyknos. In
      the center, Kyknos is falling to his knees and right elbow, his round shield still upright in
      his hand, the shield's device a snake. Kyknos is wearing a low-crested Corinthian helmet, a
      chitoniskos and greaves. He looks up at Herakles on the left, who reaches down on bent knees
      to grab Kyknos's shoulder and stab him. Herakles wields his sword in his right hand and has
      his lionskin like a cape down his back, and a baldric over his left shoulder from which is
      suspended his scabbard. On the right Ares steps up toward the fight with his spear leveled at
      Herakles. Ares is bearded and has on a low-crested Corinthian helmet, chitoniskos, corslet and
      greaves and a baldric over his right shoulder from which his scabbard hangs. In his left hand
      he is holding a round shield whose device is six black balls. Two large palmettes flank the
      scene on both sides, on the left obscuring Herakles's ankle and on the right Ares's
      heel.</P><P>Added red is used for the snake's tongue and for the inscription in
      the field.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B:</hi> hoplite combat. Three
      warriors are involved; in the center, a bearded hoplite has fallen to his knees and is leaning
      perilously backward. He holds his spear in his right hand, aiming it at the hoplite on the
      right. Blood is flowing from a wound in his chest, and with his left arm he holds his round
      shield in a parrying posture against the spear attack by the warrior on the right. The fallen
      warrior is wearing a low-crested Corinthian helmet pulled over his face, greaves, a chiton, a
      himation or chlamys wound around his waist, and a baldric over his right shoulder from which
      his scabbard is hanging. The hoplite on the right is striding toward his opponents, his spear
      leveled in his right hand, his round shield in his left. The shield's device is a crouching,
      barking dog on a groundline. This warrior wears greaves, a helmet like the first, a chiton,
      and a scabbard. Behind the center hoplite a third stands with spear leveled in his right hand
      (held by a thong) and shield raised in his left. Like the first, he wears a garment wrapped
      around his waist, greaves, the same helmet as the other two, and a scabbard hung from a
      baldric over his right shoulder. It is not clear whether this warrior is attacking or
      defending the injured warrior, as his spear could be aimed at either of the other two. Like
      the scene on side A, two palmettes flank the action, although the number of petals differs
      from left (nine) to right (eleven).</P><P>Added red appears on the thong on the
      left-hand hoplite's spear, the blood from the center figure's wound and the
      inscription.</P><P>Relief contours are used all over the vase, except for
      Herakles's front hair, whose outline is reserved.</P><P>Discussants of the scenes
      on this vessel have pointed out, among other things, that a beardless Herakles is unusual in
      Attic red-figure painting. The cock on the tondo is an appropriate theme to appear together
      with two combat depictions, as it is understood as a symbol of the fighting
      spirit.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/aa07ea2bc253dbf17d8bbec5c373358e0c32f3b7d42c8e75d2a85d4de0c9b2cf> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
