<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/ad8abef4138dae7709ec0b125305800c855490b3da411d3c0d5d274a3c3fcff9> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Interior:</hi> Achilles killing
      Penthesilea. The hero plunges his sword into the Amazon's chest, as she falls to the ground.
      Penthesilea pushes against Achilles' arm and chest, trying to keep him from pushing his sword
      deeper; she gazes up into his face, as he stares down into hers, but except for Penthesilea's
      hands, they do not touch one another. Achilles is nude except for his armor: greaves, a
      helmet, and a mantle draped over his back; he carries a shield and a scabbard over his
      shoulder. Although the scene is the fight for Troy, Penthesilea is unarmed, wearing only a
      short chiton with short sleeves, a headband, and jewelry: earrings, bracelets and an ankle
      bracelet, which accentuate the beauty of the Amazon queen. To the right, bent around the lower
      right edge of the cup, is a fallen Amazon in Scythian costume, in frontal view with her hands
      clasped above her head and with her left leg outstretched, her right bent behind her. On the
      left, a standing warrior wearing a helmet, cuirass, greaves and a mantle and holding a spear
      and sword. These additional characters force Achilles and Penthesilea into the foreground of
      the scene. The composition fits the round shape of the cup in a masterly
      fashion.</P><P><hi rend="head">Exterior:</hi> sides A and B, youths
      arming, with horses. Youths carrying spears, holding greaves, helmets, and other armor, and
      attending horses.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/ad90ea733fca39c4841022b545243c3ea3cc655fb4db33e741659568780ae561> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<P><bibl>Hamiaux 1992, no. 80</bibl>; <bibl>Ridgway 1990a, 599f.</bibl>; <bibl>Sinn 1983, 31</bibl>; <bibl>Croissant 1983, 74f.</bibl>; <bibl>Hermary 1978, 74f.</bibl>; <bibl>Ridgway 1977, 141f.</bibl>; <bibl>Brouskari 1974, 57f.</bibl>; <bibl>Kleine 1973, 36ff.</bibl>; <bibl>Deyhle 1969, 1ff.</bibl>; <bibl>Tsirivakou-Neumann 1964, 114ff.</bibl>; <bibl>La Coste-Messelire 1942, 26ff.</bibl>; <bibl>Schrader 1939, 212ff.</bibl>; <bibl>Rumpf 1938, 41ff.</bibl>; <bibl>Payne 1950, 6ff.</bibl>; <bibl>Lechat 1900, 143ff.</bibl></P>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/ada16fcac947c62ad664ed28635e93de7a8741ac1cad2f7e6daef694ee0f4809> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Statue base (for a kouros?) with reliefs showing athletes on three sides"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/ada52e1ec4e4ae53a00c48d20ab736747db86a2053605ba6b1af187ce1cf26a0> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Leto, standing 3/4-view to the right, with her weight on her left leg,
      wearing a star-patterned himation over her shoulders, veiled over her head, and a
      medium-sleeved chiton (?), a leafy wreath over a taenia, pours a libation from a phiale held
      in her right hand, and raises her left hand, palm out; a small deer prances profile to the
      right, and raises its head and left front leg; a fruitful palm tree; Apollo Kitharoidos
      standing profile to the right, wearing a star-patterned himation, olive wreath over his long
      hair, holds a kithara in his bent left arm, and reaches his bent right arm to the right,
      pouring a libation from a phiale held in his right hand, over a low brazier; Artemis standing
      3/4-view to the left, with her weight on her right leg, himation over her left shoulder and
      around her waist, wearing a short-sleeved chiton, a red taenia with white rays, her hair in a
      bun, quiver and bow on her back, holds an oinochoe in her lowered right hand, and an enflamed
      torch in her lowered left hand; fruitful olive tree; Delos (labelled) seated 3/4-view to the
      left on an omphalos (around which is tied a wreath and a chain), legs crossed, wearing a
      sleeveless, belted chiton with cross-straps, a star-pattern himation around her lower body,
      pulled up her back and the back of her head, a rayed stephane over hair tied in a bun, a black
      snake bracelet on each wrist, holds a phiale to the left in her right hand, and raises her
      left hand, of which the palm faces out; Hermes, standing near profile to the left, with his
      right foot raised on low rock, wearing calf-length strap-boots, a chlamys bound in front of
      his neck, a winged pilos, rests his left hand on hip, and his right elbow on his right knee,
      and holds a kerykeion in his right hand; tripod.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/adb682b4e03b63d22528ad3db7035ecf75535e99107252e7c114c5f1bfbeaae1> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Interior</hi>: man and boy. The
      boy, standing on the left, is wrapped in a mantle. One hand is on his hip, while in the other
      he holds the end of a red string which is attached to a hare running away on the right. The
      bearded man leans on a staff, holding it with both hands. He wears a mantle draped to leave
      his chest free. An inscription identifies the boy as Hippodamas, a figure praised by both
      Makron and Douris.</P><P><hi rend="ital"> Side A</hi>: Judgement of
      Paris. Paris sits on a knoll surrounded by his herd (five goats). He is playing a lyre, and
      wears a simple chiton and mantle. Hermes leads the three goddesses forward Athena, Hera and
      Aphrodite. The bearded Hermes carries a staff and gestures toward Paris, explaining the task
      ahead of him. He wears chlamys, winged boots and a domed hat. Athena wears a chiton, mantle
      and snake-trimmed aegis, a crested helmet and snake armbands, and carries a spear. Hera in
      chiton, short mantle, fillet, armband and earrings carries a tall scepter. Aphrodite, standing
      at the end of the procession and surrounded by four erotes, wears a chiton and mantle, the
      mantle pulled over her head, and holds a dove in her left hand. All three have, besides their
      attribute, a red twig in one hand.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>:
      Paris leading Helen away. Paris leads Helen away to the left, holding onto her wrist and
      turning his head to look back at her. He carries two spears, and wears a chiton and mantle, a
      petasos hanging behind his right shoulder. Helen, veiled as a bride, wears a chiton, fillet
      and earrings, and a mantle pulled over her head. A companion of Paris (possibly Aeneas),
      walking behind Helen, turns to hold back Helen's sister, Timandra, who is rushing to try and
      help her. Timandra, arms outstretched, wears a chiton and mantle and a fillet. Paris'
      companion wears a short chiton and mantle. A petasos hangs behind his back and he carries two
      spears. Behind Timandra is Euopis, who turns back toward Ikarios and Tyndareos on the far
      right. Euopis holds a red flower tendril, and wears a chiton, mantle and fillet. Tyndareos,
      Helen's father, is bald and bearded and wears a long mantle. He is leans on his staff and
      gestures with his right hand, his forehead wrinkled in dismay. Ikarios, Helen's brother, is
      also bearded. He carries a staff and is dressed in chiton and
      mantle.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/adbd9a5c7b7e0952858a34e99fcfe7647d0a067e4a61d5362fa8562a9452c362> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Tondo: love-making."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/adc1d9a272d461d2e758a72ec7db32ce58ffc114822c6f0dc542dad3e3ed965f> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Eukleia (labelled in the left field), bending profile to the
      right, with her right leg bent back, wearing a belted peplos, a short, high pony tail, hoop
      earrings, proffers a large box held in both hands; a low table; a sprig of vegetation; Eunomia
      (labelled), seated 3/4-view to the left on a rock, wearing a belted peplos, a sphendone, and
      hoop earrings, rests her left hand on a rock, and holds an indistinct bird (identified by Bhr
      [1993] as a nightingale) on her right hand; another sprig; a goose; a female figure standing
      3/4-view to the left, wearing a belted peplos, and a sakkos, holds a plemmochoe and a fringed
      sash in her left hand, and reaches her right hand to the left; a kalathos; a female figure,
      Pa(i)dia (labelled), standing profile to the right, wearing a belted peplos, bends both arms
      to the right, holds a beaded necklace (?) in her lowered left hand, and a large box and a
      himation in her right hand; another female figure seated on the rocks 3/4-view to the left,
      wearing a belted, sleeveless chiton, raises an alabastron in her right hand, and rests her
      left hand on the rock, above a large box; a sash (?) hanging; a female figure, standing
      3/4-view to the left, wearing a belted peplos, a sakkos (eroded), holds drapery or a fan (?)
      in her lowered right hand, and a plemmochoe and himation in her upraised left
      hand.</P><P>Hampe (123) suggested that Eukleia was shown here welcoming Eunomia
      into her cult with a gift; this suggestion is encouraged by Bhr's interpretation of Eunomia's
      bird as a nightingale, a symbol of (the welcoming into) society, for according to <bibl
      n="Aelian NA 5.38">Aelian, NA 5.38</bibl>, the nightingale sings more
      enthusiastically when (it has an audience) in society.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/adc41999b382a8aeb85c6d718071d23ccbc7a02865027e4d9aae1283f00a4a24> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
