<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/af9a803443f213676d6cf4b6528aa252055d4ad7740d97ad7868ef0bc325c153> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Decoration: Side A: a torch racer and two other athletes. Three
      naked athletes stand in different attitudes carrying different objects. The one in the middle,
      the torch-racer, stands in a frontal position, holding his torch in his left hand. On either
      side stand two other youths. The one on the left is turned to the right and carries a strigil
      in his right hand. The one on the right also turns towards the central figure, holding a stick
      or a cane painted in white slip in his right hand; his left hand is resting on his left hip.
      The stick of the rightmost athlete, the flame on the torch and the bands that wear on their
      heads were painted in added white and have faded somewhat. </P> <P>Side B: a man
      between two women. A bearded man, draped in chiton and himation and leaning on a staff, is
      talking to a woman who stands on his right. Behind him there is another woman carrying a
      thyrsos. The woman on the left holds a large shallow basket. The woman on the right, probably
      a maenad, is draped in chiton and himation and carries a thyrsos in her right hand.</P>
      <P>Tondo: Satyr and maenad or nymph in an erotic scene (symplegma). A satyr, Chorillos,
      is making love to the maenad Paidia. Their names are written over their heads <foreign
      lang="greek">*X*O*R*I*L*L*O*S</foreign> and <foreign
      lang="greek">*P*A*I*D*I*A</foreign>. The satyr grabs her legs, holding her against
      him, practically in the air, and she puts her right arm over his left shoulder. He is naked
      and has a small horse tail. On his head he wears an ivy wreath. She is nude but a draped cloth
      floats under her body and falls behind the satyr's legs. It seems that she is lying on the
      cloth, not holding it. Her hair is dark, curly and gathered up with a band.</P>
      <P>This female figure is attested in sculpture for some goddesses, maenads and nymphs,
      usually lying on a kline or on the ground. It was used for figures representing fountains in
      the same posture as river gods. She has no special attributes identifying her like a maenad
      (thyrsos, ivy, etc.), apart from her relationship with the satyr. For this reason she might
      also be identified as a nymph. But her name is found on four more vases, given to maenads in
      the company of satyrs (see parallels). The scene on the tondo is attributed to the Jena
      Painter, while the scenes on the exterior are obviously from another hand and are considered
      to be from the Jena Painter's workshop. The tondo is framed by a maeander border. On the
      handles' sides, three set of three palmettes complete the decoration of the whole body. The
      sets of three palmettes echo the three-figure scenes on the outside in a nicely symmetrical
      composition.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

