<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b0d6aac2bb17af82ce65f4009f19339b19c49761eddd32ba68a0eb588a74b472> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A:</hi> Odysseus and his men
      encounter Circe. Odysseus, in patterned chlamys, approaches the sorceress with his sword
      drawn. Circe stirs the contents of a cup she has just taken from the man next to her, who is
      in the process of being transformed, and has the head and neck of a boar. A dog sits on the
      ground between Circe and the transforming man, looking toward the sorceress. Other of
      Odysseus' companions surround Circe, each transformed: one has the head, neck, and
      forequarters of a boar; two on the right have have heads, necks, and forequarters of a ram and
      a wolf, respectively. A fifth man runs away at left, transformed with the head and neck of a
      lion. On the right end, a bearded man in patterned chlamys escapes, looking over his shoulder.
      Added red is used for Odysseus' hair and beard; Circe's hair; parts of the dog's coat; necks
      and chests of some of the transformed men; and for the rosettes on clothing. White is used for
      Circe's skin, Odysseus' sword hilt and baldric; the teeth, tusks, and horns of the transformed
      men; and for rosette petals.<P><hi rend="ital">Side B:</hi> Odysseus and his
      men in the cave of the Cyclops Polyphemos. At center, Polyphemos has collapsed onto one knee,
      drunk. Odysseus, in an elaborate chlamys and carrying an oinochoe in his right hand, hurries
      toward a companion, who brings a full wineskin. Athena appears at left, behind the hero, in
      peplos and high-crested Athenian helmet, spear, and round shield (with the device of a swan).
      To the left of the man with the wineskin, two bearded men wearing flowered chlamydes and
      scabbards on baldrics approach with drawn swords. At the far right, a fourth bearded man in a
      patterned chlamys flees to right, similarly to the fleeing figure on Side A. A bearded
      spectator in himation stands underneath each handle. Nonsense inscriptions. Added red for
      Polyphemos' genitals and belly; hair and beards of some of the men; Athena's peplos, helmet,
      crest, hair, and the wing coverts of her shield device. White is added for Athena's skin and
      swan device; rosette petals on clothing; baldrics of the two armed men. </P><P>The
      interior is glazed, with tondo reserved. A band of lotus buds and flowers encircles the bowl,
      under the groundline upon which the figures stand. Below the lotus band, 23 base rays. The
      handles are reserved inside, and decorated on the outside with ivy vines. Added red for the
      lotus buds, and for alternating ivy leaves and the knob-tops on the handles. Added white for
      the calyxes of the lotus flowers on the lower bowl.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b0e40382649c651814f875f3c68f6a5304deb709e47a5cc7a02cd0f44d0ff26d> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
