<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b19079527af82caf3e08b9edf0ba26a15f4666a9b78661cef68fdd29433e1aad> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Crouching dog"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b1951b5ce159b75631a4fef6108e8d14d32dc34b1b77e4602c45ae0b9ac78eae> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Menelaos and Helen."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b199c70a89d89aac59ff9ad2d50d6fcb08fe99a0e07cba01cc91fb4915fe7a8b> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>On front, a hunter with a dog. The young hunter moves to left,
      looking round. He holds a javelin in his right hand with two fingers in the loop, ready to
      cast it, and in his left hand another javelin. He also holds a short club (lagobolon) used
      both for throwing and for dispatching game. A lean hound is beside him, and stands with head,
      tail, and one foreleg raised, his ears pricked, at attention. The hunter wears a short chiton,
      a chlamys, shoes, stockings, and a petasos, and has a sword in a
      scabbard.</P><P>Beazley suggests that the hunter may be Kephalos, pursued by Eos,
      because of the similarity to a fragment of a skyphos from the Acropolis in Athens, which is an
      almost exact replica of the hunter, but here pursued by
      Eos.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b19f8347847cfef348b94633849365518852d54e1812bc19bbd96f94553ba25b> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<P><bibl>Akurgal 1946, 55-80</bibl>; <bibl>Cook 1952, 104-106</bibl>; <bibl>Cook 1958, 1-34</bibl>; <bibl>Nicholls 1958, 35-137</bibl>; <bibl>Akurgal 1961, 182-3, 284-6</bibl>; <bibl>Wesenberg 1971</bibl>; <bibl>Betancourt 1977, 58-63</bibl>; <bibl>Coulton 1977, 167 n. 32</bibl>; <bibl>Schiering 1979, 77-108</bibl>; <bibl>Akurgal 1983, 63-99, figs. 37-88, pls. 49-100</bibl>; <bibl>Kuhn 1986, 39-80</bibl>.</P>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b1a3af78ea28161d26154a2530421437724cab8e1c8d9d58813b3bc44b1b948b> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Brouskari 1974, 140, fig. 268 (with previous bibliography)</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b1a4b9a068498641674d9989a02f53c028a31dbc9401dff29cfc3aaad9a50b5d> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Head of a beardless man, turned to the left"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b1aaa9af05a0126644a87eb07920a4a4fe5f7644cd9030c2465aaf4bdd8f1b58> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Herakles and Apollo
      struggling for the tripod. Herakles has the tripod and is trying to escape with it. He strides
      to the left but looks back over his shoulder at Apollo, who has grabbed one leg of the tripod.
      He holds his bow in his free hand. Herakles wears a short, pleated tunic, his lionskin belted
      over it and tied around his shoulders. His beard is painted dark red. Apollo wears a tunic
      with a short, pleated skirt and a short mantle draped over his shoulder. There is a thin
      fillet over his long hair. He has sideburns but is unbearded. On the left, Athena, in full
      panoply, stands watching the struggle. She wears a chiton with an overfold, and a piece of
      heavier, embroidered fabric is wrapped tightly over it from waist to knee. Her aegis is
      positioned over her right shoulder so that the Gorgon head is seen in full frontal-face, the
      snakes shown as separate, projecting attachments. She wears bracelets and her characteristic
      high-crested helmet. On the right another woman stands watching, holding a stylized flower to
      her nose. Another 'flower' is held in her other hand. She is dressed in a long chiton and
      mantle, and wears elaborate earrings and a wreath.</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Side B</hi>: wrestlers. Two pairs wrestle while a trainer watches. The
      central pair lunge toward one another, their left legs crossed. The man on the right has
      wrapped his arms around the other man, grabbing his right elbow from behind his back. The
      left-hand man in return grips his free hand by the wrist. The man on the left is bearded; the
      other man's face is partly hidden behind the other man's shoulder. On the right, a young man
      with side-burns has lifted a bearded man to his shoulder, his arms locked around the man's
      waist and his legs bent to take the weight. The bearded man has grabbed the younger man's
      forearms, trying to loosen his grip. The bearded man is shown frontal-face. He has a thin
      mustache as well as a beard, and his chin-length hair is parted in the middle. The younger man
      wears sideburns. A mantle hangs between the two pairs and a neck-handled amphora stands below
      it. On the far left a young man with long hair and sideburns (the trainer) stands watching. He
      holds a long staff and sniffs a large rose. His long hair falls in tresses over his shoulders
      and down his back. He wears a rosette-decorated mantle wrapped to leave one shoulder bare and
      his head partially covered. The index and middle fingers of the staff-holding hand are held
      out straight.</P><P>A crouching hare is depicted at the base of each
      handle.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b1b55fab7792cc6b6806c67f92df5b9a39dc1ddf1859b76b0362fa3b05f6d9de> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><bibl>Arias & Hirmer 1962, 299, pl. 55</bibl>;
      <bibl>Beazley 1986, 55, pls. 54.2 and 55.2</bibl>; <bibl>Boardman 1979, 55,
      pl. 88</bibl>; <bibl>Simon 1975b, 95, pl. 25</bibl>; <bibl>LIMC, 3,
      s.v. Dionysos, n. 415 with plate.</bibl>; <bibl>Carpenter 1986, pls. 19b and
      20a</bibl>; <bibl>Hedreen 1992, 87-88, pls. 29
      a-b</bibl></P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b1bea39bd022b8433a5db77740b0e3cf66f9160298a65a777628a71937b95b27> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Nike supporting incense burner on her head; dove perched on knob of lid."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b1bec65602ae8f1c51168c7e060be6e6d09e1c77ac1ce2296c7a4fff6f317dd2> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Plastically modeled aryballos in the shape of a squatting komast."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b1d1ccf1589f318ab0d64abfe8d75f1d8e565c047abe4b3a4137955e4c5a8598> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Naked youth holding the bridle of a restive horse"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b1e417853bde935629a0feeb6028a3ff2cdcd6779a70a255ce1fd509d829f55b> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The clay is pale buff, and the surface polished. The decoration
      is in dark brown glaze, applied unevenly. The body is an elongated sphere cut off for the flat
      bottom and the neck. The neck has nearly straight sides, and there is a trefoil mouth with the
      cylindrical handle attached. On the neck is a narrow band of dotted lozenges and a wide band
      of checker pattern, enclosed by horizontal lines. On the body, the main scene consists of
      animals (foxes?) in three levels, separated into groups by one man in front,one at the side,
      and a group of two men on each side of the handle. Each man has a long bent object (whip?) in
      his left hand. In the field are swastika ornaments. Above the scene is a band of small dotted
      lozenges, and a band of larger net-work lozenges, enclosed by horizontal lines. Below the
      scene is a band of small dotted lozenges and a narrow band of chevrons with dots between them,
      between horizontal lines. A serpent is painted on the handle of the
      vessel.</P><P>Davison places this in the Burly Workshop (85), but Coldstream puts
      it in the Hunt Group, with <rs type="vase">Athens 17457</rs>, <rs
      type="vase">Munich 8696</rs>, and <rs type="vase">Copenhagen 1628</rs>
      (Coldstream, 76).</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b1e456e31e5a690567a7303ea155cbe78e67c16d8f24d3865fff20dd5a567872> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Woman with a vase at a fountain"@en ;
