<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b2f5d3ceeeca4e18eb15849b7eb26857a2d48ded39b86c2813010066719e8266> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Seated Zeus with Tyche (?), Hermes, Herakles, and a mask of Acheloos"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b2f9ec816c30ff4bf77179950c5d20d5b57c32da9f33de27b64b834bf4be10e7> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Interior</hi>: seated boy opening a
      bird-cage. The boy is seated on a stool facing right holding a large bird-cage on his knees.
      He appears to be opening the cage with his right hand, for he tips the cage up with his left
      hand while the thumb and index finger of his right hand grasp the cage. The boy wears a
      fillet, and a mantle is draped over his left shoulder and around his legs. In the field above
      are a long-handled bird-clapper and an inscription.</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Side A</hi>: youths and a seated boy. The middle boy sits on a stool
      facing right, while the two youths stand facing him, leaning on sticks. All wear cloaks, and
      the right-hand youth holds out an open set of tablets in his right hand. In the field are a
      bird-clapper, a writing-tablet with stylus, and an inscription.</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Side B</hi>: youths and a seated boy. The scene is similar to that on
      side A. The seated boy in the center, again facing right, holds a stick in his left hand. On
      the left is a youth leaning on a stick and holding out an open tablet-case. He wears a cloak
      and is wreathed. The right-hand figure is missing. In the field above are a bird-clapper,
      tablets, and an inscription.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b2fca2cce30c1d3ac9247e3e3e3176b474bb57de0110ce5e6f6f4b46cfd4d760> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Brouskari 1974, 176-77, fig. 279</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b3043fefde720476ce963edba8929e6d432b71b1cb4be296538443d33919c500> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Achilles and
      Penthesilea. Achilles thrusts his spear at the retreating Penthesilea, who defends herself
      with a spear and shield. Achilles wears a short striped chiton, and carries a Boeotian shield
      and sword. Penthesilea has long hair, a high-crested helmet with cheek-pieces and a maeander
      border on the crest, a cuirass and a short striped chiton. Her shield has an ivy-wreath
      device.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: Memnon and two attendants.
      Memnon is bearded and fully armed, with a helmet (decorated with the figure of a dog), a
      decorated cuirass, a short striped chiton, shield and spear. His Ethiopian attendants are
      beardless, and carry clubs in their right hands; one has a small shield (pelta), the other
      wears a cuirass. Inscribed on either side of Memnon is <foreign
      lang="Greek">*A*M*A*S*I*S [*E*P]*O*I*H*S*N</foreign>, "Amasis [m]ade it". The vase,
      however, was probably not made by Amasis, but is attributed to Exekias. Double palmette design
      on neck; palmettes and spirals around handles; maeander, lotus and rays below
      panels.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

