<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b4071c89170fa958febe3a6d720588551ce5c35893ebbc4cea6b5bfc738f294b> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Geometric temple; near the harbor, at the north end of the Triglyph Altar of Hera Akraia."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b40bce957da4d1bb8a5df02e1e6bbec1758fdf2711e345b6e12c7134ce137771> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A:</hi> Agamemnon walks to the
      left leading Briseis away by her right hand. He wears strapped sandals and a chitoniskos under
      his armor, which includes an elaborately patterned cuirass and added red baldric with sword. A
      chlamys is draped around his shoulders and he holds his spear in his right hand. Briseis is
      totally veiled in a chiton drawn up over her head, one sleeve of which is modestly held up
      with her left hand, and a himation wrapped around her shoulders. Agamemnon's herald Talthybios
      walks behind her, wearing chitoniskos, chlamys, sword, messenger's cap, and high black boots
      with patterned tops. He carries a herald's staff in his left hand while raising his right.
      Diomedes brings up the rear, walking to the left while looking guardedly behind him. He has
      blonde hair (dilute glaze) tied in an added red fillet, a long beard, wears a chitoniskos,
      chlamys, petasos at the back of his neck with added red strap, and tall black boots with
      patterned tops. He carries two spears with their added red thongs in his right hand. A tree
      closes the scene behind him.</P><P>For the scene, cf. <bibl n="Hom. Il.
      1.312">Hom. Il. 1.312</bibl>.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side
      B:</hi> The mission to Achilles (cf. <bibl n="Hom. Il. 9.162 ff">Hom. Il. 9.162
      ff</bibl>). At left, Ajax leans on his staff, right hand on his hip, wearing a chiton
      and wrapped in himation, surveying the central scene. Dilute glaze gives him a light brown or
      blonde beard and hair tied with an added red fillet. Odysseus stands before Achilles in the
      middle, entreating him to return to the fight. He has an added red fillet in his hair which is
      framed with black glaze relief dots; the latter also cover the beard. He wears a chiton hiked
      up high at the waist, his chlamys falls from his left shoulder, and his petasos is at the back
      of the neck with added red ties. His high black boots are tied with added red laces. He leans
      on his two spears (each with added red thongs) and has a sword at back slung from his right
      shoulder with an added red baldric. An elaborate sword in its scabbard suspended on an added
      red baldric hangs on the wall between him and Achilles. The latter figure is seated on a stool
      enveloped in his himation. His short laced shoes are painted in added red. His hair is
      highlighted with some dilute glaze. A cap hangs on the wall behind him. The stool itself is an
      elaborate piece of furniture, with lion's feet and decorated with animals in metopes, and its
      cushion is patterned. Phoenix closes the scene at the right. Dressed in chiton and himation he
      leans on his staff facing the center, left hand on hip. His hair and beard is light
      brown/blonde like Ajax's with added dilute glaze. Another lion's-foot stool decorated with
      running maeander and patterned cushion is painted under the uninscribed
      handle.</P><P>The two scenes frame the beginning and middle of Homer's <hi
      rend="ital">Iliad</hi>: Agamemnon forcibly taking Briseis from Achilles causes the
      hero to retire from the fighting; when the Greeks are thereupon being beaten by the Trojans,
      the other heroes Odysseus, Ajax and Phoenix plead with Achilles to return, but to no
      avail.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b40e395e3dd33d6a95cc06830aac78caf5480841e4f5378fc5be87b1c38fec54> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Side A, neck: female head within vegetation, and Selene and Helios in
      quadrigae<lb>Side A, body: Hades and Persephone in their palace, with Orpheus and other
      denizens of the underworld<lb>Side B, neck: Nike crowning Dionysos, amid maenads and
      satyrs<lb>Side B, body: Youths and women"""@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b411209e4a6e739c1630c5371f72960523c3dae3b95670ecd5d4cd13848f051a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>On the body of the vase, a man stands facing right between two
      lions. The man is bearded, and wears a long chiton with himation. The two lions are placed
      heraldically looking back over their shoulders. On the shoulder of the vase, a hound chases a
      hare to the right. The mouth, neck, handle, lower body, and foot of the vessel are
      glazed.</P><P>The Dolphin Group decorates shoulder lekythoi and other small
      shapes, generally in a non-narrative animal style carrying over from the earlier sixth
      century. They favor symmetrical compositions as seen here. <bibl>Boardman 1974,
      183</bibl> has suggested that many of the vases of the Dolphin Group may actually be
      Euboean rather than Attic.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b415c4c1ba4e1d0878f7aa1fa3d30399757cbcbc9f49a46150a43b3eb5f68abc> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Exterior: Satyrs and maenads. Interior: Satyr and maenad."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b41957bdc3371c11596a9f5562980d90d2fa7e75de3a4f1dd0e1feebdf921c8c> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Lewes 1920</bibl>, no. 35 ter." ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b41b03009264a7e63ecc386daaa83a97eaf63cc620ba5e5d5e63c4f137126502> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Side A: Man attacking youth. Side B: Zeus and a messenger (Ganymede?)."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b42695ecce23d96315f4263d7b1a909513401aef74079e32ff88ad2d9ac0bb64> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A:</hi> At the left, a Lapith
      wearing chlamys, striped boots, and petasos at the back, struggles with the centaur whom he
      will strike with his axe. The centaur is drawn in an unusual three-quarter back view. At the
      right, another Lapith, similarly clad but wearing the petasos on his head, is about to stab a
      centaur with his spear. The centaur wields a branch with added purple leaves held above his
      head. Dilute glaze is used for the tails and cursorily drawn centaur musculature. The picture
      frame is two rows of dots. The ornament on the top of the rim is a ray pattern, on the rim
      edge two rows of dots, on the neck a network of pendant buds (side A only), and on the
      shoulder a tongue pattern.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B:</hi> Three
      youths draped in himatia stand around chatting. The outer two extend arms to the center figure
      who leans on his staff. All the figures' upper eyelids are clearly
      drawn.</P><P><bibl>Robertson 1992, 215-216</bibl>The artist paints
      bell kraters and column kraters in the Polygnotan style, the dominant style of the
      period.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b42ffd6675d6588140e9d2af5a55d5146afbe420ed6a19b82c72844c9dbceebc> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The lower part of an eagle stands on an inscribed Ionic capital"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b430d9a0fa560c091a54ea46b83cbcd610d5b8350d5435a8461330d304c6ef2e> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Nude, white, Eros (labelled), standing on a rocky piece of land (?),
      near profile to the right, bent over to tie his left sandal (drawn in tiny red lines) with
      both hands, with his weight on his slightly bent right leg, his bent left leg raised, with
      frontal, relief gold wings, red/black medium-length, curly hair, ringlets by each ear; tripod
      kanoun, with lion-claw feet, in gold-decorated relief; Pompe (labelled), standing 3/4-view to
      the left, with her head profile to the right, her weight on her right leg, and her left leg
      bent, wearing a purple (?) himation draped around her lower body, held between her knees, and
      veiled up over her back (she holds the top edge in her mouth), over a pink chiton (?), a
      relief grain wreath, a relief beaded necklace, a single bead earring, two relief bands on each
      wrist, and a white anklet on each foot (perhaps denoting the edge of each slipper?), holds an
      untied wreath between both hands, her right hand raised above her shoulder, and her left hand
      just above waist level; Dionysos (said to be labelled) seated 3/4-view to the right, in a
      chair mounted on low platform, with his head turned profile to the left, his right leg pulled
      back, his left leg extended to the right, wearing a himation draped around his lower body, and
      up over his back, slightly protruding over his shoulders, and a relief red ivy wreath, his
      hair tied back, but long ringlets cascading over his shoulder, rests his right arm on the back
      of his klismos, and holds in his upraised left hand an upright thyrsos with a gold
      finial.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b43537539e5a18193f08b272f86f8ddaf42ce4fd30e39c667812c075796abc09> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A: Apotheosis of Herakles; B: Theseus and the Minotaur"@en ;
