<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b4dca8614da26af4a03c0ceb278eb384235bab1572492b6843e2e8bd33fb1bcd> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Body</hi>: A warrior is preparing
      to depart in a chariot. The charioteer stands in the box at left, holding the reins in both
      hands and a long goad in his left hand. He is dressed in the long, belted gown normally worn
      by charioteers; the front panel of the skirt is painted red and the back is decorated with
      rosettes (red dots circled by smaller white ones). The driver's Theban shield hangs on his
      back from three white cords, which cross the man's chest. The shield device is a bucranium in
      added white. Like the other men in the scene, the charioteer has a red beard and a red fillet
      in his hair. The warrior approaches the chariot box from the right, his body obscured by the
      large round "Argive" shield on his left arm, and by the bodies and legs of the horses standing
      before him. The shield is white, with a red rim; no device was ever added. We cannot tell if
      the warrior wore a bronze cuirass, but he does carry a spear and wears a Corinthian helmet
      with a red crest, and greaves with rows of white rivets along the edge (to hold the padded
      lining). A bearded man faces the warrior and gestures toward him with his left hand; he stands
      on the near side of the chariot, on the opposite from the hoplite. The man wears a red fillet,
      white chiton with incised folds, and a red himation draped over his shoulders. The incised
      line across his throat could be a necklace. He may be the father of the hoplite, come to bid
      his son farewell as he leaves for battle or, allegorically, death. A fourth man stands at
      right, dressed in a red chiton, red fillet, and a himation decorated with red and white
      rosettes. His hair is longer than the others and is tied in a queue. His hands are hidden by
      the heads of the two near horses, but he is probably holding the bridles of the two far
      horses, who rear their heads as though in need of steadying. The horses have red manes; the
      tails, which often are painted red in such scenes, are hidden behind the gesturing
      man.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Shoulder</hi>: A mounted youth rides to the
      right between two groups of males. The horse has a red mane and tail and a red stripe near the
      rump. The rider wears a red fillet and a Thracian cloak, a <hi
      rend="ital">zeira</hi>, with woven designs of red and white. The reins are half red
      and half incised. To the left of the horse, two nude youths are running to the right, arms and
      legs pumping. Both have red hair, and the leader, on the right, has his tied in a krobylos
      with a fillet. Between the runners stands a youth in a himation decorated with red and black
      stripes and rosettes of red and white. He wears a red fillet and holds his left hand out as
      though holding a staff, though none is depicted; he may be judging the race or training the
      runners. Another youth in identical pose and costume stands on the other side of the horse,
      facing left. Beyond him, at far right, is a third young red-haired runner, racing to the left.
      Either more than one event is represented, or this is simply the daily activity in the
      palaestra, with a number of athletes practicing in different places and
      directions.</P><P>Band of tongues, alternately red and black, above the figures on
      the shoulder. Single black stripe between shoulder frieze and main panel. The main panel is
      framed laterally by ivy vines with berries and large leaves, and below by a single black
      stripe. Double stripes of added red circle the lower body below the main panel and above the
      rays on the base; single red stripes circle the middle of the foot, the top of the rim, and
      the inner edge of the brim. The mouth, neck, handles, and foot are
      black.</P><P>Top border: tongues of alternating red and
      black.</P><P>Shoulder scene: youth on horseback flanked by two standing draped and
      three running nude youths.</P><P>Panel scene: hero mounting chariot, departing for
      battle, with two spectators and charioteer.</P><P>Vertical borders: ivy
      leaves.</P><P>Above foot: rays. Foot black.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b4e0373681c24bd429c986ba97351a2fb24b898f37ca1c2e2fb2fad677d28615> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>In the tondo, a nude beardless youth runs left, in his approach
      to the long jump. He holds jumping weights (<hi rend="ital">halteres</hi>) in each
      hand, the left between his legs and the right swinging back behind him. His upper body, seen
      from the back, twists as he swings the weights. His short hair is fringed at the edge, and he
      wears a wreath with leaves in front.</P><P>Beazley compares this kylix to cups by
      the Chaire Painter, but he stops short of saying that the Yale cup is by this painter or in
      his manner. No jumpers appear on the Chaire Painter's attributed works, but other single nude
      figures, both male and female, that do occur differ sufficiently from the Yale jumper to argue
      against the attribution. <bibl>Harris 1964</bibl> has concluded on the basis of
      literary and archaeological evidence that the long jump was the only jump practiced by the
      ancient Greeks, and that the Greek long jump was a triple or more likely a double jump.
      Jumpers running with weights such as the figure shown here suggest that the Greek jump was
      done with a run. There is also evidence for practicing the synchronization of arms and legs in
      the jump by running while swinging the weights. Either this practice or the run for the jump
      itself could be the subject here.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/b4e93a482405c87a10eb40a2f497bdf1c2eacee949db16c835150067592a435d> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Side A: Head of Herakles. Side B: Head of a maiden. Rim: White ivy tendril."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

