<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/bb931a52797de256fe00a7d2a8cc6d09250a5764351dd96160729090d803e57b> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The vessel has a flaring neck, glazed inside and outside; there
      are two pairs of red painted lines around the inside. The mouth has an overhanging rim, which
      is reserved, with an inner border of tongues and an outer border of dots separated by double
      lines; the underside of the lip is reserved. A narrow painted fillet marks the junction of the
      neck and body. The shoulder panel extends between the centers of the far ends of the side
      handles, with an upper border of tongues, a double groundline, and single glaze lines at the
      sides. The body panel is set between near ends of the side handles, framed laterally by double
      lines and below by a single groundline. Over each horizontal handle are upright lotus buds and
      flowers above a double groundline, with a single glaze line enclosing the far edge. Below the
      body panel, a frieze encircles the body, separated by a double line from the 25 base rays. The
      glazed echinus foot is decorated with a painted double line. The vertical strap handle and
      side handles are glazed outside and reserved within; the near ends of the side handles are
      attached to the body with painted disks.</P><P>On the shoulder are heraldic seated
      sphinxes with their heads turned back. They are flanked on each side by a lion with reverted
      head, cock, and siren with wings spread. The following are painted red: the sirens' wing
      coverts, cock's tail bars, combs, and wattles, the breast of the right cock, the lions' manes
      and the marks on their hindquarters, the sphinxes' wing coverts and marks on their
      hindquarters. The faces, necks, chests, and second wing bars of the sirens are painted white,
      as are the wing bars of the cocks, and faces, necks, chests, and wing bars of the sphinxes.
      Vertically between the sphinxes and lions are scrambled nonsense inscriptions: on the left,
      from the top <foreign lang="Greek">*O*T*E*U*Z*P</foreign>; on the right, from the
      top <foreign lang="Greek">*Z*P*E*X*Z*I</foreign>.</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Body</hi>: In the body panel is the apotheosis of Herakles. At the center
      of the composition, Herakles walks to the left on the far side of the horses harnessed to his
      quadriga. He wears his lionskin (painted red) and carries a bow and his club. Iolaos is the
      charioteer; he wears a low-crested Corinthian helmet decorated with horns and ears, a red
      cuirass, and scabbard over his short tunic. In front of his face is the retrograde inscription
      <foreign lang="Greek">*I*O*L*E*O*S</foreign> ("Ioleos"). One of the four horses is
      painted white, while the others have details added in red. Under their bellies is the vertical
      inscription <foreign lang="Greek">*E*R*A*K*L*E*S</foreign> ("Herakles"). In front
      of them, on the left, Hermes and Demeter lead the procession. Hermes wears a short red tunic,
      chlamys, petasos, and winged boots. He is carrying his caduceus and turning to look back at
      Demeter. On his right side is the vertical inscription <foreign
      lang="Greek">*E*R*M*E*S</foreign> ("Hermes"). Demeter pulls a red himation around her
      shoulders, over her peplos with white-dotted border. She is wearing a red fillet in her hair.
      On her right side is the vertical inscription <foreign
      lang="Greek">*D*E*M*E*T*E*R</foreign> ("Demeter"). On the right, behind the chariot,
      is Artemis. She is dressed in a peplos embroidered with stars, and her hair is red. Vertically
      by her left side is the inscription <foreign lang="Greek">*A*R*T*E*M*I*S</foreign>
      ("Artemis"). Behind her is Aphrodite, also wearing a peplos; both she and Artemis hold wreaths
      in their hands and follow the chariot. Vertically on her left is the inscription <foreign
      lang="Greek">[*A]*F*R*O*D*I*T*E</foreign> ("Aphrodite"). All of the goddesses have
      white skin. Beneath the left handle is a crouching hound, whose neck and marks on his haunches
      are painted red. The <hi rend="ital">CVA</hi> notes that this cannot be the final
      arrival of Herakles to Olympus, because Zeus is not present.</P><P>In the lower
      zone is an animal frieze. On Side A, heraldic lions with reverted heads are between grazing
      stags. On Side B, a grazing goat to the right is between two panthers, and a grazing goat to
      the right facing a panther to the left. The necks of the animals and some marks on their
      bodies are painted red.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

