    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Two adult worshippers and three children, with a serving woman carrying a basket, approach a banqueting god and goddess"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/c0c5fe1d2ac253e79aae594e5059d3e2a33b4bdda1870ae84e49b35855016137> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Departure scene with three figures. A charioteer waits in his
      chariot while a hoplite and an old man look on. The scene is dominated by the quadriga in the
      foreground. The charioteer, dressed in a short-sleeved, long white robe, stands in the chariot
      holding the reins in one hand and two spears in the other. On his back is a quiver(?). A
      hoplite stands behind and is partially hidden by the hindquarters of the horses. He faces
      left, toward the charioteer, holding a shield and wearing helmet and greaves. An old man with
      long white hair and a white beard stands directly behind the hoplite, also facing left. He
      wears a long cloak and holds a staff.</P><P>Added red and white glaze are used
      liberally.</P><P>On the shoulder, six figures in a boxing scene. Two naked boxers
      fight on either side of the two stacked cauldrons in the center of the scene. They take the
      wide-legged boxing stance and have their fists raised. Next to the boxer on the left is a
      naked youth in a wide-legged stance, a cloak flung over his left shoulder and arms held bent,
      away from his sides, with clenched fists(?). On the far left stands another youth in profile,
      feet together and facing the boxers. He wears a long robe and holds his right hand to his
      chest, while his left arm is raised and bent, his hand in front of his face. Next to the boxer
      on the right is a youth wearing a diagonally draped garment which falls to his knees. He also
      takes a wide-legged stance and holds his arms bent and slightly away from his body, holding a
      stick in one hand. On the far right, a youth in profile stands facing left, his feet together
      and arms bent and held away from his body. All the figures are beardless. There is a great
      deal of added red and white.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/c0d547035fbcbccfc786e116dea7b4384abb35c47299d94e2fb19d54a0876eab> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>A seated (?) figure, perhaps Dionysos (only part of left arm
      [?] and diagonal thyrsos, remaining); Demeter, seated 3/4-view to the left on a cista (?),
      with her head profile to the right, wearing a peplos belted high over a long overfold, with
      two vertical striations (relief) down the front of her garment, a himation draped over her
      lower body, a polos with vegetal ornament, pendant earrings, a single band necklace with a
      central pendant, a beaded bracelet on her right hand, and a band bracelet on her left hand,
      leans her bent left arm on the back of her seat (the back is not visible), and holds a scepter
      in her upraised right hand; three stalks of grain; a white, nude boy, Ploutos, standing
      3/4-view to the right, with his head turned 3/4-view to the left, and his weight on his left
      leg, with his right leg relaxed, wearing a grain crown, with a colored (or white?) himation,
      draped over his bent left arm, holds in both hands, slightly to the right, a gold keras filled
      with grain stalks; a white female figure, probably Kore (only right body preserved), standing
      near frontal, with her weight on her left leg, and her right leg relaxed, wearing a white (?),
      patterned himation with gilded relief border, probably over a chiton, and a band on her right
      wrist, bends her right arm above Ploutos.</P><P>According to Clinton (50) this
      Ploutos is a perfect parallel to the boy on the Great Eleusinian relief (<rs
      type="sculpture">Athens, NM 126</rs>), except for the keras, which, "... however, ..
      is not an essential attribute of Eleusinian Ploutos. The crucial element is the display of
      grain." In this painted representation, however, we can be more confident that Ploutos rather
      than Triptolemos is intended.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/c0d909d07823fcf3f6bd8d5e4d28ff7cee189dea8015e9e858b2c2d23ae8ba4d> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: A woman is seated to
      right on a <hi rend="ital">klismos</hi>. With both hands, she offers a wreath
      drawn with added red to a bearded man wearing a wreath and a himation, who stands at the
      right; both wreaths are drawn with added red. The man leans forward on the knotty staff in his
      left hand and holds out his right hand to accept the wreath, although he is already wearing
      one. His beard and hair are fringed with strokes of dilute glaze. The woman wears a chiton,
      himation, sakkos, earrings, and bracelets, the latter in added red. Dilute glaze is used for
      the folds of the chiton and the speckled ornament on the sakkos. The woman's hair is
      represented by numerous black dots on a ground of brown, dilute glaze. On the floor between
      the two figures, behind the man's staff, is a kalathos, a wool-basket, containing wool or
      yarn. The kalathos is decorated with two bands of running S's, and its vertical weave is drawn
      with dilute glaze; the yarn is painted with added red. The composition, and the presence of
      the kalathos, suggest that this may not be a simple domestic scene, but a visit to a hetaira,
      who are sometimes shown working wool as they wait for customers.</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Side B</hi>: A man dressed in a himation stands to the left, a knotty
      staff held vertically in his outstretched right hand. His beard and hair are fringed with
      strokes of dilute glaze. The black dot on his upper himation is an accidental
      drip.</P><P>The groundline is a continuous band of slanted, addorsed palmettes,
      linked by tendrils. At the base of each handle is a single palmette, pointing down, with
      voluting tendrils. The rest of the vase is black, except for the lower edge and base of the
      foot.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

