<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/c122baa30470282157e5e469f6aac8ef666569b6c6009b40f3e908b8acb75236> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Head of veiled kore"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/c12e60282e020a3ecd82df70e61a03b1a1131569a21ae210b4a4804c920bd024> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Main Panel</hi>: quadriga led by
      Hermes. The upper left portion of the panel is missing, so that only a small part of the
      charioteer is preserved. The bottom of his red chiton and white mantle can be seen just above
      the chariot wheels. He carries a spear. The two pairs of horses are drawn so close together
      that there almost appear to be two horses rather than four. A bearded male figure stands
      behind the horses. His shoulders and the lower part of his beard are preserved above the backs
      of the rear horses. Hermes stands at the heads of the horses and is partially hidden behind
      them. He is dressed in his winged boots and chlamys. His head is not
      preserved.</P><P><hi rend="head">Lower Frieze</hi>: two pairs of
      confronted goats and panthers. In both pairs the panther stands on the left and the goat on
      the right, the goat with his head lowered.</P><P><hi rend="head">Shoulder
      Frieze</hi>: hoplites. Most of the frieze is not preserved, but portions of four figures
      marching in the left can be seen on the far right. Two are armed hoplites, the third is a
      naked youth, and the last is a bearded man in a mantle.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/c12efeb5a83330e546b8ec80d2c5fd56cad2f7d46629fd42bafde428249c1775> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Freyer-Schauenburg 1974, 207-210 no. 139, pls. 86-87</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/c131d5600b284ab7b3b77a369a91a522c9b9ad5dd20983293a84e3b0de4048da> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>On the handle is a plastically modeled spotted snake, which
      divides at the top to lie along the rim. On the lip of the vessel are white rosettes with red
      centers. The neck, foot, and lower body of the vase are black, while the upper border of the
      figural panel is decorated with a tongue pattern.</P><P>On either side of the main
      scene is a nude youth on a horse, facing towards the center. On the far side of each rider we
      can see the black outline of the warriors' horses, held by the youths. The horses which the
      youths ride are white and wear bridles. Behind each of the youths is a flying
      bird.</P><P>In the center, the two hoplites are fighting. Both wear Corinthian
      plumed helmets, greaves, and body armor. They carry shields and spears. The hoplite on the
      left stands up, lunging forward with his left leg. His shield is held in his left hand so that
      the interior, which has a scale pattern, is visible. He holds his spear up above his head with
      his right arm, and has poked the other warrior in the thigh with it. This warrior has almost
      fallen to his knees, but is still attempting to escape. His body is almost completely hidden
      by his shield, which is decorated with a Gorgon's head. He holds his spear ineffectively in
      his right hand, his back to the viewer as he looks over his shoulder at his attacker. Between
      his legs is the nonsense inscription <foreign lang="Greek">*O*G*Av*R*F</foreign>
      in retrograde. Between the heads of the two warriors is another nonsense inscription
      <foreign lang="Greek">*N*O*P*I*O*E*S</foreign>, also
      retrograde.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/c138254ec7ef599e57efb1492d7ccc95dba6b129822d8d50f799cd0f6f6859c3> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A:</hi> abduction scene, with
      three satyrs one ithyphallic carrying off maenad. On neck: Dionysos and satyr. <hi
      rend="ital">Added red:</hi> satyrs' tails, beards, stripe across brow for hair;
      alternate folds of maenad's sakkos; alternate leaves in god's ivy wreath, stripes on god's
      himation and his beard. <hi rend="ital">Side B:</hi> departure scene(?), with
      youthful charioteer, two warriors and an old man. Neck same as Side A. <hi
      rend="ital">Added red:</hi> horses' manes and tails; daub on lower part of chariot
      car; fillets in hair of old man and charioteer; perimeter of shield, stripe on crest and
      cheekpiece of helmet of warrior near chariot. A red raised fillet marks the junction between
      the vase body and foot. Use of dilute glaze for lower framing edge of tongue patterns; pairs
      of lines in dilute above the zone of rays, above the lotus chain, and above and below the zone
      of meanders; dilute for woman's hear which spills out of the sakkos, at the
      back.</P><P>M. L. Katzev attributed the vase by shape to this Class which is a
      subgroup to the Nikosthenic amphorae of more regular designs, such as the example in the
      exhibition from the Nelson Gallery-Atkins Museum in Kansas City (see <rs
      type="vase">Kansas City 52.22</rs>); it is most closely paralleled in shape by an
      amphora in Paris, <rs type="vase">Louvre F 115</rs> (<bibl>ABV, 319, no.
      4</bibl>). The neck-amphorae brought together by Beazley under the Class of Cabinet des
      Mdailles 218 were painted by divers hands (<bibl>ARV2, 1618</bibl>); concerning
      the attribution of this vase to a specific decorator, Dr. Evelyn Bell wrote (1973, in a letter
      to the Allen Museum), "... a black-figure panel amphora, <rs type="vase">San Simeon
      5482</rs>, seems to be by the same artist." Katzev believed that the artist of the
      Oberlin vase painted two other white-ground neck-amphorae. The first is in Boston, <rs
      type="vase">Boston 01.17</rs> (<bibl>ABV, 319, no. 2</bibl>) which is
      similar not only in style but in iconography, and Beazley names the artist after this vase.
      The second neck-amphora is in the collection of Dr. Peter Ludwig at Aachen which is assigned
      by Lullies to the Leagros Group (<bibl>Lullies 1968, 56-57, no.
      24</bibl>).</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

