<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/c2a6c84a2d2fa8875823be5ee61222fdefb2fb50a0bd714269bde308e6db70c2> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A:</hi> two opposing lions.
      The lions' heads are turned away from each other; the mouths are open with protruding tongues.
      The lions' tails curl elaborately above their backs. In the space between the two animals is
      an incised rosette. The neck is filled with a bearded male head looking left, with squiggles
      to either side.</P><P>Added red is used for the neck, three strokes on the body,
      and one on the flank of each lion, as well as the male's beard and a mark or fillet in his
      hair.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B:</hi> two cocks. The animals are
      confronted, with the stem of a lotus plant branching in two directions between them. The
      branches curve behind the birds' backs and end in pointed buds which tuck up against the
      birds' back feathers. On the neck a male head was represented, with only the base preserved,
      as well as the squiggles to either side.</P><P>Added red appears on the cocks'
      combs, wattles, and bodies, as well as one sickle feather and one saddle feather on each
      bird.</P><P>The outer edge of the rim and a fillet at the base of the neck also
      contain added red, as do a broad band below the main panel and another above the six rays at
      the vessel's base. Short tongues ring the body below the neck-fillet. The rim's flat upper
      surface is reserved. The mouth and neck contain glaze inside to 0.074 m.
      depth.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/c2b1d8b294c31de7fe52930796c7460a69ac75b71794a188133a51ffaf1d4728> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
