<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/ca60f1b0f62e0de09d4c66c9abc5885ae9bc617a1844a725083b57309b34a339> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>On the body of Side A, Dionysos, holding an ivy branch in his
      right hand and a kantharos in his left, moves to the right between a pair of eyes. He wears a
      long chiton, a himation wrapped diagonally over one shoulder, and a wreath. On the shoulder of
      Side A, the center of the field carries a pair of eyes with a nose. Figural scenes then flank
      this central motif. On the left, a seated man is flanked by naked youths. The man is bearded,
      dressed in a long chiton and himation. He wears a fillet in his hair and holds a staff in his
      left hand. The youths both move to the right, with the forward figure looking back over his
      shoulder. On the right, Dionysos is seated between dancing satyrs. He holds a drinking horn in
      his right hand. On the body of Side B, a warrior moving right stands between two youths, with
      the scene again placed between a set of large eyes. The unbearded youths are wrapped in
      himatia and face the warrior. The warrior wears a short chiton, a corslet, a sword at his
      waist, greaves, and a Corinthian helmet with a bit of his beard showing out from the bottom.
      He carries a spear in his right hand and a Boeotian shield on his left arm. On the shoulder of
      Side B, there is again the central motif of eyes and nose, but both flanking scenes are
      fights. On the left, two warriors move to the right, attacking a falling warrior. All the
      warriors wear corslets, short chitons, greaves and Corinthian helmets, and carry a spear and a
      shield. On the right, a single warrior moving to the right attacks a falling warrior. The
      portrayal of the warriors is the same as on the left.</P><P>On the body of the
      vase, the area under the handles is filled with a running satyr carrying an ivy branch in each
      hand. The subsidiary decoration on the vase is fairly elaborate. The scenes on the body of the
      vase are separated from the shoulder by two lines. The shoulder scene is bounded on the top by
      a zone of tongue pattern. The neck is decorated with addorsed palmettes. The lower body is
      decorated with a simple key maeander running left, above a zone of lotus buds, above a zone of
      rays.</P><P>The Antimenes Painter decorated a large body of vessels, especially
      neck-amphorae and hydriae, in the last third of the sixth century. Several features of this
      vase are typical of his work, while certain others make this vase unique. The use of eye
      decoration is most frequently found on cups, but does occur on a small number of other shapes.
      The Antimenes Painter decorated a series of neck amphorae with eyes and Dionysiac scenes.
      Dionysiac scenes are common in the artist's repertoire, and the depiction of a seated Dionysos
      as on the shoulder of Side A was especially favored by the Antimenes Painter. In his depiction
      of Dionysos, the Antimenes Painter uses both the drinking horn and the kantharos, which began
      to replace the drinking horn in the 540s, as an attribute of the god. Also typical of the
      painter's style is the choice and placement of the subsidiary decoration. This Mississippi
      vase is exceptional, however, in that it has eyes on both the body and the shoulder. It
      appears to be a combination of two types of vases decorated by the Antimenes Painter. The
      first is the Dionysos mask between eyes (also with satyrs under the handles) placed on the
      body of the vase, like <rs type="vase">London B 266</rs>. The other type features
      decoration between eyes in an enlarged shoulder zone with the rest of the body glazed, like
      <rs type="vase">Louvre F 202</rs>. One example of this type, <rs
      type="vase">New York 64.11.12</rs>, either by the Antimenes Painter or close to him,
      has the eyes and a nose in the middle and scenes to the side, like the Mississippi vase. The
      Mississippi vase is the only instance of the Antimenes Painter combining these two decorative
      schemes.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

