<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/ce641615b444c35637fb5cc1c3877b7889f3c4265401f254e670f1c4a4b11107> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Bust of youth of Ares Ludovisi type"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/ce66b0ef65bb20ad5a1c08af96c2120f228e47c15a5a1b9b3ee297f06d82d318> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A:</hi> the head of a youthful
      warrior, set between eyes. The warrior faces left; he is drawn in outline with thick forehead
      and nose and thinner nostrils, lips and throat. His right eye and pupil are prominent. He is
      wearing a short-crested Corinthian helmet with a fig leaf set between the horns and ears of a
      steer as the crest. The helmet is pushed back on his head. The garment under his head has six
      alternately red and black vertical stripes. Single incised lines, two of them wavy, mark the
      borders between red and black stripes. The large eyes' pupils are formed by concentric circles
      of black, red, white and black, going from the center to the outer circumference. Beneath each
      handle is a cock facing right, in feeding or fighting position.</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Side B:</hi> the head of a bearded warrior, between eyes. This warrior
      also faces left; he wears a high-crested Corinthian helmet with an inscribed myrtle wreath. As
      his helmet is pulled over his face, only his eye, nostril and beard fringe are visible. His
      eye has the same prominent pupil as the warrior on side A. This warrior's garment has the same
      six alternating red and black stripes as the first warrior, but the incised lines that
      separate the stripes are double and straight. The large eyes on this side are drawn with the
      same added color as the first side. In addition, added red is used for a stripe on this
      warrior's helmet crest, for the combs and wattles on the cocks, and for a wide stripe at the
      base of their wings. Added white appears on the tail feathers of one cock and on dots on the
      body of the other.</P><P>The base of the bowl is encircled by a series of black
      stripes. Between the bowl and the stem there appears a reddened fillet. The side and bottom of
      the foot are reserved. The interior of the stem is glazed toward the
      bottom.</P><P>Much scholarly discussion has been focused on busts appearing on
      Attic vases. Some major sources include <bibl>Von Vacano, Otfried, 1973, Zur Entstehung
      und Deutung gemalter seitenansichtiger Kopfbilder auf schwarzfigurigen Vasen des griechischen
      Festlandes, Bonn, pp. 41 and 233, A-169</bibl>, <bibl>Buschor, 1952, Feldmuse
      (Sitzungsberichte der Bayerischen Akademie), pp. 4 ff.</bibl>, <bibl>Marcad, 1952,
      BCH 76: 596 ff.</bibl>, and <bibl>Schauenburg, 1968, Weltkunst aus Privatbesitz.
      Ausstellung der Klner Museen Kunsthalle 18. Mai-4. August 1968, Cologne, on A 17</bibl>.
      Unlike many cups, the identity of the figures on the Toledo cup is not at all obvious. The
      effect created by these prominent busts placed between large eyes is solemn and monumental,
      rather than the lively character that accompanies identifiable mythological figures in other
      busts.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/ce7044f529edf4afde5612d5bb03e933d05435be1d19c2c9874918d3772fba70> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Knigge 1988, 61, fig. 57</bibl>; <bibl>Richter 1961, 15-16, no. 11, figs. 34-39</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/ce774d2ed76dcbfcb27eab9538e695d295946c4d69742c220bf92df9025e04a7> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Condition"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>There is some discoloration from burning, especially on the
      reverse.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435429> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/ce9109e9cbc9120dd84e5b45b825984ec07bf19164900a54ae6eda6f88faaf80> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>A potters' workshop. The subject is the same on both sides and
      continues under the handles. Under each handle is a bearded potter with his cloak around his
      waist. The potter under handle A/B sits on the base of a herm; the other sits or kneels behind
      the base of a second herm. Both potters are attaching a handle to a large belly amphora,
      possibly of type A judging by the size of the handles and the shape of the
      mouth.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: In the center is a large,
      truncated mound of clay, with a flat top and numerous comma-shaped marks to indicate texture.
      A bearded man with his cloak hitched up around his loins stands in or behind the clay,
      gesturing with his right hand while looking back to the right; he is probably wedging the clay
      by trampling it with his feet. A second bearded man with a cloak around his waist stands at
      left; he is bent over and touching the clay, perhaps working it and removing impurities, or
      possibly filling the basket which lies on its side next to him. Behind and to the right of the
      mound is a herm on a low platform, the latter decorated with a meander in added red. One of
      the potters previously described sits on the back of the platform. A bearded man in a himation
      stands in front of the herm, his head turned back to look at the man wedging the clay, but
      with both arms raised to the face of the herm; possibly he is one of the owners or potters,
      asking the god to protect his fragile pots throughout their manufacture. The bearded herm may
      represent Hermes, or possibly Dionysos, as some of the vines that wander through the
      background of both scenes seem to originate from it.</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Side B</hi>: The scene is similar to side A, being dominated by another
      mound of clay, this one with bands of dense cross-hatching which may indicate a different
      stage of preparation from the clay on the other side. As on Side A, a bearded man stands at
      left, bending over to work the clay; beside him is a basket lying on its side. The cloak
      around his waist is decorated with white dots. In the center, a man stands in or behind the
      mound, carrying a basket on his shoulder; he has either come to dump more clay on the mound or
      to gather some up, as the man at left may be doing. At right is another herm on a low,
      undecorated platform. Most of the herm's head is missing. A bearded man in a himation stands
      with his back to the herm, leaning on a walking stick and gesturing toward the men at left; he
      may be the owner of the workshop. Added red is used for the beards and the hair around the
      faces on all of the men and herms, on both sides; all wear red fillets except for the bending
      man on side A, who has a fillet of added white. White is also used for the fruit or blossoms
      on the vines.</P><P>An ivy vine framed by black stripes circles the rim. A band of
      tongues, alternately black and white, circles the lower body between paired, horizontal
      stripes. A red stripe circles the fillet between foot and body. The handles, foot, and
      interior are black.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

