<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/cf934758c65cdce6f915fc67a477c744fa4851c74b43c9d45979acc910bfb987> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: a man pursues a woman
      toward the left. The beardless man, wearing a short chiton and chlamys and sandals laced up to
      mid-calf, runs left, with his right arm outstretched toward the woman. He wears a wreath with
      leaves, and a petasos hangs down behind his back. His chlamys is fastened at his right
      shoulder by a circular brooch with a dot at its center. He carries two spears horizontally in
      his left hand, the heads of which overlap the woman's body. The woman also runs left, but she
      looks back at her pursuer. She is barefoot and wears a long chiton and himation. Her hair is
      bound in a band, supplemented by a wreath, with an additional white ribbon around the hair
      gathered at the back. As jewelry she wears a plain disk earring and a necklace with a disk at
      its center. She holds the sleeve of her chiton out in front of her with her right hand; her
      left hand is hidden under her himation.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side
      B</hi>: a woman stands facing right. She wears a long chiton, enveloped by a himation.
      She is barefoot, and her hair is bound with three narrow bands.</P><P>The Phiale
      Painter decorated numerous Nolan amphorae and lekythoi with pursuit scenes such as this. The
      Yale vase is unusual, although not unique, in showing the pursuit moving from right to left,
      rather than the reverse. Some of this painter's pursuit scenes are clearly mythological, but
      others, like the Yale vase, lack inscriptions or indisputable identifying attributes or
      costumes that would permit them to be associated securely with a specific myth. Beazley (ARV2,
      1015) proposed that spear-carrying male figures dressed like the Yale man in travelling
      clothes might be Theseus, since two such figures are identified as Theseus by inscription
      (<rs type="vase">Worcester 1903.38</rs> and <rs type="vase">Louvre G
      423</rs>). Sourvinou-Inwood (1987 and 1987a), however, in her recent studies of pursuit
      scenes, concludes that while Theseus may have provided the mythological paradigm for the
      pursuing male in these scenes, the majority of the pursuers represented on vases are simply
      young ephebes of Athens.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

