<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/d00ea73a48d913120101d685e356dda62a4dac374b4073b7524395466c690469> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>A: Themis (labelled), standing 3/4-view to the right, with
      weight on her left leg, wearing a belted, sleeved chiton with shoulder straps, a himation
      draped over her left shoulder and around her lower body, a black necklace, dotted-disk
      earrings, and a spiked crown indicated by three white vertical stripes, wears her hair in a
      long pony tail, with a curled lock on her cheek, bends her arms and holds in her right hand a
      torch to the left, and in her left arm a three-peaked kanoun to the right; Bendis (labelled)
      standing 3/4-view to the left, with weight on her right leg, wears boots, a short sleeveless
      double-chiton with a nebris over her left shoulder, and a Thracian fox-skin cap; a dog,
      standing profile to the left, behind Bendis, advances its right leg.</P><P>B: Dog
      and Kephalos (labelled), shown as a hunter; Artemis (labelled) with
      kanoun.</P><P>This skyphos, earlier presumed to have been Boiotian, has been
      conjectured by Shapiro (225) to have commemorated the arrival of the Bendis cult in Athens. In
      this context, Themis represents the Athenian "Custom" with which Bendis will be revered, e.g.
      with torches and kanouns (her attributes), as are appropriate in all Athenian religious
      festivals.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

