<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/d4a1ab5cf732bb3378951d91aa2be464be410cc280d6028deb92ff92420a827a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Wedding chariot of Peleus and Thetis. A quadriga is seen to the
      right with the bridal couple in a box. Peleus holds the reins and a goad. He wears a tunic and
      himation, the latter with red stripes and white rosettes. He has a red beard and a red fillet.
      Thetis wears a peplos and a himation which goes behind her head; she holds the himation with
      her left hand. She has white skin and a red pupil. The near trace horse has a red chest band
      and red mane. The near pole horse is white with a red mane and tail. Behind the chariot at
      left stands Dionysos holding a drinking horn (keros) and a long staff. He wears a red fillet,
      a himation with red stripes and white rosettes, and has a red beard. Apollo stands behind the
      horses playing the kithara. He wears a tunic, himation with red stripes and white rosettes,
      and a red fillet. To the right of Apollo stands Hermes who walks to right and looks back to
      left. He wears a petasos and a chlamys with red stripes and red and white rosettes. His boots
      have long red tongues. At the head of the horses at right, standing to the left is a goddess,
      probably Aphrodite. She has white skin and wears a peplos, red fillet, necklace, and himation
      with red stripes and white rosettes. She holds a wreath in her lowered right hand. At the head
      of the procession, walking right but looking left, is a red-haired youth holding a long staff
      and wearing a himation with red stripes and white rosettes.</P><P>There is a red
      fillet below the neck and simple black tongues on the upper shoulder. Above are bands of lotus
      buds.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/d4a48d552c259395a94b4038491243647fc18858596fe621c8cf162b550d4f50> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Condition"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<P>Side B is much repaired.</P>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435429> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/d4b47bce1eac29af763773333be3e06f10dc3c207a452907875bcc4629831a39> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>On the right Paris sits, holding a chelys (lyre) on a rocky
      outcrop, rather like a natural throne. He is beardless with long hair and a fringe of curls
      round his forehead. He wears a fillet and a himation which is draped over his arms. At his
      feet a mountain ram faces right, its fleece indicated with arrow-shaped marks. Three goddesses
      enter the scene, approaching him in single file from the left. First and closest to him is
      Hera in a bordered chiton with long sleeves and a himation with a sakkos and earrings on her
      head. She extends an apple to him with her left hand and holds the scepter of authority,
      surmounted with a honeysuckle flower, in her right. Following behind her comes Athena, in a
      long chiton, with her mantle or himation hanging in pteryges (wing-like folds) and her aegis,
      dotted and bordered with a fringe of snakes. On her head she wears a fillet and earrings. Her
      left hand is obscured by Hera, but her right holds a spear. She looks back to the right at
      Aphrodite who approaches last. The goddess of love wears a long chiton, a mantle which
      discreetly covers her arms and the back of her head, and a dotted fillet. Each of the
      goddesses has a wavy loop of hair brought forward over the temples.</P><P>On the
      curved outer surface of the rim there is an egg pattern. The design curves up over the
      shoulder. Above and below the main design there are bands in which two meanders, running to
      the left, alternate with dotted cross squares.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/d4bd70d3253346bffb4d76a214534f8f50274e46b1258b807ede60d769e2a484> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
