<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/d5fe0973b754820d3d36e030ead0044cea52b3b164bfdb2b79297acc5f985fe7> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Side A: wedding. Side B: two women. Side A, neck: woman. Side B, neck: man."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/d5ff82edd604ae70706346e9bb11c65152aaaacef689de271535aa76538c6279> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>A youth wearing a himation and a white fillet is leaning on his
      staff to the right. With his right hand he proffers a small white object. Facing him at right
      is a woman in a chiton and himation, whose hair is tied in a chignon with a white fillet. With
      her right hand she holds out a basket decorated with maeanders. On the ground between the two
      figures is a small chest decorated with dots and, in the center of one side, a star. On top of
      the chest is an egg in added white (not a knob, as Robinson suggested). The object held by the
      youth may therefore also be an egg, but its top is flattened. The giving of eggs, symbols of
      fertility and birth, had a significance beyond the merely culinary, as it still does in
      Greece, where eggs are featured in many ceremonies, particularly at Easter. Eggs are common in
      South Italian vase-painting, particularly Paestan. Robinson's suggestion that the objects may
      be balls is improbable, as the objects played with by some women are actually balls of wool,
      taken not from chests but from the kalathoi that are a nearly invariable feature of such
      scenes; e.g. <rs type="vase">New York 41.162.147</rs> (<bibl>ARV2, 733,
      62</bibl>.</P><P>Above the figures, at the base of the neck, is a band of
      tongues. The groundline is a band of egg pattern with dotted interstices. The side of the foot
      is reserved, and there is a very narrow reserved stripe around the inner edge of the mouth.
      The rest of the vase is black.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/d60419cedfec39fe71c1e8cc10706c60319be5305ae1015152e237e169f87ff6> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<P><bibl>Olynthus, 8, 97-98</bibl>; other Olynthus volumes; unpublished excavation notes.</P>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/d60d3b11dcd25fb4b7dad935b1df08867edaf6e119ec3247287fb5ee688287c6> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Figures: on each side of the neck, a winged figure (r.); on the
      upper shoulder, both sides, a pair of boxes with seated judges facing; main zone, maenads and
      satyrs (primarily l.) with meander below and lotus-bud frieze above. Palmettes and lotus-buds
      on the handle and lotus-bud chain around top. <hi rend="ital">Added red:</hi>
      beards of satyrs and judges, maenads' jackets and elsewhere on lower garment, palmette fans,
      judges' robes. <hi rend="ital">Added white:</hi> female flesh, dot on one
      athlete's thigh, lotus-buds on handles, and dots in series in
      palmettes.</P><P>This vase, another very much like it in Cleveland (illustrated
      and briefly discussed: <bibl>Arias & Hirmer 1962, pl. XIII and p. 294</bibl>),
      and a white-ground sub-type amphora at Oberlin College (see <rs type="vase">Oberlin
      70.11</rs>) are fine examples of this curious type Beazley attributed this vase to
      Painter "N" (<bibl>ABV, 219, no. 23</bibl>) and, indeed, the decoration on the one
      hundred or more preserved amphorae may be largely this painter's work. "Thiasos Group" is a
      classification for "vases with a picture of satyrs and maenads below the mid band." Although
      made in Athens, it is almost certain that the Nikosthenic amphora was never meant for a Greek
      clientele but, like the kyathos (see <rs type="vase">Gilson Collection (Moon No.
      69)</rs>), it was export ware for the Etruscans. Most of the amphorae of known
      provenience were found in Etruria at Caere, and the schemes used for decoration, though in
      myriad combinations, are routine and largely without much iconographic interest or complexity.
      The decoration is plentiful and garish on most, charming and calculated on a few, particularly
      those in the Six technique (see <rs type="vase">Columbia 58.12</rs>), for example
      <rs type="vase">Louvre F 114</rs> (<bibl>ABV, 226</bibl>). It is
      thought that the Six technique itself was formulated in this same workshop. Scenes of boxers,
      with caldrons and tripods for the victor, are as common on these amphorae as they were popular
      in Etruscan tomb painting, for example, in The Tomb of the Augurs, and The Tomb of the
      Monkeys.</P><P>Although the main frieze easily places this vase into a larger
      group, there are unusual features. The elaborate floral patterns (also on the Cleveland
      amphora) instead of figures (see <bibl>Moon 1979, No. 39</bibl>) on the handle are
      unusual, and the winged female on the neck of the vase appears infrequently. M. Eisman
      suggests a date between 535-520 B.C. for the Thiasos Group, and he finds it closely related to
      Chalcidian amphorae (see <bibl>Moon 1979, No. 42</bibl>) and to early examples of
      kyathoi (<bibl>"Nikosthenic Amphorai: The J. Paul Getty Museum Amphora," <hi
      rend="ital">The J. Paul Getty Museum Journal</hi> 1 [1974], nn. 7 and 8</bibl>;
      see also pp. 52-54 for an overview of the Nikosthenic workshop).</P><P>Various
      stylistic prototypes for this shape have been suggested. The flat handles, raised ribs, and
      imitation rivets at the handles indicate metal vases (<bibl>H.R.W. Smith 1932,
      101</bibl>); the conoid neck would suggest an Italian prototype, from the Villanovans
      and Etruscans. In fact, a bucchero amphora in New York,<rs type="vase">New York GR
      972</rs>, may be the type of Italic vase which directly influenced the Attic production
      (<bibl>Gallatin 1926, 76-78</bibl>). The Italic or Etruscan forerunners for the
      shape whether metal or ceramic bucchero imitating metal were not painted; the decorative
      motives one sees on the amphorae are Greek, flamboyant and exaggerated, designed to appeal to
      another side of Etruscan or Caeretan taste.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/d6111388ab7964d91a94af706c9af60ae8d6890cdde1d384dbd69626d345e7e9> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Interior</hi>: The tondo is filled
      by a large cock, standing to right, with a red comb and red tail feathers (there may have been
      additional added color, but most of the glaze has worn off). In the space at right is an
      undulating snake, its head near that of the cock. In the space above the cock is a stylized
      flower, probably a lotus. The cock symbolizes male pugnacity and fertility. The snake is a
      symbol of fertility and good luck, but in this case it may also be the cock's
      dinner.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Exterior</hi>: A continuous frieze of
      lions walk from right to left. Their long tails are alternately curled beneath them or over
      their backs, but otherwise they are nearly identical, with open, roaring mouths and added red
      on the bellies and manes; one lion also has a red tongue. Large black dots are placed
      throughout the frieze as fillers.</P><P>The tondo is framed by four stripes of
      varying width, with another reserved stripe farther out in the bowl. The handles, and foot are
      black; the lip is black except for a reserved stripe just inside the rim. The grooved area at
      the top of the stem is reserved except for a black stripe. In the handle zone on either side
      is a band of upright lotus buds, with dots in the interstices and a row of dots below. The
      lotuses are framed above by two narrow stripes and below by a broader one; below the latter,
      and above the lions, is a broad red stripe framed by groups of slender black stripes. The
      lions stand on a thick black stripe, and below this is another such stripe framed by groups of
      slender stripes.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/d625428213e3917bd5f1257c5f39a129d3642a5eebacd6f67a74dfea1e576ac1> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
