<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/dc9194a7ee57a7bfddc86033b51d2743c7dcf4ffa6923d7029d5c4a15eb58d16> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl><hi rend=\"ital\">BMCRE</hi></bibl> I, 112-3, no. 694-695, pl. C.33; <bibl><hi rend=\"ital\">RIC</hi></bibl> I, 11" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/dc923730079d3c10240c522283df322f3e29cfa76787ad51425172f1449e7628> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The edge of the lip has an egg and dot pattern between narrow
      reserved stripes, with yellowish white centers. The underside of the mouth on side A is
      decorated with laurel and dot clusters and a central rosette; on side B there are rosettes
      with white tipped petals and dot clusters.</P><P>The neck on side A has an egg and
      dot pattern, with yellowish white centers between narrow reserved relief bands. Under this
      there are alternating white, yellow and black lozenges, a white scroll on a black band between
      four narrow reserved stripes, a yellow zigzag against a black background, a yellow bead and
      reel on a black relief band, the same egg and dot pattern as above, a white swastika meander
      interspersed with yellow hollow squares with central dots on a black band between reserved
      stripes, white rays against black between two pairs of reserved stripes, white-centered
      rosettes with white-tipped petals, dot clusters above two narrow reserved stripes, a winged
      female figure (to be described below), and eggs between reserved
      stripes.</P><P>The neck on side B has the same egg pattern as on side A, Eros
      depicted between two reserved stripes (to be described below), a yellow meander on a narrow
      black band, white dots on a black relief band, a white zigzag on a black band, white-centered
      eggs between two pairs of reserved stripes, tongues, rosettes and dot clusters between two
      pairs of reserved stripes, the head of a woman looking left (to be described below) appearing
      from a campanula in an elaborate leaf setting, tendril spirals, and rosettes above reserved
      bands and black lines.</P><P>The shoulder is decorated with black gadroons. On
      both sides of the body, above the scenes is an egg and dot pattern, and beneath are a meander
      interrupted by crossed squares with dots and black gadroons. The handles are white bordered,
      with white diamonds in the center of the buds that form the handle terminations. Under both
      handles are two enclosed palmette fans superposed with a palmette fan above, as well as
      smaller palmette fans and side scrolls. A reserved band circles the vessel where the body and
      foot join. The underside of the foot is reserved.</P><P>On side A, a mourning
      Niobe is pictured in the upper register and Pelops and Hippodamia in the lower register. At
      the center of the upper register is a tomb monument; within it Niobe standing semi-frontally,
      in a mourning pose. Her head is inclined to the right, and she supports it with her left hand.
      She is wearing a peplos and a cloak pulled up over her and right arm. With her left hand she
      holds part of it. She has a necklace with a single white pendant and a bracelet. Her lower
      dress is in added white, typical of many scenes of Niobe before petrification. Four columns
      with white Ionic capitals support the tomb monument. The shafts are white with dilute glaze
      flutes, and the pediment has white floral akroteria and a black gable with a disk and a small
      floral pattern in white. The ceiling beams are reserved in white with white outlines. Niobe is
      standing between two white loutrophoroi with dilute glaze details. The lefthand vessel is of
      Type I with an ovoid body, while the righthand one is of Type II with a barrel shape. Both
      have lids and s-shaped handles. On either side of the monument two attendants wear
      short-sleeved chitons with single stripes down the front. They are also wearing mantles,
      stephanai, ribboned kekryphaloi, earrings, bracelets, double strand necklaces, and white
      shoes. The far lefthand woman is standing three-quarters right; her left hand reaches for the
      drapery on her shoulder, as does her neighbor, standing three-quarters left. The two women to
      the right of the monument stand facing each other, the first holding a phiale with spray and
      dot cluster. The second has a wreath in her right hand. Above them on a white dotted ground
      line are a white lyre and tall kalathos with a ball of wool on top.</P><P>In the
      lower register, Pelops and Hippodamia are riding left in a quadriga. Pelops is in Oriental
      dress, with long white-dotted purple sleeves and circles and rays as decoration, a Phrygian
      cap decorated in added white, two shoulder cords and a white-studded belt. Hippodamia wears
      the same jewelry as the others, a tall crown with a row of white beads on top and yellow and
      white bands and black dots on the bottom. Her sleeveless, beltless chiton has white
      dot-clusters as decoration, and she is holding part of her cloak in her left hand. The horses'
      lower hind legs and hoofs are white, as are the forelegs of the righthand trace horse. The
      bridles are dotted with white, and the reins are in added red, also used for the chariot rail
      and body. The chariot wheels and axle are white.</P><P>The neck on side A has the
      upper half of a winged female in added white and yellow. She emerges from an elaborate floral
      setting, wearing a crown with a row of white beads at the top, a necklace, a dilute glazed
      double bandolier, and bracelets. Her hair is in shades of dilute glaze, as are her facial
      features, and her wing details. She is holding the ends of the tendrils of the flower that is
      under her.</P><P>On side B, both upper and lower register belong to the same
      scene, five women at a shrine. Each woman wears a short-sleeved chiton with a ribbon and a
      single stripe down the front, a stephane, a ribboned kekryphalos, a double-stranded bead
      necklace, a double-coil bracelet on each arm, and shoes. The upper righthand woman also has a
      himation diagonally draped. In the upper register are two women standing on either side of a
      naiskos. Inside it are a white marble lekythos and two fillets. The upper lefthand woman holds
      a mirror in her right hand and leans her elbow on a dotted support. The righthand woman is
      holding a patera in her left hand and a cista in her right, decorated with white zigzag and
      meander patterns and a small spray on top. In the lower register are the other three women.
      The one on the left is holding an open box in her right hand. Her body is seated pointing
      left, but her head looks right at the center woman who looks back at the lefthand one, and
      rests her right hand on her shoulder. She is holding a mirror in her left hand. On the right,
      the woman stands facing left, leaning forward with raised right leg and right foot resting on
      a rock. In her right hand is a wreath, and a ball of wool hangs by a ribbon from her left. On
      either side of the naiskos above are two ribboned fillets with white edges, while below is an
      open box, phiale, and flowering plant.</P><P>On the neck of side B, Eros stands
      above facing left, with a mirror in his right hand and a white fillet in his left. He is
      wearing a stephane, a kekryphalos and hair ribbon, a double-stranded necklace, a bandolier,
      thigh and ankle bracelets, and shoes. The edges and other details on his wings are in added
      white. In front of him are a fillet, a rosette, a branch, and a small plant. Behind is a
      flowering plant. On the lower part of the neck is the head of a woman with a left profile. She
      is wearing a kekryphalos with a bunch of hair emerging, a white stephane, a white earring, and
      a white beaded necklace.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/dc93b29cc14a8b8cfa7845080272c4e7f3488f5f7d90d552951aacb80b98c384> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Three young warriors
      arming. The one at left has finished; he stands frontally but looks toward the warrior in the
      center, who stoops to the left to pick up his spear. The one at left holds a spear and wears a
      helmet, a short kilt, and a cloak draped over each arm. His legs are concealed by his round
      shield, which he rests on the ground; the shield-device is a snake. The warrior at center
      wears greaves and a helmet. His torso is concealed by his shield, which has a fringed leather
      apron and a kantharos device. The helmets of both warriors have raised cheek-flaps, but they
      cannot be called Attic helmets, as they lack visors and the nape-guard is not clearly set off
      from the bowl. A drop of glaze smears the face of the middle warrior. At right, a third
      warrior has only begun to don his armor. He stands in profile to the right, his left leg
      raised as he prepares to place on it the greave he holds in both hands. On the ground before
      him lies his shield, with the second greave on top of it. The shield has two dots on either
      side of a panther head, only half of which is visible). There is a cloth band around the
      youth's head and his sword hangs at his hip in its striped sheath (the baldric is incised, but
      not drawn).</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: A fight, with two
      warriors attacking a third, who faces them at right. All three carry spears and round shields.
      Only the shield device of the right-hand warrior is visible, a bull's head. That warrior wears
      a cuirass, chitoniskos, and Attic helmet and has a sword in a striped scabbard on his hip.
      Behind him is a tall, looming rock which extends beneath the handle; the warrior is not only
      outnumbered, his "back is against the wall." He nonetheless assumes a confident stance, with
      his spear held high and his shield on his outstretched left arm. His opponent in the center
      reels backwards, his spear held low and his shield turned aside. He wears a chitoniskos,
      cuirass, greaves, Attic helmet, and a spotted chlamys with a black border. At far left, a
      third warrior rushes to his comrade's aid, his shield held out in proper fashion. He is
      dressed differently from the other two, with a bare torso, fringed kilt, and what appears to
      be a Phrygian helmet. A sword hangs at his hip. The kilt has a pattern of dotted crosshatching
      in dilute glaze.</P><P>The two sides are loosely related, with the preparations
      for battle on one side and the actual combat on the other. On Attic vases, the victor is
      normally represented attacking from left to right, but in this case the outnumbered warrior at
      the right seems to be winning. It may be that the warrior at far left is not on the side of
      the hoplite at center, but rather that of the right-hand warrior, a composition not without
      parallels.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Tondo</hi>: Within a circular frame
      of stopt maeanders to left, a warrior stands with his body frontal, his legs crossed, and his
      head turned down to the right. He holds a spear in his right hand and rests his left hand on
      his shield, which sits on the ground; the shield device is a scorpion. He wears an Attic
      helmet and a short kilt with dotted crosshatching in dilute glaze, which is also used to
      render the hair on the belly. The kilt has a scalloped edge like that of the shield apron on
      Side A. Like those on Side A, the shield is tinted with dilute glaze and its outline incised
      with a compass.</P><P>The exterior groundline consists of a pair of reserved
      stripes. There is a single reserved stripe on the rim. The inside of the handles and the space
      between the handle roots are reserved, as is the side of the foot. A broad black band circles
      the reserved underside of the foot.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/dc94eb3d5ab13660027a4a52ac35a43750ee0b10893186bf639e35610172ea6a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Condition"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<P>Black glaze occasionally fired mottled-red.</P>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435429> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/dca72be043422e3fb28c160c96e0ecb2964bd27f717778e4305dfc49b75db869> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Pedley 1976, 47</bibl>; <bibl>Freyer-Schauenburg 1974, 27-31 no. 7, pls. 7-8 (with additional bibliography)</bibl>; <bibl>Richter 1968, 46 no. 56, figs. 186-89</bibl>; <bibl>Blmel 1964, no. 34</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/dca801dc6e2fd9226d90f8418db74ef0726c2c6df7e939ed267ec2aa48b17392> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Aphrodite, Adonis, and personifications"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/dcaf83d04f6405bc7b6c17988cc848c4d536ccbc6fb7c12400990367c4e5efd3> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>There is pattern work on both sides of the mouth, neck and
      shoulder; only the handle zones interrupt it. On the edge of the lip is an egg-and-dot
      pattern, while the underside of the mouth has a laurel wreath running to the left. On the
      neck, a reserved relief band is decorated with short broad black strokes, and enclosed upright
      palmettes fill a black relief band between reserved stripes. The shoulder bears a tongue
      pattern divided by narrow black lines ending in a dot. A meander band interspersed with
      crossed squares with a stroke in each corner runs around the body under the scenes. On the
      handles, black ivy decorates the reserved outer edge of the volutes, and under the handles are
      two superposed palmettes with side scrolls branching out from the core. At the root of each
      handle is a small enclosed palmette fan.</P><P>On side A, Andromeda is pictured.
      On the left, a naked youth with a black-bordered chlamys fastened at his neck ties Andromeda's
      right arm to a cut-off tree trunk. The texture of the tree trunk, from which the two posts
      were cut, is shown in dilute glaze. Andromeda stands in three-quarter left profile, with her
      head fully turned. She is wearing a sleeveless chiton with a double dot stripe running down
      the front, as well as a plain double stripe on the side, and a crown decorated with a band of
      short vertical lines on the top edge and a lotus-palmette pattern beneath. Her curly,
      shoulder-length hair is partially covered by a dotted, fringed veil. On her feet are open
      sandals. She has a black earring and a pendant necklace. Her braceleted left arm, not yet
      bound, rests against the second post. Resting on the groundline between the youth and
      Andromeda is a chest, its front leg an animal foot. Dicing decorates the edge of its lid,
      while the body is covered with a tendril and circles and a swastika meander underneath. On top
      of the box is a kalathos, its structure indicated by straight lines and cross hatches, with a
      meander pattern above. A mirror hangs in the field behind Andromeda's
      head.</P><P>Perseus and Cepheus fill the other half of the scene. Perseus is
      wearing a bordered chlamys with a brooch fastening it at the neck, a winged Phrygian helmet
      with side flaps, and a winged shoe on his right foot (the left foot not seen). He is holding
      two spears in his left hand, and with his right he grasps Cepheus's right hand. Cepheus is
      dressed in a dot-patterned chiton and a broad belt covered with rosettes and squares divided
      by diagonal lines, with a dot in each segment. He is seated with a black-bordered cloak under
      him and around his knees, and he holds a lotus-tipped scepter in his left hand. His hair and
      beard are in added white, part of which is flaked off.</P><P>On the neck of side
      A, a lion faces right with his mane in relief and his mouth open. He is confronting a bull. On
      the far right, a bearded, balding satyr sits facing left on a rock, holding a thyrsos in his
      right hand.</P><P>In the main scene of side B are two youths and a woman. The
      barefooted woman at far left wears a sleeveless chiton with a double stripe down the front and
      a belt at the waist. She also has a headband, an earring, and a pendant necklace, and she
      stands three quarters to the right. A leopard with raised left foreleg is on her outstretched
      left arm. Standing in the center of the scene, looking toward the woman, are two youths. The
      first leans on a staff and wears a cloak that partially covers him, crossing his lower torso
      and passing over his left shoulder. He holds a strigil in his right hand. The second youth is
      naked, but he too has a cloak wrapped around his left arm and falling behind him. He appears
      to be pointing with his right arm at the leopard. On the far right another woman stands,
      dressed similarly to the first, except that the double stripe on her garment runs down the
      side, and she wears shoes. She checks her appearance in a mirror held in her right hand, as
      she uses her left hand to place a fillet around her head.</P><P>On the neck of
      side B, two dogs are pursuing a hare; all three figures run to the
      left.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

