<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/dd9c33cd3aed336fbf49a023ab3577bd514baaa96bd54cb8e4bff3f13b0aa657> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Bruneau 1983, 61</bibl>; <bibl>Richter 1970b, 105 no. 110, figs. 341, 344</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/dd9e9a9c74b22156f2e1b36ee11ed4816bb6655dfbe7d1501ec11be2d9284a79> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Side A:</hi> Dionysos reclining,
      with maenad and satyr. This side of the vase is decorated in red-figure technique. Dionysos is
      in the center of the composition facing left, flanked by a dancing maenad on the left and a
      dancing satyr on the right. Dionysos reclines on a kline, resting with his left elbow propped
      on a pillow. The god is bearded and wears an ivy wreath in his hair. His legs are wrapped in a
      himation, the end of which falls across his left elbow. Dionysos gestures toward the maenad
      with his right hand and holds a kantharos in his left. An ivy branch grows from behind the god
      and curves to the left. The maenad, wearing a chiton, dances and holds krotala. The maenad's
      chiton is belted at the waist with one overfold, and the fastenings on the shoulder of the
      garment are depicted. Her hair is bound by a fillet The satyr also dances, moving to the
      right, but looking back over his shoulder. The satyr is naked and bearded. His non-human
      nature is shown by a thick horse tail, pointed ears, and a snub nose. All the figures are
      labelled with inscriptions. The kline on which Dionysos reclines is equipped with a mattress
      and a cover as well as a pillow. The preserved end of the kline has very ornate decoration on
      the leg. A table would have stood in front of the kline, but only one leg with a lion's paw
      foot is preserved.</P><P>The scene of Dionysos reclining begins in this period.
      There are two other contemporary examples of the scene preserved by painters of bilinguals.
      The scene with Dionysos appears to derive from representations of Herakles reclining, which
      become popular shortly after the middle of the century.</P><P><hi
      rend="head">Side B:</hi> Chariot of Herakles. This side of the vase is decorated in
      black-figure technique. Herakles stands beside a four-horse chariot which Iolaos mounts as the
      driver, while Hermes holds the horses. Herakles, Iolaos and the chariot face to the right,
      while Hermes turns to the left. At the left of the composition, Iolaos is in the act of
      mounting the chariot. His left foot is in the car and the right is just leaving the ground.
      Iolaos wears a cuirass over a chitoniskos. He also wears an Attic helmet with a tall crest,
      which breaks the frame, and a sword at his left side. Psiax appears to have been the first
      vase-painter to depict a male warrior with an Attic helmet, reserved until this point for
      Athena and for Amazons. In his right hand he holds a kentron. Herakles stands behind the car
      of the chariot. He wears his lionskin, with the head up, belted over a chitoniskos. His sword
      and quiver hang at his left side and he holds his club in his right hand. Hermes' face and
      upper body are hidden by the horses. Hermes wears a chitoniskos with a mantle over his
      shoulders. He is clearly identified by the kerykeion, which he holds in his left hand, the
      folded-brim petasos, and the winged boots. All the figures are labelled by
      dipinti.</P><P>This scene of the chariot of Herakles is related to scenes of
      Herakles and Athena in a chariot, which are popular in the late sixth century and interpreted
      as the Apotheosis of Herakles. Although the scene on this vase has been identified by Cohen as
      an apotheosis, it is better to interpret scenes such as this, in which Athena is not the
      driver of the chariot in a more general context.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/dda8b602a99630c191fe3844f6d91af047944776e3b57f7f58b712133a8758a3> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Swing (a woman swinging another). On the left, a woman wearing
      a chiton and sakkos, her mantle pulled down around her waist, stoops to push the girl on the
      swing. The girl swings forward, her hair and chiton flying. Her mantle is draped over her lap.
      The swing has four legs, three of which are visible. A large, close-mouthed vessel (?) is sunk
      into the ground between the two women, and a basket stands on the far left, behind the woman
      pushing the swing. A festoon hangs over her head.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/dda8d3e768732571cdd07e26aef69eaeccad25682785df581af79b983339edf5> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Rectangular building; outside the Sanctuary of Zeus (Altis), west of the Temple of Zeus, at the southeast corner of the Theokoleon."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/ddb13b65d170b6422640045d319c7b1476ee16fa260998cc5db49798e2abd9d7> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Lewes 1920</bibl>, no. 121" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

