<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/de53cd9e81db514bb90e07f8fc8886fd04058ef61a3a9837402d799a3e6bba7a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Triton with human body, fish tail"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/de622384c7b081c0e7bbea3fca23a26927a36efc9654ae47d498a21da24a8777> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>LIMC, 3 (Eros 83) pl. 100.2</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/de76020c74d061ba2030061ce55d84bbe876d8b91148fb8262fbe34f9571b28f> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>There is a figured scene on the shoulder zone between the
      handles. <hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: At left, a standing woman is pictured, with
      shoulder-length hair, a long skirt rendered in outline filled with crosshatching; she is
      holding a probable wreath. The woman is grasped at her left wrist by a man looking back at her
      while proceeding right toward the rear of a boat with two tiers of oarsmen. At the front of
      the boat is a long prow. A bird at far right is used as a filling ornament. There are dots at
      the rim. The lower body has a row of 'S's and horizontal banding. The base is solidly
      painted.</P><P>Side A has been interpreted as a mythological scene: Theseus and
      Ariadne, Paris and Helen, or Odysseus and Penelope are possibilities. This krater is the first
      representation of two rows of oarsmen on a ship. The woman is a prototype of later Geometric
      mourners and dancers. Row of 'S's and horizontal banding suggest the influence of Corinthian
      vase-painting.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/de76044e1dd3bdce8653078492e0ea9df65c71216305e659d2507ed1a9e19f4e> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "'Amphitrite' from Athena's Strife with Poseidon"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/de87375ffed015f17e1d807daf2ef3d990f0efd3ce6b5797e140fcc9b190403d> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<P><bibl>Blmel 1966, no. 69</bibl></P>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/de9cfad462995e9798f9bdef0505e83308e76cc5e62cee8f086e63af106f53dc> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The clay is a pale dull red, the decoration in slightly
      lustrous glaze, mostly red. The body of the vessel is high and rounded, with a high slightly
      concave neck and no lip. A strap handle rises from the upper edge and returns close to the
      neck to which it is attached with a bar set midway. On the neck is a large latticed meander,
      above which is a zone of water-birds with hatched bodies to the left, separated by latticed
      lozenges. Below the meander is a zone of similar birds separated by dotted circles. On the
      shoulder is a zone of metopes separated by groups of vertical lines about a hatched band or in
      one instance a row of dotted circles. In the metopes are water-birds to the right with field
      ornaments (swastika, dotted circle, latticed lozenge, latticed triangle) alternating with four
      rosettes of latticed leaves and latticed triangles between the leaves. On the body is a zone
      of opposed latticed triangles, below that a zone of water-birds to the left, then a band of
      large dots connected by diagonal lines, and finally a band of smaller dots, all separated by
      groups of horizontal lines. The lower part of the body is solidly covered with glaze. On the
      back of the handle, groups of transverse lines are separated by diagonal crosses. The cover,
      which may not belong to the vase, is nearly flat and has a flat-topped knob. On top of the
      knob is a circle divided into eighths. On the sides of the knob and top of the cover are
      encircling lines and bands of zigzag and dots, and at the circumference is a band of latticed
      triangles pointing in.</P><P>This vessel comes from a small group of related
      vases: <rs type="vase">Nauplia 4026</rs>, <rs type="vase">Bremen E
      471</rs>, and <rs type="vase">Athens, Kerameikos 821</rs>, which Coldstream
      (71) identifies as "An undistinguished group of pitchers, probably all from the same hand."
      The designs are still dominated by leaves and swan-like birds, but they are now more tightly
      packed into narrower zones. The leaves are no longer outlined, nor do they have filling
      ornament. The hatching is in one direction only.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/deb3dbd3f667bce58a6ccb29979e4640db6a83bf2c42a78880d42b10175ceabb> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
