<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e094cb50682eebdb10e488f3080a6f37bfdb664d7ce5c73a44c14ef4a635fe1c> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Painted stele of youth with palmette finial"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e09e64c40a96522de101d2f44ea5f41775ae210eb2a810e61d2096221c051663> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>On the body of the vase, a woman and a youth are depicted on
      either side of a tomb. The woman approaches the tomb from the left, carrying an offering
      wrapped in a cloth. She wears a belted chiton and a scarf in her hair. The figure is rendered
      completely in outline except for her hair painted in solid brown and the scarf in purple. The
      cloth covering the offering which the woman carries still bears traces of green paint and the
      hem is purple. The tomb has a stele topped with a palmette finial, and the stele is bound with
      red fillets. The youth stands to the right of the tomb with his right hand on his hip, wrapped
      in a himation which shows traces of dark paint. A wreath hangs in the field behind his head.
      This scene at the tomb is surmounted by a grayish meander enclosed in red lines. The
      decoration on the body does not encircle the vase but stops at the sides. The mouth, neck,
      handle, and lower body of the vase are glazed. The top of the foot is glazed while the sides
      are reserved. The shoulder is decorated with palmettes.</P><P>The white ground
      technique, which first becomes popular in the experimental stage of Attic vase painting in the
      520's, consists of covering the natural reddish ground of the clay with a thin white "slip" of
      ultra-refined clay, which remains white after firing. In the earliest usage of this white
      ground technique, decoration in the standard black-figure technique of silhouette with
      incision and added color would then be placed against this lighter background. By the early
      fifth century, many painters switch to an outline technique. As can be seen on this lekythos,
      artists using this technique enliven figures drawn in outline with areas of solid added color.
      From its inception until the mid fifth century, white ground is used primarily for small
      vases, such as lekythoi, pyxides, and alabastra, and for small areas of larger vases. After
      the middle of the fifth century, white ground is used only rarely for vases other than
      lekythoi. With these white ground lekythoi of the second half of the fifth century, there is
      also a change in iconography; the subjects depicted now being almost exclusively
      funerary.</P><P>The iconography of this vase is fairly standard for late fifth
      century funerary lekythoi. The most common scene found on such vases is the visit to the tomb,
      and the most frequently depicted visitor is a young woman. The youth on the right of the stele
      may be a male relative accompanying the woman, or he may represent the dead man
      himself.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

