<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e16dec9820a72e8cbb9dfecceb1cd08a8f7a997086aa929d5527c913960b3e67> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<P><bibl>Comstock & Vermeule 1971, no. 7</bibl></P>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e16e12b8632e9eb2c2f635a8f9d9766811c0f0a16b91ff66bf708ccdae404d48> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: The poet Mousaios and
      the Muses Terpsichore and Melousa. Mousaios stands to right, he is laurelled and beardless,
      wearing a mantle which leaves his right arm and shoulder free. His right hand rests on a
      straight laurel branch, and in his left he holds a tortoiseshell lyre. Above his head his name
      is inscribed <foreign lang="Greek">*M*O*S*A*I*O[*S]</foreign>. Terpsichore is
      seated on a chair, and plays on a fourteen-stringed harp. Her head is inclined toward the harp
      in a three-quarter view. She wears a diadem in her hair, and a chiton and peplos. Over her
      head is the inscription <foreign lang="Greek">*T*E*R*Y*I*X*O*R*A</foreign>. On the
      left stands Melousa, fitting a mouthpiece to her aulos (flute). Her hair is covered by a cap
      surmounted by a topknot, and she wears a chiton. In front of her face hangs a kithara, while
      above her head is the inscription <foreign
      lang="Greek">*M*E*L*E*L*O*S*A</foreign>. <hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: A
      female figure standing between two youths. The female's head is covered by a linen veil, and
      she wears a chiton and peplos. She stretches out her right hand to the youth on the left, as
      if addressing him. Both male figures are beardless, wear mantles, and hold staffs in their
      right hands. The one on the left wears a wreath.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e188a8a059a1151a3a2494ef83b95cbd1cb1ac979be80331022b56e71c991900> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Comstock & Vermeule 1971</bibl>, 149 no. 174" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e1902a9a0973ca91518b82c30f8c4023d4b273cce597c444f782843b30a871f3> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Hermary 1984, 9-11 no. 6, pls. 5.3-4 and 6 (with previous bibliography)</bibl>; <bibl>Bruneau 1983, 68</bibl>; <bibl>Zaphiropoulou 1983, 53</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e1948cf1bdae18d5e463f991c6b9e3bd74d10e494853f6b4f06b52bbf58f00df> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>On the shoulder of the vessel is an egg-pattern and palmettes,
      under which is a meander. In the center of the main scene is a slender stele set on a base
      with two steps; near the top above an egg moulding is drawn a Corinthian helmet with a long
      plume. Above the shaft and the base are several fillets, painted red. The eyeholes of the
      helmet are painted red. Before the stele, two nude winged figures, probably Thanatos and
      Hypnos (Death and Sleep), are bringing a dead warrior to the tomb. The figure at the left
      bends over, holding the legs of the dead person. On the body of the former are red marks
      (plumage?). He has straight hair, a curly beard, and wears a wreath. The youthful standing
      figure on the right is painted red, and he holds the dead person from behind, under the arms.
      His hair is curly. The dead youth wears a cuirass, painted with ornaments in detail, and a
      small red mantle which hangs over both arms from behind. His hair is curly and rather long.
      The whole scene is drawn in fine lines of light brown glaze, except for the wings which are
      added in coarser lines of dull black. There are some traces of the preliminary sketch with a
      dull point.</P><P>"The scene itself is most poetically conceived, no doubt
      carrying out the suggestion of the scene in the Iliad (16. 671) where Thanatos and Hypnos
      carry the body of Sarpedon to Lycia for burial" (Fairbanks, 258).</P><P>There are
      two similar lekythoi by the Thanatos Painter <rs type="vase">Munich 2777</rs> and
      <rs type="vase">Berlin F 3160</rs>. Hesiod (<bibl n="Hes. Th. 763 ff.">Hes.
      Th. 763 ff.</bibl>) and Pausanias (<bibl n="Paus. 5.18.1">Paus.
      5.18.1</bibl>) both give descriptions of the brothers Sleep and Death (<bibl>Kurtz
      1975, 211</bibl>).</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e1a1b94d516013dc00f38b10a550d3361fd886d99bb8d632a32ffff98bdaf392> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><bibl>Burn 1987, 99 M 20, pls. 11, 12a-b</bibl>;
      <bibl>LIMC, 3.21 s.v. Attike no. 5, pl. 14 (G. Berger-Doer)</bibl>;
      <bibl>Neils 1983, 274-89, figs. 8-9, 13-16; "The Cleveland Museum of Art, 1983: Year in
      Review for 1982," BullClevelandMus 70 (1983) 5 fig. 1</bibl>.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e1ab35c8118e35af4403a84a44f585be628a02c1d815d6cdd7913fb20be416ec> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Pegasos flies to right, above a built structure representing
      Peirene Spring. The figure at left is of Bellerophon holding a tether. The added white of the
      wings is eroded.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e1afa46092d2257bd20b1a4c3634347feea5c55e07d9cb3578a97db8871f38c0> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<P><bibl>Moon 1979, pp. 86-87</bibl></P>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e1b963a035cf68c3311a808d0d53f8ca7147e7440c473bcf5c5e305c9fe4df92> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: A woman rushes towards
      a statue of a nude youth, while a man grabs her from behind by her hair. The statue is a
      typical <hi rend="ital">kouros</hi> type; a nude standing youth with his arms by
      his sides, probably in this case depicting Apollo. It is roughly two-thirds life-size, and is
      placed on a platform. The woman runs up to it, almost seeming to trip over the platform. Her
      arms are extended to grasp at it, while her head is being pulled back by the tug at her hair.
      She wears a belted chiton with a cloak, and her hair is loose and flowing over her shoulders.
      The man behind her is a warrior; he wears a crested helmet and greaves (the middle part of his
      body is missing), and carries a spear and shield with a wolf (or lion?) shield device. He
      steps up on the platform with his right foot and grasps the woman's hair with his right hand.
      He appears to be gritting his teeth, and looks rather ferocious.</P><P>This scene
      has been interpreted either as Helen seized by Menelaos (Boardman, 61) or the rape of
      Kassandra by Ajax (Simon, 123).</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: A
      draped, bearded man stands, holding a staff.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

