<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e2a3fbc519aed6b03f97a8d06d5107bfecedc850c4d41e150b0ea7f937c53890> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Side A</hi>: birth of Athena.
      Athena springs from the head of Zeus, who is seated on a stool in the center of the scene
      facing right. Athena, about one-tenth the size of the other figures, is not quite free of
      Zeus. One foot still remains in his head. Although small, Athena is fully dressed and armed,
      her shield held at her left shoulder. Zeus is draped in a red and black, full-length mantle.
      His stool has a low back carved with a swan's head. A small creature, half man, half bird,
      crouches beneath it. Apollo stands to Zeus' left playing his lyre and dressed in a long chiton
      and himation. Poseidon, bearded and wearing a long chiton and a full-length mantle, stands to
      his left. The top of his triton can be seen at the crown of his head. A female figure stands
      to his left. She is wearing a long chiton and short jacket, and a fillet over her hair. Hermes
      stands on the far left in his petasos. He is bearded and has long hair, and he is dressed in a
      long chiton and mantle. He appears to be getting ready to leave the scene, perhaps to announce
      the birth, for his feet are turned to the left though he still looks to the right. Four more
      figures occupy the right side of the scene. Standing directly to the right of Zeus and Athena
      are two female figures, their right hands raised over their heads in a gesture of greeting or
      respect. Ares stands behind them in full panoply. He wears a crested Corinthian helmet and
      carries a round shield whose device is now lost. Dionysos stands on the far right, a wreath of
      grape leaves on his head. He is bearded and draped in a full-length
      mantle.</P><P><hi rend="head">Side B</hi>: chariot, charioteer,
      warriors and others. The two-horse chariot and charioteer face right in the foreground of the
      scene, surrounded by six warriors, three female figures and three other males. The bearded
      charioteer is in his chariot holding the reins. He is dressed in a short-sleeved, ankle-length
      chiton belted at the waist. Most of the figures clustered around the chariot face to the
      right, although a number turn their heads to talk to their companions. There are two warriors
      to the left of the chariot, two behind it, and two to the right. All are dressed in short
      chitons decorated with scallops or checkerboard patterns, and wear cuirasses, greaves and
      crested Corinthian helmets. One of the warriors behind the chariot carries a round shield
      whose device is now lost. The warrior on the far right carries a round shield with a snake
      device. Most of the warriors carry one or two spears. A female figure in a long chiton stands
      with the two warriors on the left. A white-bearded male figure stands behind the charioteer
      facing left. Another white-haired and white-bearded male and a second female figure stand
      behind the horses with two of the warriors. Both face to the right. A bearded male stands
      facing them wearing a long sash over his head. The third female figure stands with the
      warriors on the right, facing right. A white dog stands looking up at the horses in the lower
      right corner in front of the last warrior.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e2aa99336a7d2893006232aaa4e1f6af41f2f8b35771192a0af6bd4123dc61a1> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
