<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e311f5a7a1fa699f258d298f7016d12664982a4f12508861b2a24719f8aeeb71> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Hermes and three nymphs, behind a low rounded altar"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e32a955a0523079c21fc02ad17e81ea19a2cdb4d500c5a9c9254420060ab191b> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Side A: Herakles and Apollo fighting for the tripod<lb>Side B: Man with crab on
      shield, man in leopard suit, old man with staff"""@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e32ed6e6367e5d6cef3d2f5ec301d6ab2ed11d9de710791402bd1072de1eb896> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Side A: Dionysos rides to right on an ithyphallic donkey, the
      latter with red mane and white muzzle. Its head rears back while braying. Dionysos wears a
      white tunic and himation with red stripes and white rosettes, and a red ivy wreath. He carries
      a grape vine in his right hand and other vines issue from the donkey and his shoulder.
      Dionysos has a red beard. The donkey has a red wreath around his chest. In front at right is a
      satyr with a white dotted tortoise shell lyre in his left hand and a grape vine in his right.
      His mouth is open in song. He has a red tail, beard and forelocks, and a white wreath around
      one shoulder. A red fillet hangs from his lyre.</P><P>Side B: a nearly identical
      Dionysos and donkey, but the god's tunic is not white, and in his right hand he holds a
      drinking horn (keros). Facing them at right are a dancing satyr playing a lyre and singing.
      The satyr has a red tail, beard and forelock.</P><P>Lotus buds are on the neck.
      Black and red tongues are on the shoulder. Double red stripes are below panel and above the
      zone of rays on the lower body. Red stripes run around the foot and
      mouth.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e33e365998804b35f2d29e6b19cd8ab9606cecbc61a96ba7563386cac2438ef5> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Eros and Group of Four Men"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e34adb9f22c5b962eb90af0413dc40398817db0a86e0432b422e495515bb9441> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><bibl>Ashmead & Phillips 1976, no. 50,
      41</bibl></P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e34ee3f8e84ee3cdcd5259be606540402e7de8f5fb909e1f5dfb1a827319c712> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Comstock & Vermeule 1976, 196-197 no. 313</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e361464743fb29ecee259cbc6a164ecf80bc6b6d88f353bbba31d64b86bb5ffc> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Triton swims to the
      right, his human upper half erect and his serpentine lower body undulating behind and
      terminating in a forked tail similar to a dolphin's. The spotted tail is tinted with brown
      dilute glaze and has two pairs of fins. Triton's human half is clothed in a himation decorated
      with dot-rosettes and a chiton with folds drawn in dilute glaze. His long hair is tied in a
      krobylos secured with a wreath of added red. In his right hand he holds a scepter and in his
      left, a dolphin. The little finger of his right hand is stiffly
      extended.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: A Nereid in a belted
      chiton runs to the left; the chiton folds are drawn with diluted glaze. She seems to be in a
      state of agitation, for she stretches forth both arms and her loose hair gives her a somewhat
      wild appearance. If she is running to relate the news of her sister Thetis' capture by Peleus
      the normal task of running, agitated Nereids then the figure on Side A might not be Triton,
      but rather her father Nereus, the normal recipient of this news. Although Nereus is normally
      represented in human form in this period, on early black-figure vases he was sometimes
      depicted in fish-tailed form. On a kylix by the Kleophrades Painter, <rs
      type="vase">London E 73</rs> (<bibl>ARV2, 192, 106</bibl>), the scene of
      Nereus receiving the news from a Nereid is witnessed by the fish-tailed Triton. This too,
      then, is probably Triton, whose bizarre form the painter judged more visually interesting than
      that of Nereus.</P><P>The groundlines below each figure consist of a band of key
      pattern to right. The vase is otherwise entirely black, save for two incised lines framing the
      fillet above the foot.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e361a123e251ba9728cdbece394fb4f90d1df8b9c0411ff73aa7440dd4125fbe> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Condition"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>There are chips on the handles and elsewhere. The glaze is streaked and uneven
      in color.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435429> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e362905e7c61f721b57048bff7683916e4f5f28ea5b7a18cc4c7486dfaeefcf9> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Lawton 1995, 141-42 no. 140, pl. 74</bibl> (with previous bibliography)." ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e36db6f229342a76598bdf5ae9858dae694a807536439896fb12565973eea419> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Monumental altar in center of Sanctuary of Hera"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e3731d63db4258a8a6d4e08ad302d498d28d1cbb69e8741d78b352cf3ce62f40> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The clay is yellow-brown varying to red-brown; the decoration
      is in somewhat lustrous black glaze. The body is ovoid, with a large, nearly cylindrical neck,
      flaring at the top. There are plastically modeled serpents on the wide handles. On the lip,
      black dots are connected by diagonal lines and under the lip a row of dotted lozenges is
      bounded by horizontal lines; there are black areas under the handles separating the panels on
      the neck. These panels have a hatched meander above, a hatched meander and dotted lozenges
      below. Between them are vertical bands as follows: latticed bands, erect hatched meanders,
      checkered bands, and double chevrons, decorated by vertical lines. In panels at the top of
      body, grazing horses with a water-bird under each are represented. The ornaments in the field
      are chevrons and zigzags. Below the handles, zones of ornament are separated by horizontal
      lines. The zones are as follows (top to bottom): dotted lozenge band, complicated hatched
      meander, dotted lozenge band, frieze of water-birds with hatched bodies, latticed triangles
      below and rows of dots between the necks, opposed latticed triangles 6) a broad band of
      checker pattern 7) compressed lozenge band, and finally erect hatched triangles. On the foot
      are dotted circles connected by diagonal lines. On the handles are longitudinal lines and rows
      of dots on the serpents, and latticed triangles between their folds.</P><P>Three
      motifs and their placement on the vase help to identify this workshop: The stiff-legged birds
      in silhouette, the rendering of the double meander, the interlocking rows of "wolf teeth," or
      hatched triangles (Coldstream 67).</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e3751ec983d8ed1e36b4bb3db550a28b19ede578dd9f5d65179577fa2066c3f5> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Smith 1997</bibl>, cat. DR 16; <bibl>Lawton 1995</bibl>, 93-94 no. 23, pl. 12 (with previous bibliography); <bibl>Svoronos 1903-12, 665 no. 434.2, pl. 209.2</bibl>." ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e3772786a06601c51ef52bc82ee83d7d71e73dfbe7fc3351a966945b7440c3ce> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Judgment of Paris<lb>Floral on back"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e378c901b15aa90dd7d979f0de2d19b5f324a7de29d2bff06995850106138477> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Comstock & Vermeule 1971, 63 no. 64</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e385b2722db09a7b98b289d9ee9cc141bd90ef5840ac8c811e5e73c9724d2f9a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl><hi rend=\"ital\">BMCRE</hi></bibl> III, 146-7, no. 711; <bibl>Milkovich 1961, no. 47</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e388b9b378454aab906a05fb1482e0d58d885aa9ec7f2e01c1dcc05f8c4c2a7f> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>CVA, Louvre 8, III.Id, pl. 48.6-8</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e38be1a3485692a85d2686a6e41887ec903019f1dce29a34c7e0b2fc82936122> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Torso of a male athlete in a frontal pose, perhaps a discobolos, or spear-bearer"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e38c9f19c9659ffb1a8d91730bf6fd6728d4f699687d90f5bed072fdebd28b05> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: A woman, probably a
      maenad, is seated on a rock to right between two dancing satyrs. She is playing the double
      flutes (<hi rend="ital">auloi</hi>. She wears a chiton and a broad hair-band,
      which holds her hair in a krobylos. The rock is streaked with dilute glaze, perhaps indicating
      a rough surface. The satyrs are nude and balding. The one at left lifts his left leg in the
      air and extends his right arm for balance; in his left hand he holds a thyrsos. The satyr at
      right also raises his left leg, but it is his right arm he extends toward the flutist; his
      face is drawn in three-quarter view.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side
      B</hi>: A pair of youths wearing himatia and holding staffs vertical in their right
      hands stand on either side of a third youth (or boy), who faces the youth at left. The arms of
      the central youth are covered by his himation.</P><P>A laurel wreath circles the
      vase below the rim, and inside there are two reserved stripes at the top and bottom of the
      mouth. The groundline on either side consists of groups of three maeanders to right
      alternating with cross-squares. The side of the foot is reserved, and there is a reserved
      groove between foot and body.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e3949837abcce595a0f688f5a921111945a2301db34fc937b212d41e357a4691> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The rim of the vessel has a reserved band between two black
      bands. On the reserved band is an ivy pattern. On the base, above the foot, are red and black
      tongues.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: A woman in barbarian dress
      is shown riding on a lion and shooting at a monster. She wears a jerkin and leggings decorated
      with a diamond pattern, and a hat with long ear flaps. She rides the lion to the right, and
      aims her bow and arrow at a monster on the right. The monster has a large, egg-shaped body
      painted with black and white checks. It has the head of a donkey or rabbit, with long ears,
      and two small hoofed legs. It is breathing red fire at the woman on the lion. In between the
      combatants is a tree.</P><P>This could possibly be a griffin; both Herodotus and
      Aeschylus mention a story of the Arimaspians battling griffins in the north (Palmer, 180).
      However, representations of griffin battles don't appear in Greek art until the fourth century
      B.C. Perhaps this is simply a monster from the artist's
      imagination.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e39a6a33f05d28dae828a7cbdfa87adf5e0bfda0c4d5f7d66c2a111c44331b9f> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The shoulder and lid are elaborately decorated with standard
      tongue and gear patterns while the zone at the base of the knob and the thin foot rim are
      reserved for clarity and emphasis. The underside of the bowl, the resting surface of the foot,
      and the lid's rim and undersurface are unglazed.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e39ad9f9cbe26675edcdc0a1df71443c1819301c5a758d56262213d8110e4ab2> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Aphrodite riding side-saddle on a goose"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e3bc9f913d33e74d0f4ce600ddb428be2a1d49ab2d09b86897b0a57eca826873> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Side A: The ransom of Hektor<lb>Side B: Greek heroes"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e3c2c6125fca1b767901d875832cdf15940455f9ada3c4488ebec9c47400cbc3> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>In tondo: a satyr is trying to sit on a pointed amphora. The
      satyr is naked except for an ivy wreath and an ankle cord, and has an erect phallos. He
      thrusts his bearded head forward with eager eyes, and his tail curls behind his left leg. His
      forehead is wrinkled, brown lines model the cheek, and there is a brown line between eye and
      eyebrow. He sits on a pointed amphora, and will balance on it and kick about. Inscriptions: on
      left, <foreign lang="Greek">*L*E*A*G*R*O*S *K*A*L*O*S</foreign>; on right,
      <foreign lang="Greek">*A*Q*E*N*O*D*O*T*O*S *K*A*L*O*S</foreign>, and on the
      amphora, <foreign lang="Greek">*K*A*L*O*S</foreign>, perhaps referring to the
      satyr.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/e3c3695a02e080ee321f4ec5dc58bcaefb40ba741d441f4ff2f00737b6c49c3e> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl><hi rend=\"ital\">BMCRE</hi></bibl> I, 203, no. 17; pl. 38.14; <bibl>Perkins Collection 1902, 100, 13</bibl>; <bibl>Cohen 1880-1892) 1, Neron no. 210</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

