<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/edbe13d19b3d6f6f322bd992c3eba997b769a876cf91b86e52bd74b191835420> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Side A: Infant Herakles and snakes. Side B: Zeus sending forth Hermes and
      Iris."""@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/edc64eeaa627fcf9ac717a75e113511b3309b81e68eb77ad51767589221c830a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Boardman 1989, fig. 13</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/edc79e4bd0e402b1480d610398a1d08c4c745c21c5b3d7b30cb938bd861e59b2> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The body of the vase shows a man and a woman at a tomb. The
      center of the composition is occupied by the tomb and its stele, bound with fillets rendered
      in matte red. A woman approaches the tomb from the left, carrying a basket, decorated with a
      leaf pattern on the side, with more fillets. The woman is rendered in outline and her hair
      appears to have had a light yellowish wash. She wears a chiton with unusual sleeves. A young
      man without a beard approaches from the right, holding an oil container in his right hand. He
      too is rendered in outline except for the solid, bright red himation covering his chiton. His
      hair is indicated by a yellowish brown wash. A small, winged, naked female figure hovers in
      the air between the stele and the woman's head. This scene at the tomb is surmounted by a
      grayish stopped meander enclosed in red lines. The decoration on the body does not encircle
      the vase but stops at the sides. The mouth, neck, handle, and lower body of the vase are
      glazed. The top of the foot is glazed while the sides are reserved. The shoulder is decorated
      with a tongue pattern and palmettes.</P><P>The white ground technique, which first
      becomes popular in the experimental stage of Attic vase painting in the 520's, consists of
      covering the natural reddish ground of the clay with a thin white "slip" of ultra-refined
      clay, which remains white after firing. In the earliest usage of this white ground technique,
      decoration in the standard black-figure technique of silhouette with incision and added color
      would then be placed against this lighter background. By the early fifth century, many
      painters switch to an outline technique. As can be seen on this lekythos, artists using this
      technique enliven figures drawn in outline with areas of solid added color. From its inception
      until the mid fifth century, white ground is used primarily for small vases, such as lekythoi,
      pyxides, and alabastra, and for small areas of larger vases. After the middle of the fifth
      century, white ground is used only rarely for vases other than lekythoi. With these white
      ground lekythoi of the second half of the fifth century, there is also a change in
      iconography; the subjects depicted now being almost exclusively
      funerary.</P><P>The iconography of this vase is standard for late fifth century
      funerary lekythoi. The most common scene found on such vases is the visit to the tomb, and the
      most frequently depicted visitor is a young woman, sometimes accompanied by a male relative,
      as on this vase. The basket of fillets carried by the woman is also a common feature. The
      small winged figure on this vase is somewhat atypical. In general terms, this figure is an
      eidolon, representing the dead person. The term eidolon, however, is usually applied to fully
      represented figures in epiphany scenes, which become especially popular during the
      Peloponnesian War. The figure on this vase is part way between such representations and the
      very small, winged, hovering, mosquito-like figures, rendered in black silhouette, which also
      represent the dead (sometimes called psychai in order to differentiate them), as on <rs
      type="vase">Athens, NM 1926</rs>.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/edcc8d10b3029908bcf9f9b27cc14be0d5c97394661b984206c2b2526fe89221> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<P>Stibbe 1972.</P>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/edd92b8559b417521a44ce5bd34fa9d20d516bf528c87a53b838007ac1d57974> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>A youth stands in profile to the right; except for the cloak
      draped over his left shoulder and arm, he is naked. In his left hand he holds a staff. His
      hair is tied in a krobylos with a fillet of added red; long curls, drawn with thinned glaze,
      hang from his temples. On either side of the youth are two gesticulating women, the left one
      running away to the left, the right one standing frontally, her head turned to face the youth.
      Both women wear chitons and himations. The woman at left has her hair tied up with a double
      fillet, the trailing ends of which are drawn with added red. The woman at right wears a
      sakkos, from which wispy curls, drawn with thinned glaze, escape over the brow and temples.
      Dilute glaze is used for the minor musculature on the youth and women. The subject is
      unexplained, but from the behavior of the women, who raise their hands in apparent
      consternation, they are upset to find a naked male in their midst; they are probably,
      therefore, not hetairai, but "respectable" women, whose only normally social intercourse with
      men is with husbands and kin (but see comments below).</P><P>The groundline and
      upper frame consist of groups of three maeanders to left alternating with cross-squares; egg
      and dot pattern around the rim. The top of the mouth and the lower two thirds of the torus on
      the foot is reserved; the rest of the vase is black.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

