    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/ee439bea3c7e3d96faa0333643becffd71cffef7fa5c0e699b6428f730f36619> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<bibl>Van Straten 1995</bibl>, 45, 208-209 no. V87, fig. 45;
      <bibl>Kron 1976</bibl>, 239-41, pl. 32; <bibl>Webster 1972</bibl>,
      133; <bibl>Froning 1971</bibl>, 17-18; Histoire gnrale des religions 1. Grce-Rome
      (Paris 1944) 69; <bibl>Para</bibl>, 452; <bibl>Add</bibl>, 161; H.B.
      Walters, CVA British Museum 3 (1927) III.I.c, pl. 17.3 (with previous bibliography); C.
      Robert, "Sopra i Vasi di Polignoto," <hi rend="ital">MonAnt</hi> 9 (1899) pl. 1;
      A. Milchhfer, "Bacchische Siegesfeier," <hi rend="ital">AZ</hi> 38 (1880) 183; E.
      Gerhard, Auserlesene Vasenbilder (Berlin 1840-1858) pl. 243""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/ee46d2aaab208d5f8a39ee74b07daafd953745a740338423db39c594494cad65> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Ext. Side A</hi>: Both Sides A and
      B are executed in red figure. Between the eyes and palmettes with open fronds is a running
      satyr. He is carrying a pelta (shield) and a rhyton (drinking horn), and wearing an ivy
      wreath. He crouches as he runs. There is a horizontal inscription, <foreign
      lang="Greek">*E*P*I*K*T*E*T*O*S</foreign> ("Epiktetos").</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Side B</hi>: Eyes and palmettes as on Side A. Another satyr, carrying a
      pelta and oinochoe, runs to the left. He looks over his shoulder and blows a trumpet as he
      runs. The inscription on this side is <foreign
      lang="Greek">*E*G*R*A*S*F*E*N</foreign> ("drew [me]").</P><P><hi
      rend="ital">Interior</hi>: The interior is done in black figure. A horseman is
      depicted, riding to the right. He wears a cloak and wreath, and is carrying two spears. The
      lower pleated edge of his short chiton just shows from under his cloak. The horse is shown in
      a lively position, rearing up slightly, with its mouth open. Although riding bareback, the
      horseman does hold reins. There is an inscription running around the tondo: <foreign
      lang="Greek">*I*S*K*U*L*O*S *E*P*O*I*E*S*E*N</foreign> ("Hischylos made
      [me]").</P><P>Epiktetos' red figure work is well drawn, "An exquisite balance of
      line and detail" (Boardman, 58). This is particularly apparent on Side B, where the satyr is
      turned in a rather inventive position, so that the viewer sees his back. This and the
      crouching pose of the first satyr exemplifies Epiktetos' early interest in the study of
      anatomy (Cohen, 427).</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/ee57aec2885de6882bbd00502755b899e7244700c3a7e5a9a19c8227f0a42549> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
