<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f03524107c96779d90d435fb69a32b008f17fb0cfcb233cde744a1541c8ba86c> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Most of the body is covered with bands of geometric ornament. Between the handles is a
      frieze comprised largely of a maeander, with a horse, shown profile toward the center of the
      vase, next to each handle."""@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f035aa7f91c143e3164511604b6516a6dd1b5b6aa4ea8481bc10b533eb68baf2> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Cohen 1880-1892, 3.413, no. 80, Mars on reverse</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f0372c1b83baf520b9c80b40391354203c6420eab45463c82714c375fa134f99> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Side A: From the left, Pelopeia, bejeweled (with earrings,
      bracelets, necklace, and beaded fillet in yellow) and wearing a chiton, himation veiled over
      her head, and sandals, stands in 3/4-view to the left, turning away from the scene, and raises
      her hands in horror. Aigisthos, her son and brother, stands beside her, also turning to the
      left, leaning on a tall spear. He is nude, except for a chlamys falling down his back and a
      baldric on his left thigh. He holds a sword in his lowered left hand, however. Next is father
      Thyestes, running 3/4-view to the left, but turning his head back to the right. He is bearded
      and wears a pilos, chiton, and chlamys clasped with a brooch at his throat. He also holds a
      sword (with which he has just murdered Atreus) in his upraised right hand, and the scabbard in
      his lowered left hand. At the center is the cushioned throne of Atreus, mostly white
      (imitating ivory), shown frontally, on which the corpse of Atreus is sprawled upside down.
      Atreus is fully dressed in himation, chiton over tunic, and sandals, but he is clearly dead,
      with closed eyes, a bloody chest wound, and arms hanging loose below his head. His tall
      scepter stands to the left of the throne. To his right is another group of three characters. A
      winged fury named Poine (the personification of Vengeance) runs toward the king, with her
      right hand on his throne, but turns her eyes away from the sight. She wears high boots, a
      short belted chiton, and a short cloak over her left shoulder. She has snakes in her hair, and
      holds a red staff. She looks at a pair of women who are identified by inscription as slave
      women. They both wear long belted chitons, earrings, necklaces, and slippers, and embrace each
      other, while gesturing at the murdered king. The right one is short-haired, like a slave, but
      the left one wears a himation, a sakkos or hair net, and a bracelet. Spindles on the ground
      below them may indicate that they were interrupted in their spinning
      work.</P><P>Side B: On the left, in 3/4-view to the right, stand a maenad, wearing
      a chiton, and holding a mirror and a iunx wheel, and a nude satyr, who holds yellow and white
      grapes and a tympanum (drum). A maenad, perhaps Ariadne, is seated in 3/4-view to the left,
      but turns to the right to look at Dionysos, who likewise sits in 3/4-view to the right, but
      turns toward her. She wears a long belted chiton, himation, slippers and jewelry, and holds a
      thyrsos and a bunch of white grapes; he is nude, except for a himation draped down his back
      and a white taenia around his head, and holds an opened box and an empty kantharos. To his
      right stands another maenad, in 3/4-view to the left, wearing a chiton, slippers, and jewelry,
      and holding a torch and a situla.</P><P>The stem of the vessel is decorated with a
      ray design. A continuous frieze of women, youths, and erotes, all bearing gifts surrounds the
      entire vessel in the lower frieze, with a band of maeanders serving as a groundline. Bands of
      tendrils enclose the upper frieze on both sides. The neck is decorated with a painted
      decoration that imitates ribbing, surmounted by a band of rosettes and finally a tall
      lotus-palmette frieze. The rim is decorated with a vine
      tendril.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f0376702bdb458189edc849cab20b921256f282ade6c6ace38fa4cae841facdd> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
