<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f5127c35a127bedb674a6af0385888ea793e160d6faf22dc3e727855e57bf8ac> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The body of the vessel is in the form of a wide, covered galley
      on three legs. It has a long sharp prow, deck works in front (on which was a detachable
      figure), and a high stern against which the steersman (painted black) reclines. In the center
      of the vessel is a mouth for filling, and the drinking hole is at the end of the stern. At the
      top of the sides is a band of strokes and dots between horizontal lines; on each side of the
      prow is an eye. The deck works are marked off by rectangles. There is a row of dots around the
      mouth and an abbreviated meander along each side of the deck.</P><P>Vermeule (181)
      notes that this vase is "sometimes thought of as the poet Hesiod on his nervous journey from
      Aulis to Chalkis to sing in the funeral games, a distance of some six hundred yards which
      inspired in him remarkable advice on how evil it is for a man to desire uncomfortable sea
      journeys (<bibl n="Hes. WD 618">Hes. WD
      618</bibl>).</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

