<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f574055b18dc123c7c4f9f29de872637a2485ccabdcff6625893a7d30432d090> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: The Judgement of
      Paris. From left to right, the goddesses Hera, Athena and Aphrodite are led by Hermes in the
      presence of Paris. In first place marches Hera, as the queen of the goddesses. She is a tall
      figure, wrapped in a chiton and himation and holding up a flower (?) in her left hand. Her
      gesture suggests that she seems to be speaking. Following her, Athena wears a helmet and holds
      a spear in her right hand. The last goddess, as in the literary sources, is Aphrodite. All
      three goddesses wear chiton and himations with red bands as decorations. Hera and Athena's
      chitons are decorated with small stars or flowers in white. Aphrodite wears plain folded
      chiton. All of them have long hair tied in the back and red bands or taenias in their heads.
      Athena's helmet breaks the frame above the figures. The faces, hands and feet are painted in
      bright added white.</P><P>Facing the group of goddesses are Hermes and Paris. They
      are standing at right, their bodies facing right, but turning their heads to look at the trio.
      Their two bodies overlap. Hermes is closer to Hera, gesturing with his right hand, which
      breaks the frame above. He is bearded, dressed like a messenger, with <hi
      rend="ital">kerykeion</hi> and petasos, special shoes and a mantle or chlamys. There
      is added red on his dress and his beard, and the top of his hat is white. Paris is also
      bearded, with long hair, wrapped in a chiton and himation and carrying a stick or staff. He
      goes to meet the divine procession. Hermes' <hi rend="ital">kerykeion</hi> and
      Paris' staff are parallel and break the decorative palmettes growing from the
      handles.</P><P>The scene follows faithfully the Archaic sources which speak about
      the Judgement of Paris: the <hi rend="ital">Iliad</hi> and the <hi
      rend="ital">Cypria</hi> (<bibl n="Hom. Il. 24.25-30">Hom. Il.
      24.25-30</bibl>; <bibl n="Cypr. (EGF 17)">Cypr. (EGF
      17)</bibl>).</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: A young horseman
      flanked by two men. In the middle of the scene is a horseman dressed in Thracian dress with
      very wide sleeves in black and white, carrying two spears. He is a young man with short hair;
      the hair over his forehead is red. The horse is a massive animal with a red mane. Under the
      horse is a dog. On either side of the youth stands a bearded man looking inwards at the young
      horseman. The man at right, in front of the horse, is wrapped in a himation and leans on a
      stick. He is standing, almost overlapping the decorative palmette above. There is some added
      red on his hair and his clothes. The man at left is naked except for a short mantle or cloak
      over his shoulders, and carries two spears. He is moving to left but turning his head to look
      backwards at the horseman. There is added red on his cloak, hair and
      beard.</P><P>The figures are drawn with incision. Only half of the body of the
      vase is occupied by the picture. The lower part has a simple meander band, a linked lotus-bud
      chain, and thick rays coming from the bottom. Under the handles a rich floral motif formed by
      a lotus flower pointing down linked through four tendrils with four palmettes growing from the
      handles, two at the top and two at the bottom. On the neck, floral
      ornament.</P><P>The Antimenes Painter decorated a large number of vessels,
      especially neck-amphorae and hydriae, in the last third of the sixth century. Several features
      of this vase are typical of this painter, such as the choice and placement of the subsidiary
      decoration.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

