<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f5f1c1497d59ac63459e16f62afd4dce1074d1bf5b0c609268d84d7d8a95c54a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Woman sitting on a rock."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f5f64b426b3bea09a755b1d95e26da4d0f0ebea76d00c29b48270a6be7bb8a21> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Judgment of Paris. Paris, dressed as a Trojan, sits on a rock
      in the middle of the composition. He wears trousers, a long overgarment with long sleeves, and
      a Phrygian cap. He is depicted as young and unbearded with long hair. He rests his left hand
      on the rock and holds a staff in his right. The three goddesses surround him. On the left,
      Aphrodite, standing at a higher level, looks toward a winged Eros, which flutters over Paris'
      head. Athena is to the right of Paris also on a higher level. She is identified by her helmet,
      spear, and shield, which rests against the rock next to Paris. Hera wearing a long chiton and
      polos stands to the right of Athena on the lower ground line. At the far right of the
      composition, another female figure, perhaps an attendant of Hera, sits wrapped in a mantle
      with it drawn up partly over her face. Hermes is at the far left of the composition. He
      crosses one leg over the other and leans on a staff. He wears a short chiton, a chlamys, boots
      and a petasos. In the upper left corner of the scene a reclining female, nude to the waist
      with a mantle wrapped around her legs, gestures with her right hand toward the scene. In the
      upper right corner a small scale figure of Pan carries an animal skin draped over his left
      arm. The artist makes heavy use of relief gilding and thick matte
      paint.</P><P>This rendition of the Judgment of Paris contains many elements
      typical of depictions of the myth in the Classical period. Paris begins to be shown as an
      unbearded youth in the first half of the fifth century. In the second half of the fifth
      century, the composition with the goddesses gathered around Paris begins, as does the use of
      figures ranged on several levels. Paris wears Oriental trousers and a Phrygian cap by the end
      of the fifth century.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f6109fd35759b7926932279458ee90432639e0fc2881f2ccb1603978027d32e3> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
