<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f78f416ac43e98380cada43ab5e52fd62dceafecb88e110fc30a84187af6267c> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Comstock & Vermeule 1976, 207 no. 327 (ill., with previous bibliography)</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f7970e05a885f2df864a3c1bf2c2891ed2246ae52f6a83dcaa857a1874ddc51c> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Side A</hi>: Herakles and the
      Nemean Lion. Herakles grapples with the lion while Athena and Iolaos look on. Herakles, shown
      naked on the left, has grabbed the upper jaw of the lion and is wrestling him to the ground.
      He is pushing forward and down on the lion's head so that his legs are bent and his body
      parallel to the ground. The lion's tongue hangs from his open mouth as his head is forced to
      the ground, and he can be reconstructed as pushing at Herakles' head with his left hind paw.
      Athena stands to the right behind Herakles holding her shield and spear. She is wearing a
      short-sleeved chiton decorated with a circle-filled criss-cross pattern with meander patterns
      and squiggles bordering sleeves, neck and hem. Her shield is decorated with a Gorgon's head.
      Iolaos is standing behind the lion on the right holding Herakles' club and gesturing in
      excitement with his left hand. He is facing right, but has turned his head to look back at
      Athena on the left. His upper body is naked (his lower body is not preserved) and he is shown
      with a full beard and short, curly hair.</P><P><hi rend="head">Side
      B</hi>: Dionysos and Ariadne. Dionysos and Ariadne face one another in the center of the
      scene while a satyr and maenad dance. The satyr dances behind Ariadne and the maenad behind
      Dionysos. Ariadne, on the left, is shown in profile to the right holding her twins, Oinopion
      and Staphylos, the children of Dionysos. Although the upper part of the figure is missing, the
      dangling feet of the twins can be seen hanging by Ariadne's waist. Ariadne is wearing a chiton
      decorated with panels of red and black scale patterns on the side and a meander band on the
      hem. Dionysos, facing her on the right, wears a chiton and a full mantle colored red and
      black. He is holding a large grape vine in his left hand and a kantharos in his right. Only
      the lower legs and raised arms of the dancing satyr on the left are preserved. The maenad on
      the right is shown facing forward but with her head turned toward Dionysos on the left. She
      has one foot off the ground as she dances with arms bent and held out to either side of her
      body. Her clothing is almost identical to Athena's on Side
      A.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f79c36d4189235fcbad5dedd9c816634331376120a042bf5a8e5aec12fc1526f> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Grave stele of the young warrior Aristonautes"@en ;
