<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f7cd6957d96f017c5d29431b01e204949428d0a50ab6682c71c11c51b0207c4c> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Peripteral temple; on the west side of the Agora, on the Kolonos Agoraios."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f7e85119bceaaa845dd6402807294fc1da62b9901c58115b8014a3d0be58c8ed> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Herakles and three
      Amazons. In the center of the composition, Herakles, at the left, attacks a fallen Amazon.
      Herakles wears his lion skin with the head drawn up and a chitoniskos. He carries a sword and
      quiver, but wields a club in his right hand against the amazon. The Amazon wears a short
      chiton, a helmet, greaves, and perimeridia on her thighs. She carries a Boeotian shield
      decorated with two panther heads, and aims her spear at Herakles. At the left of this group,
      another amazon runs away from the action to the left. She wears a short chiton, a corslet and
      a helmet. She is armed with a sword, a spear and a round shield. On the right, the third
      Amazon, dressed and armed like the second, rushes toward the central
      group.</P><P><bibl n="Apollod. 2.5.9.7-8">Apollod. 2.5.9.7-8</bibl>
      relates a mythic tradition that as his ninth labor for King Eurystheus Herakles had to bring
      him the belt of Hippolyte, the queen of the Amazons. Herakles' conflict with the Amazons was a
      very popular theme in Attic black figure, numbering nearly 400 examples. These depictions
      differ, however, from the literary tradition preserved in Apollodorus in that the vases label
      the queen of the Amazons as Andromache, and there is no sign of the belt. In Attic black
      figure the battle of Herakles and the Amazons reaches its greatest popularity circa 525. The
      Antimenes Painter decorates four other vases with such scenes. Both Robinson in the <hi
      rend="ital">CVA</hi> and <bibl>Burow 1989</bibl> identify the central
      Amazon on the Mississippi vase as Andromache, but she is not
      labelled.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: Combat, two warriors
      parted by a bearded man. The warrior on the left wears a corslet over a chitoniskos, greaves,
      a sword at his waist, and a Corinthian helmet. He carries a round shield on his left arm and a
      spear in his upraised right hand. The warrior on the right is similarly dressed and armed,
      with the exception being that he carries a Boeotian shield. In the center of these two
      figures, a bearded man wrapped in a mantle rushes in with both arms raised to stop the
      fighting.</P><P>Robinson in the <hi rend="ital">CVA</hi> interpreted
      the scene as Agamemnon separating Odysseus and Ajax, who are fighting over the arms of
      Achilles. Once again, however, there is a problem between generic and specific depictions in
      Archaic art. In the last third of the sixth century, apparently generic scenes of quarreling
      warriors being separated were very popular with black figure painters. Similar compositions
      with the figures labelled as Ajax and Odysseus are not found until the first quarter of the
      fifth century. Therefore, it is probably best to interpret the scene on the Mississippi vase
      as general rather than specific.</P><P>The secondary decoration of the vase
      follows the standard usage of the Antimenes Painter. The neck carries addorsed palmettes. The
      lower body has a running meander over a zone of lotus buds, over rays. The area under the
      handles is filled with a palmette and lotus bud motif.</P><P>The Antimenes Painter
      continues the black figure technique in the period when red figure is beginning to dominate.
      He decorated mainly neck amphorae and hydriae, and favored scenes with
      Herakles.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f7e97a47ea2d3e27221b41b3cdae38c4923e61279f9ae1275d8396021c5b422d> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Torso of discus thrower"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f7ef8b849a62e6824a1e942afa56f540bf235d988092f662fc52173b01cdc3c3> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>A woman stands in frontal view, looking left, her arms
      outstretched to either side. She holds a distaff and spindle, one in each hand, and the rather
      heavy thread she is spinning reaches from one to the other across her neck. The woman wears a
      long chiton and a himation. Her hair is enclosed in a sakkos.</P><P>Figures of
      women spinning and working wool are rare in black-figure vase painting. They enter the
      repertoire of red-figure in the Late Archaic period, not appearing among surviving early
      red-figure vases, and proliferate in the Early Classical and Classical periods. Many such
      scenes occur on hydriai, and they are often distinguishable from other scenes of women in
      domestic interiors only by the presence of the tall wool basket. Woolworking appears to have
      been a standard task of Athenian women, and <bibl>Beazley 1974</bibl> and others
      have viewed spinning as inextricably associated with respectability and domestic virtue. But
      <bibl>Rodenwaldt 1932</bibl> and <bibl>Keuls 1983</bibl> conclude that
      at least some of the spinning scenes, such as <rs type="vase">Berlin inv.
      31426</rs>, a kylix by the Eauion Painter, which include men holding money pouches,
      represent hetairai negotiating with their clients.</P><P>Beazley attributed three
      white lekythoi to the Two-Row Painter, who was primarily a painter of white ground alabastra.
      The figures on the alabastra are generally taller than this one, but the quick straight
      drapery lines, the prominent drapery weights, and the large chin and breast of the Yale figure
      are all typical of the painter's style.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f7f587e48e2fc1942cc9552228ab4bd836aefce31757c15dd1ecebe98058eb9f> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Toes of a left foot (of a kouros?), and the front of a rounded plinth to which they were attached"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f7fb0b0c407eeca1aad348b65e5f1e6d311b6242d4f3bc208f7dded3e2bb2efc> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>On side A, Herakles battles with Kyknos while Athena and Hermes
      look on from the left, and Ares and Aphrodite look on from the right. On the neck are
      confronted lions with dot rosettes between. In the lower register is a standing youth between
      seated lions with averted heads, all flanked by sirens. On side B is a draped man standing
      between two women, flanked by seated sphinxes. On the neck are confronted lions with a
      palmette-lotus hybrid between them. In the lower register is a seated lion with head turned.
      To either side are sirens with reaised wings, themselves flanked by
      lions.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f7fd1cb55524261c69097645e5106b5817b8ccb30cd1796fae88c9a8c87af2e9> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Condition"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Repaired from many large fragments, with some gaps filled in (with plaster). The painted
      surface is not restored, but only a few portions are missing (such as Atreus' face, and the
      head of Io).""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435429> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f7ff71384a0392e4b5d54d080cb07f3450f809ad3d85c0d9844d3d031b8be8a9> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The clay is a light dull red, of firm texture. The decoration
      is in slightly lustrous dark brown glaze. The body of the vessel is ovoid, with a high,
      sloping shoulder, narrow high neck, and trefoil mouth. The handle is attached to the mouth. On
      the lip are hatched triangles, on the neck a high meander of hatched bands, separated by
      horizontal lines from bands of dotted lozenges above and below. On the body is a band of
      hatched erect triangles, a plain hatched band, a band of dotted lozenges, another band of
      hatched triangles, and a band of erect zigzag lines. They are all separated from one another
      by horizontal lines. On the handle are two longitudinal rows of dots separated by
      lines.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f7ffa8d8a05d5385b1febee5f7047c71e9c2a8a1a3edb0bbfe80cfd4d8d31e91> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The clay is a pale warm brown, orange-brown where the
      transparent surface slip remains. The decoration is in slightly lustrous black glaze. The body
      is slightly ovoid with a wide base. On the front are two small knobs (breasts). The slender
      neck is marked off by a ridge, and the vessel has a trefoil mouth and strap handle. The mouth
      is black except for a latticed space toward the front edge. On the neck there are horizontal
      lines and a compressed zigzag above and below an upright hatched meander. At the base of the
      neck is a band of small latticed triangles. At the top of the body is a band of large latticed
      triangles; the relief knobs are in a black area flanked by vertical lines and a latticed band.
      There is a hatched zigzag in the remainder of the zone, then a broad zone with connected
      groups of concentric circles (false spiral). Finally there is a compressed zigzag band and two
      broad black bands, separated by horizontal lines. The handle has a ladder
      pattern.</P><P>This vase is in Coldstream's Group iv; the body is more attenuated,
      and has a higher neck than earlier types. Boeotian Late Geometric wares were influenced by
      Attic LG I, in the replacement of the dark ground by a light ground, the evolution of a
      consistent figure style, and the fondess for equal square metopes arranges in horizontal
      bands.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f804af4c1d5d16aa531eceb96a69fd9c7096f7ae5e1b0f41d259414001978112> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Satyrs attacking a sleeping maenad"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f805e65802a20c8a606f86ab381a6e4dabb8ee483492bff2cc323b46bd7becd4> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Clairmont 1993, 3.120-21, cat. 3.284 (ill.)</bibl>; <bibl>Karouzou 1968, 118-19, pl. 40b</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f8072beefbd2cb2100454ac3596c839facf0a97ce5ba969c2ccdbb83fcea7ed7> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><bibl>Boardman 1974, fig.
      151.</bibl></P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f8149fc38d28f486fef823d9e8493bea078f443473f515ddc628995a9f3c9783> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Body:</hi> Woman looking back. In
      the main panel a woman moves swiftly to the right. In her left hand she holds a twig with
      black leaves; in her right she holds a fruit, perhaps a pomegranate. She is dressed in a Doric
      chiton with kolpos or overfold, bordered with red. On her head she wears a sakkos or snood and
      earrings. Her head is turned back to the left towards what she is fleeing
      from.</P><P>The lip and bottom are black. The neck and shoulder are red. The white
      slip is of a brownish type. On the shoulder, four crude palmettes are connected by tendrils
      with short rays/tongues at the junction with the neck. Above the design is a running black
      meander.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f8238f1add10ae352c7a9b2740cac27f7b804291e7545f6068e988a70348546e> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Brouskari 1974, 23-24</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f83306e0e4116dc3fc59e4e7c48343ec13cd0249f39058731c90f600b95200ef> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><bibl>Caskey & Beazley, 3,
      39-40</bibl></P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f837d40606891275371425975e9fe0e312ee03554a9d2c6041ac27a391bd3276> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Amphitrite, Poseidon, Amymone, and Aphrodite appear from right
      to left in a landscape. Amphitrite stands on the groundline of the scene at the far right,
      facing left toward Poseidon. The wife of the sea god displays her status with the <hi
      rend="ital">analakypsis</hi> gesture. She wears a long chiton, a himation with an
      embroidered border of spirals, and a stephane. A laurel bush grows out of the groundline to
      Amphitrite's right. Poseidon stands in a rocky landscape, his body in a contrapposto pose
      shown in three-quarter view. Bearded, his head faces left, and his left hand rests on his hip.
      He is nude except for a chlamys, decorated with a pattern of crosses and draped over his
      shoulders, and a laurel wreath on his head. He holds his trident upright in his hand, its butt
      resting on the ground. Poseidon looks toward Amymone, who is seated on a rock to the god's
      right. The preserved lower part of her body, dressed in a long chiton, faces Poseidon,
      suggesting that she returns his gaze, although it is also possible that she looked at
      Aphrodite, to her right. Amymone holds her ankles crossed, and the shaft of Poseidon's trident
      passes between her knee and foot. She holds a <hi rend="ital">tyle</hi> on her
      lap, and to her left a large hydria (undecorated, and thus probably bronze) rests on the
      ground.</P><P>Aphrodite is separated from the other three figures by two laurel
      bushes, between which is a hare. The goddess sits on a rock at far left, facing left with her
      head turned back to the right. She wears a long chiton, a himation with a pattern of crosses,
      a necklace, and sandals. Her hair is tied with a ribbon. She holds a small piece of her
      himation out with her right hand and the end of a sceptre in her left. Beside the goddess, on
      a small rock, is a decorated box or small chest, shown in three-quarter view. A laurel bush
      growing out of a small hillock on the groundline beyond Aphrodite at the far left closes the
      composition.</P><P>Traditional scenes of Poseidon and Amymone show the sea god
      pursuing Amymone or ambushing her as she draws water from a fountain. Here the scene is
      transformed, however, by the presence of Amphitrite, Poseidon's wife, and by the quiet
      demeanor of the figures and the elaborate rocky landscape which they inhabit. Aphrodite is
      often found in the traditional fountain scenes, as she is here. In some cases she sends Eros
      with a bridal crown for Amymone, as may originally have been true on this vase, where a figure
      of Eros may have appeared in the missing section above Amymone. Here, as noted by
      <bibl>Kaempf-Dimitriadou 1979, 29 and n. 216</bibl>, Aphrodite may also serve as a
      reference to marriage, suggested by the elaborately decorated chest, reminiscent of bridal
      gifts on other vases, that rests beside her. E. Simon (<bibl>LIMC, I, 752</bibl>)
      describes the Yale Amymone as "bride-like," and it is possible that this vase was a wedding
      gift. The prominence of Amphitrite, emphasized by her position in a foreground plane in front
      of the other figures, adds to the wedding iconography. <bibl>Simon 1982,
      147-48</bibl> has related scenes of Amymone to the satyr play of that name by Aeschylus,
      although no satyrs appear on the Yale lekythos to relate it specifically to the
      play.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f83e4bcd380117c04f7135d6b44dd782819a4273ae86facef5528703775c832f> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl><hi rend=\"ital\">RIC</hi></bibl> IV.2, 153, no. 3; pl. 11, 7; <bibl>Cohen 1880-1892, 4.523, 3</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f8450b57173040e9eb28ce40ff3cf86bdaff71c91669821e931cfbf6242493f5> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Smith 1997</bibl>, cat. DR 43; <bibl>Lawton 1995</bibl>, 103 no. 45, pl. 24 (with previous bibliography); <bibl>Svoronos 1903-12, 666 no. 441.1, pl. 215.1</bibl>." ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f852381d1fd688ae4fa8f7491e0e0429d1e38e248a8d6179e3231d3d9737fbd9> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Condition"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Most of added red lost. Surface damaged, especially around lioness and swan on
      front to left side of vase. Some spalls, especially in damaged area around lioness and
      swan.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435429> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f8561597dc07d15ec9a22a4f2db5b7b11e75933935083dbaa252ecfddd2a44da> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Artemis with quiver"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f8618d2396fe5851eb765c3c73f2212c9b39186d6156c6dd60bb7fd1b02cf84c> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "For general type, see <bibl>Mionnet 1806-1838</bibl> IV, no. 61" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f862ec3aa2f12f15949c6e3509a118346df35375e733098a647e1aa3cf6fbfa5> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Side A: Herakles and the Boar. Side B: Woman between warriors."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

