<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f9044c65dd0eefd053efc31fd5b271038754aa414e1d208d954b3d0e1ecaba08> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Side A: young warrior sitting with two women standing nearby<lb>Side B: two women
      and man holding armor"""@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f905dba3e4c0975a8f7278962e3110fbd195ef1fe35d8daebabe27d51d7a8bd0> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Side A:</hi> Hermes flying,
      carrying the infant Herakles. Hermes moves to the right, looking back over his right shoulder.
      He carries the infant Herakles in his left arm and carries his kerykeion in his right hand.
      The bearded god wears a chitoniskos and a mantle. He also wears a petasos with a folded-brim
      and winged boots. The tip of a scabbard can be seen at Hermes' left side. The flying motion is
      conveyed by the use of a knielauf schema and winged boots. The infant Herakles is wrapped in a
      mantle and wears a fillet in his hair. Both figures are labelled.</P><P><hi
      rend="head">Side B:</hi> Chiron. The centaur Chiron is depicted as a creature having
      the rear portion of a horse and the forelegs and torso of a human. The head and face of Chiron
      is human and bearded, but with the addition of horse ears. He wears a himation with decorated
      border which covers the transition between the equine and human parts of the body. Over his
      left shoulder, he carries a branch, a regular attribute, on which is shown his hunting catch,
      two birds and two rabbits. He gestures with his right hand. A dog accompanies the centaur,
      standing behind him. Chiron faces right.</P><P>The painter makes use of a great
      deal of added color. Added red is used on Hermes' mantle and chitoniskos, the brim of the
      petasos, part of the winged boots, and on his beard; on the mantle and fillet of Herakles; and
      on the hair, beard and himation of Chiron. Added white is used to decorate the garments of
      Hermes, Herakles, and Chiron. It is also used for the crown of Hermes' petasos, and details of
      the rabbits and the dog.</P><P>This representation of Hermes delivering the infant
      Herakles to Chiron to be raised is probably unique. A white-ground lekythos <rs
      type="vase">Copenhagen, National Museum 6328</rs>, depicting Hermes carrying an
      infant to Chiron, probably is meant to represent the infant Achilles, rather than Herakles.
      The tradition of Chiron educating Herakles is preserved in literature in <bibl>Schol.
      Theokr. 13, 7/9b.</bibl> The iconography of the scene appears to be derived from the
      much more common representation of Hermes carrying the infant Achilles to
      Chiron.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f91839443293c2b7de55c672270e562a6a6e65f943114353344c6b94c0f65e4b> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Temple; on the ridge marking the southern extent of the city, east of the Temple of Concord."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f92deb37eb7beff39fb3a8654a582349f72c45cd995b1c84fbb74b2816a041e1> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The wedding of Menelaus and Helen."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/f9300f9563abd8f6ccb3a3f169cc0a470267c19e1bc408ff0735cd8b104c2a05> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Wine-making scene. A satyr at left stands on a table crushing
      grapes in a basket, one handle of which he holds. Juice flows out to right into a kylix. Below
      the table grows a lotus bud. A maenad with white skin dances to right in red-striped peplos.
      She holds a drinking horn (keros) in her right hand and balances a kantharos on her left. She
      wears a red fillet. On her head to left is a tiny ithyphallic satyr, defecating and playing
      the aulos. He has a red tail and, like the first satyr, red hair and beard. At right, Dionysos
      stands below a vine in a red-striped himation with red hair. He holds a drinking horn (keros).
      This bearded man is presumably Dionysos, but is shorter than the maenad. There is
      pseudo-inscription in the field behind the maenad.</P><P>Red and black tongues are
      seen on the shoulder.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

