<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fa3b34f26014340e7b17e6cbaf20bd20a9a6c97c8dc5f1d191ce9b9706e5c73a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Eunomia (labelled), standing 3/4-view to the left, with her
      weight on her left leg, her right leg relaxed, wearing a sleeveless chiton, a himation draped
      around her legs and over her left shoulder, a long pony tail, and a black band necklace, holds
      a tray of fruit at waist level in her left hand, and hangs her right hand at her side; Adonis,
      seated 3/4-view to the left on a klismos, with his head turned profile to the right, wearing a
      himation around his hips and legs, and a grain crown, places his feet on a footstool, holds
      his right hand in front of his body, where it meets the hand of Eros, and kisses Eros,
      standing 3/4-view to the left, with his weight on his left leg, his right leg relaxed, both
      arms reaching around Adonis; Eukleia (labelled), standing nearly frontal, with her head
      profile to the left, and her weight on her right leg, wearing a sleeveless, belted chiton,
      part of which is rewrapped around her hips, and a black band necklace, holds a lyre in her
      bent left hand, and raises to the left her right hand, on which a small bird is
      perched.</P><P>Adonis has alternately been identified as Phaon (Beazley).
      Wehgartner suggests that this vase is a warning of danger in excess, and that Eunomia and
      Eukleia are here balancing Eros' lack of restraint.""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fa405d3349e28190c0d6125ccbbdcaa038a8f1ff179f6ab3663d6ef0e6146efd> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl><hi rend=\"ital\">BMCRE</hi></bibl> IV, 203, no. 1266; pl. 29.1; <bibl>Cohen 1880-1892, 2.338, 682 and 684 (obverse), 685 (reverse)</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fa43ff0b2049a295953f1357f225abd63dbda9a0cfad16261969ff49cb84e48d> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Seated figure wearing a himation--perhaps Apollo, Ge or Themis--with an omphalos held in his/her left hand"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fa493ab2397863df15883a8d0698fb7f8890f0400e6e0ea795073aad2ebecfc3> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Dionysos stands
      between a seated satyr, playing the double flutes (<hi rend="ital">auloi</hi>),
      and a maenad beating a tympanum, a type of large tambourine. The god stands with his torso and
      left leg frontal, his right leg in profile to the left, and his head turned toward the maenad
      at right. He wears an ivy wreath with berries in added white, a himation, and a chiton, the
      upper half of which is speckled with dots of dilute glaze. His beard is long and pointed, and
      his long hair falls in ringlets on his shoulders. In his right hand he holds a thyrsos, with
      white berries among the ivy leaves, and with his left hand pours a libation from a kantharos,
      the stream of wine rendered in added white. The thyrsos is drawn as though it were a
      continuous branch, but as ivy does not grow so straight, it may be the usual stalk of fennel
      with some ivy leaves stuck on the end. The satyr sits with his right leg crossed over the
      left. His seat is a rock or hummock, drawn with a single line of added white. He is balding,
      but has curly hair in back; his beard is short and stringy. He wears an ivy wreath like that
      of Dionysos. The maenad is dancing as she plays the tympanum, which has two rectangular
      handles on either side, like a bronze laver. She looks down, avoiding the god's eyes, as she
      steps forward with her right leg. She wears a belted peplos and, around her head, a "fillet"
      of two snakes mottled with dots of dilute glaze. The white fillet above her head is attached
      to one of the tympanum handles, so that it whips around as she
      dances.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: Orpheus is being killed by
      two Thracian women, who resented the fact that his music so bemused their husbands that they
      lost interest in their wives. They cut off his head and flung it in the sea, whereupon it
      floated to Lesbos, singing all the way, and became an oracle. Orpheus falls to the right, but
      looks back at the woman at left, who plunges a spear into his breast. He is nude except for a
      fillet and a cloak wrapped around his left arm and over his right shoulder. With his left knee
      and hand he supports himself on the sketchy groundline, drawn with added white, and with his
      right hand raises his lyre over his head. The instrument has one too many strings drawn with
      relief lines eight and a sound box made from a tortoiseshell. The maenad at right clutches one
      arm of the lyre with her left hand, and with the other hand wields a <hi
      rend="ital">harp</hi>, a short, curved sword like the one Perseus used to decapitate
      Medusa. Both women wear peploi, sphendones, and radiate fillets. The woman at left also wears
      a cloak pinned at the right shoulder by a black brooch. The cloak looks like a Greek chlamys,
      but may be intended to suggest a Thracian cloak. Otherwise, none of the characters wears
      Thracian costume, nor do the women have tattoos or other features to indicate they are
      barbarians.</P><P>A laurel wreath circles the vase below the lip. On either side,
      the rather broad and poorly executed groundline consists of groups of three maeanders to right
      alternating with saltire-squares. Bands of egg-pattern nearly circle the handle roots. The
      space between the handles roots is reserved, as is the side of the foot. An incised groove
      separates foot and body. Reserved stripes circle the inside of the rim and the top of the body
      inside.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fa4a95a2cb7c262ac6829b0c65736c636d98b12143176c1a026d4aeb07af3a2a> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
