<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fafdfb4ad27ef87f1f4d57a11b473148ae6262d650cc05481d6dd74aaa16f086> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl><hi rend=\"ital\">BMCRE</hi></bibl> I, 135, no. 108" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fb049395e97c019cbbc3407f5129baae5f87b022b2867947ec9463cdb5b6926b> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The vessel has a flaring mouth, and a flat and unglazed top.
      The neck is glazed on the inside to a depth of 5.0 cm. The handles are round, and entirely
      glazed. The foot is an echinus shape, with rays above. The panels are framed at the sides and
      the bottom by a thin glaze line, and surmounted by hanging lotuses and lotus buds between
      glaze lines.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Iris(?) and Zeus(?)
      are depicted. The winged female is represented to the right in the archaic running position
      (knielauf) with her head turned back. In front of her, a bearded male figure raises his hand.
      To the left are two bearded spectators. Iris wears a belted nebris over a short-sleeved
      chiton, a necklace, and a fillet in her hair; her skin is white. The male figures wear chitons
      and himatia.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: A girl is on a swing,
      with onlookers around her. She sits to the right on a rope swing, the seat of which has been
      fashioned from a diphros and the ropes of which are suspended from the ornamented border
      above. Two men watch on each side; all but the closest to her on the right are bearded. The
      furthest on the right raises his right hand, thumb extended. He is wearing a chlamys, the
      other men wear chitons and himatia. The girl wears a peplos, necklace, and a fillet in her
      hair; her skin is white</P><P>Beazley (BSA 32 [1931-1932]) suggested that the
      winged figure on Side A was Iris. Representations of swinging are rare in black figure vase
      painting, but there is another vase by the Swing Painter (<rs type="vase">Louvre F
      60</rs>) which also depicts it. Two red figure representations of swinging have been
      connected with the Aiora, or Feast of Swings (<bibl>Deubner 1932, 118 ff.</bibl>),
      but there is no evidence to warrant associating the Boston amphora with this
      festival.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fb059f70c7a236817ef92952476c94b58d60a756f0159d1ed5fdf5f3c543fc04> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl><hi rend=\"ital\">BMCRE</hi></bibl> III, 536, no. 1870; <bibl>Cohen 1880-1892, 2.251, no. 48</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fb061746c29881b16aaaa62fa6b79e1267ae732cbd8a08dee63a7beb0cbe0f10> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Side A</hi>: pentathalon scene with
      three figures. The naked and bearded athlete on the left, an acontist, faces right holding two
      javelins upright in his left hand, his right hand on his hip. The central athlete, who is also
      naked and bearded, again faces right, swinging a large, white diskos, his weight back on his
      right leg as he gets ready to throw. His left arm is raised in the air for balance. The
      right-hand figure, probably the trainer, is bearded and wears a long cloak which is fastened
      on his left shoulder, leaving his right arm and shoulder bare. He is facing the two athletes,
      and holds a long stick.</P><P><hi rend="head">Side B</hi>: boxing
      contest with four figures. In the center of the scene are two naked, bearded men, fists raised
      against each other. Both men wear himantes around their fists and little caps. To their left
      is a bearded man, fully clothed, who faces right and holds a stick toward them; he may be
      their trainer. On the right, a nude, beardless youth faces left toward the contest; he is
      wearing a band around his head and holds a bunch of himantes in his right hand. The hair and
      beards of the figures are painted red.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fb1726876f574f0047060c33b5cd9ed8395a788effee62b4446e2025b23dc24f> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl><hi rend=\"ital\">BMCRE</hi></bibl> III, 73, no. 305, pl. 14, 11 " ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fb18ace05328ff9244545d06f8d17ebc419646bf4be58388fd1072bfc521ead3> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl><hi rend=\"ital\">BMC</hi></bibl> Pontus, Paphlagonia, Bithynia, Bosporus, no. 3, with star in rev. field; see also <hi rend=\"ital\">Sylloge Nummorum Graecorum, Deutschland, Sammlung von Aulock</hi>, vol. 1, no. 297: both with obverse heads to right" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fb1ae0d2806af8c81c4b05e8dcb438d8c6d7d3b36226418271608bd771b8a8c5> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<P><bibl>Rossiter 1977, 288-89</bibl>; <bibl>PECS, 549-50</bibl>; <bibl>Leekley and Noyes 1976, 50-51</bibl></P>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fb2bd0a01a485ae55275b15e4a562b138106acef012891b78c248b1c53b649cb> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Tondo:</hi> full-striding figure of
      Dionysos with a satyr skipping merrily behind him. Dionysos, holding the end of a
      long-tendrilled grapevine in his left hand and a kantharos in his right, casts a startling
      glance back over his right shoulder at the satyr who appears to have just slapped the god on
      his back. Max Wegner points out the amusing juxtaposition of these two figures the massive,
      dour figure of Dionysos, and the light-hearted satyr who attracts as much attention for his
      spirited antics as does the god by the space he fills (<bibl>Wegner 1968,
      155</bibl>). Both figures are set on a ground line, the space below reserved in red
      clay. The border alternates single meanders and saltire squares; the meanders with minor
      exceptions run alternately clockwise and counterclockwise.</P><P>The exterior is
      decorated with figures of satyrs and maenads, arranged alternately, a group of five figures on
      each side, and complex palmette-designs at each handle. Wegner analyzes these compositions in
      depth in a comparison with six other cups bearing Dionysiac scenes (<bibl>Wegner 1968,
      pp. 156ff.</bibl>). As in the tondo scene, Douris has played off the naked, raucous
      satyrs against the heavily clad, more ponderous female figures.</P><P>Douris's
      favorite vase-shape by far was the kylix. Beazley in <bibl>ARV2</bibl> and
      <bibl>Para.</bibl>, lists 256 cups and cup-skyphoi to a mere 24 examples of all
      other shapes combined in Douris' oeuvre. It is surprising then, that of the three Douris vases
      in Cleveland, only one should be a kylix and the other two lekythoi.</P><P>In 1915
      the kylix was put on permanent loan to the Museum from the estate of Hinman B. Hurlbut. The
      cup, heavily restored at the time it was given to Cleveland, went unrecognized until 1953 when
      the adhesives disintegrated and the cup collapsed into a heap of sherds. These fragments were
      subsequently divested of large areas of puritanical overpaint and rejoined. The drawing thus
      unveiled was fine enough to spur the then curator, Sherman E. Lee, to send photos to Sir John
      Beazley who wrote back, "... the red-figured cup is a very good and important piece, the work
      of Douris in his late middle-period, about 480 or a little after." See <bibl>S. E. Lee,
      "A Cup by Douris," <hi rend="ital">AJA</hi> 58 (1954) 230, pls. 41,
      42</bibl>. Letter from J. D. Beazley, dated March 17, 1953. See also <bibl>ARV2,
      436, no. III</bibl>.</P><P>The collection of vases in Cleveland was not a
      particularly impressive one, and perhaps Beazley was being kind in his assessment, for the
      kylix is only a rather typical example of Douris' later work. The subject matter on all sides
      is perfectly straight-forward for this period.</P><P>Douris' drawing is best in
      the tondo figures, especially Dionysos' face, the satyr's kicked-up left foot, and in the
      left-hand satyr figures in both scenes of the underside of the cup. Other areas are either
      obscured by damage or are rather clumsily or casually drawn. The tight, patterned groups of
      lines for drapery folds and the heavily drawn hemlines are typical for this period of Douris'
      work, and along with the tondo border-decoration, are guides for dating the
      cup.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fb2dee2944ad228715377f2b9ba4a7e07ff4a4841d7df9c0124f6d45d756aff3> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl>Hermary 1984, 11-13 no. 7, pl. 7 (with previous bibliography)</bibl>; <bibl>Bruneau 1983, 68</bibl>; <bibl>Zaphiropoulou 1983, 53</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fb2f17c064e46a6a1e5d1983d1b50b5deb12746c08e82f803ce3809cec822902> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Asklepios and goddesses"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fb390ada1d14bde2330b29c174a519a7c00d15d5d0679080caf74a5f780fb95f> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Side A:</hi> Herakles kitharodos
      with Athena. Herakles stands at the left of the composition, facing right, playing the
      kithara, with his left foot resting on a bema. Athena stands at the right on the other side of
      the bema. Herakles wears a lionskin, belted at the waist, with the head drawn up. Under the
      lionskin, he wears a short chiton with a decorated border. He carries a quiver on his back and
      a short sword on his left side. Athena faces left. She wears a peplos, the aegis without
      gorgoneia, but with snaky edges, and an Attic helmet with a high crest. She carries a spear in
      her left hand, resting the end of the spear on the platform. She gestures with her right hand
      closed in a fist.</P><P><hi rend="head">Side B:</hi>Frontal chariot.
      The four-horse chariot carries a warrior and a driver. The four horses are shown in frontal
      view with the heads of the two outer horses facing outwards, and those of the inside horses
      facing inwards. The driver, the figure on the left, faces left. He is depicted as unbearded,
      wearing a long chiton and a fillet in his hair. He carries a kentron. The warrior, behind and
      to the right, facing right, is bearded, wears a Corinthian helmet with a high crest, and
      carries two spears.</P><P><bibl n="Theokr. 24, 109-110">Theokr. 24,
      109-110</bibl> mentions Herakles learning to play the kithara as part of his education.
      Scenes of Herakles kitharodos become popular in Attic vase-painting in the period circa
      530-500. They usually show the hero mounting the bema, and almost always include Athena. The
      scene of Herakles kitharodos appears to have no mythological or ritual connections.
      <bibl>Boardman 1975, 10-11</bibl> was the first to suggest that the scene has
      political implications. He sees an overall increase in scenes depicting Herakles in the second
      half of the sixth century B.C. and the creation of a new iconography as being due to an
      identification of Peisistratos with Herakles. In this interpretation the Herakles Kitharodos
      motif is connected with the introduction of recitals at the
      Panathenaia.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fb3e3b5d35d5e2e3103a6ebcc2107061ed27095f2d44eb06fb28d361c2d7782c> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl><hi rend=\"ital\">BMCRE</hi></bibl> IV, 437 f., nos. 384-388; pl. 60.3 (shield described as hexagonal)" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fb3ee01119314b74db72768acd1b0c5d10cbbef309f47e67b0dd0412cd89756d> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<bibl><hi rend=\"ital\">RIC</hi></bibl> VII, 451, no. 208; pl. 13:  4 1/2 Solidi; <bibl>Romans and Barbarians, no. C121</bibl>" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fb440dcb48b2c49cfe8dd007fd7e0552c9ee776ce8d74f05dca4c03bb55bd6da> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The main decoration of the vase is placed in a panel. Dionysos
      reclines at the right of the composition facing left toward a maenad who stands playing the
      kithara. Dionysos reclines on a kline leaning on his left elbow. In his right hand he holds a
      kantharos. His torso is bare, but a himation is wrapped around his legs, and he wears a
      wreath. The maenad, standing at the left of the composition facing right, wears a chiton and
      himation. A dog lies under the kline. Vines fill the field behind the figures. The lip of the
      vessel carries ivy tendrils and the neck, palmettes.</P><P>The Painter of Sevres
      100 decorates oinochoai and is related to the Athena Painter. The majority of his vases carry
      Dionysiac themes. The scene with Dionysos reclining appears to derive from representations of
      Herakles reclining, which become popular shortly after the middle of the century. The
      kantharos replaces the drinking horn as the most common attribute of the god in the
      540's.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fb55eeb71f73d5aa2a22fb0e04a425628416c11896c7d0547fd6e23a4f707e02> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
