<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fc86652217e946833247a0dfbc072eef23fd74a905f0ac511131b3119b9a3d72> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Standing figure of Athena, on a two-stepped base, striding and armed"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fc902e2aa68c887ec99d23df391c0936885812464d5df495d54a017cbf30f4b9> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A:</hi> heraldic crows, which
      move towards the handles but turn their heads inward, flank a large Siren with spread wings
      who moves to the right. <hi rend="ital">Side B:</hi> heraldic Sphinxes with
      sickle-shaped wings and tails which curl beneath the handles, flank a large swan which moves
      to the right. A total of nine incised rosettes in the field. <hi rend="ital">Added
      red:</hi> On A, crows' heads and central portions of wings of crows and Siren. On B,
      part of neck and wing of swan, Sphinxes' haunches, central portions of wings and large dots on
      bodies.</P><P>The deep cup or kotyle (often called a skyphos) which appears in
      Late Corinthian Geometric, is one of the major shapes for Corinthian proper, and remains
      popular until early Hellenistic times. Considered from the point of view of shape and
      proportion our vase fits neatly into a large group of Middle and Late Corinthian kotylai. The
      diameter of the foot is slightly more than half (0.53) the diameter of the mouth. More
      important, the foot-ring profile and its decoration strongly suggest a Middle Corinthian date.
      For an excellent discussion of the shape's development see D. A. Amyx and P. Lawrence, <hi
      rend="ital">Archaic Corinthian Pottery and the Anaploga Well, Corinth VII,</hi> 2
      (Princeton 1975)73-78.</P><P>The syntax of the vase again adheres closely to the
      Middle Corinthian canon (cf. H. Payne, <hi rend="ital">Necrocorinthia</hi> [Oxford
      1931] pl. 33, 1, 3, 7-9, 11 and <hi rend="ital">CVA</hi> Denmark 2, Copenhagen 2,
      pl. 91). Our artist derived his figures from his older and more talented colleagues. The
      Dodwell Painter, an influential artist of early Middle Corinthian vases, is credited with a
      fine pyxis in Munich (j211: J. Sieveking and R. Hackl, <hi rend="ital">Die Knigliche
      Vasensammlung zu Munchen,</hi> vol. 1 [Munich 1912] 26, ill. 37; pl. 10, no. 327; <hi
      rend="ital">EAA</hi> III, p. 154, fig. 186) which provides models for the distinctive
      crows associated with Sphinxes. Similar Sphinxes flanking Sirens appear on two pyxides in the
      Hearst Collection (ssw 9985 and 9500: D. A. Amyx, <hi rend="ital">University of
      California Publications in Classical Archaeology</hi> 1.9 [1943] pls. 28; 30,d; for
      other vases of this type see J. L. Benson, <hi rend="ital">Geschichte der korinthischen
      Vasen</hi> [Basel 1953] 45, 53) and heraldic Sirens of the type seen on our vase, flank
      birds on two pyxides in New York (Metropolitan Museum 21.88.63 and CP.54: Amyx, <hi
      rend="ital">supra,</hi> pl. 31, e-f. For other examples see Amyx, <hi
      rend="ital">AJA</hi> [1961] pls. 1-2). This artist has taken these compositional
      formulae (Sphinxes flanking a Siren; Sirens flanking birds) and "diluted" them by employing
      only one of the complex original elements on either side of the kotyle (crows replace Sphinxes
      on A; a bird replaces the Siren on B; for the latter cf. C. Dugas, <hi rend="ital">Dlos
      X</hi> [Paris 1928] pl. xxxiv, no. 481 and <hi rend="ital">CVA</hi> Denmark
      2, Copenhagen 2, pl. 89,5). The process of degeneration seen on our vase and others associated
      with the so-called Samos Group (Payne, <hi rend="ital">supra</hi> p. 309, c) has
      been discussed by Patricia Lawrence in <hi rend="ital">Hesperia</hi> 33 (1964)
      98-99 (cf. Amyx and Lawrence, <hi rend="ital">Corinth</hi> VII, 2, <hi
      rend="ital">supra,</hi> p. 57). The Putnam kotyle stands near the beginning of this
      process. It maintains a certain respectable grace through the careful incision of its hybrid
      creatures, especially the Sphinxes, and the delicate use of added color. It has only a hint of
      that perfunctory quality which characterizes the paintings on most kotylai of the next
      generation of Corinthian artists.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fca2fab92c7764262222bb2a14a32bbec881e262609081c114a69383877794a8> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "<hi rend=\"ital\"><bibl>BMCRR</bibl></hi> I, 397, no. 3264; pl. 42.12" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fcac1fa3fb10930b2cff210015257c310685b61bd2ceec5dbf10fa1e625a32e2> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: Athena stands, facing
      right, holding a shield in her left hand and a spear in her upraised right hand. The shield is
      turned so that the shield-device cannot be seen. She wears a belted chiton, a high crested
      helmet, and a cloak is draped over her shoulders. On either side of her is a Doric column,
      surmounted by a Nike statuette. Running down the shaft of the left column is the inscription
      <foreign lang="Greek">*N*I*K*H*T*H*S *A*R*X*W*N</foreign> ("Niketes [being]
      archon"). The inscription running down the shaft of the right column reads <foreign
      lang="Greek">*T*W*N *A*Q*H*N*H*Q*E*N *A*Q*L*W*N</foreign> ("One of the prizes from
      Athens").</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: <hi
      rend="ital">Pancration</hi> (wrestling match). On the left, a nude athlete stands
      frontally, his right hand on his hip, and his left arm extended at his side. To the right, 2
      nude athletes are engaged in a wrestling match. The one on the left is bearded. His left arm
      is wrapped around his opponent's face, and his right is raised to deliver a punch. His
      opponent bends forward and attempts to pull away the arm wrapped around his face. Behind them,
      to the right, stands a bearded man wearing a white wreath in his hair and a himation. He holds
      a palm branch in his right hand.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fcb1866fb271c471f9b2394cff3914dad8ef9a488d1fc3d97b6b1832e1f51962> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Charon in boat."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fcbd953bd41d92f09243c5c1e2b04fe60767352df10df4a22df2243c2c848504> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Eros between Diotima and Sokrates"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fcbdf93c174070a034d87efab2b2f48995e6bc4083e6a5f22f0a2d6f193d1562> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="head">Body:</hi> Judgment of Paris.
      Paris, at the right of the composition, moves away to the right, looking back and fleeing from
      Hermes and the three goddesses, Hera, Athena, and Aphrodite. Paris is depicted as a bearded
      adult. He wears a long chiton, a mantle draped over one shoulder, and boots. He carries a
      scepter in his right hand. Hermes, to the left of Paris, moves toward him gesturing with his
      left hand. Hermes is bearded. He wears a short chiton, a chlamys, boots and a petasos, and he
      carries the kerykeion in his right hand. On the left side of the composition, there are the
      three goddesses, facing right. The middle goddess is clearly Athena, identified by her snaky
      aegis, the helmet held in her left hand and the spear in her right. A small deer stands behind
      her. The other two goddesses are not differentiated. They each hold a scepter in the right
      hand and gesture toward Paris with the left. Added white is used for female flesh, and added
      red appears on beards and garments.</P><P>The Judgment of Paris is popular from
      the second quarter of the sixth century into the fifth. The iconography of this vase depicting
      a mature bearded Paris is usual for the late sixth century. The depiction of Paris as fleeing
      the approaching gods is one of two representations of the theme found in this period. In the
      other version, Paris calmly waits for them. The Antimenes Painter uses the theme of the
      Judgment of Paris on other hydriae, where he also shows Athena with a deer, although this
      animal is more appropriate for Artemis.</P><P><hi
      rend="head">Shoulder:</hi> Achilles pursues a mounted Troilos to the right, flanked
      by running women and Scythian archers. Achilles moves rapidly to the right holding a spear in
      his right hand and carrying his shield, shown in profile view, on his left arm. He wears a
      Corinthian helmet with a tall crest, a short chiton, a corslet, a mantle tied around his
      waist, and greaves. Troilos, mounted on one horse and leading another, gallops away to the
      right. The broken hydria is shown under the horses. The young, unbearded Troilos wears a cloak
      over his shoulder. On either side of this central scene, a woman in a peplos and mantle runs
      away looking back and raising her arms in a gesture of panic. Presumably one of these women is
      meant to be Polyxena. Bearded Scythian archers frame the composition. The figure on the left,
      standing facing right, wears a short chiton, a long-sleeved shirt, and a pointed Scythian cap.
      He carries a bow in his left hand and has a quiver at his waist. The Scythian at the right of
      the composition runs to the right, looking back over his shoulder. He wears the standard
      Scythian outfit of long pants, long-sleeved shirt, and pointy cap, with a quiver at his
      waist.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fcbf4510dbc74ada61c36e442ee97895a84903b8ff38746a190aef5672f6e7be> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Side A: Ganymede with a hoop<lb>Side B: Zeus"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fcc22c798493e3aa09291f20f136cebae9248815e5240576d8dd13d5206cd0cb> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Bibliographic references"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><bibl>CVA, USA 2, RISD 1, p.24, pl.15:
      1a-b.</bibl></P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026497> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fcce40b49757ff7de6976e1064d52c8373cf5084818e52ff0a40ab6a7dfc9cef> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Maenad."@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fcdb4c85e10c5bd0d22a016e17523accd36618021c66cc5c99c81fd703ac36ef> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>Neck: siren and wild boar. A siren occupies the neck on one
      side. On the other side is a wild boar with head lowered.</P><P>Body: animal
      friezes. Five animal friezes cover the body. On the shoulder, between the handles, sphinxes
      frame a siren. Below, roaring lions frame a wild boar to the left. Behind them are rams.
      Underneath are lions alternating with deer and rams. The frieze below shows lions and bulls.
      The bottom frieze contains lions, rams and boars. There is a ray motif above the foot. In all
      the friezes, all available space is filled with incised rosettes and irregular 'space-fillers'
      which are also incised.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fce36096156ee3ab384a35525de64f05846c8d58717dc32446a6913162304b65> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
