<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fe23182ae468320d6e23cd5bc952ab1277f22cf6c23d9db3f023644756bd68d8> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Tall palmette"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fe29872a89794b3c4f52d7a3eaa19cd1552c7eac612755941f2d40c063d9b4ee> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Condition"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P>The preserved portions of the vase are in good condition, with some pitting and
      wear, especially on the areas painted white.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435429> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fe30a8e3278f77a4577693bd41730172879ee71a1666d9c22d181474f24edd92> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Summary"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Relief, probably from a catalogue of liturgists with a representation of Eutaxia"@en ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300080102> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fe42a80b278151f351750a98b1cf056b1c369db58b801572a232f0c6b38da786> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Iconographic description"@en ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """<P><hi rend="ital">Side A</hi>: A hoplite has just
      taken leave of an older man, probably his father, and is preparing to board a chariot, which
      has already been already mounted by a charioteer. The chariot and horses are in profile to the
      right, and the warrior and father stand behind them. The father's legs and lower body are
      largely hidden by the horses. The warrior's body is obscured from chin to mid-thigh by his
      round shield, while his legs are largely concealed by the tails of the horses. The father, his
      age indicated by his bald head, wears a chiton, himation, and red fillet and carries a staff.
      The warrior wears greaves and a Corinthian helmet with a stilted crest, the latter overlapping
      the upper border and edged with a stripe of added red that trails off into two strands. The
      shield has a red rim and a white device, the latter now effaced and unrecognizable. The
      warrior's two spears cross his father's staff, which in turn crosses the goad of the
      charioteer. The latter stands erect in the chariot box, pulling the reins taut with both
      hands. He has a red beard and forelock and wears a long gown, the added white of which is
      largely worn off. The trace horse in the foreground has a red tail and mane, the latter now
      badly worn.</P><P><hi rend="ital">Side B</hi>: A warrior stands in
      profile to the left between two frontal horsemen, their mounts so severely foreshortened that
      only their legs, tails, breasts, and heads are visible. Both horses have red manes and both
      turn to look toward the warrior. The riders look away from the warrior, to left and right;
      they are beardless youths, each with a red forelock and short tunic and carrying a long spear.
      The warrior is represented in the same way as the one on Side A, with a round shield covering
      his body from chin to mid-thigh. He wears greaves and a Corinthian helmet with a stilted
      crest, which overlaps the upper border. He carries a pair of spears, and a swag of drapery
      emerging from behind the shield implies a cloak of some kind. The added white of the shield
      device has worn off, but one can see that it was a disembodied human leg, bent to suggest
      running (cf. Harvard 1960.314). The rim of the shield is red.</P><P>On either side
      of the neck is a lotus and palmette chain; added red is used for the cuffs of the lotuses and
      the cores of the palmettes. On either side of the shoulder, above the figures, is a band of
      black tongues. The groundline consists of a band of key pattern to right, framed by triple
      stripes. Below this, separated by triple stripes, are a band of upright lotus buds and a band
      of rays. Below the handles on either side is a symmetrical quatrefoil of palmettes linked by
      coiling tendrils, with a lotus flower on either side of the central junction and a third
      pendant below it; in the heart of the intersection is a cross. The fillets between foot and
      body and between body and neck are painted red, and there is a red stripe around the edge of
      the rim. The mouth is black inside and out, but reserved on top. The foot is black, as are the
      outsides of the handles.</P>""" ;
    crm:P2_has_type <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435430> .

<https://aa.perseus.org/id/text/fe44907540a80bd89db7ea8576d49da5bd489999c766f0cecabdc87546c9f693> a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
